ce, base }, null, 2));\\n'\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Pick game title with crypto.randomInt\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3%2Fwork/01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151/terminal/call-f323999c-6010-476c-aa1a-c58a53612948-7.log\",\"total_bytes\":207}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-f323999c-6010-476c-aa1a-c58a53612948-8","content":"{\"type\":\"Bash\",\"output\":[84,77,80,68,73,82,61,47,116,109,112,47,103,46,116,85,69,78,66,111,10,83,82,67,61,47,116,109,112,47,115,114,99,46,87,120,113,66,113,101,10,67,108,111,110,105,110,103,32,105,110,116,111,32,39,47,116,109,112,47,115,114,99,46,87,120,113,66,113,101,39,46,46,46,10,67,76,79,78,69,95,79,75,32,47,116,109,112,47,115,114,99,46,87,120,113,66,113,101,10,50,100,98,100,56,50,57,53,57,57,57,50,51,97,102,98,57,53,57,50,52,98,51,55,50,51,54,101,55,52,56,102,50,49,98,48,51,102,53,57,10,65,71,69,78,84,83,46,109,100,10,67,76,65,85,68,69,46,109,100,10,68,79,67,84,82,73,78,69,46,109,100,10,71,69,77,73,78,73,46,109,100,10,76,73,67,69,78,83,69,10,80,76,65,78,46,109,100,10,82,69,65,68,77,69,46,109,100,10,100,97,116,97,10,100,111,99,115,10,101,110,103,105,110,101,10,101,115,108,105,110,116,46,99,111,110,102,105,103,46,106,115,10,101,118,97,108,115,10,102,105,120,116,117,114,101,115,10,105,110,100,101,120,46,104,116,109,108,10,108,97,98,115,10,108,101,97,110,10,112,97,99,107,97,103,101,45,108,111,99,107,46,106,115,111,110,10,112,97,99,107,97,103,101,46,106,115,111,110,10,112,108,97,121,103,114,111,117,110,100,46,104,116,109,108,10,112,108,97,121,119,114,105,103,104,116,46,99,111,110,102,105,103,46,116,115,10,115,99,114,105,112,116,115,10,115,116,114,121,107,101,114,46,99,111,110,102,105,103,46,106,115,111,110,10,116,101,115,116,115,10,116,111,111,108,115,10,116,115,99,111,110,102,105,103,46,97,112,112,115,46,106,115,111,110,10,116,115,99,111,110,102,105,103,46,98,97,115,101,46,106,115,111,110,10,116,115,99,111,110,102,105,103,46,99,104,101,99,107,46,106,115,111,110,10,116,115,99,111,110,102,105,103,46,101,110,103,105,110,101,46,106,115,111,110,10,116,115,99,111,110,102,105,103,46,106,115,111,110,10,116,115,99,111,110,102,105,103,46,116,101,115,116,115,46,106,115,111,110,10,118,105,116,101,46,99,111,110,102,105,103,46,116,115,10,120,46,106,115,10],\"output_for_prompt\":\"exit: 0\\nTMPDIR=/tmp/g.tUENBo\\nSRC=/tmp/src.WxqBqe\\nCloning into '/tmp/src.WxqBqe'...\\nCLONE_OK /tmp/src.WxqBqe\\n2dbd829599923afb95924b37236e748f21b03f59\\nAGENTS.md\\nCLAUDE.md\\nDOCTRINE.md\\nGEMINI.md\\nLICENSE\\nPLAN.md\\nREADME.md\\ndata\\ndocs\\nengine\\neslint.config.js\\nevals\\nfixtures\\nindex.html\\nlabs\\nlean\\npackage-lock.json\\npackage.json\\nplayground.html\\nplaywright.config.ts\\nscripts\\nstryker.config.json\\ntests\\ntools\\ntsconfig.apps.json\\ntsconfig.base.json\\ntsconfig.check.json\\ntsconfig.engine.json\\ntsconfig.json\\ntsconfig.tests.json\\nvite.config.ts\\nx.js\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"command\":\"export TMPDIR=$(mktemp -d /tmp/g.XXXXXX) && echo \\\"TMPDIR=$TMPDIR\\\" && src=$(mktemp -d /tmp/src.XXXXXX) && echo \\\"SRC=$src\\\" && git clone --depth 1 https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge \\\"$src\\\" && echo \\\"CLONE_OK $src\\\" && git -C \\\"$src\\\" rev-parse HEAD && ls \\\"$src\\\" | head -40 && echo \\\"$src\\\" > /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/tmp/grok-goal-0593a9c03d8d/implementer/engine-src.txt && echo \\\"$TMPDIR\\\" > /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/tmp/grok-goal-0593a9c03d8d/implementer/tmpdir.txt\",\"truncated\":false,\"signal\":null,\"timed_out\":false,\"description\":\"Clone my-3dge engine at depth 1\",\"current_dir\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work\",\"output_file\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3%2Fwork/01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151/terminal/call-f323999c-6010-476c-aa1a-c58a53612948-8.log\",\"total_bytes\":520}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-f323999c-6010-476c-aa1a-c58a53612948-9","content":"{\"type\":\"WebFetch\",\"Content\":{\"url\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\",\"content\":\"# AI Hub Session Protocol v1\\n\\nAn **AI Hub session** is up to four players, each on their own device,\\nplaying rounds of games one after another. The hub runs everything around a\\nround: the invite, the lobby, the seats and names, the network, the clock, the\\nscoreboard and the results. This page defines how a game takes part in a\\nround, and how the copies of a game send data to each other.\\n\\n**https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md**\\n\\nThe hub tests every game when it joins the library, with the same test\\ndescribed in *Test it*, and keeps a game that fails it out of sessions.\\n\\n- Client: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js\\n- Harness: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\\n\\nTo ask an agent for it, add this to the game's prompt:\\n\\n> This game must support AI Hub sessions. Follow\\n> https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md\\n> (AI Hub Session Protocol v1) and pass the harness's automatic check.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Modes\\n\\nA game declares one mode.\\n\\n| Mode | What each copy runs | What the copies send each other |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `parallel` | its own copy of the round | nothing |\\n| `ghost` | its own copy of the round | each copy's own player: where it is and what it is doing |\\n| `shared-world` | one world for all players, run by the host's copy | what each player does, to the host; the world, from the host |\\n\\n- **Parallel**: every player plays their own copy of the round, from the same\\n  start. Each copy reports its score to the hub.\\n- **Ghost**: as parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\\n  characters where they are, when they are in the same area of the game.\\n  Those characters do not affect anything in the copy that shows them.\\n- **Shared world**: all players play in one world. The host's copy runs that\\n  world; the other copies send it what their own player does, and show the\\n  world the host sends them.\\n\\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` is played as ghost,\\n`co-op` and `versus` as shared world.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## A round\\n\\n1. The hub loads the game on every player's device, and the players get 10\\n   to 30 seconds with the game's card: what the game is, how to win, the\\n   controls. The card is the hub's, from the game's tags, or the game's own\\n   (*The card*).\\n2. The game connects and calls `ready()`.\\n3. The round starts on the hub's clock, on every device at once.\\n4. The game reports how it goes.\\n5. The round ends when every player has a result, when the host of a shared\\n   world finishes it, or when the hub calls time. The hub shows the results\\n   over the game, then removes it.\\n\\n### Tags\\n\\nIn the game's `<head>`:\\n\\n```html\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session\\\" content=\\\"1\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-players\\\" content=\\\"MIN-MAX\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-mode\\\" content=\\\"MODE\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-goal\\\" content=\\\"GOAL\\\">\\n<meta name=\\\"hub:session-howto\\\" content=\\\"CONTROLS\\\">\\n```\\n\\n- `hub:session-players`: the smallest and largest number of players the game\\n  takes in a session. The hub deals the game only when the number of players\\n  is in that range.\\n- `hub:session-mode`: `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world`.\\n- `hub:session-goal` and `hub:session-howto`: how to win and the game's\\n  controls, shown on the hub's card.\\n\\nOptional:\\n\\n- `hub:session-minutes`: how many minutes a round of the game takes. The hub\\n  shows it and uses it to decide when to call time.\\n- `hub:webgpu` with `required`: the game cannot run without WebGPU. The hub\\n  then deals it only when every player's browser has WebGPU.\\n\\n### The client\\n\\n[session.js](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js)\\nis pasted into the game unmodified, as the first script in `<head>`, right\\nafter `<meta charset>`. In a session it:\\n\\n- seeds `Math.random`, in parallel and ghost, before any of the game's own\\n  scripts run, so it gives the same numbers on every copy;\\n- gives the game an empty `localStorage` and `sessionStorage` of its own, so\\n  nothing stored on the device carries into the round, and nothing the round\\n  stores reaches the device's own storage;\\n- hands `requestFullscreen()` and `exitFullscreen()` to the hub, which takes\\n  its whole page fullscreen; `document.fullscreenElement` and\\n  `fullscreenchange` follow the hub's page.\\n\\nThe hub replaces an unmodified copy of the client with each new version. An\\nedited copy is not replaced.\\n\\n### Joining\\n\\n- `AIHub.active` is `true` from the first line when the hub opened the page.\\n- In a session, the game opens straight into the round: no title screen,\\n  menus or prompts before it, other than its own card (*The card*).\\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats: [min, max] })` returns a promise of the\\n  round's `info`, or of `null` when there is no hub after all; with `null`\\n  the game runs as it does on its own.\\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the game has loaded and the round can\\n  start, with no key press: the players answer the card.\\n- The round starts when the card's countdown ends or every player pressed\\n  Ready, and every game has called `ready()`. A game still loading then gets\\n  20 more seconds; after that its player sits the round out.\\n\\n`info` (also on `AIHub` after `connect()`):\\n\\n| Field | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `me` | this copy's player id |\\n| `players` | `[{ id, name, color }]`, ids `0..N-1` in seat order |\\n| `host`, `isHost` | the id of the copy that runs a shared world, and whether it is this one |\\n| `server` | `true` only on a copy that hosts a shared world with no player of its own |\\n| `mode`, `round`, `limitMs` | the mode, the round number, and the round's hard limit after the start |\\n| `seed` | a number, the same on every copy, behind `AIHub.rng(label)` |\\n\\nNames come from the hub and are never inserted into the page as HTML. Colours\\nfollow the seat: `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`.\\n\\n### The card\\n\\nBefore every round the players get 10 to 30 seconds to learn what comes\\nnext and say when they are ready. By default the hub shows its own card over\\nthe game. A game can make the card its own, as part of the game:\\n\\n```js\\nAIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card: true });\\n```\\n\\nThe hub's card then steps aside when the game calls `ready()`, and the game's\\nscreen is the card until `start`. How it looks and sounds is the game's: its\\ntitle, its music, its world, the players lining up. Introduce the game the way\\nit deserves.\\n\\nWhile it is on, the card shows:\\n\\n- the game's name;\\n- the players, each in their colour, and which of them are ready;\\n- how to win the round;\\n- the controls this player will use;\\n- the time left, from the `card` event.\\n\\nAnd it takes two answers, from the keyboard, a controller, or a tap or click\\non the card:\\n\\n- **Ready**: Enter, Cross (button 0), or a tap on Ready:\\n  `AIHub.answer('ready')`.\\n- **More time**: Triangle (button 3), a key the card names, or a tap on More\\n  time: `AIHub.answer('more')`. Answering `more` again takes it back.\\n\\nNothing done on the card changes the round. It has no menus, settings or\\nchoices, and play begins only at `start`. In parallel and ghost it draws no\\nnumbers from `Math.random`, which would change the round's start: use\\n`AIHub.rng('card')`.\\n\\n`card` arrives whenever it changes, and every second while the countdown runs:\\n\\n| Field | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `left` | milliseconds until the round starts by itself; `null` while paused or starting |\\n| `paused` | `true` while most players asked for more time |\\n| `starting` | `true` once the countdown is over or everyone is ready: `start` follows when every game has loaded |\\n| `ready` | the ids of the players who are ready |\\n| `asking` | how many players asked for more time (never who) |\\n| `asked` | `true` when this player asked for more time |\\n\\n### The start\\n\\n`start` arrives when the round starts. Play begins three seconds later, when\\n`AIHub.time()` reaches 0. `AIHub.time()` is the same timeline on every device,\\ngive or take about 15 ms: negative during the countdown, `-Infinity` before\\n`start`.\\n\\n### Reporting\\n\\n- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })`, in parallel and ghost: the player's\\n  standing during the round. `score` is a number, higher is ahead; `label` is\\n  text for the hub's scoreboard. The hub takes the latest about once a second.\\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })`, in parallel and\\n  ghost, once, when the player's round is over. `time` is milliseconds of\\n  `AIHub.time()`, lower wins; `score` is a number, higher wins.\\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })`, in a shared world, called once,\\n  by the host only:\\n  - `standings`: arrays of player ids, best first; players in the same array\\n    tie. It lists every player still in the round once; anyone left out is\\n    placed last.\\n  - `scores` (optional): `{ id: number }`, shown on the scoreboard.\\n  - `won` (optional): `true` or `false` when the round is a team result.\\n\\n### Time-up and the end\\n\\n- The hub may call time while the round is still going. On `time-up`, the\\n  game reports within `graceMs`: a `result()` in parallel and ghost, a\\n  `finish()` from the host in a shared world. A shared world with no\\n  `finish()` by then is void.\\n- `end` is the last message of a round. After it, the game takes no input and\\n  sends nothing, and its sound stops. The hub shows its results over the\\n  game, then removes it.\\n\\n| `end` reason | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `finished` | the round counted; `standings` is the final order |\\n| `void` | it didn't count (no result, a fatal error, skipped) |\\n| `host-left` | the host of a shared world left |\\n| `sat-out` | this player missed the ready deadline |\\n| `session-ended` | the session closed |\\n\\n### Leaving\\n\\n- `leave` arrives with `{ id, reason }` when a player leaves: `left`,\\n  `disconnected`, `not-ready` or `kicked`. It is final for the round.\\n- A parallel or ghost round goes on whoever leaves, the host included.\\n- A shared-world round is void when its host leaves, isn't ready, or has its\\n  tab hidden for more than 20 seconds.\\n- `AIHub.fail(message)`: the game cannot run in this browser. From the host\\n  of a shared world it voids the round; from any other copy it counts as\\n  that player leaving.\\n\\n### Scoring\\n\\n- Parallel and ghost: players with a `time` rank first, lowest first; then\\n  the rest by `score`. A player with no result is ranked by their last\\n  progress.\\n- Shared world: the host's `standings`. With `won`, the results show a team\\n  result instead of medals.\\n- Each player gets one point for every player they beat.\\n\\n`result()` and `finish()` may also carry `stats` and `awards`, shown with the\\nresults:\\n\\n- in `result()`: `stats` is `{ name: value }` and `awards` is\\n  `[{ title, detail }]`, for this player;\\n- in `finish()`: `stats` is `{ id: { name: value } }` and `awards` is\\n  `[{ id, title, detail }]`, for each player.\\n\\nPlaces and points come only from the standings, `time` and `score`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Sending data between copies\\n\\n### Parallel\\n\\nThe copies send nothing to each other. `send()` is not used.\\n\\n### Ghost\\n\\nEach copy sends its own player's position and what the player is doing to\\nthe other copies with `send()`. Each copy shows the other players' characters\\nonly while they are in the same area of the game as its own player, and those\\ncharacters do not affect its world. `Math.random` is seeded as in parallel.\\n\\n### Shared world\\n\\n- The host's copy (`AIHub.isHost`) runs the world. The other copies send the\\n  host what their own player does, and show the world the host sends them.\\n- The hub can run the host's copy with no player of its own: `AIHub.server`\\n  is `true` and `AIHub.me` is not among `AIHub.players`. The host's copy runs\\n  the world in that case too, with no local player, camera or input.\\n- `Math.random` is not seeded. `AIHub.rng(label)` gives the same numbers on\\n  every copy.\\n\\n### `send()` and `message`\\n\\n- `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` sends `data` to every other copy, or\\n  to the one player `to`. It returns `false` when the message is dropped.\\n- `AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => …)` receives it; `from` is the\\n  sender's player id. A copy never receives its own messages. Messages sent\\n  before another copy has loaded are queued for it.\\n- `data` is JSON, at most 16 KB.\\n- At most 20 messages a second leave each copy, with bursts up to 40; the\\n  client drops the rest.\\n- Reliable messages (the default) arrive in the order they were sent.\\n  Messages sent with `reliable: false` may be lost or arrive out of order. An\\n  unreliable message larger than about 1,200 bytes travels as several packets\\n  and is lost whole if any of them is.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Rules\\n\\nIn a session:\\n\\n- **Network only through the hub.** No WebSocket, WebRTC, or `fetch` to a\\n  game server or public broker. Loading the game's own files is fine.\\n- **No communication between players.** No chat, voice, emotes or custom\\n  names. Names come from the hub.\\n- **The page stays.** No `alert`, `confirm` or `prompt`; no `window.open`,\\n  new tabs or navigating away. Quitting, restarting or returning to a title\\n  screen does nothing; the hub starts and ends rounds.\\n- **Create + Options is the hub's.** On a controller, holding the two\\n  together opens the hub's corner bar; the game ignores Options while Create\\n  is held.\\n\\nEverything on this page applies only in a session (`AIHub.active`). Outside\\none, the game is not affected.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Test it\\n\\nThe harness plays the hub: it loads the game 1–4 times on a simulated network\\nand runs the checks the hub runs when a game joins the library. Serve it next\\nto the game:\\n\\n```bash\\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/harness.html\\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/bridge.js\\ncurl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/round.js\\npython3 -m http.server 8000\\n```\\n\\nThen open `http://localhost:8000/harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=2&auto=1`.\\nThe hub's own test also runs ghost and shared-world games with\\n`&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`, again with\\n`&scenario=host-leave`, and shared worlds once more with\\n`&players=2&scenario=server`.\\n\\nThe checklist fills in as it runs. **A game passes when\\n`window.__hubCheck.pass` is true**; `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes\\n`\\\"true\\\"` at the end. It checks:\\n\\n- the tags, `connect()`, `ready()` with no key press, and the start;\\n- with the game's own card, that Enter answers Ready;\\n- parallel and ghost: `Math.random` the same on every copy, and progress from\\n  every copy; parallel sends nothing, ghost sends;\\n- shared world: traffic both ways, and a round hosted by a copy with no\\n  player of its own;\\n- a player leaving, the host leaving, time-up, a valid result, and every copy\\n  told the round ended;\\n- no protocol problems, errors, dialogs, popups or sockets.\\n\\n**New round**, **Time up**, **Remove player** and **Hide host tab** run the\\ncases by hand; clicking a player's frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\\n`&limit=` sets the round's length in seconds and `&seed=` fixes the seed. The\\ngame is served from the harness's own origin.\\n\\nIn the AI Hub repository, `node scripts/hub-check.mjs <game>` runs the same\\nchecks with Playwright, then opens the game outside a session to see it still\\nruns there.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Reference\\n\\n### Tags\\n\\n| Tag | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `hub:session` | `1`: this protocol |\\n| `hub:session-players` | `MIN-MAX`: the number of players the game takes in a session |\\n| `hub:session-mode` | `parallel`, `ghost` or `shared-world` |\\n| `hub:session-goal` | how to win, shown on the hub's card |\\n| `hub:session-howto` | the controls, shown on the hub's card |\\n| `hub:session-minutes` | optional: how many minutes a round takes |\\n| `hub:webgpu` | optional: `required` when the game cannot run without WebGPU |\\n\\n### API\\n\\n| | |\\n|---|---|\\n| `AIHub.active` | `true` in a session, from the first line |\\n| `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats, card? })` | → `Promise<info \\\\| null>`; `null`: no hub. `card: true`: the game shows its own card |\\n| `AIHub.ready()` | once, when the round can start |\\n| `AIHub.answer('ready' \\\\| 'more')` | on the game's own card, before `start` |\\n| `AIHub.time()` | milliseconds since play began: negative in the countdown, `-Infinity` before `start` |\\n| `AIHub.on(event, fn)` | returns a function that removes the handler |\\n| `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` | parallel and ghost |\\n| `AIHub.result({ time \\\\| score, stats?, awards? })` | parallel and ghost, once |\\n| `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores?, won?, stats?, awards? })` | shared world, the host, once |\\n| `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` | ghost and shared world |\\n| `AIHub.rng(label)` | a generator of numbers in `[0, 1)`, the same on every copy, one stream per label |\\n| `AIHub.seeded` | `true` when the client seeded `Math.random` |\\n| `AIHub.fail(message)` | the game cannot run in this browser |\\n\\n`AIHub.probe()` and `AIHub.inspect()` exist for older games; the hub does not\\nread them.\\n\\n| Event | Arguments |\\n|---|---|\\n| `card` | `{ left, paused, starting, ready, asking, asked }`, with the game's own card |\\n| `start` | `{ in }`: milliseconds until play begins |\\n| `message` | `data, from` |\\n| `leave` | `{ id, reason }` |\\n| `time-up` | `{ graceMs }` |\\n| `end` | `{ reason, standings }` |\\n\\nEvents that arrive before a handler is attached are kept, and delivered in\\norder when the first handler is attached. Fields and events a newer hub adds\\nreach the game through the client unchanged.\\n\\n### Versions\\n\\nThis is protocol **1**, client **1.4.0**. Changes within version 1 only add:\\ngames on older clients keep working. A change that would break a game becomes\\nprotocol 2, with its own `hub:session` value.\\n\",\"content_type\":\"text/plain; charset=utf-8\",\"status_code\":200,\"bytes\":17587}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"0289e3e5-af91-4ca4-9279-38480d9970c6"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_8","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The project title is \"Trauma Compass\". The base name is \"Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4\".\n\nEngine commit is 2dbd829599923afb95924b37236e748f21b03f59. I will scaffold the game with new-game.mjs, run npm ci, and study AGENTS.md and API docs to understand the starter structure.","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The title is Trauma Compass. Next I’ll scaffold the engine workspace and read the APIs the game has to sit on."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-128a108e-1682-485b-86b8-9ab7a2949653-10","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/tmp/src.WxqBqe/tools/game/new-game.mjs","limit":150}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-128a108e-1682-485b-86b8-9ab7a2949653-11","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/tmp/src.WxqBqe/AGENTS.md","limit":200}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-128a108e-1682-485b-86b8-9ab7a2949653-12","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"^#{1,3} ","path":"/tmp/src.WxqBqe/docs/API.md","head_limit":80}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-128a108e-1682-485b-86b8-9ab7a2949653-13","name":"list_dir","input":{"target_directory":"/tmp/src.WxqBqe/labs"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":10665,"output_tokens":449,"cache_read_input_tokens":49920,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"823b502e-9be3-48e6-b71b-6f5564bff112"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-128a108e-1682-485b-86b8-9ab7a2949653-10","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→#!/usr/bin/env node\\n/**\\n * @file Makes a game from the my-3dge engine, and moves a game to a newer engine (PLAN.md WP 10.7, ADR-0024): the\\n * one command an agent runs to start a game. Plain JavaScript for any Node from 18 with nothing installed, since it\\n * runs straight after `git clone`, before setup. What a game gets, and whose each file is, is tools/game/files.mjs.\\n *\\n * Create: `node <engine>/tools/game/new-game.mjs <dir> [--name <name>]` writes a game into <dir> (made if missing;\\n * an empty folder, or a fresh repository's root): the engine's files, copied as they are and pinned by\\n * engine-lock.json; the starter game, labs/game/; the game's root files from tools/game/template/ (`TEMPLATES`); and\\n10→ * package.json and package-lock.json with the engine's pins (`gamePackage`, `gameLockfile`, from tools/game/files.mjs).\\n * Then `git init` when the folder is in no repository. It never overwrites: a README.md or LICENSE already there is\\n * kept, a .gitignore gains the lines it lacks, an AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md or GEMINI.md gets the game's after its own\\n * text, and any other file in the way stops it before it writes anything.\\n *\\n * Update: `node <newer engine>/tools/game/new-game.mjs --update <dir>` (what `node x engine --update` runs) puts this\\n * checkout's engine files in place of the game's, removes those it dropped, moves package.json to its pins, brings\\n * the root files the game never edited to its templates (`refreshTemplates`) and writes the new lock. Nothing of the\\n * game's is replaced or removed: it stops, having written nothing, on an engine file the game changed without\\n * declaring it under \\\"Local engine patches\\\" in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md (a declared one is kept), on a file of the game's\\n20→ * where the newer engine adds one, and on a package.json entry of the game's that the newer engine sets otherwise\\n * (`npmClashes`); `--force` lets the engine's replace them. It names the package.json entries it drops, or sets back\\n * where the game had edited the engine's (`npmLost`): the engine's pins win, as its files do.\\n *\\n * `--verify <dir>` prints `verifyGame` (tools/game/files.mjs) as JSON. Exit codes: 0 done, 1 stopped (the message says\\n * why and what to do), 2 a usage error.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * // node /tmp/my-3dge/tools/game/new-game.mjs ../dragon-run → a game in ../dragon-run, named dragon-run\\n * // node /tmp/my-3dge/tools/game/new-game.mjs . --name dragon-run → in the current folder (a fresh repository)\\n30→ * // node /tmp/my-3dge-new/tools/game/new-game.mjs --update . → the game in . on the newer engine\\n * gameName('/tmp/Dragon Run!'); // 'dragon-run'\\n * render('# {{name}}', { name: 'dragon-run' }); // '# dragon-run'\\n * @see tools/game/new-game.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process';\\nimport { copyFileSync, existsSync, mkdirSync, readFileSync, rmSync, statSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { basename, dirname, join, relative, resolve, sep } from 'node:path';\\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\\nimport {\\n40→  engineIdentity,\\n  FEEDBACK_FILE,\\n  gameLockfile,\\n  gamePackage,\\n  hashFile,\\n  hashText,\\n  LOCK_FILE,\\n  lockFor,\\n  npmClashes,\\n  npmLost,\\n50→  plan,\\n  sameDependencies,\\n  SOURCE,\\n  verifyGame,\\n} from './files.mjs';\\n\\nexport { gameLockfile, gamePackage, npmClashes };\\n\\n/** The game's root files: template (in tools/game/template/) → where it goes. */\\nexport const TEMPLATES = Object.freeze([\\n60→  ['AGENTS.md.tmpl', 'AGENTS.md'],\\n  ['CLAUDE.md.tmpl', 'CLAUDE.md'],\\n  ['GEMINI.md.tmpl', 'GEMINI.md'],\\n  ['README.md.tmpl', 'README.md'],\\n  ['ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md.tmpl', FEEDBACK_FILE],\\n  ['gitignore.tmpl', '.gitignore'],\\n  ['index.html.tmpl', 'index.html'],\\n  ['claude-settings.json.tmpl', '.claude/settings.json'],\\n]);\\n\\n70→/** Files a target may hold: a README.md or LICENSE is kept; a .gitignore gains the template's lines it lacks. */\\nconst KEPT = new Set(['README.md', 'LICENSE', '.gitignore', 'AGENTS.md', 'CLAUDE.md', 'GEMINI.md']);\\n\\n/** Instruction files the agents read: one already there keeps its text, and the game's follows it (`appendOnce`). */\\nconst APPENDED = new Set(['AGENTS.md', 'CLAUDE.md', 'GEMINI.md']);\\n\\n/** A game's name: an npm package name, from the folder's name by default (`Dragon Run!` → `dragon-run`). */\\nexport function gameName(target, name) {\\n  const folder = basename(resolve(target)).toLowerCase();\\n  const chosen = name ?? folder.replace(/[^a-z0-9._-]+/g, '-').replace(/^[-._]+|[-._]+$/g, '');\\n80→  if (!/^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._-]{0,213}$/.test(chosen)) {\\n    throw new Error(`\\\"${chosen}\\\" cannot name a game: give --name with lower-case letters, digits, '-', '.' or '_'`);\\n  }\\n  return chosen;\\n}\\n\\n/** Fills a template's `{{name}}` and `{{engine.*}}`; any other placeholder is an error. */\\nexport function render(text, values) {\\n  return text.replace(/\\\\{\\\\{([a-z.]+)\\\\}\\\\}/g, (whole, key) => {\\n    if (key in values) return values[key];\\n90→    throw new Error(`the template's ${whole} has no value: new-game.mjs fills ${Object.keys(values).join(', ')}`);\\n  });\\n}\\n\\n/** The values of the templates' placeholders for the game `game` on the engine `identity`. */\\nfunction templateValues(game, identity) {\\n  return {\\n    name: game,\\n    'engine.repository': identity.repository,\\n    'engine.commit': identity.commit,\\n100→    'engine.short': identity.commit.slice(0, 7),\\n    'engine.date': identity.date,\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n/** A template's text in the engine at `source`. */\\nconst templateText = (source, template) => readFileSync(join(source, 'tools/game/template', template), 'utf8');\\n\\n/** Reads a JSON file. */\\nconst readJson = (file) => JSON.parse(readFileSync(file, 'utf8'));\\n110→\\n/** Writes JSON with two spaces and a final newline (what Prettier keeps). */\\nfunction writeJson(file, value) {\\n  mkdirSync(dirname(file), { recursive: true });\\n  writeFileSync(file, `${JSON.stringify(value, null, 2)}\\\\n`);\\n}\\n\\n/** What writing `to` under `root` meets, if anything: the file itself, or a file where one of its folders goes. */\\nfunction inTheWay(root, to) {\\n  const parts = to.split('/');\\n120→  for (let i = 1; i <= parts.length; i++) {\\n    const path = join(root, ...parts.slice(0, i));\\n    if (existsSync(path) && (i === parts.length || !statSync(path).isDirectory())) return path;\\n  }\\n  return undefined;\\n}\\n\\n/** Copies one file, making its folder. */\\nfunction copyInto(source, from, root, to) {\\n  mkdirSync(dirname(join(root, to)), { recursive: true });\\n130→  copyFileSync(join(source, from), join(root, to));\\n}\\n\\n/** Makes a game in `target` from the engine at `source`; on a clash it throws, having written nothing. */\\nexport function createGame({ target, name, source = SOURCE, git = true }) {\\n  const root = resolve(target);\\n  if (existsSync(root) && !statSync(root).isDirectory()) throw new Error(`${root} is a file: name a folder`);\\n  const game = gameName(root, name);\\n  const identity = engineIdentity(source);\\n  const items = plan(source);\\n140→  const writes = [\\n    ...items.map((item) => item.to),\\n    ...TEMPLATES.map(([, to]) => to),\\n    'package.json',\\n    'package-lock.json',\\n  ];\\n  const clashes = [...writes, LOCK_FILE].filter((to) => !KEPT.has(to) && inTheWay(root, to));\\n  if (clashes.length > 0) {\\n    const named = clashes.slice(0, 5).join(', ') + (clashes.length > 5 ? ` and ${clashes.length - 5} more` : '');\\n    throw new Error(\\n150→      `${root} already holds ${named}: make the game in an empty folder or a fresh repository's root (a README.md, ` +\",\"content_concise\":\"1→#!/usr/bin/env node\\n/**\\n * @file Makes a game from the my-3dge engine, and moves a game to a newer engine (PLAN.md WP 10.7, ADR-0024): the\\n * one command an agent runs to start a game. Plain JavaScript for any Node from 18 with nothing installed, since it\\n * runs straight after `git clone`, before setup. What a game gets, and whose each file is, is tools/game/files.mjs.\\n *\\n * Create: `node <engine>/tools/game/new-game.mjs <dir> [--name <name>]` writes a game into <dir> (made if missing;\\n * an empty folder, or a fresh repository's root): the engine's files, copied as they are and pinned by\\n * engine-lock.json; the starter game, labs/game/; the game's root files from tools/game/template/ (`TEMPLATES`); and\\n10→ * package.json and package-lock.json with the engine's pins (`gamePackage`, `gameLockfile`, from tools/game/files.mjs).\\n * Then `git init` when the folder is in no repository. It never overwrites: a README.md or LICENSE already there is\\n * kept, a .gitignore gains the lines it lacks, an AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md or GEMINI.md gets the game's after its own\\n * text, and any other file in the way stops it before it writes anything.\\n *\\n * Update: `node <newer engine>/tools/game/new-game.mjs --update <dir>` (what `node x engine --update` runs) puts this\\n * checkout's engine files in place of the game's, removes those it dropped, moves package.json to its pins, brings\\n * the root files the game never edited to its templates (`refreshTemplates`) and writes the new lock. Nothing of the\\n * game's is replaced or removed: it stops, having written nothing, on an engine file the game changed without\\n * declaring it under \\\"Local engine patches\\\" in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md (a declared one is kept), on a file of the game's\\n20→ * where the newer engine adds one, and on a package.json entry of the game's that the newer engine sets otherwise\\n * (`npmClashes`); `--force` lets the engine's replace them. It names the package.json entries it drops, or sets back\\n * where the game had edited the engine's (`npmLost`): the engine's pins win, as its files do.\\n *\\n * `--verify <dir>` prints `verifyGame` (tools/game/files.mjs) as JSON. Exit codes: 0 done, 1 stopped (the message says\\n * why and what to do), 2 a usage error.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * // node /tmp/my-3dge/tools/game/new-game.mjs ../dragon-run → a game in ../dragon-run, named dragon-run\\n * // node /tmp/my-3dge/tools/game/new-game.mjs . --name dragon-run → in the current folder (a fresh repository)\\n30→ * // node /tmp/my-3dge-new/tools/game/new-game.mjs --update . → the game in . on the newer engine\\n * gameName('/tmp/Dragon Run!'); // 'dragon-run'\\n * render('# {{name}}', { name: 'dragon-run' }); // '# dragon-run'\\n * @see tools/game/new-game.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process';\\nimport { copyFileSync, existsSync, mkdirSync, readFileSync, rmSync, statSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { basename, dirname, join, relative, resolve, sep } from 'node:path';\\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\\nimport {\\n40→  engineIdentity,\\n  FEEDBACK_FILE,\\n  gameLockfile,\\n  gamePackage,\\n  hashFile,\\n  hashText,\\n  LOCK_FILE,\\n  lockFor,\\n  npmClashes,\\n  npmLost,\\n50→  plan,\\n  sameDependencies,\\n  SOURCE,\\n  verifyGame,\\n} from './files.mjs';\\n\\nexport { gameLockfile, gamePackage, npmClashes };\\n\\n/** The game's root files: template (in tools/game/template/) → where it goes. */\\nexport const TEMPLATES = Object.freeze([\\n60→  ['AGENTS.md.tmpl', 'AGENTS.md'],\\n  ['CLAUDE.md.tmpl', 'CLAUDE.md'],\\n  ['GEMINI.md.tmpl', 'GEMINI.md'],\\n  ['README.md.tmpl', 'README.md'],\\n  ['ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md.tmpl', FEEDBACK_FILE],\\n  ['gitignore.tmpl', '.gitignore'],\\n  ['index.html.tmpl', 'index.html'],\\n  ['claude-settings.json.tmpl', '.claude/settings.json'],\\n]);\\n\\n70→/** Files a target may hold: a README.md or LICENSE is kept; a .gitignore gains the template's lines it lacks. */\\nconst KEPT = new Set(['README.md', 'LICENSE', '.gitignore', 'AGENTS.md', 'CLAUDE.md', 'GEMINI.md']);\\n\\n/** Instruction files the agents read: one already there keeps its text, and the game's follows it (`appendOnce`). */\\nconst APPENDED = new Set(['AGENTS.md', 'CLAUDE.md', 'GEMINI.md']);\\n\\n/** A game's name: an npm package name, from the folder's name by default (`Dragon Run!` → `dragon-run`). */\\nexport function gameName(target, name) {\\n  const folder = basename(resolve(target)).toLowerCase();\\n  const chosen = name ?? folder.replace(/[^a-z0-9._-]+/g, '-').replace(/^[-._]+|[-._]+$/g, '');\\n80→  if (!/^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._-]{0,213}$/.test(chosen)) {\\n    throw new Error(`\\\"${chosen}\\\" cannot name a game: give --name with lower-case letters, digits, '-', '.' or '_'`);\\n  }\\n  return chosen;\\n}\\n\\n/** Fills a template's `{{name}}` and `{{engine.*}}`; any other placeholder is an error. */\\nexport function render(text, values) {\\n  return text.replace(/\\\\{\\\\{([a-z.]+)\\\\}\\\\}/g, (whole, key) => {\\n    if (key in values) return values[key];\\n90→    throw new Error(`the template's ${whole} has no value: new-game.mjs fills ${Object.keys(values).join(', ')}`);\\n  });\\n}\\n\\n/** The values of the templates' placeholders for the game `game` on the engine `identity`. */\\nfunction templateValues(game, identity) {\\n  return {\\n    name: game,\\n    'engine.repository': identity.repository,\\n    'engine.commit': identity.commit,\\n100→    'engine.short': identity.commit.slice(0, 7),\\n    'engine.date': identity.date,\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n/** A template's text in the engine at `source`. */\\nconst templateText = (source, template) => readFileSync(join(source, 'tools/game/template', template), 'utf8');\\n\\n/** Reads a JSON file. */\\nconst readJson = (file) => JSON.parse(readFileSync(file, 'utf8'));\\n110→\\n/** Writes JSON with two spaces and a final newline (what Prettier keeps). */\\nfunction writeJson(file, value) {\\n  mkdirSync(dirname(file), { recursive: true });\\n  writeFileSync(file, `${JSON.stringify(value, null, 2)}\\\\n`);\\n}\\n\\n/** What writing `to` under `root` meets, if anything: the file itself, or a file where one of its folders goes. */\\nfunction inTheWay(root, to) {\\n  const parts = to.split('/');\\n120→  for (let i = 1; i <= parts.length; i++) {\\n    const path = join(root, ...parts.slice(0, i));\\n    if (existsSync(path) && (i === parts.length || !statSync(path).isDirectory())) return path;\\n  }\\n  return undefined;\\n}\\n\\n/** Copies one file, making its folder. */\\nfunction copyInto(source, from, root, to) {\\n  mkdirSync(dirname(join(root, to)), { recursive: true });\\n130→  copyFileSync(join(source, from), join(root, to));\\n}\\n\\n/** Makes a game in `target` from the engine at `source`; on a clash it throws, having written nothing. */\\nexport function createGame({ target, name, source = SOURCE, git = true }) {\\n  const root = resolve(target);\\n  if (existsSync(root) && !statSync(root).isDirectory()) throw new Error(`${root} is a file: name a folder`);\\n  const game = gameName(root, name);\\n  const identity = engineIdentity(source);\\n  const items = plan(source);\\n140→  const writes = [\\n    ...items.map((item) => item.to),\\n    ...TEMPLATES.map(([, to]) => to),\\n    'package.json',\\n    'package-lock.json',\\n  ];\\n  const clashes = [...writes, LOCK_FILE].filter((to) => !KEPT.has(to) && inTheWay(root, to));\\n  if (clashes.length > 0) {\\n    const named = clashes.slice(0, 5).join(', ') + (clashes.length > 5 ? ` and ${clashes.length - 5} more` : '');\\n    throw new Error(\\n150→      `${root} already holds ${named}: make the game in an empty folder or a fresh repository's root (a README.md, ` +\",\"absolute_path\":\"/tmp/src.WxqBqe/tools/game/new-game.mjs\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":150,\"raw_output\":\"#!/usr/bin/env node\\n/**\\n * @file Makes a game from the my-3dge engine, and moves a game to a newer engine (PLAN.md WP 10.7, ADR-0024): the\\n * one command an agent runs to start a game. Plain JavaScript for any Node from 18 with nothing installed, since it\\n * runs straight after `git clone`, before setup. What a game gets, and whose each file is, is tools/game/files.mjs.\\n *\\n * Create: `node <engine>/tools/game/new-game.mjs <dir> [--name <name>]` writes a game into <dir> (made if missing;\\n * an empty folder, or a fresh repository's root): the engine's files, copied as they are and pinned by\\n * engine-lock.json; the starter game, labs/game/; the game's root files from tools/game/template/ (`TEMPLATES`); and\\n * package.json and package-lock.json with the engine's pins (`gamePackage`, `gameLockfile`, from tools/game/files.mjs).\\n * Then `git init` when the folder is in no repository. It never overwrites: a README.md or LICENSE already there is\\n * kept, a .gitignore gains the lines it lacks, an AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md or GEMINI.md gets the game's after its own\\n * text, and any other file in the way stops it before it writes anything.\\n *\\n * Update: `node <newer engine>/tools/game/new-game.mjs --update <dir>` (what `node x engine --update` runs) puts this\\n * checkout's engine files in place of the game's, removes those it dropped, moves package.json to its pins, brings\\n * the root files the game never edited to its templates (`refreshTemplates`) and writes the new lock. Nothing of the\\n * game's is replaced or removed: it stops, having written nothing, on an engine file the game changed without\\n * declaring it under \\\"Local engine patches\\\" in ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md (a declared one is kept), on a file of the game's\\n * where the newer engine adds one, and on a package.json entry of the game's that the newer engine sets otherwise\\n * (`npmClashes`); `--force` lets the engine's replace them. It names the package.json entries it drops, or sets back\\n * where the game had edited the engine's (`npmLost`): the engine's pins win, as its files do.\\n *\\n * `--verify <dir>` prints `verifyGame` (tools/game/files.mjs) as JSON. Exit codes: 0 done, 1 stopped (the message says\\n * why and what to do), 2 a usage error.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * // node /tmp/my-3dge/tools/game/new-game.mjs ../dragon-run → a game in ../dragon-run, named dragon-run\\n * // node /tmp/my-3dge/tools/game/new-game.mjs . --name dragon-run → in the current folder (a fresh repository)\\n * // node /tmp/my-3dge-new/tools/game/new-game.mjs --update . → the game in . on the newer engine\\n * gameName('/tmp/Dragon Run!'); // 'dragon-run'\\n * render('# {{name}}', { name: 'dragon-run' }); // '# dragon-run'\\n * @see tools/game/new-game.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process';\\nimport { copyFileSync, existsSync, mkdirSync, readFileSync, rmSync, statSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { basename, dirname, join, relative, resolve, sep } from 'node:path';\\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\\nimport {\\n  engineIdentity,\\n  FEEDBACK_FILE,\\n  gameLockfile,\\n  gamePackage,\\n  hashFile,\\n  hashText,\\n  LOCK_FILE,\\n  lockFor,\\n  npmClashes,\\n  npmLost,\\n  plan,\\n  sameDependencies,\\n  SOURCE,\\n  verifyGame,\\n} from './files.mjs';\\n\\nexport { gameLockfile, gamePackage, npmClashes };\\n\\n/** The game's root files: template (in tools/game/template/) → where it goes. */\\nexport const TEMPLATES = Object.freeze([\\n  ['AGENTS.md.tmpl', 'AGENTS.md'],\\n  ['CLAUDE.md.tmpl', 'CLAUDE.md'],\\n  ['GEMINI.md.tmpl', 'GEMINI.md'],\\n  ['README.md.tmpl', 'README.md'],\\n  ['ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md.tmpl', FEEDBACK_FILE],\\n  ['gitignore.tmpl', '.gitignore'],\\n  ['index.html.tmpl', 'index.html'],\\n  ['claude-settings.json.tmpl', '.claude/settings.json'],\\n]);\\n\\n/** Files a target may hold: a README.md or LICENSE is kept; a .gitignore gains the template's lines it lacks. */\\nconst KEPT = new Set(['README.md', 'LICENSE', '.gitignore', 'AGENTS.md', 'CLAUDE.md', 'GEMINI.md']);\\n\\n/** Instruction files the agents read: one already there keeps its text, and the game's follows it (`appendOnce`). */\\nconst APPENDED = new Set(['AGENTS.md', 'CLAUDE.md', 'GEMINI.md']);\\n\\n/** A game's name: an npm package name, from the folder's name by default (`Dragon Run!` → `dragon-run`). */\\nexport function gameName(target, name) {\\n  const folder = basename(resolve(target)).toLowerCase();\\n  const chosen = name ?? folder.replace(/[^a-z0-9._-]+/g, '-').replace(/^[-._]+|[-._]+$/g, '');\\n  if (!/^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._-]{0,213}$/.test(chosen)) {\\n    throw new Error(`\\\"${chosen}\\\" cannot name a game: give --name with lower-case letters, digits, '-', '.' or '_'`);\\n  }\\n  return chosen;\\n}\\n\\n/** Fills a template's `{{name}}` and `{{engine.*}}`; any other placeholder is an error. */\\nexport function render(text, values) {\\n  return text.replace(/\\\\{\\\\{([a-z.]+)\\\\}\\\\}/g, (whole, key) => {\\n    if (key in values) return values[key];\\n    throw new Error(`the template's ${whole} has no value: new-game.mjs fills ${Object.keys(values).join(', ')}`);\\n  });\\n}\\n\\n/** The values of the templates' placeholders for the game `game` on the engine `identity`. */\\nfunction templateValues(game, identity) {\\n  return {\\n    name: game,\\n    'engine.repository': identity.repository,\\n    'engine.commit': identity.commit,\\n    'engine.short': identity.commit.slice(0, 7),\\n    'engine.date': identity.date,\\n  };\\n}\\n\\n/** A template's text in the engine at `source`. */\\nconst templateText = (source, template) => readFileSync(join(source, 'tools/game/template', template), 'utf8');\\n\\n/** Reads a JSON file. */\\nconst readJson = (file) => JSON.parse(readFileSync(file, 'utf8'));\\n\\n/** Writes JSON with two spaces and a final newline (what Prettier keeps). */\\nfunction writeJson(file, value) {\\n  mkdirSync(dirname(file), { recursive: true });\\n  writeFileSync(file, `${JSON.stringify(value, null, 2)}\\\\n`);\\n}\\n\\n/** What writing `to` under `root` meets, if anything: the file itself, or a file where one of its folders goes. */\\nfunction inTheWay(root, to) {\\n  const parts = to.split('/');\\n  for (let i = 1; i <= parts.length; i++) {\\n    const path = join(root, ...parts.slice(0, i));\\n    if (existsSync(path) && (i === parts.length || !statSync(path).isDirectory())) return path;\\n  }\\n  return undefined;\\n}\\n\\n/** Copies one file, making its folder. */\\nfunction copyInto(source, from, root, to) {\\n  mkdirSync(dirname(join(root, to)), { recursive: true });\\n  copyFileSync(join(source, from), join(root, to));\\n}\\n\\n/** Makes a game in `target` from the engine at `source`; on a clash it throws, having written nothing. */\\nexport function createGame({ target, name, source = SOURCE, git = true }) {\\n  const root = resolve(target);\\n  if (existsSync(root) && !statSync(root).isDirectory()) throw new Error(`${root} is a file: name a folder`);\\n  const game = gameName(root, name);\\n  const identity = engineIdentity(source);\\n  const items = plan(source);\\n  const writes = [\\n    ...items.map((item) => item.to),\\n    ...TEMPLATES.map(([, to]) => to),\\n    'package.json',\\n    'package-lock.json',\\n  ];\\n  const clashes = [...writes, LOCK_FILE].filter((to) => !KEPT.has(to) && inTheWay(root, to));\\n  if (clashes.length > 0) {\\n    const named = clashes.slice(0, 5).join(', ') + (clashes.length > 5 ? ` and ${clashes.length - 5} more` : '');\\n    throw new Error(\\n      `${root} already holds ${named}: make the game in an empty folder or a fresh repository's root (a README.md, ` +\\n\",\"total_lines\":348}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-128a108e-1682-485b-86b8-9ab7a2949653-11","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→# AGENTS.md: rules for every change to my-3dge\\n\\nmy-3dge is a fully 3D web game engine written and maintained only by AI agents. DOCTRINE.md governs everything here:\\nread it first. The plan and its ledger are PLAN.md; generated docs start at docs/INDEX.md. Making a game? Not here:\\nread lean/README.md, or README.md's first section for a repository of its own.\\n\\n## How to work\\nWhere the doctrine and the plan are silent, use your judgment and record the call. When going against the doctrine\\nlooks best, escalate with your reasoning (below); never deviate quietly. On-demand work packages wait for their\\n10→measured trigger (PLAN.md §9.1); the playground (Phase 13, ADR-0025) is what fires them now.\\nTesting budget (ADR-0022): verify with cheap, objective checks (suites, hashes, counts), never wall-clock time on\\nthis shared machine. Long tests never hold up work (ADR-0023): the full run goes on GitHub after each merge, nobody\\nwaiting on it. Unclear after a cheap test means no proven benefit: stop, record it as an open issue starting \\\"Owner:\\\"\\nand move on.\\n\\n## Setup\\nbash scripts/setup.sh (idempotent; the SessionStart hook runs it) installs Node 24.22.2 (.nvmrc), resolves the\\nread-only sources, $MY3D2DGE_SRC (my-3d2dge@e37e4ee) and $SHARDFALL_SRC (shardfall@fa2dab6), cloning them into\\n.cache/src-* when unset, and runs npm ci. Never edit the sources. Offline it keeps Node 22: everything runs, but Node\\n20→goldens are recorded on 24. npm's warning that an .npmrc allow-scripts setting is ignored is expected.\\n\\n## The doctrine, as checks\\n1. Agent-readable: one concept per file, ≤ 400 lines (hard 600), a JSDoc file comment; docs come from comments.\\n   → npm run check, x docs --check\\n2. Agent-operable: every capability through npm scripts, node x or __engine, never only in a page.\\n   → command smoke tests, the help() check\\n3. Verifiable without a display: the sim runs headless; visual checks are numbers (ID pass, metrics, thumbnails);\\n   the HTML/CSS UI is checked through DOM queries. → npm test, x replay, npm run e2e\\n4. Assets: procedural first, then text and data; binary only with the owner's approval (fonts are pre-approved).\\n30→   → x check, x esc\\n5. Reproducible: fixed 60 Hz, recorded intents, named seeded RNG. Game code writes the standard Math; the sim swaps\\n   in fdlibm ports while it steps, so one golden per replay holds in Node and Chromium.\\n   → ESLint sim bans, replays, the drift test\\n6. WebGPU only, no WebGL fallback. Gameplay runs only on the CPU: sim-side code never imports gfx, the renderer, the\\n   DOM or Web Audio, and no GPU result reaches the sim. → ESLint layer rules, tests/e2e/parity.spec.ts\\n7. Common ground: game code meets npm, Vite, Vitest, Playwright and a public API in plain terms; a bespoke\\n   game-facing tool only where nothing fits, with the reason in its file comment. → verifier, ADR-0019\\n8. Quality under the hood: game code imports only engine/index.ts and engine/sim-api.ts; behind them, the best\\n   approach you can execute well. Every public export is documented and every error names its fix.\\n40→   → ESLint public-API rule, x docs --check, ADR-0020\\n9. Mastery: only the pins tools/deps.json records, and an upgrade is a work package of its own; write the pinned\\n   release's idioms (docs/THREE-DELTA.md). → x deps --check, banned names\\n10. Discovery first: target this container (Node 24, Chromium 153, WebGPU on SwiftShader); no production hardening\\n    outside Phase H, except ADR-0021's touch controls and the one-file playground (ADR-0023 amendment 5).\\n\\n## Escalate, don't stall\\nOwner approval needed (a binary asset, a new dependency, spending money, a repo setting, any other outward action;\\npushes, stage PRs, merges and branch deletions are pre-approved, ADR-0021), a principle can't be met, or going\\nagainst the doctrine looks best: node x esc open … → post it → wait up to 15 minutes, doing the preferred\\n50→alternative, never the action itself → answered: act on it. No answer: commit, decide, node x esc decide …,\\ncontinue, and report later conflicts without stopping. Record every escalation, conflict and call.\\nThe owner answers in the game: each escalation awaiting their review gets a decide check, in plain words with its\\nchoices, in labs/playground/ui/checkList.ts (gone once answered), and whatever only they can judge (feel, looks,\\nsound, devices) a try check there, never a checklist in prose alone; their pasted answers come back to record.\\n\\n## The loop\\n1. node x deps --update: adopt compatible releases first, in a commit of their own.\\n2. Read your WP in PLAN.md §9 and only the sections it cites, and the file comments of the modules you touch (not\\n   whole files). The sources being ported are read-only (node x src).\\n60→3. Edit. The after-edit hook formats and lints the file: fix what it reports.\\n4. npm run check → npm test. Browser specs (npm run e2e -- <spec>) only for what only they check that you touched\\n   (rendering, the page, the DOM) or a failure you are finding and fixing, never the whole set while you work.\\n5. Read out/**/report.json. Open images only when a metric points at one (use the visual-reviewer subagent).\\n6. Show it in the box or a fixture test. Update the ledger row in PLAN.md §14 (in ultracode lanes, report it).\\n7. Commit \\\"<area>: <what> (WP-x.y)\\\". Report the commit, the report paths and any escalations.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- SI units, +Y up, characters face +Z. Gameplay time is in 60 Hz sim ticks. Randomness comes from named streams.\\n- DOCTRINE.md changes only when the owner asks. Never edit out/ or node_modules/ by hand.\\n70→- Never skip, disable or loosen a test to get green. Baselines change only with a written reason.\\n- Merge on the quick gate (ADR-0023): squash-merge a PR once node x ci --local is green on its head. GitHub then\\n  runs the full run on main; nobody waits on it. node x ci --status (the SessionStart hook prints it) says how\\n  main's last one went: a red main is fixed before other work.\\n- At each gate and at the end of a goal: push, open the stage's PR, merge it, then wait for main's full run and\\n  report it. Start the next stage from the updated main (PLAN.md §11.1).\\n- No deployment: the engine is distributed and tested as playground.html, the playground in one self-contained page\\n  (ADR-0023 amendment 5, the owner's call of 2026-11-02; Vercel is gone). A change that alters it commits it rebuilt\\n  (node x html --write); the quick gate fails while it is stale (x html --check). The touch controls have no tests\\n  (ADR-0021); fix what the owner reports from the file.\\n80→- Never downgrade silently: every downgrade emits an advice code.\\n- Ask the entry, never the id: shared code reads registry hooks and never compares ids.\\n- No game content in the engine; the box's own content stays in labs/box/, the playground's in labs/playground/\\n  (PLAN.md §5.7).\\n- Docs are generated from comments: never restate a fact in prose (x docs --check).\\n- Deviations from PLAN.md become ADR amendments in docs/decisions/, with the plan edited in the same commit (§11.4).\\n- Blocked by the core? Write the smallest core change as a proposal in your WP notes. Don't widen your WP.\\n- Say plainly what you did not verify.\\n\\n## Done means\\n90→Verify commands green; npm run check and npm test green; the specs for what only T2 checks that you touched green;\\nshown in the box or a fixture; file comments and docs current; a template for any kind written by hand a third time;\\nQA for kinds with a QA family; a verifier review for L-size WPs; no new advice codes; escalations recorded; ledger\\nupdated.\\n\\n## Where things are\\nTask guides (docs/INDEX.md opens with them), each read when the task needs it:\\n- docs/PROGRESS.md: read first, every session: where things stand, what is next, the open issues.\\n- docs/TESTING.md: the tiers, and which unit tests and specs a change selects.\\n- docs/PLAYGROUND.md: the playground (Phase 13): the site, the move set and its reference values, the five cameras,\\n100→  the three looks, and how feel and looks are checked.\\n- docs/HANDOFF.md: handing the engine to game agents (the prompt, many games at once), starting in a new environment,\\n  and the box against my-3d2dge's stress-world.\\n- lean/: the default handoff, the engine as a library (ADR-0024 amendment 1); docs/GAME-GUIDE.md and labs/game/, the\\n  full workspace's guide and starter. Keep lean/README.md, lean/game.html, the guide and the starter true when a\\n  public API changes (`x docs --write` rebuilds lean/my3dge.js and writes the generated blocks).\\n  tools/game/new-game.mjs makes a game; `node x engine --test` proves one passes its own gate.\\n- labs/box/README.md: the box's scenes, settings and quick loop; adding a scene or a variant (a variant is a new\\n  scene file or a settings preset, never a change of meaning in a passing scene).\\n- tests/replays/README.md: the replay format; checking, recording and making replays.\\n110→- tools/templates/README.md: the kinds `node x new <kind> <id>` writes from a template and checks (a module, scene,\\n  prop, weapon, level…), where each goes, what shows it, and how to add a kind (`node x new` lists them).\\n- .claude/skills/: the skills, each loaded when its description says (x-loop after any change, add-entry for a new\\n  entry of a kind, stress-box for the box, determinism-debugging when hashes part, animation-authoring, visual-qa,\\n  perf-budgets, rapier, three-webgpu, escalation).\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→# AGENTS.md: rules for every change to my-3dge\\n\\nmy-3dge is a fully 3D web game engine written and maintained only by AI agents. DOCTRINE.md governs everything here:\\nread it first. The plan and its ledger are PLAN.md; generated docs start at docs/INDEX.md. Making a game? Not here:\\nread lean/README.md, or README.md's first section for a repository of its own.\\n\\n## How to work\\nWhere the doctrine and the plan are silent, use your judgment and record the call. When going against the doctrine\\nlooks best, escalate with your reasoning (below); never deviate quietly. On-demand work packages wait for their\\n10→measured trigger (PLAN.md §9.1); the playground (Phase 13, ADR-0025) is what fires them now.\\nTesting budget (ADR-0022): verify with cheap, objective checks (suites, hashes, counts), never wall-clock time on\\nthis shared machine. Long tests never hold up work (ADR-0023): the full run goes on GitHub after each merge, nobody\\nwaiting on it. Unclear after a cheap test means no proven benefit: stop, record it as an open issue starting \\\"Owner:\\\"\\nand move on.\\n\\n## Setup\\nbash scripts/setup.sh (idempotent; the SessionStart hook runs it) installs Node 24.22.2 (.nvmrc), resolves the\\nread-only sources, $MY3D2DGE_SRC (my-3d2dge@e37e4ee) and $SHARDFALL_SRC (shardfall@fa2dab6), cloning them into\\n.cache/src-* when unset, and runs npm ci. Never edit the sources. Offline it keeps Node 22: everything runs, but Node\\n20→goldens are recorded on 24. npm's warning that an .npmrc allow-scripts setting is ignored is expected.\\n\\n## The doctrine, as checks\\n1. Agent-readable: one concept per file, ≤ 400 lines (hard 600), a JSDoc file comment; docs come from comments.\\n   → npm run check, x docs --check\\n2. Agent-operable: every capability through npm scripts, node x or __engine, never only in a page.\\n   → command smoke tests, the help() check\\n3. Verifiable without a display: the sim runs headless; visual checks are numbers (ID pass, metrics, thumbnails);\\n   the HTML/CSS UI is checked through DOM queries. → npm test, x replay, npm run e2e\\n4. Assets: procedural first, then text and data; binary only with the owner's approval (fonts are pre-approved).\\n30→   → x check, x esc\\n5. Reproducible: fixed 60 Hz, recorded intents, named seeded RNG. Game code writes the standard Math; the sim swaps\\n   in fdlibm ports while it steps, so one golden per replay holds in Node and Chromium.\\n   → ESLint sim bans, replays, the drift test\\n6. WebGPU only, no WebGL fallback. Gameplay runs only on the CPU: sim-side code never imports gfx, the renderer, the\\n   DOM or Web Audio, and no GPU result reaches the sim. → ESLint layer rules, tests/e2e/parity.spec.ts\\n7. Common ground: game code meets npm, Vite, Vitest, Playwright and a public API in plain terms; a bespoke\\n   game-facing tool only where nothing fits, with the reason in its file comment. → verifier, ADR-0019\\n8. Quality under the hood: game code imports only engine/index.ts and engine/sim-api.ts; behind them, the best\\n   approach you can execute well. Every public export is documented and every error names its fix.\\n40→   → ESLint public-API rule, x docs --check, ADR-0020\\n9. Mastery: only the pins tools/deps.json records, and an upgrade is a work package of its own; write the pinned\\n   release's idioms (docs/THREE-DELTA.md). → x deps --check, banned names\\n10. Discovery first: target this container (Node 24, Chromium 153, WebGPU on SwiftShader); no production hardening\\n    outside Phase H, except ADR-0021's touch controls and the one-file playground (ADR-0023 amendment 5).\\n\\n## Escalate, don't stall\\nOwner approval needed (a binary asset, a new dependency, spending money, a repo setting, any other outward action;\\npushes, stage PRs, merges and branch deletions are pre-approved, ADR-0021), a principle can't be met, or going\\nagainst the doctrine looks best: node x esc open … → post it → wait up to 15 minutes, doing the preferred\\n50→alternative, never the action itself → answered: act on it. No answer: commit, decide, node x esc decide …,\\ncontinue, and report later conflicts without stopping. Record every escalation, conflict and call.\\nThe owner answers in the game: each escalation awaiting their review gets a decide check, in plain words with its\\nchoices, in labs/playground/ui/checkList.ts (gone once answered), and whatever only they can judge (feel, looks,\\nsound, devices) a try check there, never a checklist in prose alone; their pasted answers come back to record.\\n\\n## The loop\\n1. node x deps --update: adopt compatible releases first, in a commit of their own.\\n2. Read your WP in PLAN.md §9 and only the sections it cites, and the file comments of the modules you touch (not\\n   whole files). The sources being ported are read-only (node x src).\\n60→3. Edit. The after-edit hook formats and lints the file: fix what it reports.\\n4. npm run check → npm test. Browser specs (npm run e2e -- <spec>) only for what only they check that you touched\\n   (rendering, the page, the DOM) or a failure you are finding and fixing, never the whole set while you work.\\n5. Read out/**/report.json. Open images only when a metric points at one (use the visual-reviewer subagent).\\n6. Show it in the box or a fixture test. Update the ledger row in PLAN.md §14 (in ultracode lanes, report it).\\n7. Commit \\\"<area>: <what> (WP-x.y)\\\". Report the commit, the report paths and any escalations.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- SI units, +Y up, characters face +Z. Gameplay time is in 60 Hz sim ticks. Randomness comes from named streams.\\n- DOCTRINE.md changes only when the owner asks. Never edit out/ or node_modules/ by hand.\\n70→- Never skip, disable or loosen a test to get green. Baselines change only with a written reason.\\n- Merge on the quick gate (ADR-0023): squash-merge a PR once node x ci --local is green on its head. GitHub then\\n  runs the full run on main; nobody waits on it. node x ci --status (the SessionStart hook prints it) says how\\n  main's last one went: a red main is fixed before other work.\\n- At each gate and at the end of a goal: push, open the stage's PR, merge it, then wait for main's full run and\\n  report it. Start the next stage from the updated main (PLAN.md §11.1).\\n- No deployment: the engine is distributed and tested as playground.html, the playground in one self-contained page\\n  (ADR-0023 amendment 5, the owner's call of 2026-11-02; Vercel is gone). A change that alters it commits it rebuilt\\n  (node x html --write); the quick gate fails while it is stale (x html --check). The touch controls have no tests\\n  (ADR-0021); fix what the owner reports from the file.\\n80→- Never downgrade silently: every downgrade emits an advice code.\\n- Ask the entry, never the id: shared code reads registry hooks and never compares ids.\\n- No game content in the engine; the box's own content stays in labs/box/, the playground's in labs/playground/\\n  (PLAN.md §5.7).\\n- Docs are generated from comments: never restate a fact in prose (x docs --check).\\n- Deviations from PLAN.md become ADR amendments in docs/decisions/, with the plan edited in the same commit (§11.4).\\n- Blocked by the core? Write the smallest core change as a proposal in your WP notes. Don't widen your WP.\\n- Say plainly what you did not verify.\\n\\n## Done means\\n90→Verify commands green; npm run check and npm test green; the specs for what only T2 checks that you touched green;\\nshown in the box or a fixture; file comments and docs current; a template for any kind written by hand a third time;\\nQA for kinds with a QA family; a verifier review for L-size WPs; no new advice codes; escalations recorded; ledger\\nupdated.\\n\\n## Where things are\\nTask guides (docs/INDEX.md opens with them), each read when the task needs it:\\n- docs/PROGRESS.md: read first, every session: where things stand, what is next, the open issues.\\n- docs/TESTING.md: the tiers, and which unit tests and specs a change selects.\\n- docs/PLAYGROUND.md: the playground (Phase 13): the site, the move set and its reference values, the five cameras,\\n100→  the three looks, and how feel and looks are checked.\\n- docs/HANDOFF.md: handing the engine to game agents (the prompt, many games at once), starting in a new environment,\\n  and the box against my-3d2dge's stress-world.\\n- lean/: the default handoff, the engine as a library (ADR-0024 amendment 1); docs/GAME-GUIDE.md and labs/game/, the\\n  full workspace's guide and starter. Keep lean/README.md, lean/game.html, the guide and the starter true when a\\n  public API changes (`x docs --write` rebuilds lean/my3dge.js and writes the generated blocks).\\n  tools/game/new-game.mjs makes a game; `node