initial-scale=1, maximum-scale=1, user-scalable=no\\\" />\\n    <meta name=\\\"theme-color\\\" content=\\\"#15171c\\\" />\\n    <link rel=\\\"stylesheet\\\" href=\\\"../../engine/ui/touch.css\\\" />\\n    <link rel=\\\"icon\\\" href=\\\"data:,\\\" />\\n    <title>The starter game: my-3dge</title>\\n    <!--\\n      The starter game's page (labs/game/README.md): labs/game/main.ts starts the engine on the scene game:play and\\n      shows the round in the HUD. Ready or error: until the engine publishes window.__engine, the inline script below\\n      records a script that fails to load or throws, and shows it in #msg, so a broken page is one line of text,\\n      never a blank one; `node x shot labs/game` reports the same headless.\\n    -->\\n    <style>\\n      html,\\n      body {\\n        margin: 0;\\n        height: 100%;\\n        overflow: hidden;\\n        background: #15171c;\\n      }\\n      canvas {\\n        display: block;\\n        width: 100vw;\\n        height: 100vh;\\n        height: 100dvh;\\n        outline: none;\\n        touch-action: none;\\n      }\\n      #msg {\\n        position: fixed;\\n        inset: 0;\\n        display: flex;\\n        align-items: center;\\n        justify-content: center;\\n        padding: 24px;\\n        font:\\n          18px system-ui,\\n          sans-serif;\\n        color: #c9ccd4;\\n        text-align: center;\\n        white-space: pre-line;\\n        pointer-events: none;\\n      }\\n      #msg[hidden] {\\n        display: none;\\n      }\\n    </style>\\n    <script>\\n      window.__engine = { ready: false, errors: [] };\\n      function gameFailed(message) {\\n        if (!window.__engine || typeof window.__engine.help === 'function') return; // the engine reports now\\n        var fix = 'the console names the cause; node x shot labs/game reports it headless';\\n        var record = { code: 'APP_START', message: 'the page failed to start: ' + message, fix: fix };\\n        if (window.__engine.errors.length === 0) {\\n          var msg = document.getElementById('msg');\\n          if (msg) msg.textContent = record.code + ': ' + record.message + '\\\\nFix: ' + fix;\\n        }\\n        window.__engine.errors.push(record);\\n      }\\n      addEventListener('error', function (event) {\\n        gameFailed(event.message || 'a script failed');\\n      });\\n      addEventListener('unhandledrejection', function (event) {\\n        gameFailed(String((event.reason && event.reason.message) || event.reason));\\n      });\\n    </script>\\n  </head>\\n  <body>\\n    <canvas id=\\\"view\\\"></canvas>\\n    <div id=\\\"msg\\\" role=\\\"status\\\" aria-live=\\\"polite\\\">Loading…</div>\\n    <script type=\\\"module\\\" src=\\\"./main.ts\\\" onerror=\\\"gameFailed('labs/game/main.ts did not load')\\\"></script>\\n  </body>\\n</html>\\n\",\"total_lines\":76}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"58d767e5-caa0-491f-b464-9387faecaf1f"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_10","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The game is scaffolded. I will not run scripts/setup.sh.\n\nI will run npm ci. I am reading the API documentation for scene definitions, custom intents, geometry, and engine configuration. The simulation will be pure TypeScript hosted by the scene. I need to respect line limits and engine constraints.\n\nI am checking how npm test includes labs/game tests, eslint import restrictions, oneFile inlineBuild, and GAME-GUIDE recipes. The simulation must cover all tool/target pairs with effects or refusals, ensure the bot wins every case, give strains distinct transitions, validate story content, and support the sequencer and balance sliders.\n\nThe simulation will be a pure TypeScript module imported by tests. The engine scene will call sim.step(hands) from fixedUpdate and read state for rendering. Tests can import the module directly. Engine replays will feed hands through inspector.input.axis.\n\nThe simulation must use a seeded RNG instead of Math.random. Engine fdlibm replaces Math inside the scene, but native Math is used in Node tests. Small float differences could shift bot outcomes, though a robust bot should still win in both environments.\n\nI will avoid trigonometry in gameplay and round positions to 1/256 m. State hashes will be deterministic JSON of quantized values. I am checking the labs eslint config and check script for import rules and line limits.\n\nI will replace the starter scene and its tests rather than delete them. The engine's own tests must still pass. I need to update main.ts if play.ts is removed. Golden replays break whenever play changes.\n\nThe verify script runs `node x replay labs/game/replays --browser sim`. I need to ensure this passes. I am weighing three options: keep a trivial scene replay, record a short replay of the new game, or see if an empty replays folder is acceptable. The starter bot.replay.json will break once the scene changes.\n\nI will remove the starter replay and generate a new one once the game works. I am now reading the replay tool behavior.\n\nThe simulation design is being finalized so it can be written quickly. PLAN.md referenced Appendix A for balance keys, but that appendix was missing, so I will define reasonable defaults from sections 3-7 and expose them as sliders for vitals drain and bleed rates.\n\nI am defining the operation state types. ToolId covers scalpel, forceps, suture, drain, laser, syringe, gel, and scanner. TargetKind enumerates pools, shards, wounds, guides, masses, nodules, bleeder nodes, abrasions, numbs, flaps, cocoons, lulls, matrons, solaces, reveries, decoys, balm nodes, and balm cells.\n\nTarget fields are being defined for pools, guides, wounds, and other body parts. Seat state will track tool selection, score, and chain.\n\nSeat state now includes bestChain, gift status and charges, pulse timing, held targets, syringe charge, laser heat, gel state, and lastGiftStart. Patient state tracks vitals, maxVitals, and sites. Operation state holds caseId, seed, difficulty tier, and the full seat array.\n\nOperation state now includes patients, site, targets, phase, field ticks, clockLeft, slips, slipLimit, status, failReason, code, events, ratings, balance, attract, tick, and hash. The simulation step order will read hands and update cursors first, then run tool actions.\n\nThe simulation step order now includes strains, bleeding and vitals, clock, phase checks, and events. Field time delta is 25 ticks during Stillpoint and 100 otherwise. Second Pulse removes slowdown, prevents vitals from falling, heals +1.5 for clean actions, and doubles chain counts. The scalpel tool records points along a guide path as the player nears its start.\n\nScalpel mechanics are now defined. Cutting distance is sampled against thresholds to rate completion. A slip occurs if no guide is within one meter for 0.2 seconds. Completed cuts spawn or mark the mass as liftable.\n\nForceps attaches to nearby grabbables like shards, masses, cocoons, tendril handles, and flap handles. The item releases if the cursor moves more than 1.2 meters away. Pulling faster than 8 m/s causes a rough injury with minor bleed. Flap lifting depends on cursor distance from the hinge; exceeding 80 percent in the band clips the patient for eight seconds.\n\nSuture mechanics begin with wound segments.\n\nSuture stitching will place zigzag stitches each time the cursor crosses the wound line, provided it is at least 0.45 meters from the last stitch. The required number of stitches equals the wound length divided by spacing. Completion is rated on spacing evenness and time.\n\nDrain mechanics use real time while the field clock slows. Tool progress runs at full speed in simulation steps, but bleeding and pool shrinkage follow field time.\n\nLaser mechanics are now defined. Holding over a nodule for 1.2 seconds pops it. Over a bleeder it cauterizes and deals parasite damage. Laser heat lasts six seconds before a two-second cooldown. One second on healthy tissue causes a slip.\n\nSyringe mechanics are now defined. The stabilizer charges for one second on hold and injects on release; a band of 0.75 to 0.95 yields +24 TEXTBOOK. The binder requires holding for 0.4 seconds on a marked weak point.\n\nGel paints a 0.7 radius coverage grid. It heals at ninety percent of an abrasion, numb, or closed wound, and dissolves balm cells within that radius.\n\nScanner reveals buried targets within three meters for six seconds when held.\n\nUsing a tool on the wrong target produces a refusal event with a line but no penalty.\n\nRatings scale TEXTBOOK by 1.5, CLEAN by 1, ROUGH by 0.5, and SLIP resets the chain. Chain gains +0.1 per TEXTBOOK or CLEAN, capped at ×2. Second Pulse doubles chain gain to +0.2.\n\nFlatline code mechanics are next.\n\nVitals drain to zero triggers an eight-second Code sequence. Both paddles must press during the green band window. Two tries are allowed. Success restores twenty-five vitals. Failure ends the operation.\n\nThe green band is a charge that oscillates. It is active when the phase sits in a window. Bots should press when `code.band` is true.\n\nLull mechanics are now defined. Its aura drains vitals at 0.6 per second while alive and dims the ECG flag. Every six seconds it telegraphs a nuzzle for 1.5 seconds before applying a numb patch. Laser exposure for 1.5 seconds kills it and leaves a cocoon.\n\nMatron mechanics are now defined. It is armored except during a 2.5-second yawn every eight seconds. The binder hits 50 percent in its maw, and it spawns a lull every ten seconds.\n\nSolace splits into the next generation when a scalpel stroke crosses it at least 1.5 meters. Laser damage is restricted to generation three. After five seconds of latching, it telegraphs for two seconds before bleeding and draining five vitals. The cap is twelve, and the mother sits under a flap.\n\nReverie remains hidden until scanned. Laser hits last three and five seconds across three exposures. Decoys pop on scan, and a lance telegraph precedes them.\n\nBalm cells spread across the field. Lacquer blocks stitching and draining. Gel clears them, but laser clears them faster while dealing half a vital. Two binder doses kill a node and fade the film over six seconds.\n\nMercy phases are being outlined.\n\nMercy phase one uses motes as shields, laser exposure for 0.6 seconds each, sleep rings, and three binders to advance. Phase two triggers heart stop Code without costing a try if either shock lands, followed by lacerations across two rounds. Phase three burrows the scalpel guide, spawns five tendrils, offers a free gift, demands three binders within that gift, then final code.\n\nStrain behaviors will be deterministic and bot-aware via `needs` and `vulnerable` state flags. Cases are defined as data with phases containing spawn lists and `until` conditions such as `clear`, `stitched`, `killed`, or specific counts.\n\nThe bot reads hints and target states rather than hardcoding case IDs. It follows the strain state machine's `objective` for complex sequences like Mercy phases.\n\nEach tick it checks for Code first. If vitals are low and a syringe is available, it charges and injects. It drains pools covering items or large pools, and sutures deep open wounds.\n\nThe bot will follow scalpel paths as waypoints along a polyline. It moves the cursor toward points at human speed or faster for tests, sets tools, and holds them when actions require it. Cases are expected to finish within four-to-eight-minute clocks, so the bot runs at roughly eight meters per second.\n\nEight-minute cases need roughly twenty-eight thousand steps. Twenty-four test runs should finish in about twenty seconds at normal speed. The bot can run at twelve meters per second if needed, but the long clocks make that unnecessary.\n\nThe verification plan does not require a dead stretch check. The bot must simply win all eight story cases.\n\nStory scenes will run 12 to 35 short boxes each. There are roughly 240 boxes total across the sixteen case scenes plus an opening and ending. Voice synthesis will use a deterministic hash of letters to syllables, and the test will verify this determinism. A never-use names scan will filter out forbidden names. Postcards will be generated per rank for each patient.\n\nThe director flow is now mapped end to end. It runs title, story, briefing, operation, debrief, postcard, and credits in sequence, unlocking free surgery at the end. Tests will drive this entirely without a browser by pairing the director with the bot.\n\nThe UI will be an HTML overlay with large text and data-game attributes. Screens include title, board, story, briefing, operation HUD, pause, handbook, settings, scrub-in, about, dev mode, attract mode, touch gate, no WebGPU notice, and hub card.\n\nAudio will use defineSong and defineSfx if the API is quick to learn, plus Web Audio synthesis inside the page code.\n\nThe audio test requires an AnalyserNode to detect music at 0.1 volume, a synthesized effect, and no audio files. I will try the engine's defineSong and defineSfx first, falling back to raw Web Audio if the API is too complex. For the visual field, a live mesh is needed to fill the frame substantially; the title screen will use a 3D OR instead of a delicate sheet.\n\nThe launch check requires a substantially filled title or operating field. I will boot the engine with a lit room containing boxes, a lamp, and a table so the title screen is never blank. Then I will add the sheet mesh for operations and overlay the HTML UI. I need to read the GAME-GUIDE for prop spawning and look definitions. The createEngine call must only use known settings plus game.* keys I define, because unknown keys refuse to start.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-8a623feb-8949-47f6-bc5d-81c1b29d958c-18","name":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"export TMPDIR=/tmp/g.tUENBo\nexport CHROMIUM_PATH=/home/micha/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium-1243/chrome-linux64/chrome\ncd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731\nnpm ci --ignore-scripts 2>&1 | tail -30\necho NPM_EXIT:$?","description":"Install engine npm dependencies"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-8a623feb-8949-47f6-bc5d-81c1b29d958c-19","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"^## |^### ","path":"/tmp/src.WxqBqe/docs/GAME-GUIDE.md","head_limit":60}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-8a623feb-8949-47f6-bc5d-81c1b29d958c-20","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"defineGeometry|defineLook|defineSong|defineSfx|createAudio|axis\\(|defineProp|spawnProp|boxGeometry|defineCamera","path":"/tmp/src.WxqBqe/docs/API.md","head_limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-8a623feb-8949-47f6-bc5d-81c1b29d958c-21","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/package.json","limit":80}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8124,"output_tokens":4327,"cache_read_input_tokens":68992,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"6b9a986c-2669-430a-9511-8ce9a9e66360"}
