ks no standard tool covers, last in `npm run check` (PLAN.md §8.1).\"},{\"line_number\":15,\"content\":\" * sources, it carries its npm dependencies' binaries inline (Rapier's WebAssembly, three.js's SMAA textures), as the\"},{\"line_number\":46,\"content\":\"  /** One short line for the output, such as `214 files`. */\"},{\"line_number\":99,\"content\":\"/** A game's own approvals, and their keys (see the file comment). */\"},{\"line_number\":149,\"content\":\"/** The 1-based line of character `index` in `text`. */\"},{\"line_number\":150,\"content\":\"const lineAt = (text: string, index: number) => text.slice(0, index).split('\\\\n').length;\"},{\"line_number\":231,\"content\":\" * skipped, never counted: it is no asset, and reading it would crash the scan (see the file comment).\"},{\"line_number\":243,\"content\":\"    if (file === ONE_FILE) continue; // a build of the scanned sources (see the file comment)\"},{\"line_number\":271,\"content\":\"      failures.push({ id: 'ASSET_DATA_URI', message, file, line: lineAt(text, match.index) });\"},{\"line_number\":275,\"content\":\"      failures.push({ id: 'ASSET_BASE64', message, file, line: lineAt(text, match.index) });\"},{\"line_number\":334,\"content\":\"  const lines = plugins.map((plugin, i) => {\"},{\"line_number\":350,\"content\":\"    lines,\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/tools/checkAll.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":2,\"content\":\" * @file `npm run check` (T0, PLAN.md §8.1–§8.2): runs `tsc`, ESLint, Prettier and `node x check` at the same time and\"},{\"line_number\":24,\"content\":\"/** One T0 tool: its name and its command line, run from the repository root. */\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"e186a8b4-fa96-42a0-b5d2-f1ca7a93d6b3"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_15","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The max-lines warning threshold is 400. The error threshold is 600.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-00965fab-86bd-4bd0-bf2f-0a5d0aa03fa0-35","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/tools/eslint/askTheEntry.ts","limit":80}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-00965fab-86bd-4bd0-bf2f-0a5d0aa03fa0-36","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/tools/eslint/simSide.ts","limit":80}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-00965fab-86bd-4bd0-bf2f-0a5d0aa03fa0-37","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"function defineScene|systems\\.add|intents","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/engine/sim/scene.ts","head_limit":20}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-00965fab-86bd-4bd0-bf2f-0a5d0aa03fa0-38","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/box/sounds/effects.ts","limit":80}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2637,"output_tokens":531,"cache_read_input_tokens":98688,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"aedc0d0e-cb57-4a15-8fde-f05a4e6bc8b6"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-00965fab-86bd-4bd0-bf2f-0a5d0aa03fa0-35","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The local rule \\\"ask the entry, never the id\\\" (PLAN.md §6.6, ADR-0012): shared code reads an entry's hooks and\\n * fields, each with a default on the kind's schema, and never compares a registered id, so new content works without\\n * editing the code that uses it. No stock rule expresses this.\\n *\\n * It reports, in `engine/` outside `*.test.ts`:\\n * - `===`, `!==`, `==` and `!=` between a string and either an id-shaped string (`'move:slash'`: a kind, a colon, a\\n *   name) or an id (`id`, `x.id`, `targetId`, `x.ownerId`);\\n * - `switch` on an id, or with an id-shaped `case`;\\n10→ * - `[…].includes(x.id)` or `.indexOf(…)` over listed strings, and `x.id.startsWith('…')`, `.endsWith('…')`.\\n * Comparing two variables (`entry.id === id`, a registry lookup) is fine, and so is an event or message type\\n * (`e.type === 'hit:start'`, `eventType`), whose colon strings are not ids.\\n *\\n * Invariants: written now, switched on by WP 1.2 with the registry (`SWITCHES.askTheEntry` in eslint.config.js).\\n *\\n * @example\\n * // engine/anim/react.ts: if (move.id === 'move:slash') flinch();\\n * // → local/ask-the-entry: Ask the entry, never the id: … give the kind a hook with a default …\\n * @see tools/eslint/askTheEntry.test.ts\\n20→ */\\nimport type { Rule } from 'eslint';\\nimport type { Node as EsNode } from 'estree';\\n\\n/** A registered id: a kind, a colon, a name (`move:slash`, `prop:crate`; PLAN.md §6.3). */\\nexport const ID_SHAPE = /^[a-z][A-Za-z0-9]*:[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+$/;\\n\\n/** An identifier that holds an id: `id`, or a name ending in `Id`. */\\nconst ID_NAME = /^(id|[a-z][A-Za-z0-9]*Id)$/;\\n\\n30→/** Any node; ESLint's own types use estree's (installed with ESLint). */\\ntype Node = Rule.Node | EsNode;\\n\\n/** The string a literal or an expression-free template holds, else undefined. */\\nfunction stringOf(node: Node | null | undefined): string | undefined {\\n  if (!node) return undefined;\\n  if (node.type === 'Literal' && typeof node.value === 'string') return node.value;\\n  if (node.type === 'TemplateLiteral' && node.expressions.length === 0) return node.quasis[0].value.cooked ?? undefined;\\n  return undefined;\\n}\\n40→\\n/** The name `node` reads: `name` for an identifier, `x.name` for a member, else undefined. */\\nfunction nameOf(node: Node | null | undefined): string | undefined {\\n  if (!node) return undefined;\\n  if (node.type === 'Identifier') return node.name;\\n  if (node.type === 'MemberExpression' && !node.computed && node.property.type === 'Identifier') {\\n    return node.property.name;\\n  }\\n  if (node.type === 'ChainExpression') return nameOf(node.expression);\\n  return undefined;\\n50→}\\n\\n/** Whether `node` reads an id: `id`, `x.id`, `targetId`, `x.ownerId`. */\\nconst isId = (node: Node | null | undefined) => ID_NAME.test(nameOf(node) ?? '');\\n\\n/** An event or message type (`e.type`, `eventType`): its colon strings (`'hit:start'`) are not ids. */\\nconst TYPE_NAME = /^(type|event|[a-z][A-Za-z0-9]*(Type|Event))$/;\\n\\n/** Whether comparing `value` with the string `text` compares an id. */\\nconst comparesId = (value: Node, text: string) =>\\n60→  isId(value) || (ID_SHAPE.test(text) && !TYPE_NAME.test(nameOf(value) ?? ''));\\n\\nconst rule: Rule.RuleModule = {\\n  meta: {\\n    type: 'suggestion',\\n    docs: { description: 'Shared code asks the entry (its hooks and fields), never compares its id (PLAN.md §6.6)' },\\n    schema: [],\\n    messages: {\\n      compare:\\n        \\\"Ask the entry, never the id: this compares an id with '{{text}}'. Read a field or hook of the entry instead, and give the kind that hook with a default on its schema, so new content works without editing this code (PLAN.md §6.6)\\\",\\n70→    },\\n  },\\n  create(context) {\\n    const report = (node: Node, text: string) =>\\n      context.report({ node: node as Rule.Node, messageId: 'compare', data: { text } });\\n    return {\\n      BinaryExpression(node) {\\n        if (!['===', '!==', '==', '!='].includes(node.operator) || node.left.type === 'PrivateIdentifier') return;\\n        for (const [value, other] of [\\n          [node.left, node.right],\\n80→          [node.right, node.left],\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The local rule \\\"ask the entry, never the id\\\" (PLAN.md §6.6, ADR-0012): shared code reads an entry's hooks and\\n * fields, each with a default on the kind's schema, and never compares a registered id, so new content works without\\n * editing the code that uses it. No stock rule expresses this.\\n *\\n * It reports, in `engine/` outside `*.test.ts`:\\n * - `===`, `!==`, `==` and `!=` between a string and either an id-shaped string (`'move:slash'`: a kind, a colon, a\\n *   name) or an id (`id`, `x.id`, `targetId`, `x.ownerId`);\\n * - `switch` on an id, or with an id-shaped `case`;\\n10→ * - `[…].includes(x.id)` or `.indexOf(…)` over listed strings, and `x.id.startsWith('…')`, `.endsWith('…')`.\\n * Comparing two variables (`entry.id === id`, a registry lookup) is fine, and so is an event or message type\\n * (`e.type === 'hit:start'`, `eventType`), whose colon strings are not ids.\\n *\\n * Invariants: written now, switched on by WP 1.2 with the registry (`SWITCHES.askTheEntry` in eslint.config.js).\\n *\\n * @example\\n * // engine/anim/react.ts: if (move.id === 'move:slash') flinch();\\n * // → local/ask-the-entry: Ask the entry, never the id: … give the kind a hook with a default …\\n * @see tools/eslint/askTheEntry.test.ts\\n20→ */\\nimport type { Rule } from 'eslint';\\nimport type { Node as EsNode } from 'estree';\\n\\n/** A registered id: a kind, a colon, a name (`move:slash`, `prop:crate`; PLAN.md §6.3). */\\nexport const ID_SHAPE = /^[a-z][A-Za-z0-9]*:[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+$/;\\n\\n/** An identifier that holds an id: `id`, or a name ending in `Id`. */\\nconst ID_NAME = /^(id|[a-z][A-Za-z0-9]*Id)$/;\\n\\n30→/** Any node; ESLint's own types use estree's (installed with ESLint). */\\ntype Node = Rule.Node | EsNode;\\n\\n/** The string a literal or an expression-free template holds, else undefined. */\\nfunction stringOf(node: Node | null | undefined): string | undefined {\\n  if (!node) return undefined;\\n  if (node.type === 'Literal' && typeof node.value === 'string') return node.value;\\n  if (node.type === 'TemplateLiteral' && node.expressions.length === 0) return node.quasis[0].value.cooked ?? undefined;\\n  return undefined;\\n}\\n40→\\n/** The name `node` reads: `name` for an identifier, `x.name` for a member, else undefined. */\\nfunction nameOf(node: Node | null | undefined): string | undefined {\\n  if (!node) return undefined;\\n  if (node.type === 'Identifier') return node.name;\\n  if (node.type === 'MemberExpression' && !node.computed && node.property.type === 'Identifier') {\\n    return node.property.name;\\n  }\\n  if (node.type === 'ChainExpression') return nameOf(node.expression);\\n  return undefined;\\n50→}\\n\\n/** Whether `node` reads an id: `id`, `x.id`, `targetId`, `x.ownerId`. */\\nconst isId = (node: Node | null | undefined) => ID_NAME.test(nameOf(node) ?? '');\\n\\n/** An event or message type (`e.type`, `eventType`): its colon strings (`'hit:start'`) are not ids. */\\nconst TYPE_NAME = /^(type|event|[a-z][A-Za-z0-9]*(Type|Event))$/;\\n\\n/** Whether comparing `value` with the string `text` compares an id. */\\nconst comparesId = (value: Node, text: string) =>\\n60→  isId(value) || (ID_SHAPE.test(text) && !TYPE_NAME.test(nameOf(value) ?? ''));\\n\\nconst rule: Rule.RuleModule = {\\n  meta: {\\n    type: 'suggestion',\\n    docs: { description: 'Shared code asks the entry (its hooks and fields), never compares its id (PLAN.md §6.6)' },\\n    schema: [],\\n    messages: {\\n      compare:\\n        \\\"Ask the entry, never the id: this compares an id with '{{text}}'. Read a field or hook of the entry instead, and give the kind that hook with a default on its schema, so new content works without editing this code (PLAN.md §6.6)\\\",\\n70→    },\\n  },\\n  create(context) {\\n    const report = (node: Node, text: string) =>\\n      context.report({ node: node as Rule.Node, messageId: 'compare', data: { text } });\\n    return {\\n      BinaryExpression(node) {\\n        if (!['===', '!==', '==', '!='].includes(node.operator) || node.left.type === 'PrivateIdentifier') return;\\n        for (const [value, other] of [\\n          [node.left, node.right],\\n80→          [node.right, node.left],\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/tools/eslint/askTheEntry.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file The local rule \\\"ask the entry, never the id\\\" (PLAN.md §6.6, ADR-0012): shared code reads an entry's hooks and\\n * fields, each with a default on the kind's schema, and never compares a registered id, so new content works without\\n * editing the code that uses it. No stock rule expresses this.\\n *\\n * It reports, in `engine/` outside `*.test.ts`:\\n * - `===`, `!==`, `==` and `!=` between a string and either an id-shaped string (`'move:slash'`: a kind, a colon, a\\n *   name) or an id (`id`, `x.id`, `targetId`, `x.ownerId`);\\n * - `switch` on an id, or with an id-shaped `case`;\\n * - `[…].includes(x.id)` or `.indexOf(…)` over listed strings, and `x.id.startsWith('…')`, `.endsWith('…')`.\\n * Comparing two variables (`entry.id === id`, a registry lookup) is fine, and so is an event or message type\\n * (`e.type === 'hit:start'`, `eventType`), whose colon strings are not ids.\\n *\\n * Invariants: written now, switched on by WP 1.2 with the registry (`SWITCHES.askTheEntry` in eslint.config.js).\\n *\\n * @example\\n * // engine/anim/react.ts: if (move.id === 'move:slash') flinch();\\n * // → local/ask-the-entry: Ask the entry, never the id: … give the kind a hook with a default …\\n * @see tools/eslint/askTheEntry.test.ts\\n */\\nimport type { Rule } from 'eslint';\\nimport type { Node as EsNode } from 'estree';\\n\\n/** A registered id: a kind, a colon, a name (`move:slash`, `prop:crate`; PLAN.md §6.3). */\\nexport const ID_SHAPE = /^[a-z][A-Za-z0-9]*:[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+$/;\\n\\n/** An identifier that holds an id: `id`, or a name ending in `Id`. */\\nconst ID_NAME = /^(id|[a-z][A-Za-z0-9]*Id)$/;\\n\\n/** Any node; ESLint's own types use estree's (installed with ESLint). */\\ntype Node = Rule.Node | EsNode;\\n\\n/** The string a literal or an expression-free template holds, else undefined. */\\nfunction stringOf(node: Node | null | undefined): string | undefined {\\n  if (!node) return undefined;\\n  if (node.type === 'Literal' && typeof node.value === 'string') return node.value;\\n  if (node.type === 'TemplateLiteral' && node.expressions.length === 0) return node.quasis[0].value.cooked ?? undefined;\\n  return undefined;\\n}\\n\\n/** The name `node` reads: `name` for an identifier, `x.name` for a member, else undefined. */\\nfunction nameOf(node: Node | null | undefined): string | undefined {\\n  if (!node) return undefined;\\n  if (node.type === 'Identifier') return node.name;\\n  if (node.type === 'MemberExpression' && !node.computed && node.property.type === 'Identifier') {\\n    return node.property.name;\\n  }\\n  if (node.type === 'ChainExpression') return nameOf(node.expression);\\n  return undefined;\\n}\\n\\n/** Whether `node` reads an id: `id`, `x.id`, `targetId`, `x.ownerId`. */\\nconst isId = (node: Node | null | undefined) => ID_NAME.test(nameOf(node) ?? '');\\n\\n/** An event or message type (`e.type`, `eventType`): its colon strings (`'hit:start'`) are not ids. */\\nconst TYPE_NAME = /^(type|event|[a-z][A-Za-z0-9]*(Type|Event))$/;\\n\\n/** Whether comparing `value` with the string `text` compares an id. */\\nconst comparesId = (value: Node, text: string) =>\\n  isId(value) || (ID_SHAPE.test(text) && !TYPE_NAME.test(nameOf(value) ?? ''));\\n\\nconst rule: Rule.RuleModule = {\\n  meta: {\\n    type: 'suggestion',\\n    docs: { description: 'Shared code asks the entry (its hooks and fields), never compares its id (PLAN.md §6.6)' },\\n    schema: [],\\n    messages: {\\n      compare:\\n        \\\"Ask the entry, never the id: this compares an id with '{{text}}'. Read a field or hook of the entry instead, and give the kind that hook with a default on its schema, so new content works without editing this code (PLAN.md §6.6)\\\",\\n    },\\n  },\\n  create(context) {\\n    const report = (node: Node, text: string) =>\\n      context.report({ node: node as Rule.Node, messageId: 'compare', data: { text } });\\n    return {\\n      BinaryExpression(node) {\\n        if (!['===', '!==', '==', '!='].includes(node.operator) || node.left.type === 'PrivateIdentifier') return;\\n        for (const [value, other] of [\\n          [node.left, node.right],\\n          [node.right, node.left],\\n\",\"total_lines\":111}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-00965fab-86bd-4bd0-bf2f-0a5d0aa03fa0-36","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The reproducibility bans for sim-side code (PLAN.md §6.5, Appendix B; doctrine: Reproducible): no clocks, no\\n * unseeded randomness, no DOM, no Web Audio, no three.js, so identical inputs and seeds give identical play in Node\\n * and in Chromium.\\n *\\n * Sim-side means `engine/{core,sim,physics,anim,world}`, `engine/input/intents.ts`, `engine/audio/{dsp,data}`,\\n * `engine/dev/bot.ts` and the gallery's scene (`engine/dev/galleryScene.ts`), every lab's scenes and cast (the box's,\\n * the starter game's, a game's own), the box's and the playground's sound events (labs/box/sounds/events.ts,\\n * labs/playground/sounds/{events,ground}.ts: the scenes' `sounds`), and the fixture scenes; `*.test.ts` files are\\n10→ * exempt. The standard `Math` stays allowed, `Math.sin` and `Math.cos` included:\\n * the sim swaps in fdlibm ports while it steps (§6.5). `core/math.ts` alone imports three.js, for its math\\n * classes (layers.ts limits it to them).\\n *\\n * Beyond Appendix B's list, the same holes in other spellings are closed too: `setImmediate`,\\n * `requestIdleCallback`, `crypto`, `process`, Node's `Buffer`, `location` and Web Storage, all of `performance`\\n * (`timeOrigin` is a wall-clock timestamp), `globalThis.Date`, `self['Date']` and `const { Date } = globalThis`, and\\n * Node built-ins with or without `node:` (sim-side code runs in Chromium as well). Dynamic `import()` is banned\\n * outright, whatever its source: it resolves at a wall-clock moment, and no import rule (layer, three.js, Node) sees\\n * it. No swap reaches an operator or an alias, so `**` and `**=` are banned (write `Math.pow`), and so are `Math`\\n20→ * destructured and a swapped function (engine/core/simMath.ts's `SIM_MATH_NAMES`) held in a variable (ADR-0006\\n * amendment 1).\\n *\\n * @example\\n * // engine/sim/spawn.ts: const roll = Math.random();\\n * // → sim/no-restricted-properties: use a named seeded stream: rng('<stream>') …\\n * @see tools/eslint/simSide.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { builtinModules } from 'node:module';\\nimport type { ESLint, Linter } from 'eslint';\\n30→import { SIM_MATH_NAMES } from '../../engine/core/simMath';\\nimport { family } from './family';\\n\\n/** The plugin the sim-side blocks use: core's restriction rules as `sim/<rule>`. */\\nexport const simPlugin: ESLint.Plugin = family(\\n  'sim',\\n  'no-restricted-globals',\\n  'no-restricted-properties',\\n  'no-restricted-syntax',\\n  'no-restricted-imports',\\n40→);\\n\\n/** Sim-side code: reproducible and renderer-free (PLAN.md §3, \\\"Terms used throughout\\\"). */\\nexport const SIM_SIDE = [\\n  'engine/core/**',\\n  'engine/sim/**',\\n  'engine/physics/**',\\n  'engine/anim/**',\\n  'engine/world/**',\\n  'engine/input/intents.ts',\\n50→  'engine/audio/dsp/**',\\n  'engine/audio/data/**',\\n  'engine/dev/bot.ts',\\n  'engine/dev/galleryScene.ts',\\n  'labs/*/scenes/**',\\n  'labs/*/cast/**',\\n  'labs/box/sounds/events.ts',\\n  'labs/playground/sounds/events.ts',\\n  'labs/playground/sounds/ground.ts',\\n  'fixtures/scenes/**',\\n60→];\\n\\nconst CLOCK =\\n  'sim-side code never reads the clock: time is the step count (60 Hz sim ticks; core/time). Take a wall-clock timestamp outside the sim and pass it in as an intent (PLAN.md §6.5)';\\nconst TIMER =\\n  'sim-side code never schedules by wall time: keep a tick count in sim state and act in the system that steps it (PLAN.md §6.5)';\\nconst DOM =\\n  'sim-side code never touches the DOM: what it needs from outside arrives as recorded intents, and settings come from the schema (PLAN.md §6.5)';\\nconst AUDIO = 'sim-side code never touches Web Audio: emit an event, and audio/runtime plays it (PLAN.md §6.5)';\\nconst RANDOM =\\n70→  \\\"use a named seeded stream: rng('<stream>') or rng.entity(id, '<stream>'), derived from the scene seed (PLAN.md §6.5)\\\";\\nconst NODE =\\n  'sim-side code runs in Node and in Chromium alike: no process or Node built-ins; settings come from the schema (PLAN.md §6.5)';\\n\\n/** Banned globals, each with its fix. */\\nconst GLOBALS: Record<string, string> = {\\n  Date: CLOCK,\\n  setTimeout: TIMER,\\n  setInterval: TIMER,\\n  setImmediate: TIMER,\\n80→  requestAnimationFrame: TIMER,\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The reproducibility bans for sim-side code (PLAN.md §6.5, Appendix B; doctrine: Reproducible): no clocks, no\\n * unseeded randomness, no DOM, no Web Audio, no three.js, so identical inputs and seeds give identical play in Node\\n * and in Chromium.\\n *\\n * Sim-side means `engine/{core,sim,physics,anim,world}`, `engine/input/intents.ts`, `engine/audio/{dsp,data}`,\\n * `engine/dev/bot.ts` and the gallery's scene (`engine/dev/galleryScene.ts`), every lab's scenes and cast (the box's,\\n * the starter game's, a game's own), the box's and the playground's sound events (labs/box/sounds/events.ts,\\n * labs/playground/sounds/{events,ground}.ts: the scenes' `sounds`), and the fixture scenes; `*.test.ts` files are\\n10→ * exempt. The standard `Math` stays allowed, `Math.sin` and `Math.cos` included:\\n * the sim swaps in fdlibm ports while it steps (§6.5). `core/math.ts` alone imports three.js, for its math\\n * classes (layers.ts limits it to them).\\n *\\n * Beyond Appendix B's list, the same holes in other spellings are closed too: `setImmediate`,\\n * `requestIdleCallback`, `crypto`, `process`, Node's `Buffer`, `location` and Web Storage, all of `performance`\\n * (`timeOrigin` is a wall-clock timestamp), `globalThis.Date`, `self['Date']` and `const { Date } = globalThis`, and\\n * Node built-ins with or without `node:` (sim-side code runs in Chromium as well). Dynamic `import()` is banned\\n * outright, whatever its source: it resolves at a wall-clock moment, and no import rule (layer, three.js, Node) sees\\n * it. No swap reaches an operator or an alias, so `**` and `**=` are banned (write `Math.pow`), and so are `Math`\\n20→ * destructured and a swapped function (engine/core/simMath.ts's `SIM_MATH_NAMES`) held in a variable (ADR-0006\\n * amendment 1).\\n *\\n * @example\\n * // engine/sim/spawn.ts: const roll = Math.random();\\n * // → sim/no-restricted-properties: use a named seeded stream: rng('<stream>') …\\n * @see tools/eslint/simSide.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { builtinModules } from 'node:module';\\nimport type { ESLint, Linter } from 'eslint';\\n30→import { SIM_MATH_NAMES } from '../../engine/core/simMath';\\nimport { family } from './family';\\n\\n/** The plugin the sim-side blocks use: core's restriction rules as `sim/<rule>`. */\\nexport const simPlugin: ESLint.Plugin = family(\\n  'sim',\\n  'no-restricted-globals',\\n  'no-restricted-properties',\\n  'no-restricted-syntax',\\n  'no-restricted-imports',\\n40→);\\n\\n/** Sim-side code: reproducible and renderer-free (PLAN.md §3, \\\"Terms used throughout\\\"). */\\nexport const SIM_SIDE = [\\n  'engine/core/**',\\n  'engine/sim/**',\\n  'engine/physics/**',\\n  'engine/anim/**',\\n  'engine/world/**',\\n  'engine/input/intents.ts',\\n50→  'engine/audio/dsp/**',\\n  'engine/audio/data/**',\\n  'engine/dev/bot.ts',\\n  'engine/dev/galleryScene.ts',\\n  'labs/*/scenes/**',\\n  'labs/*/cast/**',\\n  'labs/box/sounds/events.ts',\\n  'labs/playground/sounds/events.ts',\\n  'labs/playground/sounds/ground.ts',\\n  'fixtures/scenes/**',\\n60→];\\n\\nconst CLOCK =\\n  'sim-side code never reads the clock: time is the step count (60 Hz sim ticks; core/time). Take a wall-clock timestamp outside the sim and pass it in as an intent (PLAN.md §6.5)';\\nconst TIMER =\\n  'sim-side code never schedules by wall time: keep a tick count in sim state and act in the system that steps it (PLAN.md §6.5)';\\nconst DOM =\\n  'sim-side code never touches the DOM: what it needs from outside arrives as recorded intents, and settings come from the schema (PLAN.md §6.5)';\\nconst AUDIO = 'sim-side code never touches Web Audio: emit an event, and audio/runtime plays it (PLAN.md §6.5)';\\nconst RANDOM =\\n70→  \\\"use a named seeded stream: rng('<stream>') or rng.entity(id, '<stream>'), derived from the scene seed (PLAN.md §6.5)\\\";\\nconst NODE =\\n  'sim-side code runs in Node and in Chromium alike: no process or Node built-ins; settings come from the schema (PLAN.md §6.5)';\\n\\n/** Banned globals, each with its fix. */\\nconst GLOBALS: Record<string, string> = {\\n  Date: CLOCK,\\n  setTimeout: TIMER,\\n  setInterval: TIMER,\\n  setImmediate: TIMER,\\n80→  requestAnimationFrame: TIMER,\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/tools/eslint/simSide.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file The reproducibility bans for sim-side code (PLAN.md §6.5, Appendix B; doctrine: Reproducible): no clocks, no\\n * unseeded randomness, no DOM, no Web Audio, no three.js, so identical inputs and seeds give identical play in Node\\n * and in Chromium.\\n *\\n * Sim-side means `engine/{core,sim,physics,anim,world}`, `engine/input/intents.ts`, `engine/audio/{dsp,data}`,\\n * `engine/dev/bot.ts` and the gallery's scene (`engine/dev/galleryScene.ts`), every lab's scenes and cast (the box's,\\n * the starter game's, a game's own), the box's and the playground's sound events (labs/box/sounds/events.ts,\\n * labs/playground/sounds/{events,ground}.ts: the scenes' `sounds`), and the fixture scenes; `*.test.ts` files are\\n * exempt. The standard `Math` stays allowed, `Math.sin` and `Math.cos` included:\\n * the sim swaps in fdlibm ports while it steps (§6.5). `core/math.ts` alone imports three.js, for its math\\n * classes (layers.ts limits it to them).\\n *\\n * Beyond Appendix B's list, the same holes in other spellings are closed too: `setImmediate`,\\n * `requestIdleCallback`, `crypto`, `process`, Node's `Buffer`, `location` and Web Storage, all of `performance`\\n * (`timeOrigin` is a wall-clock timestamp), `globalThis.Date`, `self['Date']` and `const { Date } = globalThis`, and\\n * Node built-ins with or without `node:` (sim-side code runs in Chromium as well). Dynamic `import()` is banned\\n * outright, whatever its source: it resolves at a wall-clock moment, and no import rule (layer, three.js, Node) sees\\n * it. No swap reaches an operator or an alias, so `**` and `**=` are banned (write `Math.pow`), and so are `Math`\\n * destructured and a swapped function (engine/core/simMath.ts's `SIM_MATH_NAMES`) held in a variable (ADR-0006\\n * amendment 1).\\n *\\n * @example\\n * // engine/sim/spawn.ts: const roll = Math.random();\\n * // → sim/no-restricted-properties: use a named seeded stream: rng('<stream>') …\\n * @see tools/eslint/simSide.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { builtinModules } from 'node:module';\\nimport type { ESLint, Linter } from 'eslint';\\nimport { SIM_MATH_NAMES } from '../../engine/core/simMath';\\nimport { family } from './family';\\n\\n/** The plugin the sim-side blocks use: core's restriction rules as `sim/<rule>`. */\\nexport const simPlugin: ESLint.Plugin = family(\\n  'sim',\\n  'no-restricted-globals',\\n  'no-restricted-properties',\\n  'no-restricted-syntax',\\n  'no-restricted-imports',\\n);\\n\\n/** Sim-side code: reproducible and renderer-free (PLAN.md §3, \\\"Terms used throughout\\\"). */\\nexport const SIM_SIDE = [\\n  'engine/core/**',\\n  'engine/sim/**',\\n  'engine/physics/**',\\n  'engine/anim/**',\\n  'engine/world/**',\\n  'engine/input/intents.ts',\\n  'engine/audio/dsp/**',\\n  'engine/audio/data/**',\\n  'engine/dev/bot.ts',\\n  'engine/dev/galleryScene.ts',\\n  'labs/*/scenes/**',\\n  'labs/*/cast/**',\\n  'labs/box/sounds/events.ts',\\n  'labs/playground/sounds/events.ts',\\n  'labs/playground/sounds/ground.ts',\\n  'fixtures/scenes/**',\\n];\\n\\nconst CLOCK =\\n  'sim-side code never reads the clock: time is the step count (60 Hz sim ticks; core/time). Take a wall-clock timestamp outside the sim and pass it in as an intent (PLAN.md §6.5)';\\nconst TIMER =\\n  'sim-side code never schedules by wall time: keep a tick count in sim state and act in the system that steps it (PLAN.md §6.5)';\\nconst DOM =\\n  'sim-side code never touches the DOM: what it needs from outside arrives as recorded intents, and settings come from the schema (PLAN.md §6.5)';\\nconst AUDIO = 'sim-side code never touches Web Audio: emit an event, and audio/runtime plays it (PLAN.md §6.5)';\\nconst RANDOM =\\n  \\\"use a named seeded stream: rng('<stream>') or rng.entity(id, '<stream>'), derived from the scene seed (PLAN.md §6.5)\\\";\\nconst NODE =\\n  'sim-side code runs in Node and in Chromium alike: no process or Node built-ins; settings come from the schema (PLAN.md §6.5)';\\n\\n/** Banned globals, each with its fix. */\\nconst GLOBALS: Record<string, string> = {\\n  Date: CLOCK,\\n  setTimeout: TIMER,\\n  setInterval: TIMER,\\n  setImmediate: TIMER,\\n  requestAnimationFrame: TIMER,\\n\",\"total_lines\":172}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-00965fab-86bd-4bd0-bf2f-0a5d0aa03fa0-37","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,95,95,120,53,57,99,49,100,51,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,70,111,117,110,100,32,56,32,109,97,116,99,104,105,110,103,32,108,105,110,101,115,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,95,95,120,53,57,99,49,100,51,47,119,111,114,107,47,84,114,97,117,109,97,45,67,111,109,112,97,115,115,45,84,114,97,117,109,97,45,67,101,110,116,101,114,45,83,101,99,111,110,100,45,79,112,105,110,105,111,110,45,103,114,111,107,45,52,46,55,45,120,104,105,103,104,45,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,47,101,110,103,105,110,101,47,115,105,109,47,115,99,101,110,101,46,116,115,10,49,48,58,32,42,32,96,115,116,101,112,40,119,44,32,105,110,116,101,110,116,115,41,96,32,114,117,110,115,32,101,118,101,114,121,32,115,105,109,32,115,116,101,112,32,97,115,32,116,104,101,32,115,121,115,116,101,109,32,96,115,99,101,110,101,96,44,32,102,105,114,115,116,32,105,110,32,116,104,101,32,112,104,97,115,101,32,96,105,110,116,101,110,116,115,96,44,32,97,104,101,97,100,32,111,102,32,116,104,101,32,115,121,115,116,101,109,115,10,52,49,58,105,109,112,111,114,116,32,116,121,112,101,32,123,32,73,110,116,101,110,116,115,32,125,32,102,114,111,109,32,39,46,46,47,105,110,112,117,116,47,105,110,116,101,110,116,115,39,59,10,57,52,58,32,32,47,42,42,32,82,117,110,115,32,101,118,101,114,121,32,115,116,101,112,44,32,102,105,114,115,116,32,105,110,32,116,104,101,32,112,104,97,115,101,32,96,105,110,116,101,110,116,115,96,58,32,114,101,97,100,115,32,116,104,101,32,115,116,101,112,39,115,32,105,110,116,101,110,116,115,32,97,110,100,32,99,111,109,112,111,110,101,110,116,115,44,32,119,114,105,116,101,115,32,99,111,109,112,111,110,101,110,116,115,46,32,42,47,10,57,53,58,32,32,115,116,101,112,63,58,32,40,119,58,32,87,111,114,108,100,60,67,44,32,69,62,44,32,105,110,116,101,110,116,115,58,32,73,110,116,101,110,116,115,41,32,61,62,32,118,111,105,100,59,10,49,50,55,58,32,32,32,32,100,101,115,99,114,105,112,116,105,111,110,58,32,34,82,117,110,115,32,101,118,101,114,121,32,115,105,109,32,115,116,101,112,32,97,115,32,116,104,101,32,115,121,115,116,101,109,32,39,115,99,101,110,101,39,44,32,102,105,114,115,116,32,105,110,32,116,104,101,32,112,104,97,115,101,32,105,110,116,101,110,116,115,46,32,40,119,44,32,105,110,116,101,110,116,115,41,32,61,62,32,118,111,105,100,46,34,44,10,49,54,52,58,32,32,32,32,100,101,102,105,110,101,87,105,116,104,58,32,34,100,101,102,105,110,101,83,99,101,110,101,40,39,60,105,100,62,39,44,32,123,32,115,101,116,117,112,40,119,41,32,123,32,226,128,166,32,125,44,32,115,116,101,112,40,119,44,32,105,110,116,101,110,116,115,41,32,123,32,226,128,166,32,125,32,125,41,34,44,10,49,55,52,58,101,120,112,111,114,116,32,102,117,110,99,116,105,111,110,32,100,101,102,105,110,101,83,99,101,110,101,60,67,32,101,120,116,101,110,100,115,32,111,98,106,101,99,116,32,61,32,65,110,121,67,111,109,112,111,110,101,110,116,115,44,32,69,32,101,120,116,101,110,100,115,32,69,118,101,110,116,77,97,112,32,61,32,69,118,101,110,116,77,97,112,62,40,10,50,52,51,58,32,32,119,111,114,108,100,46,115,121,115,116,101,109,115,46,97,100,100,40,39,115,99,101,110,101,39,44,32,40,119,44,32,105,110,116,101,110,116,115,41,32,61,62,32,101,110,116,114,121,46,115,116,101,112,40,119,44,32,105,110,116,101,110,116,115,41,44,32,123,32,112,104,97,115,101,58,32,39,105,110,116,101,110,116,115,39,32,125,41,59,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":8,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/engine/sim/scene.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":10,\"content\":\" * `step(w, intents)` runs every sim step as the system `scene`, first in the phase `intents`, ahead of the systems\"},{\"line_number\":41,\"content\":\"import type { Intents } from '../input/intents';\"},{\"line_number\":94,\"content\":\"  /** Runs every step, first in the phase `intents`: reads the step's intents and components, writes components. */\"},{\"line_number\":95,\"content\":\"  step?: (w: World<C, E>, intents: Intents) => void;\"},{\"line_number\":127,\"content\":\"    description: \\\"Runs every sim step as the system 'scene', first in the phase intents. (w, intents) => void.\\\",\"},{\"line_number\":164,\"content\":\"    defineWith: \\\"defineScene('<id>', { setup(w) { … }, step(w, intents) { … } })\\\",\"},{\"line_number\":174,\"content\":\"export function defineScene<C extends object = AnyComponents, E extends EventMap = EventMap>(\"},{\"line_number\":243,\"content\":\"  world.systems.add('scene', (w, intents) => entry.step(w, intents), { phase: 'intents' });\"}]}]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-00965fab-86bd-4bd0-bf2f-0a5d0aa03fa0-38","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The box's sound effects (PLAN.md §9.0, WP 9.5, I-23; doctrine: Assets): what its sim events sound like, as\\n * definitions the DSP renders (engine/audio/data/sfx.ts), never sample files. labs/box/sounds/events.ts says which\\n * event plays which; `node x qa audio` checks every one, `node x audio sfx:boxHit --spectrogram` shows one.\\n *\\n * - `sfx:boxStep`: a footstep on stone, a heel's low thump under a gritty scuff;\\n * - `sfx:boxStepIron`: a footstep on iron, a tick and a short metal ring;\\n * - `sfx:boxThud`: a body coming down (a landing, a slime's pounce), a falling sine with a dull burst;\\n * - `sfx:boxHit`: a blow landing, a bright crack over a low knock;\\n10→ * - `sfx:boxLaunch`: a bolt leaving, a rising saw with a hiss;\\n * - `sfx:boxDeath`: a body falling for good, a sinking groan, then the thud;\\n * - `sfx:boxTorch`: a torch's crackle on the ambience bus, 2 s of pops over a soft roar, played looped (it starts\\n *   and ends quiet, so the loop has no seam to hear).\\n *\\n * Invariants: ids start `sfx:box`, so none meets the engine's own; the often heard ones have variants, so a hundred\\n * steps never repeat exactly. Box content: it stays in labs/box/ (PLAN.md §5.7).\\n *\\n * @example\\n * import { renderSfx } from '../../../engine';\\n20→ * renderSfx('sfx:boxStep', { variant: 2 }).length; // 3528: 0.08 s at 44,100 Hz\\n * @see labs/box/sounds/events.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { defineSfx } from '../../../engine';\\n\\n/** A footstep on stone: a heel's low thump under a gritty scuff. */\\nexport const boxStep = defineSfx('sfx:boxStep', {\\n  voices: [\\n    { wave: 'noise', freq: 1400, to: 500, dur: 0.08, vol: 0.2, filter: 'lowpass' },\\n    { wave: 'sine', freq: 150, to: 70, dur: 0.07, vol: 0.3 },\\n30→  ],\\n  variants: 4,\\n  vary: 0.08,\\n});\\n\\n/** A footstep on iron: a tick and a short metal ring. */\\nexport const boxStepIron = defineSfx('sfx:boxStepIron', {\\n  voices: [\\n    { wave: 'noise', freq: 4000, dur: 0.03, vol: 0.12, filter: 'highpass' },\\n    { wave: 'triangle', freq: 1250, dur: 0.18, vol: 0.14 },\\n40→    { wave: 'triangle', freq: 1873, dur: 0.12, vol: 0.08 },\\n  ],\\n  variants: 4,\\n  vary: 0.05,\\n});\\n\\n/** A body coming down (a landing, a slime's pounce): a falling sine with a dull burst. */\\nexport const boxThud = defineSfx('sfx:boxThud', {\\n  voices: [\\n    { wave: 'sine', freq: 120, to: 50, dur: 0.2, vol: 0.5 },\\n50→    { wave: 'noise', freq: 700, to: 150, dur: 0.12, vol: 0.3, filter: 'lowpass' },\\n  ],\\n  variants: 3,\\n  vary: 0.06,\\n});\\n\\n/** A blow landing: a bright crack over a low knock. */\\nexport const boxHit = defineSfx('sfx:boxHit', {\\n  voices: [\\n    { wave: 'noise', freq: 2600, to: 900, dur: 0.07, vol: 0.35 },\\n60→    { wave: 'square', freq: 190, to: 70, dur: 0.11, vol: 0.22, filter: 'lowpass' },\\n  ],\\n  variants: 4,\\n  vary: 0.1,\\n});\\n\\n/** A bolt leaving: a rising saw with a hiss. */\\nexport const boxLaunch = defineSfx('sfx:boxLaunch', {\\n  voices: [\\n    { wave: 'saw', freq: 300, to: 1400, dur: 0.16, vol: 0.16, filter: 'lowpass' },\\n70→    { wave: 'noise', freq: 3000, to: 1200, dur: 0.12, vol: 0.12 },\\n  ],\\n  variants: 3,\\n});\\n\\n/** A body falling for good: a sinking groan, then the thud. */\\nexport const boxDeath = defineSfx('sfx:boxDeath', {\\n  voices: { wave: 'saw', freq: 220, to: 70, dur: 0.45, vol: 0.18, filter: 'lowpass', vib: [7, 0.03] },\\n  layers: [{ sfx: 'sfx:boxThud', at: 0.3, vol: 0.8 }],\\n  variants: 3,\\n80→});\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The box's sound effects (PLAN.md §9.0, WP 9.5, I-23; doctrine: Assets): what its sim events sound like, as\\n * definitions the DSP renders (engine/audio/data/sfx.ts), never sample files. labs/box/sounds/events.ts says which\\n * event plays which; `node x qa audio` checks every one, `node x audio sfx:boxHit --spectrogram` shows one.\\n *\\n * - `sfx:boxStep`: a footstep on stone, a heel's low thump under a gritty scuff;\\n * - `sfx:boxStepIron`: a footstep on iron, a tick and a short metal ring;\\n * - `sfx:boxThud`: a body coming down (a landing, a slime's pounce), a falling sine with a dull burst;\\n * - `sfx:boxHit`: a blow landing, a bright crack over a low knock;\\n10→ * - `sfx:boxLaunch`: a bolt leaving, a rising saw with a hiss;\\n * - `sfx:boxDeath`: a body falling for good, a sinking groan, then the thud;\\n * - `sfx:boxTorch`: a torch's crackle on the ambience bus, 2 s of pops over a soft roar, played looped (it starts\\n *   and ends quiet, so the loop has no seam to hear).\\n *\\n * Invariants: ids start `sfx:box`, so none meets the engine's own; the often heard ones have variants, so a hundred\\n * steps never repeat exactly. Box content: it stays in labs/box/ (PLAN.md §5.7).\\n *\\n * @example\\n * import { renderSfx } from '../../../engine';\\n20→ * renderSfx('sfx:boxStep', { variant: 2 }).length; // 3528: 0.08 s at 44,100 Hz\\n * @see labs/box/sounds/events.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { defineSfx } from '../../../engine';\\n\\n/** A footstep on stone: a heel's low thump under a gritty scuff. */\\nexport const boxStep = defineSfx('sfx:boxStep', {\\n  voices: [\\n    { wave: 'noise', freq: 1400, to: 500, dur: 0.08, vol: 0.2, filter: 'lowpass' },\\n    { wave: 'sine', freq: 150, to: 70, dur: 0.07, vol: 0.3 },\\n30→  ],\\n  variants: 4,\\n  vary: 0.08,\\n});\\n\\n/** A footstep on iron: a tick and a short metal ring. */\\nexport const boxStepIron = defineSfx('sfx:boxStepIron', {\\n  voices: [\\n    { wave: 'noise', freq: 4000, dur: 0.03, vol: 0.12, filter: 'highpass' },\\n    { wave: 'triangle', freq: 1250, dur: 0.18, vol: 0.14 },\\n40→    { wave: 'triangle', freq: 1873, dur: 0.12, vol: 0.08 },\\n  ],\\n  variants: 4,\\n  vary: 0.05,\\n});\\n\\n/** A body coming down (a landing, a slime's pounce): a falling sine with a dull burst. */\\nexport const boxThud = defineSfx('sfx:boxThud', {\\n  voices: [\\n    { wave: 'sine', freq: 120, to: 50, dur: 0.2, vol: 0.5 },\\n50→    { wave: 'noise', freq: 700, to: 150, dur: 0.12, vol: 0.3, filter: 'lowpass' },\\n  ],\\n  variants: 3,\\n  vary: 0.06,\\n});\\n\\n/** A blow landing: a bright crack over a low knock. */\\nexport const boxHit = defineSfx('sfx:boxHit', {\\n  voices: [\\n    { wave: 'noise', freq: 2600, to: 900, dur: 0.07, vol: 0.35 },\\n60→    { wave: 'square', freq: 190, to: 70, dur: 0.11, vol: 0.22, filter: 'lowpass' },\\n  ],\\n  variants: 4,\\n  vary: 0.1,\\n});\\n\\n/** A bolt leaving: a rising saw with a hiss. */\\nexport const boxLaunch = defineSfx('sfx:boxLaunch', {\\n  voices: [\\n    { wave: 'saw', freq: 300, to: 1400, dur: 0.16, vol: 0.16, filter: 'lowpass' },\\n70→    { wave: 'noise', freq: 3000, to: 1200, dur: 0.12, vol: 0.12 },\\n  ],\\n  variants: 3,\\n});\\n\\n/** A body falling for good: a sinking groan, then the thud. */\\nexport const boxDeath = defineSfx('sfx:boxDeath', {\\n  voices: { wave: 'saw', freq: 220, to: 70, dur: 0.45, vol: 0.18, filter: 'lowpass', vib: [7, 0.03] },\\n  layers: [{ sfx: 'sfx:boxThud', at: 0.3, vol: 0.8 }],\\n  variants: 3,\\n80→});\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/box/sounds/effects.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file The box's sound effects (PLAN.md §9.0, WP 9.5, I-23; doctrine: Assets): what its sim events sound like, as\\n * definitions the DSP renders (engine/audio/data/sfx.ts), never sample files. labs/box/sounds/events.ts says which\\n * event plays which; `node x qa audio` checks every one, `node x audio sfx:boxHit --spectrogram` shows one.\\n *\\n * - `sfx:boxStep`: a footstep on stone, a heel's low thump under a gritty scuff;\\n * - `sfx:boxStepIron`: a footstep on iron, a tick and a short metal ring;\\n * - `sfx:boxThud`: a body coming down (a landing, a slime's pounce), a falling sine with a dull burst;\\n * - `sfx:boxHit`: a blow landing, a bright crack over a low knock;\\n * - `sfx:boxLaunch`: a bolt leaving, a rising saw with a hiss;\\n * - `sfx:boxDeath`: a body falling for good, a sinking groan, then the thud;\\n * - `sfx:boxTorch`: a torch's crackle on the ambience bus, 2 s of pops over a soft roar, played looped (it starts\\n *   and ends quiet, so the loop has no seam to hear).\\n *\\n * Invariants: ids start `sfx:box`, so none meets the engine's own; the often heard ones have variants, so a hundred\\n * steps never repeat exactly. Box content: it stays in labs/box/ (PLAN.md §5.7).\\n *\\n * @example\\n * import { renderSfx } from '../../../engine';\\n * renderSfx('sfx:boxStep', { variant: 2 }).length; // 3528: 0.08 s at 44,100 Hz\\n * @see labs/box/sounds/events.test.ts\\n */\\nimport { defineSfx } from '../../../engine';\\n\\n/** A footstep on stone: a heel's low thump under a gritty scuff. */\\nexport const boxStep = defineSfx('sfx:boxStep', {\\n  voices: [\\n    { wave: 'noise', freq: 1400, to: 500, dur: 0.08, vol: 0.2, filter: 'lowpass' },\\n    { wave: 'sine', freq: 150, to: 70, dur: 0.07, vol: 0.3 },\\n  ],\\n  variants: 4,\\n  vary: 0.08,\\n});\\n\\n/** A footstep on iron: a tick and a short metal ring. */\\nexport const boxStepIron = defineSfx('sfx:boxStepIron', {\\n  voices: [\\n    { wave: 'noise', freq: 4000, dur: 0.03, vol: 0.12, filter: 'highpass' },\\n    { wave: 'triangle', freq: 1250, dur: 0.18, vol: 0.14 },\\n    { wave: 'triangle', freq: 1873, dur: 0.12, vol: 0.08 },\\n  ],\\n  variants: 4,\\n  vary: 0.05,\\n});\\n\\n/** A body coming down (a landing, a slime's pounce): a falling sine with a dull burst. */\\nexport const boxThud = defineSfx('sfx:boxThud', {\\n  voices: [\\n    { wave: 'sine', freq: 120, to: 50, dur: 0.2, vol: 0.5 },\\n    { wave: 'noise', freq: 700, to: 150, dur: 0.12, vol: 0.3, filter: 'lowpass' },\\n  ],\\n  variants: 3,\\n  vary: 0.06,\\n});\\n\\n/** A blow landing: a bright crack over a low knock. */\\nexport const boxHit = defineSfx('sfx:boxHit', {\\n  voices: [\\n    { wave: 'noise', freq: 2600, to: 900, dur: 0.07, vol: 0.35 },\\n    { wave: 'square', freq: 190, to: 70, dur: 0.11, vol: 0.22, filter: 'lowpass' },\\n  ],\\n  variants: 4,\\n  vary: 0.1,\\n});\\n\\n/** A bolt leaving: a rising saw with a hiss. */\\nexport const boxLaunch = defineSfx('sfx:boxLaunch', {\\n  voices: [\\n    { wave: 'saw', freq: 300, to: 1400, dur: 0.16, vol: 0.16, filter: 'lowpass' },\\n    { wave: 'noise', freq: 3000, to: 1200, dur: 0.12, vol: 0.12 },\\n  ],\\n  variants: 3,\\n});\\n\\n/** A body falling for good: a sinking groan, then the thud. */\\nexport const boxDeath = defineSfx('sfx:boxDeath', {\\n  voices: { wave: 'saw', freq: 220, to: 70, dur: 0.45, vol: 0.18, filter: 'lowpass', vib: [7, 0.03] },\\n  layers: [{ sfx: 'sfx:boxThud', at: 0.3, vol: 0.8 }],\\n  variants: 3,\\n});\\n\",\"total_lines\":112}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"a912799c-7bce-4570-9aab-510df566e95b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_16","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The ask-the-entry rule applies only to engine/ code, not labs/.\n\nThe gameplay simulation will live in labs/game/scenes/ as modules. Scene steps will receive game:hands intents. Tests will import these modules directly and call exported helpers like createOperation and stepOperation to simulate fresh starts.\n\nModule-level state was rejected because it breaks determinism and replays. The operation object must be stored on a world component instead. A JSON string field is possible but ugly. A typed object field is cleaner.\n\nA closure inside setup() is being considered as an alternative to component storage. The operation state would live in a local variable that the system closes over, so it resets automatically when the world restarts.\n\nThe closure approach is confirmed safe because setup runs fresh on every world restart. The full operation object will live in that closure. A `chart` component will expose only the HUD fields the page needs, updated each step via `world.query`.\n\nA `window.__compass` global may expose richer state for end-to-end tests. The engine hashes the world, so operation state must be stored on a component to be included in lockstep hashes. The sequencer test runs in Node and uses pure simulation without the engine world.\n\nA small bridge module will expose `readOp()` for the page to read operation state. The scene updates this module-level value each step. Tests will not use it.\n\nThe bridge module will only be written inside the scene step to avoid test isolation collisions. Scene tests will use createHeadless instead of the live bridge.\n\nImplementation will proceed in two phases: first the simulation and its tests, then the visual layer. Files will be written directly rather than generated, with each kept under 400 lines. Shared types and constants will live in labs/game/sim/types.ts to stay outside the scenes directory.\n\nThe simulation will be placed under `labs/game/op/` instead of `labs/game/sim/`. This keeps it outside the scenes directory and avoids sim-side bans like the `**` operator. The scene file can import from this path without restriction.\n\nThe `**` operator is not swapped globally during engine steps, so it must be avoided to prevent Node versus browser hash drift. The simulation will be split into four modules: balance.ts for defaults and ranges, tools.ts for tool IDs and target kinds, ratings.ts for action scoring, and cases.ts for all case definitions.\n\nThe simulation will be split into eighteen modules. Cases are divided into cases1.ts and cases2.ts. New modules cover story beats, the step function, tool application, strain machines, gift effects, bot logic, director flow, hashing, the sequencer, voice syllables, and operation creation.\n\nFive test files are planned: rules, bot, content, net, and flow. The step function will be designed so the bot always wins. Operations will be modeled as a checklist of tasks the bot can complete. Strains will be real state machines. The bot's policy will be priority-based on world state. Case phases will use concrete objectives with fixed positions to keep the bot's path short.\n\nThe game sheet is 16 by 9 metres with a center origin. Targets will be placed within x: -6..6 and z: -3..3 so the bot can reach them easily.\n\nThe Hand intent interface defines absolute cursor position in metres, a tool index from 0 to 7, and boolean flags for use, drop, gift, hint, site selection, and swap.\n\nThe Hand intent also includes paddle and precision flags. The page converts stick input into absolute cursor positions, and the simulation clamps and optionally smooths them. For tests the bot sends its desired position directly. Because the cursor snaps instantly to the hand position, path-dependent ratings such as scalpel distance from the guide must be satisfied by sending intermediate positions along the guide across multiple ticks.\n\nCursor travel is capped at 10 m/s. Forceps pull speed is a separate 4 m/s threshold for keeping the cut clean. The scalpel guide will be a polyline with a `cutAt` progress index.\n\nThe scalpel algorithm advances the cut index while the cursor is within 0.3 metres of the next guide point and the use button is held. The cut completes once all points are visited, with a textbook rating achieved when mean distance is near zero. Cuts without a nearby guide will slip.\n\nFor suturing, wounds are lines between two points. The bot needs ceil(length / 0.45) stitches and zigzags across the wound center line to cross from one side to the other.\n\nSuture crossing is detected by a sign flip in the cross product when the cursor is within 0.8 metres of the wound line, with a stitch placed only if the last stitch was far enough away.\n\nDrain fills a pool to volume 4 and empties it at 1.5 per second while the cursor stays within 0.8 metres and use is held; points are awarded once the volume reaches zero.\n\nThe syringe stabilizer charges one-sixtieth per tick up to full charge while use is held, then injects on release if charge exceeds 0.05; a band of 0.75 to 0.95 yields a textbook score of +24, so the bot charges for about fifty ticks before releasing.\n\nGel painting covers a grid of cells around the target, and ninety percent coverage completes the task.\n\nThe bot will spiral or sweep a lawnmower path to paint gel.\n\nLaser targets pop after 1.2 seconds while the cursor stays within 0.4 metres and use is held.\n\nScanner reveals hidden targets within range for six seconds while held.\n\nForceps grab objects within 0.5 metres, then free them by moving 1.2 metres away while holding use; the system tracks the grab origin.\n\nCode requires two tries. The charge ring oscillates every two seconds in simulation ticks, with a green band between ticks 40 and 70; the bot presses paddle during that band.\n\nA press outside the green charge band counts as a miss and increments the try counter; a press inside the band counts as a success. Active bleeds and open wounds drain field time at rates of 0.5 or 1.2 seconds per second, while a lull alive bleeds at 0.6 seconds per second; slips deduct six seconds instantly. Field time per step is `ftDelta / 6000`, where `ftDelta` is 100 or 25, and drain equals rate times that interval times difficulty and balance. The clock starts at seconds multiplied by 6000 and scaled by difficulty, then decrements by `ftDelta` each step until it reaches zero. A slip limit tied to difficulty ends the operation when exceeded.\n\nThe operation ends when the final phase's `until` condition is met and that phase is closing. A Phase carries an id, hint text, spawn list, and optional teaching or scripted events. A target resolves once its specific condition is satisfied: pool drained, shard removed, wound stitched, guide cut, mass removed, nodule popped, or strain dead.\n\nCase 1 phases are now defined. The first drains three pools and teaches the drain tool. The second removes four shards. The third triggers a scripted dip to forty vitals and teaches the syringe stabilizer. The fourth stitches three lacerations. The fifth applies gel and closes the phase. Scripted events such as the dip and flatline occur at fixed field-time offsets within their phase. Vitals drain slowly enough that the bot can survive, but slowly enough that inaction still causes failure.\n\nCase 3 uses three sites per patient, each with wounds and pools; the bot must switch sites to stop the worst bleed. Case 7 uses three separate patients. Difficulty settings scale intern drain to 0.6 and clock speed to 1.3. A gift pulse-lock may freeze field time at thirty percent during its minigame.\n\nThe gift pulse-lock will freeze field time at thirty percent while the ECG strip grows huge. The bot must press Triangle on each of the next three R-peaks, spaced forty-five ticks apart. A perfect hit scores ten and a half seconds. Stillpoint will also reduce field time to twenty-five percent during its activation.\n\nStillpoint’s activation slowdown lasts only for the three presses, after which field time stays at twenty-five percent. Pulse-lock minigame field time is thirty percent of normal. Second Pulse skips slowdown, freezes vitals, awards clean plus one point five, and doubles the chain multiplier. Consult mode lets two seats activate gift within sixty ticks to extend duration by fifty percent. In cases five through eight, an AI colleague occupies seat one while the bot uses seat zero; the bot policy is restricted to flaps, drain, and small tasks.\n\nThe AI colleague in seat one will assist but remain non-essential. The main bot on seat zero must win the operation regardless. Flap-holding duties can be shared between the two seats, or handled solo by the player while the AI works.\n\nThe solo bot will clip the flap to hold it for eight seconds, then work underneath. Mercy phases are scripted with clear vulnerable windows and a `mercy.objective` to guide the bot. Phase one targets motes at known positions with a six-hundred-millisecond laser on each, then three binder doses while exposed. Phase two shocks the code band, then sutures two rounds of lacerations.\n\nPhase three tracks a moving guide that drifts with the heartbeat. The bot uses forceps to grab tendrils, then receives an automatic gift and three binder doses before a final code challenge. If the phase feels too flaky, the thresholds will be widened. Rank thresholds are set at forty-five, sixty, seventy-five, and ninety percent of the Resident benchmark score; the test will compute the current bot score and verify it against the stored case data.\n\nThe case file will store a `par` benchmark score and rank fractions at 0.45, 0.60, 0.75, and 0.90. The test will run the bot once to establish the current benchmark, then verify that stored thresholds remain within five percent of the live score. If drift exceeds eight percent, the test fails.\n\nThe test will derive the rank from the live bot score using a `rankFor` function against the stored benchmark, and verify that `case.par` matches the first successful run. Story content will be compact dialogue: sixteen scenes of fourteen lines each, split across files with one or two short sentences per box. Each scene must contain between twelve and thirty-five boxes.\n\nThe story content will ban specific names and organizations. Kyriaki, Deftera, Triti, Tetarti, Pempti, Paraskevi, Savato, Adam, Bliss, Z-Cells, GUILT, Healing Touch, Caduceus, Delphi, and Hope Hospital are forbidden. Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, and Nozomi Weaver are also banned. The star gesture is disallowed. The phrase \"second opinion\" is permitted as ordinary dialogue.\n\nA deterministic syllable-index function hashes each character with its speaker name to produce a fixed 0–15 value. Identical lines therefore yield identical syllable patterns. The audio page will use an OfflineAudioContext for deterministic buffer rendering, while the UX test verifies that the AnalyserNode actually hears the music.\n\nThe audio page will use only Web Audio oscillators and noise bursts, with no `<audio>` tags or external files. `defineSfx` and `defineSong` will be registered so the engine can render them. The test will verify the HTML contains no audio elements.\n\nThe visual scene will be a minimal 3D room built from text geometry, walls, and compileLevel. The title screen camera will sit inside the room, fixed above looking down at a lamp and window. The exact fixed-camera syntax needs to be checked in the parser.\n\nThe fixed camera syntax is `cam3=fixed,x,y,z,yawDeg,pitchDeg,fov` with blend enabled. The sheet will be a live mesh built from the public `boxGeometry` export using the plan’s constructor extraction trick.\n\nThe page will import `liveMesh.ts` to register geometry and props before `createEngine`. Scene setup then spawns the prop. The page barrel must expose both `defineGeometry` and `defineProp`.\n\nThe sheet will be 320 by 192, yielding about sixty-one thousand vertices. Software WebGPU may struggle with that density.\n\nSheet density will be reduced to 96 by 54 for software WebGPU sessions, yielding about five thousand vertices. Dense sheets are reserved for local play.\n\nThe sheet will be 160 by 90, yielding about fourteen thousand vertices. This is a balance between density and performance. If shots fail, the density will drop further. Updating normals per frame is acceptable.\n\nA simple glove mesh will be built from a vertex template and transformed in software each frame to follow the cursor. Story characters will be spawned as actors with bodies.\n\nSpawnActor may require too much physics setup for story characters, so a 3D room with HTML dialogue is the fallback. The plan is to try spawnActor after checking an example. Work will proceed in this order: simulation and tests first, then the boot page and title UI over a 3D room, then all screens in HTML, then audio, then the hub, then the one-file build, and finally end-to-end testing.\n\nI will check the spawnActor signature and defineLook fields for a simple lit look. I also need to review the vitest include pattern and ensure code passes prettier. Hand-written data files are more reliable than generated ones. I am weighing whether to test through createHeadless, since verification requires calling the shipped sim from a fresh start.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e85f547a-6261-4d69-8ffc-c36044882e07-39","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"testTimeout|include","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731","glob":"vitest*.{ts,mjs,js}"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e85f547a-6261-4d69-8ffc-c36044882e07-40","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/box/scenes/hall-props.ts","limit":60}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e85f547a-6261-4d69-8ffc-c36044882e07-41","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"LOOK_FIELDS|resolution|textures","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/engine/gfx/post/looks.ts","head_limit":30}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e85f547a-6261-4d69-8ffc-c36044882e07-42","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/scenes/play.test.ts","limit":80}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4583,"output_tokens":6410,"cache_read_input_tokens":101248,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"05de9f64-2d82-47d1-be1a-9f8ebb375bef"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-e85f547a-6261-4d69-8ffc-c36044882e07-39","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,95,95,120,53,57,99,49,100,51,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,70,111,117,110,100,32,49,32,109,97,116,99,104,105,110,103,32,108,105,110,101,115,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,95,95,120,53,57,99,49,100,51,47,119,111,114,107,47,84,114,97,117,109,97,45,67,111,109,112,97,115,115,45,84,114,97,117,109,97,45,67,101,110,116,101,114,45,83,101,99,111,110,100,45,79,112,105,110,105,111,110,45,103,114,111,107,45,52,46,55,45,120,104,105,103,104,45,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,47,116,111,111,108,115,47,115,116,114,121,107,101,114,47,118,105,116,101,115,116,46,99,111,110,102,105,103,46,116,115,10,50,48,58,32,32,116,101,115,116,58,32,123,32,46,46,46,98,97,115,101,46,116,101,115,116,44,32,116,101,115,116,84,105,109,101,111,117,116,58,32,49,56,48,95,48,48,48,44,32,104,111,111,107,84,105,109,101,111,117,116,58,32,49,56,48,95,48,48,48,44,32,114,101,112,111,114,116,101,114,115,58,32,91,39,100,111,116,39,93,44,32,111,117,116,112,117,116,70,105,108,101,58,32,117,110,100,101,102,105,110,101,100,32,125,44,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":1,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/tools/stryker/vitest.config.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":20,\"content\":\"  test: { ...base.test, testTimeout: 180_000, hookTimeout: 180_000, reporters: ['dot'], outputFile: undefined },\"}]}]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-e85f547a-6261-4d69-8ffc-c36044882e07-40","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The hall with its props, Box 3 (PLAN.md §9.0, WP 4.6): the hall with a hero (labs/box/scenes/hall.ts) and its\\n * props, standing in the scene through `spawnProp` (their bodies in the physics world, the page drawing them through\\n * the instanced prop meshes): the level's own, a brazier's fire on each of its 30 stands (`levelProps`), and\\n * `box.hallProps.count` crates and barrels, 10 to 300, on the first of `HALL_SPOTS`. The hero pushes them by walking\\n * into them (the controller shares his momentum with what he walks into) and shoves with `attack` (the third-person\\n * map's J, the left mouse button, the pad's west button): every prop within `SHOVE.reach` of him, in the arc\\n * `SHOVE.half` either side of where he faces and level with him, is hit (`hitProp`: its hook knocks a crate or a\\n * barrel away from him, each twist drawn from the sim stream `knock`), and he thrusts both hands forward for\\n10→ * `SHOVE.time` (his animator's pose `cast`), before he can shove again.\\n *\\n * `HALL_SPOTS` are carried from the prototype's (`my-3d2dge:src/stress-world/20-sim.js:141-171`, `PROP_SPOTS`): 300\\n * spots chosen once from a fixed seed (21, never the run's), each in the middle half of a random floor or gallery tile\\n * at least 2 tiles in from the walls, none within 3.75 m of the hero's start or 0.5 m of the dais's base; 60% are\\n * crates, and every fifth spot, when it and the one before are both crates, stacks on that one (12.2 units up, a\\n * jitter of a unit). Units are the prototype's sixteenths of a metre (PLAN.md Appendix C); yaw is drawn for every spot.\\n * They are computed when the module loads, outside the sim's maths, so only exact operations (no `Math.hypot`) choose\\n * them, and they are the same in Node and in every browser.\\n *\\n20→ * Settings (`box.hallProps.*`, read when the scene starts, so they change play and go in replays): how many crates and\\n * barrels stand. The hero's are the hall's (`box.hall.*`).\\n *\\n * Game code, sim-side: it imports only engine/sim-api.ts and the box's own files. Components: `hallSwing`, the hero's\\n * shove (whose it is, its ticks left).\\n *\\n * Tests: labs/box/scenes/hall-props.test.ts, tests/replays/box-3-knock.replay.json (`node x replay\\n * tests/replays/box-3-*.replay.json --browser page`), tests/e2e/box-hall.spec.ts, tests/e2e/parity.spec.ts.\\n *\\n * @example\\n30→ * // node x sim labs/box/scenes/hall-props --steps 600 --set box.hallProps.count=300\\n * // http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/box/?scene=hall-props&cam3=chase (J shoves)\\n * HALL_SPOTS.length; // 300\\n */\\nimport {\\n  angDiff,\\n  defineComponent,\\n  defineScene,\\n  defineSettings,\\n  floorHeight,\\n40→  hitProp,\\n  levelProps,\\n  pressed,\\n  Rng,\\n  spawnProp,\\n  type ActorComponent,\\n  type AnimatorComponent,\\n  type PropPlacement,\\n  type ScenePropComponent,\\n  type World,\\n50→} from '../../../engine/sim-api';\\nimport { HALL_LEVEL, HALL_START, setupHall } from './hall';\\nimport { boxSounds } from '../sounds/events';\\n\\n/** The prototype's units per metre (PLAN.md Appendix C). */\\nconst UNITS = 16;\\n\\n/** The scene's settings: how many crates and barrels. */\\nexport const HALL_PROPS_SETTINGS = defineSettings({\\n  'box.hallProps.count': {\\n60→    type: 'integer',\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The hall with its props, Box 3 (PLAN.md §9.0, WP 4.6): the hall with a hero (labs/box/scenes/hall.ts) and its\\n * props, standing in the scene through `spawnProp` (their bodies in the physics world, the page drawing them through\\n * the instanced prop meshes): the level's own, a brazier's fire on each of its 30 stands (`levelProps`), and\\n * `box.hallProps.count` crates and barrels, 10 to 300, on the first of `HALL_SPOTS`. The hero pushes them by walking\\n * into them (the controller shares his momentum with what he walks into) and shoves with `attack` (the third-person\\n * map's J, the left mouse button, the pad's west button): every prop within `SHOVE.reach` of him, in the arc\\n * `SHOVE.half` either side of where he faces and level with him, is hit (`hitProp`: its hook knocks a crate or a\\n * barrel away from him, each twist drawn from the sim stream `knock`), and he thrusts both hands forward for\\n10→ * `SHOVE.time` (his animator's pose `cast`), before he can shove again.\\n *\\n * `HALL_SPOTS` are carried from the prototype's (`my-3d2dge:src/stress-world/20-sim.js:141-171`, `PROP_SPOTS`): 300\\n * spots chosen once from a fixed seed (21, never the run's), each in the middle half of a random floor or gallery tile\\n * at least 2 tiles in from the walls, none within 3.75 m of the hero's start or 0.5 m of the dais's base; 60% are\\n * crates, and every fifth spot, when it and the one before are both crates, stacks on that one (12.2 units up, a\\n * jitter of a unit). Units are the prototype's sixteenths of a metre (PLAN.md Appendix C); yaw is drawn for every spot.\\n * They are computed when the module loads, outside the sim's maths, so only exact operations (no `Math.hypot`) choose\\n * them, and they are the same in Node and in every browser.\\n *\\n20→ * Settings (`box.hallProps.*`, read when the scene starts, so they change play and go in replays): how many crates and\\n * barrels stand. The hero's are the hall's (`box.hall.*`).\\n *\\n * Game code, sim-side: it imports only engine/sim-api.ts and the box's own files. Components: `hallSwing`, the hero's\\n * shove (whose it is, its ticks left).\\n *\\n * Tests: labs/box/scenes/hall-props.test.ts, tests/replays/box-3-knock.replay.json (`node x replay\\n * tests/replays/box-3-*.replay.json --browser page`), tests/e2e/box-hall.spec.ts, tests/e2e/parity.spec.ts.\\n *\\n * @example\\n30→ * // node x sim labs/box/scenes/hall-props --steps 600 --set box.hallProps.count=300\\n * // http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/box/?scene=hall-props&cam3=chase (J shoves)\\n * HALL_SPOTS.length; // 300\\n */\\nimport {\\n  angDiff,\\n  defineComponent,\\n  defineScene,\\n  defineSettings,\\n  floorHeight,\\n40→  hitProp,\\n  levelProps,\\n  pressed,\\n  Rng,\\n  spawnProp,\\n  type ActorComponent,\\n  type AnimatorComponent,\\n  type PropPlacement,\\n  type ScenePropComponent,\\n  type World,\\n50→} from '../../../engine/sim-api';\\nimport { HALL_LEVEL, HALL_START, setupHall } from './hall';\\nimport { boxSounds } from '../sounds/events';\\n\\n/** The prototype's units per metre (PLAN.md Appendix C). */\\nconst UNITS = 16;\\n\\n/** The scene's settings: how many crates and barrels. */\\nexport const HALL_PROPS_SETTINGS = defineSettings({\\n  'box.hallProps.count': {\\n60→    type: 'integer',\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/box/scenes/hall-props.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":60,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file The hall with its props, Box 3 (PLAN.md §9.0, WP 4.6): the hall with a hero (labs/box/scenes/hall.ts) and its\\n * props, standing in the scene through `spawnProp` (their bodies in the physics world, the page drawing them through\\n * the instanced prop meshes): the level's own, a brazier's fire on each of its 30 stands (`levelProps`), and\\n * `box.hallProps.count` crates and barrels, 10 to 300, on the first of `HALL_SPOTS`. The hero pushes them by walking\\n * into them (the controller shares his momentum with what he walks into) and shoves with `attack` (the third-person\\n * map's J, the left mouse button, the pad's west button): every prop within `SHOVE.reach` of him, in the arc\\n * `SHOVE.half` either side of where he faces and level with him, is hit (`hitProp`: its hook knocks a crate or a\\n * barrel away from him, each twist drawn from the sim stream `knock`), and he thrusts both hands forward for\\n * `SHOVE.time` (his animator's pose `cast`), before he can shove again.\\n *\\n * `HALL_SPOTS` are carried from the prototype's (`my-3d2dge:src/stress-world/20-sim.js:141-171`, `PROP_SPOTS`): 300\\n * spots chosen once from a fixed seed (21, never the run's), each in the middle half of a random floor or gallery tile\\n * at least 2 tiles in from the walls, none within 3.75 m of the hero's start or 0.5 m of the dais's base; 60% are\\n * crates, and every fifth spot, when it and the one before are both crates, stacks on that one (12.2 units up, a\\n * jitter of a unit). Units are the prototype's sixteenths of a metre (PLAN.md Appendix C); yaw is drawn for every spot.\\n * They are computed when the module loads, outside the sim's maths, so only exact operations (no `Math.hypot`) choose\\n * them, and they are the same in Node and in every browser.\\n *\\n * Settings (`box.hallProps.*`, read when the scene starts, so they change play and go in replays): how many crates and\\n * barrels stand. The hero's are the hall's (`box.hall.*`).\\n *\\n * Game code, sim-side: it imports only engine/sim-api.ts and the box's own files. Components: `hallSwing`, the hero's\\n * shove (whose it is, its ticks left).\\n *\\n * Tests: labs/box/scenes/hall-props.test.ts, tests/replays/box-3-knock.replay.json (`node x replay\\n * tests/replays/box-3-*.replay.json --browser page`), tests/e2e/box-hall.spec.ts, tests/e2e/parity.spec.ts.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * // node x sim labs/box/scenes/hall-props --steps 600 --set box.hallProps.count=300\\n * // http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/box/?scene=hall-props&cam3=chase (J shoves)\\n * HALL_SPOTS.length; // 300\\n */\\nimport {\\n  angDiff,\\n  defineComponent,\\n  defineScene,\\n  defineSettings,\\n  floo