{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_22","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Confirmed that boxGeometry is available in the page barrel.\n\nVerifying defineGeometry, defineMaterial, and defineProp are on the index. Starting the simulation core in step.ts with a linear design to prevent bot stalls. Sketching actTool behaviors: cursor position clamped to [-8, 8] by [-5, 5], tool switching emits a swap event, and use-edge tracks seat.wasUse.\n\nDesigning the scalpel tool logic. It selects the nearest unfinished guide within 1.2 meters or continues an active cut. If no guide is found, it slips once per gesture after 0.2 seconds of off-tick. Otherwise it advances along the path, samples distance to the next point, and finishes when the cut index reaches the end.\n\nFinalizing scalpel scoring: mean distance below 0.18 earns textbook, 0.4–0.7 rough, and 1.0 or above slip. Loop guides spawn a mass at their center if absent, or mark the linked mass grabbable. Releasing the use button clears the active cut but preserves progress for resumption.\n\nDesigning forceps grab logic: nearest grabbable within 0.55 meters is picked up on press; holding state is tracked.\n\nForceps release logic is now defined. Pull distance and speed determine whether the grab is clean or rough. A target is marked done on release. Flaps are lifted by pull fraction up to 1.2; if lift exceeds 0.8, the flap clips for eight seconds instead of closing. Releasing a held flap below that threshold causes it to flop.\n\nSuture tool rules are being drafted. It can only operate on an unfinished wound that has not been lacquered. If lacquer is present near the wound, suture use is refused with a gel-first message.\n\nSuture logic now tracks side changes via cross-product sign. A stitch is placed when the cursor crosses to a new side and enough distance has passed since the last stitch. The total stitch count is capped by the wound length divided by spacing.\n\nDrain tool rules are defined. It can only operate on a pool with volume above zero and no lacquer. Each use tick reduces volume by drainRate over sixty seconds. When volume reaches zero the task finishes and scores the pool.\n\nLaser tool mechanics are drafted. Heat builds up each tick until it exceeds six, then the beam cools for two seconds. While active it seeks targets within 0.45 meters.\n\nLaser target logic is now fully defined. Healthy targets accumulate a one-second slip timer before failing. Nodules burn for 1.2 seconds to finish. Bleeders burn for 0.4 seconds. Lulls burn for 1.5 seconds and spawn a cocoon on death. Solace generation three burns for one second and dies; lower generations refuse the laser. Motes burn for 0.6 seconds and die. Reverie surfaced burns until exposure ends. Matrons are refused unless a special condition applies. Balm cells clear themselves and reduce vitals by 0.5. Mercy is unavailable. Syringe mechanics are next.\n\nSyringe mechanics are now defined. It has two modes: stabilizer and binder. In stabilizer mode, pressing the use button charges a dose over time; releasing it above a threshold injects the band and triggers a two-second cooldown. In binder mode, pressing near a binder point accumulates a hold timer; after 0.4 seconds it doses and finishes if enough doses remain.\n\nGel tool rules are drafted. While the use button is held near eligible targets such as abrasions, numbs, closed wounds, or balm cells, a coverage counter ticks up. Each target requires twenty ticks of painting to be fully covered.\n\nGel coverage is simplified to a per-tick counter. A target needs thirty ticks times an area factor, and the task finishes at ninety percent completion. Balm cells clear themselves each tick within the gel radius.\n\nScanner logic is now defined. Pressing use reveals hidden targets on the site for six seconds and emits a find event. Decoys pop when scanned.\n\nRefusals trigger when the nearest target within 0.7 meters has no effect for the current tool and no effect was applied this tick; there is no penalty.\n\nTargets carry a phase index that controls phase advancement.\n\nPhase advancement requires all required targets of that phase to be done and script timers to expire. Each phase can define scripted events that fire at specific field seconds from its start.\n\nSpawn recipes will be data-driven and handled by a `spawnFrom` function.\n\nThe gala shift case includes two patients with wounds, shards, a lull, a balm node, and one flap. The goal is to close both patients before the four-minute thirty-second mark.\n\nFree surgery afterglow lists cases on the board. The bot test only needs the flow flag checked.\n\nColleague case details are next.\n\nCases 5 through 8 are flagged as colleague cases. During solo play the simulation adds an internal helper that drains pools or holds flaps when the player is idle. The bot test will not require the colleague to win; seat zero can complete the task alone if flaps can be clipped. Colleague logic will still spawn for the feature when multiple hands are present.\n\nThe simulation helper for colleague cases is visible and drains pools or holds flaps. In the two-seat bot test both seats are players, so no extra helper runs.\n\nSwap logic exchanges gift types between seats using a lead flag. Solo play uses it to switch between player and AI control. The bot can ignore this; the test will call swap to verify gifts exchange.\n\nShip roll case 5 introduces a periodic sine drift every seven seconds. The bot compensates by aiming at absolute targets each tick, so the hazard is manageable.\n\nScoring will use par scores filled after measurement. Rank ★ requires no slips and an S score on Attending for the Attending role; tests can verify this function directly.\n\nA test structure for tool pairs is being drafted.\n\nThe test structure iterates over every tool and target kind. Each pair is exercised as a full action: if an effect exists, the tool must trigger a state change or event; if not, it must refuse with no slips. This validates both successful interactions and correct refusals.\n\nA `probePair` helper will test each tool-target combination by creating a minimal operation and applying the tool, returning whether state changed or a refusal fired. Time-consuming effects like laser burns will be exercised across enough simulation steps. Empty space counts as healthy tissue for scalpel and laser only; using other tools on empty space triggers a slip.\n\nDrain on healthy tissue is a refusal with no penalty. The implementation is starting now.\n\nThe first milestone is verifying the skeleton and one-file boot. Sim tests will be written before packaging. Prettier formatting will run after writing files. TypeScript strict mode requires careful typing with no unused variables or enums; const objects replace enums. Vitest tests in labs/game can import from labs/game/op without engine dependencies. Starter tests remain, and npm test runs all tests including engine tests.\n\nI will not break engine tests. I will write files in this order: types and tools, simulation core, strains, cases, bot, story and director, then tests. I will run a focused test after writing to avoid CPU contention.\n\nTo stay under the 600-line cap, the simulation core will be split into five focused files: create, act, body, strains, and step. The Hand type is being drafted with position, tool selection, and button states.\n\nThe Hand type now includes a site index for switching sites, plus swap and hint flags. The Seat interface is being drafted with position, tool selection, score, chain, gift state, charges, gift timer, and lock mechanics.\n\nThe Seat interface is being expanded with lockSlow, syringe state and cooldown, laser heat and duration, hold target coordinates, cutting state, suture progress, stitch gaps, healthy tissue counter, slip flag, last gift tick, binder hold timer, memo, and color. The Op interface is also being drafted.\n\nThe Op interface is being completed with game state fields: difficulty, tick, field time, clock, slips, status, phase, sites, patients, seats, targets, nextId, code, balance, attract mode, and colleague flag.\n\nThe Op interface is being completed with helper state, roll flag, swapOk, events, bestChain, injected, giftUsed, taught, rankPar, closing, mercy, scriptDone, hint, and log. A fieldDelta function is drafted to freeze the clock during attract mode.\n\nGift activation logic is being defined. Pressing a gift during an active lock checks timing against the next beat. Otherwise it starts a new lock if charges remain, attract mode is active, or the gift is free. The lock begins with zero presses and a beat interval of thirty ticks. Field time slows by thirty percent while locked. Heart rate determines the beat interval as sixty plus eighty percent of the vitals gap from one hundred.\n\nAt seventy vitals the beat interval is roughly forty-three ticks. The gift window is about eight ticks, with perfect timing at half that width. A lock ends after three presses or four missed beats and sets giftLeft. Simple gifts last six seconds via a boolean flag. Stillpoint duration is measured in field ticks at one quarter speed, so ten point five seconds of effect equals forty-two real seconds.\n\nGift duration is now measured in simulation ticks rather than field ticks, so stillpoint extends the real-time window. The second pulse gift kind prevents vitals from falling and heals on clean hits.\n\nGifts started within sixty ticks of each other trigger a CONSULT event and double the duration for both seats. When vitals reach zero, a code block activates with eight minutes of field time, two tries, and a patient index. A band check every ninety ticks determines whether the paddle can revive the patient to twenty-five vitals or consume another try. The mercy code path reuses this same mechanic with free tries.\n\nMercy code will be implemented as a normal code block that the bot must pass; if it fails, the operation fails. A phase advance hook will trigger flatline once when the first site's wounds close. A `timeScale` option is being considered to allow multiple simulation steps per frame for end-to-end testing.\n\nThe simulation runs one step per call; the director page handles multiple steps when timeScale exceeds one. The bot moves at eight meters per second.\n\nCase 1 pools three volumes at (-3,0), (0,1), and (3,-1) with volume 2.5 and bleed 0.4. Phase 1 shards into four pieces. Phase 2 scripts vitals down to forty until a syringe injection occurs. Phase 3 adds three wounds. Phase 4 applies one gel abrasion before closing. Vitals start at seventy without gifts.\n\nCase 2 uses a single long guide approximated as a seven-meter polyline.\n\nCase 2 spawns bleeders along the cut after the guide finishes. Phase 2 reveals a hidden mass via scanner, then laser nodules and forceps it. Phase 3 stitches and closes with gel.\n\nCase 3 has three sites starting with two wounds each. Phase 0 clears all sites before flatline code triggers. Phase 1 waits for code resolution. Phase 2 applies three arterial bleeds at once and teaches stillpoint. Phase 3 closes all sites with gel.\n\nCase 4 follows a lull-to-matron-to-close sequence.\n\nCase 5 follows a flap, solace generations, mother under flap, and roll sequence. Case 6 uses two sites with reverie, decoys, and a consult that the bot can complete solo. Case 7 treats three patients with balm and one code per patient. Case 8 runs mercy phases before closing. The gala combines a small set of these elements.\n\nStrain spawning is being unified inside phase enter functions as data objects. Laser targets share a single health value that doubles as seconds of laser required: lull is 1.5, mote is 0.6, solace generation is 1, and nodule is 1.2.\n\nLull death spawns a cocoon. Matron laser is refused unless the target is vulnerable and the tool is a syringe. Solace laser only works on generation three. Revere requires three surfaced-time exposures totaling five seconds. The laser table must enforce these conditional effects.\n\nThe probe will test solace laser only in generation three, listing it as an effect. Refusals for lower generations are handled by the act function.\n\nLaser on matron is always refused and replaced by a binder. Solace splits when the cursor passes through it with a scalpel stroke of at least 1.5 meters; this replaces the target with two smaller generations unless it is generation three, which only splits via laser. The total alive count is capped at twelve. Reverie remains hidden until scanned, and laser may only strike it while surfaced.\n\nRevere surfaces for three seconds when hit by laser while hidden, accumulating damage only during that window. It submerges after three seconds and requires another scan to surface again. The bot can surface it immediately with a single laser strike, but the full five-second damage must be delivered across up to three separate surfaced periods.\n\nRevere will surface for two seconds so the bot must re-scan at least twice to deliver five seconds of damage. Decoys pop when scanned and are not the real target. Balm cells sit on a grid. Stitching or draining a lacquered wound applies balm if any cell is within half a meter. Gel clears cells in radius. Laser clears them faster and drains half a point of vitals. A node binder uses two doses via syringe; once done the cells fade over six seconds. Draining a balm cell is refused.\n\nStitching a balm cell is refused. Flap targets carry an `under` identifier and remain locked until the flap lifts above seventy percent or is clipped. A new `award` function assigns TEXTBOOK, CLEAN, ROUGH, or SLIP ratings to seats, resetting the chain on SLIP and failing the operation if the slip limit is exceeded.\n\nThe `award` function now computes score as `Math.round(base * mult * Math.min(chain, 2))`. Chain starts at zero and increments by 0.1 for every non-ROUGH rating, capped at one so the multiplier reaches two. A second pulse gift doubles that increment to 0.2.\n\nBase point values are now fixed: stitch 20, pool 30, shard 100, cut 200, mass 300, parasite 500, and phase 1000. End-of-operation bonuses award 10 points per second remaining, 20 per vital, 2000 for zero slips, and 1000 for an unused gift. The director flow test will use a 180000 ms timeout to accommodate all eight cases. Bot cursor speed is raised to 14 m/s so cases finish faster; drain rates and laser durations remain the main real-time bottlenecks.\n\nEight cases across three seeds should finish in about twelve seconds, so a sixty-second timeout is sufficient. The bot will assign each seat a target using a modulo check, falling back to help other seats if their targets are done. The simulation applies a site switch immediately when the hand site changes. Hints will appear as events carrying the operation's hint text.\n\nRefusals will name the first tool that has an effect on the target kind. The original Odie voice text will be used for refusal lines rather than copied source. Files will be composed carefully. PROGRESS.md will be updated periodically and written at the end. The plan checklist will not be marked complete until title, workspace, npm ci, verify, and one-file boot are all finished; a verify skeleton can be added early.\n\nThe implementation order is simulation, test, verify script, then page. A minimal one-file boot will use engine props like crate, barrel, and pillar to build a quick room for the OR. The level text will be a simple grid with an @ player marker.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-aa8e590b-87f6-4cc9-9859-842f557c25ce-55","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"cam3=fixed","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731","glob":"*.{ts,md}","head_limit":15}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-aa8e590b-87f6-4cc9-9859-842f557c25ce-56","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"prop:pillar|prop:crate|id: 'prop:","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/engine/world","glob":"*.ts","head_limit":20}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":478,"output_tokens":6699,"cache_read_input_tokens":126976,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"53534a20-c753-4a4d-81e4-c404a3e1b547"}
