{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_26","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Writing the core simulation now. I'll try to get it right on the first attempt.\n\nDesigning the effect table for act.ts. Each tool and kind pair maps to a measurable outcome or a refusal event. Probing will create an operation with one target in a vulnerable state, apply the action sequence, and verify whether effects occur or refusals happen.\n\nDefining partial effects for each tool-target pair. Scalpel cuts guide and solace. Forceps holds shards. Suture records stitches. Drain lowers pool volume. Laser burns nodules. Syringe doses binders. The stabilizer injects the patient rather than a target kind.\n\nSyringe on a wound will refuse as a stitch, not a dose. The patient body is kind `body`, so syringe probes charge and release to raise vitals. Drain probes reduce pool volume in one step.\n\nEach tool kind will be set up so one step produces a clear effect. Forceps holds a shard. Gel raises cover. Scanner reveals. Laser burns. Suture sets side. A scripted multi-step sequence may be needed for suturing.\n\nA `demonstrate` helper will run the correct script for each tool and kind pair, returning whether an effect occurs, state changes, or a refusal happens. Refusals will be tested with a single step of use. Specific refusal messages are being defined: scalpel on a wound says \"That is already open. Suture.\" Forceps targets include shard, mass, cocoon, tendril, and flap. Suture and drain only affect wounds that are not lacquer.\n\nLaser targets include nodules, bleeder, lull, solace, mote, surfaced reverie, balmCell, and mercy. Syringe covers body, binder, and matron. Gel covers abrasion, numb, closed wound, and balmCell. Scanner covers hidden mass, reverie, decoy, and nodule.\n\nLaser on mercy causes a refusal. Scalpel on mercy is refused until the phase 3 guide exists. Laser on body causes a slip after one second.\n\nLaser on body is an effect that produces a slip after sixty steps. Scalpel on body without a guide also slips after 0.2 seconds. Drain on body refuses with no penalty.\n\nThe full kind list now contains twenty-three entries, giving one hundred eighty-four tool-kind pairs. The effect table will default to refusals and then override with actual effects.\n\nThe effect table is now a flat Record keyed by tool-kind strings. A `pair()` helper returns either `{ effect: true }` or `{ effect: false, line }`. Every tool-kind combination must be covered; the test will assert no missing pairs. The `demonstrate()` helper will verify that effect pairs change state and refusal pairs do not penalize the player. Running all 184 demonstrations may be slow but each probe takes only a few steps.\n\nScanner on a nodule only reveals when hidden. Gel on a wound only heals when the wound is already closed. Suture on a lacquer wound is refused. Drain on a lacquer pool is refused. Laser on solace only works for generation three.\n\nSyringe on matron only works when the target is vulnerable. The `demonstrate` helper will be a generic switch that snapshots state before and after each script, then checks for refusal events. Missing cases will surface as test failures.\n\nThe `demonstrate` helper will snapshot targets, vitals, slips, and score before and after each script. Refusal probes move to the single target and use for five ticks; they expect no state change, a refusal event, and zero slips. Slip effects are detected by a positive slip count. Laser on body requires seventy ticks of use. The probe generator places exactly one target per pair so that a tool only affects its intended kind.\n\nThe `mini()` helper will place every target and the player at (0, 0) with the tool equipped. Special states are now defined for each kind: a guide path, a two-step wound line, generation-three solace with one health, a surfaced reverie, a vulnerable matron, and hidden mass or nodule for scanner probes.\n\nFinalizing target states for each kind: a pool with volume two, a lull at half health, and binders or flaps at the origin. The demonstration scripts will be mechanical hand sequences driven by a `runDemo` helper. The test file will contain these scripts while the shipped `act.ts` handles the core logic.\n\nThe test will iterate every tool-kind pair and assert that effect pairs change state or progress, while refusal pairs trigger a refusal with zero slips and no target movement. Setup and driving logic will live in the test file as input sequences rather than inside the shipped simulation.\n\nReconsidering the test structure. Instead of duplicating setup logic in the test file, the simulation itself should export `pairFixture` and `pairDrive`. The test would then only verify outcomes. This risks a false positive if the drive helper reimplements success conditions incorrectly.\n\nThe test will not reimplement the rules. It will only assert that the world state changes in a kind-appropriate way after each probe. A typed EffectName union will map every tool-kind pair to its expected outcome.\n\nThe simulation will export a `Spawn` interface to define every target's exact state, including position, health, generation, path, and visibility. A separate test will verify that laser on early solace still emits the warmth refusal line even though the effect table marks it as an effect.\n\nThe Spawn interface now includes optional x2, z2, under, and tag fields. PhaseDef defines an id, hint, spawn list, until condition, optional onEnter trigger, and teach line. CaseDef structures a full surgical case with surgeon and patient details, vitals, postcards, time limit, sites, and ordered phases.\n\nCaseDef now includes flags for gift, charges, colleague, swap, roll, code, and newRule. Mercy is being restructured as a multi-phase surgical case rather than a standalone strain module. Case 8 begins with an open phase requiring a guide incision on the heart. A motes phase spawns six motes and waits for all motes and three binders to finish. A kindness phase triggers flatline on entry, clears the code flag, spawns three wounds, and repeats via an onEnter counter.\n\nMercy case phase four spawns tendrils and a moving guide, then grants the gift, spawns binders that require it, and waits for final code. Phase five closes with gel and stitch. The mercy strain step will handle mote respawns and sleep rings. Motes respawn only while fewer than six are alive and fewer than six have been killed; once six unique motes die, spawning stops and an exposed window opens.\n\nThe mercy case win condition is now defined as no motes and three binders. Mote respawns stop once all current motes die, opening an exposed window that lasts five seconds; if the bot fails to place three binders in time, six motes return. The scalpel solace split logic is being drafted to accumulate strokes while the cursor moves inside the target.\n\nSolace strokes accumulate while the cursor moves inside the target. Once a gen-one solace reaches 1.5 strokes, it splits into two gen-two pieces. Each gen-two then splits into two gen-three pieces. The simulation caps total solace at twelve; if the cap is reached, a stroke kills one instead of spawning. Mother solace is a special gen-one variant hidden under a flap and treated as solace with tag \"mother\". Initial spawns place two gen-one solace.\n\nThe solace splitting math is verified: two initial gen-one pieces plus the mother solace can reach twelve total alive, peaking at eight gen-three pieces. The bot will stroke a gen-two solace by moving the cursor from x minus 0.9 to x plus 0.9 while holding use, accumulating 1.8 strokes to trigger split.\n\nFor the reverie case, the bot will scan until the target is visible, then laser it to surface and keep burning. It will rescan if the target hides again.\n\nReverie surface lasts 2.2 seconds and hides the target again after five total laser ticks, so the bot must rescan before each new burn. Balm case will use a 4x4 grid of sixteen gel cells spread over time; gelling is faster than lasering them.\n\nSweeping the gel grid at eight meters per second clears it quickly. Lacquer blocks stitch, so the bot must gel nearby cells before stitching a wound. The lull case uses a one-and-a-half-second laser followed by forceps to cocoon. The matron case waits for vulnerability, then syringes two binders that each deal fifty percent damage; the bot can land both during one two-and-a-half-second yawn. Numb patches drain vitals at zero point two per second, so the bot prioritizes syringing them when vitals fall below fifty.\n\nNumb patches will drain vitals at 0.15 per second, but the bot will gel them only when no urgent target is present. The stabilizer provides +24 vitals with a two-second cooldown, which can sustain the bot through minor drains.\n\nThe clock field delta is set to 100 per tick, so field time matches real seconds outside stillpoint. When the final closing phase clears its targets, the status becomes won and an end bonus is added. Fail messages will reuse the original lines.\n\nA hash function is being added. A network sequencer test is being drafted with a Net interface for latency, jitter, loss, and packet queues, plus a tick loop that delivers packets and routes unreliable client inputs to the host.\n\nThe host will build hands from the latest received counters and positions, then broadcast them to clients. Host ticks will be sent in batches every four ticks to achieve fifteen hertz. Clients will step using the exact same hand sequence in the same order so that hashes match at each applied tick index.\n\nInput loss is handled deterministically: the host keeps the last known hand for a seat when no new packet arrives, and a leaver at tick 1000 causes the host to drop that hand. Clients do not simulate their own local cursor, so every copy applies the same broadcast hands and hashes match. The test will compare hashes to catch mismatches. A Save interface for the Director is being drafted.\n\nThe Save interface now tracks difficulty, cleared cases, ranks, postcards, and free or attending flags. The Director struct holds the current mode, save data, case index, story key and timestamp, active operation, and last rank. Four core functions are defined: newGame, advanceStory, startOp, and finishOp.\n\nThe Director flow is now defined: newGame starts with the story before case one, then brief, op, debrief, and story again. After case eight the story sets the credits flag and transitions to the free mode. Story keys are named s1a, s1b, and so on.\n\nA playStory function is being drafted to run a seeded resident game through all cases. It advances story, brief, and debrief modes automatically while stepping operations, and exits once the credits or free mode is reached.\n\nThe playStory loop now steps operations with bot hands while the status is playing, then finishes the operation. It returns the free flag, cleared cases, current mode, and guard count.\n\nStory content will be sixteen scenes of twelve lines each, written directly into the file with earnest specificity rather than placeholder text.\n\nPostcards will be drafted for patients Tomás, Gwen, Hal, Ana, Achterberg, three donors, and Voss.\n\nA rank function is being drafted to assign C, B, A, S, or ★ based on score divided by par, with a special star rank for perfect scores on attending difficulty. Par will be updated from measured bot scores; the test will validate that stored par stays within fifteen percent of the current score.\n\nA dev balance live-update API is being added with clamped slider setters and a reset function, plus an exported keys array. An `opView` helper will expose operation state as a plain object for the page HUD. The simulation design is now complete and ready to be written.\n\nA module-level `bridge.ts` will hold a live operation reference so the page and scene can share it. Scene tests may write to it, but simulation tests will not touch it. The `game:hands` intent is plain data, so it decodes directly into the operation step.\n\nHand objects will be encoded as plain number arrays for the intent channel, with `encodeHands` and `decodeHands` helpers. The simulation step accepts `Hand[]`, the scene decodes incoming data, and tests pass raw arrays directly. The code will be written in multiple file calls while preserving the starter files, with required file comments added. The `**` operator will be avoided, and TypeScript imports will use `import type` where possible.\n\nUnused exports will be kept to avoid ESLint warnings, and `any` types will be avoided. The main source files are estimated at roughly 200 to 400 lines each, with act.ts potentially exceeding 600 lines and needing a split. Writing is beginning now, starting with the bot hand selection pseudocode.\n\nThe bot hand selection logic is being drafted. It starts with a base hand using the seat's position, tool, and default false flags. Pulse lock in progress triggers gift on the correct beat. Code mode triggers paddle during a thirty-to-fifty-five tick band. Low vitals will prompt syringing the worst patient.\n\nThe bot hand selection now includes a low-vitals branch that syringes the worst patient when vitals fall below forty-two and no binder is nearby. The target picker filters out completed cases and only selects targets in the current operation phase.\n\nTarget urgency scores are now defined for each case kind, from deep wounds at one hundred to gel finish at forty. Equal-urgency targets are assigned by seat index modulo player count. The bot will prioritize pool bleeds and deep wounds first, then proceed to shards, mass, and tendril cases with forceps, guide cases with scalpel paths, and wound cases using suture weave.\n\nBot hand selection now covers every remaining case kind. Nodule, bleeder, lull, and mote targets use laser sit and use. Solace uses scalpel stroke memo when generation is below three, otherwise laser. Reverie uses hidden scanner if present, otherwise laser. Decoy uses scanner. Binder uses syringe hold. Matron waits when vulnerable, otherwise syringes. Abrasion and numb use gel sit and use. Balm cell uses gel. Balm node uses syringe binder. Flap uses forceps lift. Mass hidden uses scanner.\n\nThe seat memo format is `k:${target.id}:${step}`. It resets whenever the target id changes.\n\nThe pull sequence has two steps. Step zero moves to the target, uses when close, then sets step one. Step one moves to a destination offset by (1.4, 0), moves at five meters per second, and keeps using until done.\n\nGrab mechanics now use the bot's current position as the origin when a grab starts. The bot must be on the target before moving away.\n\nScalpel memo path following begins at index zero. The bot moves to the first point without using until within 0.15 distance, then uses and proceeds along the cut path.\n\nSuture memo follows a zigzag step by step. The bot keeps using while weaving.\n\nSyringe charge memo tracks charging state. The bot uses while syringe level is below 0.85, then stops for one tick.\n\nGift teaching is now defined. The bot presses gift once when the phase teaches stillpoint and charges are available, provided the pulse lock is finished and gift is not already active. The operation stores the phase teach string on entry so the bot can detect it. The next check to add is whether a flap is open.\n\nA flap is considered open when its clipped count is positive or its lift exceeds 0.75. The target under field stores the flap identifier.\n\nThe mercy guide path advances by 0.015 meters per tick, but the motion speed is being reduced to 0.2 meters per second so the bot can track it reliably. The cut index advances when the bot reaches within 0.22 of the current point.\n\nShip roll will also be added.\n\nShip roll will gently drift each seat cursor by 0.12 meters every seven seconds, applied after the hand is processed so the bot can correct on the next tick. This keeps suturing and scalpel work within safe margins. The plan requires this cursor drift, so it will be implemented as written.\n\nA race condition was spotted in the target selection logic. When multiple bots run in parallel, they may both pick the same target before either writes its memo. The director calls all bot hands before advancing the step, so the memo is not yet set when the next bot evaluates targets. Both bots could then attempt to grab the same shard at once. Forceps will let one hold and the other slip or refuse.\n\nA claim field will be added to each target so the first bot to evaluate it can reserve it. pickTarget checks this claim and sets it when no seat holds it, preventing duplicate grabs. The claim clears once the target is completed.\n\nFor multi-seat operations, some phase-zero spawns will be duplicated and offset by 0.8 meters so extra seats have their own targets.\n\nParasite health scales with the number of seats using a balance.coopHp multiplier defaulting to 0.6.\n\nPhase clearing rules are now defined. The injected phase clears once the operation flag is set. The code phase clears when the codeCleared flag is true, which resets on flatline and sets on success. The gift phase needs no clear condition. The clear phase waits until all targets in that phase are finished.\n\nPhase transitions will spawn the next phase's targets automatically. onEnter effects run only once per phase entry. Mercy phase logic will stay inside phases rather than strains.\n\nCase 8 is being structured as a repeating loop. The first phase is the incision guide, followed by motes that spawn binders once cleared. The second phase is code until success, with flatline on entry. The third phase is three wounds, and the plan is to repeat this sequence rather than use a round counter.\n\nCase 8 now has eight phases. Kindness and wounds alternate twice. Then comes the moving guide, tendrils, binders during gift with a charge grant on entry, final code, and close gel plus one stitch.\n\nThe mercy opening phase is just a guide.\n\nCase 4 is being structured as a reopen guide, three lulls, then cocoons spawned by lull death that extend the current phase until all are cleared, followed by a matron phase.\n\nMatron phase lasts until the matron dies. Cocoons spawn mid-phase via `addTarget` when lulls die, so the phase waits for them. Matron birth is capped at two extra lulls every ten seconds to prevent overload; the bot will laser them and they spawn cocoons too. Matron births stop once the matron dies. The strains module will run every step to drain vitals from any active lull aura and manage the lull nuzzle timer.\n\nThe strains module will also manage the matron yawn timer and births, the solace latch timer, reverie submerge, balm spread, mercy mote respawn and sleep, guide motion, flap clip countdown, reveal timeout, and numb drain. Vitals will sum all active drains from targets and effects.\n\nVitals drains are now quantified. Pools bleed at 0.5× balance bleed per second. Wounds bleed 0.5 or 1.2 depending on depth. Lulls and the matron drain 0.6. Numb drains 0.2. Balm cells drain 0.01. Pulse lock suppresses all drains. Vitals are capped at 100 and clamped to zero.\n\nWounds stay bleeding until stitched. Nearby pools grow from open bleeds at 0.02 volume per tick, capped at 8, so treating one site worsens others.\n\nPools grow by 0.15 volume per second when their site is unoccupied, capped at the pool's maximum. Code starts fail immediately if the case has no code flag or if tries are exhausted.\n\nCase 7 will allow one code attempt per patient. The attract phase caps vitals at 30 and prevents failure while freezing all other effects. I am ready to write the implementation files now, and I will compose sixteen story scenes with box counts between 12 and 35.\n\nThe story scene structure is now defined with Box and Scene interfaces. Speakers will vary across scenes. A deterministic voice module maps each character to a fixed pitch profile by hashing syllables, ensuring identical audio output for the same input.\n\nUI strings will avoid banned words and TODO placeholders. The scene file will use the id `game:main`. Settings keys are distinct enough to prevent double-registration.\n\nThe engine import path is confirmed valid for tests. Coordinates will stay within -4 to 4 so the camera sees them, though the simulation ignores bounds. Stitching needs three points per 1.2 meter wound with 0.45 meter spacing, and a line helper function is being drafted.\n\nA line helper is being drafted to generate evenly spaced points between two coordinates. An arc helper will produce roughly seven meters of curve.\n\nThe loop guide will trace a circular path. Once the bot cuts through it, the center mass will be locked until the guide completes, then become grabbable. The phase order is being adjusted so the guide finishes before the mass forceps phase spawns the mass.\n\nCase 2 is now a single phase. The buried mass, nodules, and loop guide all appear after the scan. The mass stays locked until the guide finishes. The bot will scan first to reveal hidden targets, then laser nodules and cut the guide. It will not attempt to grab the locked mass.\n\nScanner priority will target the centroid of hidden required targets. Masses will carry a `locked` flag. The plan checklist will be updated as items are completed. Code comments must avoid placeholders, TODOs, stubs, and lorem text.\n\nScalpel ratings will be TEXTBOOK, CLEAN, ROUGH, or SLIP based on mean cut distance. Forceps speed is measured as distance per second; a pull exceeding eight speed units marks ROUGH and spawns a small pool bleed. Suture evenness is judged by the standard deviation of stitch gaps. Samples are recorded only while cutting a locked guide. Character names Adam and Bliss are avoided, along with Greek weekday names.\n\nSuture ratings are now defined by stitch gap tolerance: gaps within 0.15 of spacing earn TEXTBOOK, within 0.3 earn CLEAN, and wider gaps earn ROUGH. Inject ratings depend on charge level, awarding TEXTBOOK at 0.75–0.95, CLEAN at 0.5–0.75, and ROUGH otherwise, with a two-second cooldown. A chain display will be added. The test file will be written alongside the implementation for quick validation. Case par will be verified by running the bot once and checking that its score yields rank S while half that score yields rank C.\n\nThe `writePar` function will store the case par value. A test for `case:first-cut` will run the bot once, assert a win, and verify the returned score stays within five percent of the stored par. The total score is the sum of all seat scores, and team rank derives from that total. If the bot loses, the operation object will include failure details, phase, and target summaries to aid debugging.\n\nA `debugOp` helper will format operation state for debugging. Bot tests may be split into separate `it` blocks per case with a 60-second timeout so individual failures are visible. Real drain rules will be used instead of fast variants.\n\nBalance drain is set to 2.2 volume per second, so three pools clear in about three seconds plus travel time. Laser durations are now tied to balance and difficulty scaling: nodules take 1.2 seconds, while lulls scale by a base 1.5 seconds multiplied by the target’s strain health and a difficulty multiplier of 0.75 for interns, 1 for residents, and 1.25 for attendings. The bot will simply hold the laser use button until each target finishes, without tracking exact durations. I also need to prevent accidental SLIP from laser heat.\n\nLaser heat accumulates during use and decays when idle. The bot will hold the laser button through the two-second cooldown so the beam resumes once the target is done. Scanner range is three meters, and the bot will move to the centroid of grouped targets within that radius.\n\nA handbook module will derive page text from the tools and effects tables to prevent stale data. The director must walk through every operation, debrief, credits, and free surgery in sequence. Story scenes will be short and punchy with fourteen boxes each for margins.\n\nThe automated flow test will begin at the title screen and drive the shipped sequence through story, briefing, operation, debrief, and credits. A postcard is part of the debrief. After the final story, the test sets `save.free` and `save.attending` to true. The board and title are excluded from the automated test because it only verifies the flow starting from the title.\n\nA `bootDirector` function will initialize the director in title mode. A `choose` helper will select new game, board, continue, or credits. The automated flow test will boot a director, choose new game, step through story and operations, then verify that free mode is unlocked and the sequence reaches credits. Save data will live inside the director and persist through a store.\n\nA stable hash will be built by rounding numbers to three decimals and sorting keys before JSON.stringify. Events are cleared at the start of each step so the page reads the previous step’s events on the next frame. An `op.score` getter will sum seat scores. The code will be written in tight parallel batches.\n\nSuture stitch logic now requires at least one crossing per stitch, so alternating sides works correctly. The guide cut threshold is set to 0.22 meters between points, preventing the bot from skipping waypoints. For loop guides, the path includes the return to the start as the final point, and the cut completes once the bot reaches that last point.\n\nA guide will unlock its mass target when it completes. After a guide finishes, three small bleeders spawn at the incision point. The shared types file will be written first, followed by the rest of the code. TypeScript index signatures will be explicit to avoid complaints. Tests will run after the core is in place. A colleague character is optional.\n\nA solo AI colleague will be added automatically for cases five through eight. When a solo operation definition specifies a colleague, `createOp` will inject a second AI seat. The bot test runs every seat with `botHand`, so the extra seat is handled without breaking solo assertions.\n\nThe AI colleague will be an internal policy inside `stepOp` rather than a separate seat. It only activates when the operation has exactly one player seat and a colleague definition. It then targets the nearest unfinished pool, small lull, or closed flap to drain it slightly each tick.\n\nThe AI colleague will also lift a closed flap by clipping its under target. It reports its current action in the operation state so the page can display a second hand. If the colleague drains pools, the player may finish alone, but the bot must still demonstrate solace.\n\nThe AI colleague will drain pools at 0.3 volume per second so it cannot instantly finish primary objectives. The real help is holding flaps closed. A swap action will toggle the lead seat and exchange gift personas between stillpoint and second. Both seats pressing gift within thirty ticks will each receive a 1.5x duration bonus.\n\nConsult timing will be tested by pressing gift simultaneously on both seats. The first press starts a six-second pulse lock, and the second within sixty ticks triggers a 1.5x duration bonus for both seats. Stillpoint will be verified by comparing field advancement over sixty steps with and without the gift active. A second pulse test will use a bleeding wound: activating pulse lock prevents vitals from dropping, and a clean action afterward restores them by 1.5.\n\nA simple gift mode will be toggled by an `op.simple` boolean defaulting to false. The bot will use the full pulse lock in this path, and the separate test will cover gift rules. Case 3 will use nasty arterials so the bot cannot win without stillpoint, ensuring the gift is exercised.\n\nThe bot will use stillpoint during teach-mode cases so the playthrough exercises the mechanic. A beat-timing lock is being added: pressing gift starts a 40-tick window, and the bot scores good or perfect based on how close its press lands to that target tick.\n\nThe beat-timing lock will score perfect on a 4-tick window, good on an 8-tick window, and miss otherwise. The bot will press gift exactly when `op.tick` equals `seat.lockBeat`. A tick-ordering bug was spotted: the bot reads the current tick before step increments it, which would misalign the timing check. The fix is to either increment the tick at the end of the step or have the bot use the tick that step will apply.\n\nThe tick contract is now fixed: `botHand` receives the tick about to be simulated, and `stepOp` increments the tick only after processing that hand. This resolves the off-by-one timing bug.\n\nThe lock beat is scheduled as an absolute tick number. The bot presses gift exactly when `op.tick` equals that beat, and the step function records the press at that same tick for a perfect score. If the bot misses three beats in a row, it still receives a three-second duration bonus. A perfect lock yields 10.5 seconds of gift time.\n\nThe lock duration formula caps at 10.5 seconds and scales with perfect and good beats. A perfect lock yields 630 ticks of gift time. Stillpoint applies a field delta of 25 while the gift is active. The finishLock function converts seconds into ticks, sets the gift kind, marks the lock complete, records the last gift tick, and checks for consult. Surgeons carry a gift type while residents have none; pressing gift does nothing for a resident. Case 1 starts with zero charges, so the bot will not press.\n\nSeat 0 will carry the case gift, with seat 1 receiving second if present. In solo cases, seat 0 defaults to wren stillpoint and the swap action flips it to second. Seats 2 and 3 remain without gifts.\n\nFor case 6, a solo operation with a colleague will have the AI colleague start a gift within one second of the player's press. This triggers consult timing while the test itself uses two direct seats.\n\nA debug report module will be added at `labs/game/op/report.ts`. A custom test operation builder is being considered to verify that sites worsen over time, since a tiny custom op is simpler than modifying case 3.\n\nA crosstalk test will use case 3 phase 0 to verify that scripted hands on site 0 increase site 1 pool volume. The slip limit test will use case 1: laser on the body for enough time to exceed the limit. The resident slip limit is five, so the sixth slip fails the operation. Interns get ten slips and attendings get three.\n\nA custom short clock will be created by mutating the clock field on a standard operation after creation. This avoids adding an extra drill case to the board list.\n\nFlatline tests will use case 3. Setting vitals to zero makes code available immediately. The test will step until bandage and paddle, expecting vitals to reach at least twenty-five. If the bot never paddles, the operation should fail after eight seconds of field time.\n\nA difficulty test will verify that intern, resident, and attending operations scale with longer clocks and stricter slip limits. Parasite health on case 4 lull will be checked. The code will be written in chunks and run as tests. JSDoc `@file` tags will be added to the top of each file.\n\nThe `@file` JSDoc tag will be placed at the top of each file. A null hand value means no change, so stepOp will not move cursors or consume field time; the world state remains paused until a valid hand arrives.\n\nA `runBot` cap and failure dump will be added to the test harness. The score par will be measured dynamically with a node script, since tests check stored values. Nine case IDs are now defined: `case:first-cut`, `case:quiet-lump`, `case:crosstalk`, `case:it-moves`, `case:struck-off`, `case:reverie`, `case:gala-night`, `case:mercy`, and `case:gala-shift`. A free surgery selectable gift will be added so the board can override a seat's gift via `createFromCase(id, { gift: 'second' })`.\n\nThe story will be generated programmatically from authored arrays, with each scene requiring fourteen boxes; fewer than twelve will fail the test. Eight drama cuts are planned across the narrative, including the chief's suspension, Sabine's second opinion, the chief's scar, confession, Voss's fall, Odie's moral line, and the final \"CASE FILE REMAINS OPEN\" line.\n\nA mass-flinch drama event will fire when the `flinch` target tag is removed from a case. The verify script will be added early so `npm run verify` is available, though it will only pass once the test suite is green.\n\nThe HTML UI architecture is being planned to avoid structural conflicts. The page will host the hub, inline session logic, a WebGPU check, a canvas, and a DOM root. The simulation will run on the page rather than inside the engine step, so end-to-end tests can read `window.__compass` state directly.\n\nThe engine scene renders the room and sheet from `liveOp` state. The page calls `stepOp` on a timer or inside `onFrame`. Because `onFrame` delta varies, the simulation must step at a fixed 60Hz. The hub path will advance the sim explicitly using `AIHub.time() * 60 / 1000`, stepping until the due tick is reached. The solo path may instead step once per frame based on wall clock.\n\nSolo stepping will accumulate `frame.dt` and step the operation once per frame at a fixed 60Hz. Replays are handled by the engine, so solo simulation does not need cross-machine determinism. The starter replay remains unaffected.\n\nThe simulation will be driven by the engine's `step` method with a `game:hands` intent, keeping a single clock for both attract and bot. The page will still set axis each frame and call the engine step for hub tests. A headless scene runner is an alternative but heavier.\n\nThe page and Node paths will both call `stepOp` directly, bypassing `engine.step`. The engine scene renders the view while the shared function drives gameplay. This keeps the lockstep hash in sync without needing the engine world to stay in lockstep.\n\nThe page will step the simulation inside `onFrame` once per frame, while the hub sequencer advances it on its own tick aligned to `AIHub.time()`. When the hub is active, `onFrame` will not step the simulation to avoid double-stepping. The mesh will be updated from the operation state each frame to keep visuals in sync. The engine scene still needs a fixed camera and room setup so the view remains visible even though the simulation runs outside the engine loop.\n\nThe page will use `engine.inspector.set` and `inspector.seed` to switch between the OR set and the HTML story overlay. If `set` alone does not restart setup, `seed` will be called explicitly. The 3D background will adjust camera position and prop visibility for each scene.\n\nA simpler architecture is chosen: one OR diorama stays visible at all times, with the story overlay using the same room and HTML dialogue. The field sheet appears during operations. Spawning real actors is considered but may be fragile; the first version will rely on the room, sheet, and HTML for visuals. If the API proves easy, actors can be added later.\n\nThe page module will define a level and a player hidden under the floor for lighting. Props for the lamp, table, and sheet will be spawned via `defineProp` before engine start. The live mesh implementation follows the plan's geometry pattern exactly.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-9afa715a-430f-4444-84b3-a57c11c99dcd-61","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"setAttribute\\('position'","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731","glob":"*.ts","head_limit":15}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-9afa715a-430f-4444-84b3-a57c11c99dcd-62","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/engine/gfx/geometry/box.ts","limit":40}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":220,"output_tokens":16715,"cache_read_input_tokens":139008,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"e82849a9-6ac3-46a0-ad52-a885b6879dca"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-9afa715a-430f-4444-84b3-a57c11c99dcd-61","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,95,95,120,53,57,99,49,100,51,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,70,111,117,110,100,32,53,32,109,97,116,99,104,105,110,103,32,108,105,110,101,115,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,95,95,120,53,57,99,49,100,51,47,119,111,114,107,47,84,114,97,117,109,97,45,67,111,109,112,97,115,115,45,84,114,97,117,109,97,45,67,101,110,116,101,114,45,83,101,99,111,110,100,45,79,112,105,110,105,111,110,45,103,114,111,107,45,52,46,55,45,120,104,105,103,104,45,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,47,101,110,103,105,110,101,47,103,102,120,47,103,101,111,109,101,116,114,121,47,119,114,105,116,101,114,46,116,115,10,49,52,50,58,32,32,32,32,32,32,103,101,111,109,101,116,114,121,46,115,101,116,65,116,116,114,105,98,117,116,101,40,39,112,111,115,105,116,105,111,110,39,44,32,110,101,119,32,70,108,111,97,116,51,50,66,117,102,102,101,114,65,116,116,114,105,98,117,116,101,40,112,111,115,105,116,105,111,110,115,44,32,51,41,41,59,10,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,95,95,120,53,57,99,49,100,51,47,119,111,114,107,47,84,114,97,117,109,97,45,67,111,109,112,97,115,115,45,84,