{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_45","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The plan is to write cases.ts, patch world.ts for under-links, write bot.ts, and add a test.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-3230987f-9028-4853-a034-c4a93fa083bb-22","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/scenes/play.test.ts","limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-3230987f-9028-4853-a034-c4a93fa083bb-23","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"max-lines|labs/game","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/eslint.config.js"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":20572,"output_tokens":240,"cache_read_input_tokens":69760,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"71fdccf0-94d2-4a49-b7ad-c73ed8bf1582"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-3230987f-9028-4853-a034-c4a93fa083bb-22","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The starter game's tests (labs/game/scenes/play.ts): what every game made from it starts with, and keeps as it\\n * grows. They run headless in Node (`npm test`), in a few seconds:\\n * - its golden replay (labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json, the bot's play) gives its golden hashes, as `x replay`\\n *   checks it: on another platform than the one that recorded them, two runs agree instead;\\n * - a run continues exactly after a capture and a restore;\\n * - the bot (labs/game/bot.ts) wins on 10 seeds, so the game can be won, however the gems fall;\\n * - the clock runs out on a player who stands still, and the settings change play;\\n * - two actors that reach a gem in the same step take it once.\\n10→ *\\n * When the game's play changes on purpose, record the goldens again: `node x replay labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json\\n * --browser sim --update` (in Node and Chromium), and say why in the commit.\\n * @see labs/game/scenes/play.ts\\n */\\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';\\nimport { createHeadless } from '../../../engine';\\nimport { defineScene, type World } from '../../../engine/sim-api';\\nimport { botIntents, playBot, roundOf, viewOf } from '../bot';\\n20→import { spawnPlayer } from '../cast/player';\\nimport { play, type GameComponents } from './play';\\n\\n/** The golden replay: the bot's play from seed 1, with the goldens `x replay --update` recorded. */\\nconst REPLAY = readFileSync(new URL('../replays/bot.replay.json', import.meta.url), 'utf8');\\n\\n/** This platform's golden key, as replays write it. */\\nconst PLATFORM = `${process.platform}-${process.arch}`;\\n\\n/** Plays `text` from step 0 in a fresh engine and returns its hashes at the replay's checkpoints. */\\n30→async function replayed(text: string): Promise<Record<string, string>> {\\n  const engine = await createHeadless({ scene: play });\\n  const result = await (engine.replay as (text: string) => Promise<{ hashes: Record<string, string> }>)(text);\\n  return result.hashes;\\n}\\n\\ndescribe('game:play', () => {\\n  it('replays the bot’s play to its golden hashes', async () => {\\n    const goldens = (JSON.parse(REPLAY) as { hashes: Record<string, Record<string, string>> }).hashes;\\n    const first = await replayed(REPLAY);\\n40→    if (goldens[PLATFORM]) expect(first).toEqual(goldens[PLATFORM]);\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The starter game's tests (labs/game/scenes/play.ts): what every game made from it starts with, and keeps as it\\n * grows. They run headless in Node (`npm test`), in a few seconds:\\n * - its golden replay (labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json, the bot's play) gives its golden hashes, as `x replay`\\n *   checks it: on another platform than the one that recorded them, two runs agree instead;\\n * - a run continues exactly after a capture and a restore;\\n * - the bot (labs/game/bot.ts) wins on 10 seeds, so the game can be won, however the gems fall;\\n * - the clock runs out on a player who stands still, and the settings change play;\\n * - two actors that reach a gem in the same step take it once.\\n10→ *\\n * When the game's play changes on purpose, record the goldens again: `node x replay labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json\\n * --browser sim --update` (in Node and Chromium), and say why in the commit.\\n * @see labs/game/scenes/play.ts\\n */\\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';\\nimport { createHeadless } from '../../../engine';\\nimport { defineScene, type World } from '../../../engine/sim-api';\\nimport { botIntents, playBot, roundOf, viewOf } from '../bot';\\n20→import { spawnPlayer } from '../cast/player';\\nimport { play, type GameComponents } from './play';\\n\\n/** The golden replay: the bot's play from seed 1, with the goldens `x replay --update` recorded. */\\nconst REPLAY = readFileSync(new URL('../replays/bot.replay.json', import.meta.url), 'utf8');\\n\\n/** This platform's golden key, as replays write it. */\\nconst PLATFORM = `${process.platform}-${process.arch}`;\\n\\n/** Plays `text` from step 0 in a fresh engine and returns its hashes at the replay's checkpoints. */\\n30→async function replayed(text: string): Promise<Record<string, string>> {\\n  const engine = await createHeadless({ scene: play });\\n  const result = await (engine.replay as (text: string) => Promise<{ hashes: Record<string, string> }>)(text);\\n  return result.hashes;\\n}\\n\\ndescribe('game:play', () => {\\n  it('replays the bot’s play to its golden hashes', async () => {\\n    const goldens = (JSON.parse(REPLAY) as { hashes: Record<string, Record<string, string>> }).hashes;\\n    const first = await replayed(REPLAY);\\n40→    if (goldens[PLATFORM]) expect(first).toEqual(goldens[PLATFORM]);\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/scenes/play.test.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file The starter game's tests (labs/game/scenes/play.ts): what every game made from it starts with, and keeps as it\\n * grows. They run headless in Node (`npm test`), in a few seconds:\\n * - its golden replay (labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json, the bot's play) gives its golden hashes, as `x replay`\\n *   checks it: on another platform than the one that recorded them, two runs agree instead;\\n * - a run continues exactly after a capture and a restore;\\n * - the bot (labs/game/bot.ts) wins on 10 seeds, so the game can be won, however the gems fall;\\n * - the clock runs out on a player who stands still, and the settings change play;\\n * - two actors that reach a gem in the same step take it once.\\n *\\n * When the game's play changes on purpose, record the goldens again: `node x replay labs/game/replays/bot.replay.json\\n * --browser sim --update` (in Node and Chromium), and say why in the commit.\\n * @see labs/game/scenes/play.ts\\n */\\nimport { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';\\nimport { createHeadless } from '../../../engine';\\nimport { defineScene, type World } from '../../../engine/sim-api';\\nimport { botIntents, playBot, roundOf, viewOf } from '../bot';\\nimport { spawnPlayer } from '../cast/player';\\nimport { play, type GameComponents } from './play';\\n\\n/** The golden replay: the bot's play from seed 1, with the goldens `x replay --update` recorded. */\\nconst REPLAY = readFileSync(new URL('../replays/bot.replay.json', import.meta.url), 'utf8');\\n\\n/** This platform's golden key, as replays write it. */\\nconst PLATFORM = `${process.platform}-${process.arch}`;\\n\\n/** Plays `text` from step 0 in a fresh engine and returns its hashes at the replay's checkpoints. */\\nasync function replayed(text: string): Promise<Record<string, string>> {\\n  const engine = await createHeadless({ scene: play });\\n  const result = await (engine.replay as (text: string) => Promise<{ hashes: Record<string, string> }>)(text);\\n  return result.hashes;\\n}\\n\\ndescribe('game:play', () => {\\n  it('replays the bot’s play to its golden hashes', async () => {\\n    const goldens = (JSON.parse(REPLAY) as { hashes: Record<string, Record<string, string>> }).hashes;\\n    const first = await replayed(REPLAY);\\n    if (goldens[PLATFORM]) expect(first).toEqual(goldens[PLATFORM]);\\n\",\"total_lines\":89}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-3230987f-9028-4853-a034-c4a93fa083bb-23","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,95,95,120,53,57,99,49,100,51,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,70,111,117,110,100,32,51,32,109,97,116,99,104,105,110,103,32,108,105,110,101,115,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,95,95,120,53,57,99,49,100,51,47,119,111,114,107,47,84,114,97,117,109,97,45,67,111,109,112,97,115,115,45,84,114,97,117,109,97,45,67,101,110,116,101,114,45,83,101,99,111,110,100,45,79,112,105,110,105,111,110,45,103,114,111,107,45,52,46,55,45,120,104,105,103,104,45,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,47,101,115,108,105,110,116,46,99,111,110,102,105,103,46,106,115,10,55,49,58,32,32,32,32,32,32,112,108,117,103,105,110,115,58,32,123,32,106,115,100,111,99,44,32,39,104,97,114,100,45,99,97,112,39,58,32,102,97,109,105,108,105,101,115,46,102,97,109,105,108,121,40,39,104,97,114,100,45,99,97,112,39,44,32,39,109,97,120,45,108,105,110,101,115,39,41,32,125,44,10,55,52,58,32,32,32,32,32,32,32,32,39,109,97,120,45,108,105,110,101,115,39,58,32,91,39,119,97,114,110,39,44,32,123,32,109,97,120,58,32,52,48,48,32,125,93,44,10,55,53,58,32,32,32,32,32,32,32,32,39,104,97,114,100,45,99,97,112,47,109,97,120,45,108,105,110,101,115,39,58,32,91,39,101,114,114,111,114,39,44,32,123,32,109,97,120,58,32,54,48,48,32,125,93,44,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":3,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/eslint.config.js\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":71,\"content\":\"      plugins: { jsdoc, 'hard-cap': families.family('hard-cap', 'max-lines') },\"},{\"line_number\":74,\"content\":\"        'max-lines': ['warn', { max: 400 }],\"},{\"line_number\":75,\"content\":\"        'hard-cap/max-lines': ['error', { max: 600 }],\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"4295ce8b-7cf8-497a-b013-6417b4ab6bc6"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_46","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'll write compact case data and a smart bot.\n\nI'll patch world.ts for spawnPhase linkUnders and check the birth() type issue. The bot logic will prioritize code bands, then gift pulses at lock beats.\n\nThe bot pauses all movement during seat locks. It then triggers gift presses when needed, picks a target, and may inject if the phase is still open.\n\nThe bot will inject when the phase is still open and un-injected. It switches sites to work on unfinished targets elsewhere, then moves to a target site or begins working. Gift triggers require an active gift tag, zero giftLeft, remaining charges, and no active lock.\n\nTarget selection prioritizes hidden blockers, dangerous enemies, current-phase targets, optional high-bleed pools, and cocoons. Seats will claim targets by index to prevent multiple bots from attacking the same target.\n\nDrain tools can stack safely. Forceps require distinct targets since only one seat can hold them.\n\nThe candidate filter keeps unfinished required or urgent targets from the current phase. Urgency covers pools that bleed, lulls, and bleeders from any phase. Cocoons spawn in the current phase. Optional numbs can be skipped unless vitals are needed. Optional pools from solace bleed 0.5 and may be drained if no required work remains.\n\nIf a required target is hidden on the current site, the bot will scan it first. Locked targets are skipped until unlocked; a guide cut must happen before a mass becomes reachable.\n\nSolace targets need special handling. Gen 3 and above require laser, but rightTool returns scalpel first because it matches the first tool in TOOLS order. The bot must explicitly use laser for gen 3+ and scalpel for lower generations. Hidden reverie targets still require a scanner.\n\nReverie targets need laser to surface if revealed but not surfaced, or to resurface if already surfaced; a scanner is needed again if the target becomes hidden again.\n\nMatron targets should be avoided with syringes unless vulnerable. The bot will idle nearby instead, or do other work like lulls.\n\nBinder gift presses must start before approaching a target.\n\nFlap targets require opening the flap first when something underneath remains unfinished.\n\nGuide targets follow the cut path.\n\nSuture targets use a zigzag pattern.\n\nInject targets are charged at clear spots when no other required work exists.\n\nThe bot will track syringe charge in seat.memo and hold use until it reaches 0.85 before releasing to fire inject. It will avoid scalpel or laser on empty space to prevent slipping. Gel phases will target abrasions rather than wounds, since finishStitch marks wounds done. Compact case data will be built with a CARD helper.\n\nI'm adding compact builder functions for phase definitions, pool spawns, wound spawns, and shard spawns. I'm also starting a guide path helper using arc coordinates.\n\nThe guide path helper now generates a half-circle arc for the 7-meter incision. A straight line with slight bow is simpler and still curved enough. The bot will reach it in about one second. I'm also adding a linePath builder that splits a straight segment into points spaced 0.34 meters apart.\n\nSpawn positions will jitter by ±0.08 meters, but the guide path itself stays fixed so the bot follows the intended cut route. Wound x2 coordinates are jittered with the same offset while preserving length. The bot reads live target paths, so drift only occurs if a tag moves.\n\nI'm adding a stitchPoint helper to calculate suture positions. It offsets 0.35 meters perpendicular to the wound line at parameter u, alternating sides to create the zigzag pattern.\n\nStitch spacing will be about 0.45 meters along the wound, derived from the suture length. The bot tracks stitch progress and side using target.stitches and seat.sutureSide instead of memo fields. It alternates sides at each crossing point and advances to the next point once stitches increase.\n\nThe cut bot will approach the guide path point by point. It holds use until within 0.4 meters of a point, then releases to cut. The first point requires being within 0.45 meters before cutting can begin.\n\nCutting near a guide point does not count as body contact, so the bot avoids slipping. It advances to the next path point once it lands within 0.24 meters, and it should reach about 0.15 meters. At 8 m/s the bot moves roughly 0.133 meters per step, which is slower than the 0.24 threshold, so overshoot is unlikely. The average approach distance may be around 0.15 meters, which suggests the result should be CLEAN rather than SLIP.\n\nDrain targets are reached within 0.7 meters before use. Laser targets are reached within close range, but use is deferred during travel to avoid body contact slips.\n\nForceps pulls require holding the target ID. The bot moves toward the origin point when not holding, then pulls away from it at 4 m/s until the distance exceeds 1.2 meters.\n\nForceps grabs record the seat position as the origin point. The bot then moves away from it and does not drop.\n\nSolace targets are split by oscillating across the body. The aim shifts left and right in 0.2-meter steps. Use fires when the bot is inside the target radius plus 0.25 meters. Stroke distance accumulates while the bot remains inside that window.\n\nSolace splits are completed in a single oscillation across the body. The bot then picks up the two spawned child targets.\n\nReverie laser work requires staying on a surfaced target. If it becomes hidden, the bot scans it within three meters and resumes once surfaced again.\n\nPaddle use is restricted to codeBand phases to avoid wasted attempts outside those windows.\n\nSite switches update hand.site exactly once per target.\n\nCo-op seats filter candidates by seat index.\n\nClaimable targets are sorted by priority and then distributed round-robin across seats. The priority ladder is: closed flaps with work underneath, hidden reverie or mass targets, lulls and bleeder pools, regular pools, solace, guides, and nodule decoys. Matron is last unless it is vulnerable.\n\nUnstitched wounds are now priority 7. Shard, mass, cocoon, and tendril targets are priority 8. Binder targets are priority 9. Balm cells are priority 10. Balm nodes are priority 8. If wounds exist, the node must be killed before stitching lacquered wounds because the gel fades in six seconds. Balm work should therefore precede wound stitching.\n\nThe bot will bind balm nodes in a dedicated phase. It must kill the node quickly so cells born before its death receive the six-second fade timer. Cells spawned after the node dies will not get that timer, so any cell without one must be gelled.\n\nCase 7 will use four phases: pools first, then nodes, then wounds, then gel. The gala shift is a shorter two-patient mix starting at 4:30. Par is set to 1 and can be updated after the bot run.\n\nPar will be hardcoded after the bot run once scores are known. The `runBot` function is being drafted with a 15-minute cap and a loop that tracks status changes.\n\nThe `runBot` loop now tracks a signature built from phase, unfinished targets, and code state. If that signature stays unchanged for over 600 ticks, the bot marks itself lost. This catches stalls during code waits, matron locks, and gift locks without triggering on normal idle.\n\nBalm fade stalls are safe because the signature stays unchanged for six seconds. The bot must rescan reverie targets after each submerge to avoid false stalls. A five-second reverie burn needs three surface windows with brief submerges between them, so rescan timing matters. Guide cuts are also safe because the signature ignores cut progress; a seven-meter cut takes about one second.\n\nThe stall signature now includes cut progress, stitch count, volume, burn, dose, and surface state so that active work resets the stall counter. The inject phase has no targets until injection completes.\n\nThe inject phase will move to (0,0) if the bot stands on a target that blocks injection. Case 1 is now sequenced as pools, shards, dip with stabilizer teaching, then wounds, then gel.\n\nCase 2 will use a seven-meter curved guide cut with two spawn-bleeders. Those bleeders spawn during the cut and must be finished before the phase advances, so they stay in the same phase. The hunt phase includes three hidden nodules, one hidden locked mass, and a guide loop that unlocks the mass.\n\nThe hunt phase will cut the guide first, scan for nodules, laser them, and then grab the mass. The guide tag carries a 'flinch' flag that triggers drama.\n\nCase 3 will use three triage sites with one pool and two short wounds each. The flatline phase waits for code teaching without spawning targets. Three bleeders appear at each site, and the bot will auto-gift once per still phase if charges are available.\n\nCase 3's final phase will gel abrasions across all sites, possibly with one remaining short wound per site. The stillpoint gift will be used at the start of any phase whose ID contains 'still' if charges are available. Case 4 opens with a short guide, then three lulls spawn cocoons on death that must be pulled.\n\nCase 4 will combine lulls and matron into a single phase so the bot handles births. The priority order is vulnerable matron first, then lulls, then cocoons.\n\nCase 5 places a flap tagged 'lid' with solace tagged 'under:lid'. Both appear in the same phase, so the bot can finish them together. The storm roll flag is set for the entire case.\n\nCase 5 can be a single phase: the bot splits and burns the solace under the open flap, then closes the wound. The colleague holds the flap open for the solo bot. The notice() call requires an open flap, so actAll must run before the colleague steps body.\n\nCase 5 can use the optional second-pulse gift on the storm phase. Case 6 will use two guides at sites 0 and 1.\n\nCase 6 will place a hidden reverie target under the heart flap, guarded by two decoys. The colleague holds the flap open for the bot. The plan calls for three exposures.\n\nA flaw was found in the decoy logic. If the nearest scanned target is a decoy, calling pop() only destroys that decoy and leaves hidden targets like mass, nodule, or reverie unrevealed. reveal() must be used to expose all hidden targets in range.\n\nDecoy placement is refined so the bot aims at the reverie target. Because the bot stands directly on the reverie, it is closer than any decoy within three meters, ensuring the nearest scan hits the real target and reveal() pops all decoys in range.\n\nCase 7 will use three patients with vitals at eighty. The node phase spawns one balm node per site. The wound phase places one wound and one pool at each site after the nodes are dead, avoiding lacquered wounds.\n\nCase 8 Mercy will open with a short guide. Six mercy-motes will spawn and must be killed before three binders appear; the phase ends once the binders are done. A flatline phase waits for code teaching. Three short wounds follow.\n\nCase 8 Mercy continues with a moving guide, five close tendrils, and binders tagged 'gift' that require the bot to gift before binding. The final phase is an onEnter flatline until code succeeds. Phase IDs must start with 'mercy' for the mercy() function, so the opening guide should not carry that prefix.\n\nCase 8 will use two gift charges, so the bot can afford to gift once during binding. The three-press lock is the default and the bot handles it. In the final flatline phase, vitals are set to zero before code begins; the bot paddles through the last step to win.\n\nMercy’s second flatline phase works because the first never sets coded. The advance guard will not skip a phase that finishes immediately. Tendril reattachment takes about two and a half seconds. The moving guide uses four short points tagged moving. Mote positions are spread to keep travel reasonable. At 1.25x health scale, mote burn is 0.75 seconds, lull is 1.875 seconds, and reverie is 6.25 seconds.\n\nExtra seats in case 1 spawn roughly two additional pools, which is manageable. Hash stability is deferred for now.\n\n`linkUnders()` will be called at the end of `spawnPhase`. The `birth()` type can be checked after tests compile. Cases will stay compact under six hundred lines.\n\nEvery patient needs original S/A/B/C postcard lines. Story ids use 'before-1', 'after-1', etc. A new rule field will hold the rule sentence.\n\nThe bot will use an `approach` helper to move toward a target seat at eight meters per second. The plan also includes a speed slider that can scale the bot’s movement via `balance.cursor`.\n\nThe bot will read its movement speed from `op.balance.cursor` so the slider test can adjust it. Small drift during hand application is corrected by aiming at the target each step, which is acceptable.\n\nFor the inject phase, the bot will first approach a clear spot at (0, 2) to avoid collisions. It will then release the hand if the seat’s syringe level is already high enough, or continue approaching until the seat is within range.\n\nSyringe injection waits for the cooldown to expire before charging and releasing. Gel application increments cover by one step per tick while inside radius, with a default need of sixteen; lowering it to eight speeds things up. Scanner targets hidden objects by approaching them and using the tool, then reveals all hidden targets in range so the bot can pick the real one next. Forceps pull at four meters per second, capped by the cursor speed.\n\nForceps speed is calculated from the distance between the current and previous seat positions. The bot approaches until within 0.45 meters, then uses the tool. While holding, the target position tracks the seat and the pull continues. It releases on drop or when use is false, and stops after completion. For flaps, the bot pulls until lift reaches 0.8, which requires pulling roughly 0.96 meters with a default forceps value of 1.2.\n\nA flaw was found in the flap logic: marking a clipped flap as done would break `flapOpen`, which still checks for clipped or lifted status. The phase therefore never completes, so the bot cannot advance past it.\n\nThe fix for the stuck phase is to set `done = true` when `lift` reaches 0.8, alongside `clipped`. This lets the phase advance past the flap without breaking `flapOpen`, because a done target is ignored. Both the bot and the colleague will mark the target done at that moment.\n\nOnce lift reaches 0.8, the flap stays open permanently because `flapOpen` checks `lift >= 0.8` even after the clip timer expires. The bot will mark the target done at that moment so the phase can advance. A new cocoon target in the same phase will be pulled by the bot without being marked done.\n\nSolace split children remain in the same phase as their parent, so the phase advances once the parent is done.\n\nThe matron dose logic was fixed: each dose subtracts one health point from a scaled starting value, and the target finishes after two doses or when health reaches zero. With 1.25x health scale, the intern starts at 1.5 and needs two doses to die; the attending starts at 2.5 and needs two doses to die. Two vulnerable windows of 2.5 seconds each allow one dose per window, plus lulls, which keeps the timing manageable.\n\nThe bot will not stillpoint during the matron phase unless absolutely necessary, because stillpoint slows field time and lengthens yawns. It will not auto-gift on case 4.\n\nCode band presses are gated by `codeBand` and `op.tick`, so the hand is computed before each step. The first press succeeds and clears the code; extra presses outside the band do nothing. Inside the band, only the initial press counts.\n\nFlatline phases wait for vitals to exist before they begin.\n\nThe bot clears code during applyHands, so the same step can win if it is the final phase. The test will call runBot to step through the case. Par scores will start at one and be updated from measured results later.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-bd1644fb-7e60-42a3-bc08-3ab89ba21eab-24","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/op/body.ts","offset":270,"limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-bd1644fb-7e60-42a3-bc08-3ab89ba21eab-25","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/op/world.ts","offset":410,"limit":25}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1169,"output_tokens":9055,"cache_read_input_tokens":90240,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"7931a5ca-f1cb-4271-8b25-d77b442749ea"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-bd1644fb-7e60-42a3-bc08-3ab89ba21eab-24","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"270→\\n/** A seat the tests can poke. */\\nexport function seatOf(op: Op, id = 0): Seat {\\n  return op.seats[id];\\n}\\n\\n/** Heal hook used by Second Pulse tests through award. Re-export path stays in ratings. */\\nexport function touchHeal(op: Op, seat: Seat, amount: number): void {\\n  heal(op, seat, amount);\\n}\\n280→\\n/** Spawn helper re-exported for strains that birth a lull. */\\nexport function birth(op: Op, kind: 'lull' | 'numb' | 'binder' | 'mote' | 'wound' | 'guide' | 'tendril', x: number, z: number, phase: number, extra: Record<string, unknown> = {}): void {\\n  addTarget(op, { kind, x, z, site: 0, patient: 0, ...extra }, phase);\\n}\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"270→\\n/** A seat the tests can poke. */\\nexport function seatOf(op: Op, id = 0): Seat {\\n  return op.seats[id];\\n}\\n\\n/** Heal hook used by Second Pulse tests through award. Re-export path stays in ratings. */\\nexport function touchHeal(op: Op, seat: Seat, amount: number): void {\\n  heal(op, seat, amount);\\n}\\n280→\\n/** Spawn helper re-exported for strains that birth a lull. */\\nexport function birth(op: Op, kind: 'lull' | 'numb' | 'binder' | 'mote' | 'wound' | 'guide' | 'tendril', x: number, z: number, phase: number, extra: Record<string, unknown> = {}): void {\\n  addTarget(op, { kind, x, z, site: 0, patient: 0, ...extra }, phase);\\n}\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/op/body.ts\",\"offset\":270,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"\\n/** A seat the tests can poke. */\\nexport function seatOf(op: Op, id = 0): Seat {\\n  return op.seats[id];\\n}\\n\\n/** Heal hook used by Second Pulse tests through award. Re-export path stays in ratings. */\\nexport function touchHeal(op: Op, seat: Seat, amount: number): void {\\n  heal(op, seat, amount);\\n}\\n\\n/** Spawn helper re-exported for strains that birth a lull. */\\nexport function birth(op: Op, kind: 'lull' | 'numb' | 'binder' | 'mote' | 'wound' | 'guide' | 'tendril', x: number, z: number, phase: number, extra: Record<string, unknown> = {}): void {\\n  addTarget(op, { kind, x, z, site: 0, patient: 0, ...extra }, phase);\\n}\\n\",\"total_lines\":284}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-bd1644fb-7e60-42a3-bc08-3ab89ba21eab-25","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"410→}\\n\\n/** Spawn a phase's targets. Exported for the phase runner. */\\nexport function spawnPhase(op: Op, phase: PhaseDef, index: number): void {\\n  const extra = Math.max(0, op.seats.length - 1);\\n  const ordinary = phase.spawn.filter((spawn) => !UNIQUE_KIND.has(spawn.kind));\\n  for (const spawn of phase.spawn) placeSpawn(op, spawn, index);\\n  const duplicates = Math.round(ordinary.length * 0.25 * extra);\\n  for (let n = 0; n < duplicates; n++) {\\n    const spawn = ordinary[n % ordinary.length];\\n420→    placeSpawn(op, { ...spawn, x: spawn.x + 0.8 * (1 + Math.floor(n / ordinary.length)), z: spawn.z + 0.4 }, index);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n/** One spawn, jittered a hair by the seed so three seeds are not the same picture. */\\nfunction placeSpawn(op: Op, spawn: Spawn, phase: number): Target {\\n  const jx = jitter(op.seed, op.nextId) ;\\n  const jz = jitter(op.seed, op.nextId + 17);\\n  return addTarget(op, { ...spawn, x: spawn.x + jx, z: spawn.z + jz, x2: spawn.x2 !== undefined ? spawn.x2 + jx : undefined, z2: spawn.z2 !== undefined ? spawn.z2 + jz : undefined }, phase);\\n}\\n430→\\n/** A stable offset in -0.08..0.08. Seed 1 is not zero, so paths still differ. */\\nexport function jitter(seed: number, n: number): number {\\n  const x = Math.sin(seed * 12.9898 + n * 78.233) * 43758.5453;\\n  return (x - Math.floor(x) - 0.5) * 0.16;\",\"content_concise\":\"410→}\\n\\n/** Spawn a phase's targets. Exported for the phase runner. */\\nexport function spawnPhase(op: Op, phase: PhaseDef, index: number): void {\\n  const extra = Math.max(0, op.seats.length - 1);\\n  const ordinary = phase.spawn.filter((spawn) => !UNIQUE_KIND.has(spawn.kind));\\n  for (const spawn of phase.spawn) placeSpawn(op, spawn, index);\\n  const duplicates = Math.round(ordinary.length * 0.25 * extra);\\n  for (let n = 0; n < duplicates; n++) {\\n    const spawn = ordinary[n % ordinary.length];\\n420→    placeSpawn(op, { ...spawn, x: spawn.x + 0.8 * (1 + Math.floor(n / ordinary.length)), z: spawn.z + 0.4 }, index);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n/** One spawn, jittered a hair by the seed so three seeds are not the same picture. */\\nfunction placeSpawn(op: Op, spawn: Spawn, phase: number): Target {\\n  const jx = jitter(op.seed, op.nextId) ;\\n  const jz = jitter(op.seed, op.nextId + 17);\\n  return addTarget(op, { ...spawn, x: spawn.x + jx, z: spawn.z + jz, x2: spawn.x2 !== undefined ? spawn.x2 + jx : undefined, z2: spawn.z2 !== undefined ? spawn.z2 + jz : undefined }, phase);\\n}\\n430→\\n/** A stable offset in -0.08..0.08. Seed 1 is not zero, so paths still differ. */\\nexport function jitter(seed: number, n: number): number {\\n  const x = Math.sin(seed * 12.9898 + n * 78.233) * 43758.5453;\\n  return (x - Math.floor(x) - 0.5) * 0.16;\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/op/world.ts\",\"offset\":410,\"limit\":25,\"raw_output\":\"}\\n\\n/** Spawn a phase's targets. Exported for the phase runner. */\\nexport function spawnPhase(op: Op, phase: PhaseDef, index: number): void {\\n  const extra = Math.max(0, op.seats.length - 1);\\n  const ordinary = phase.spawn.filter((spawn) => !UNIQUE_KIND.has(spawn.kind));\\n  for (const spawn of phase.spawn) placeSpawn(op, spawn, index);\\n  const duplicates = Math.round(ordinary.length * 0.25 * extra);\\n  for (let n = 0; n < duplicates; n++) {\\n    const spawn = ordinary[n % ordinary.length];\\n    placeSpawn(op, { ...spawn, x: spawn.x + 0.8 * (1 + Math.floor(n / ordinary.length)), z: spawn.z + 0.4 }, index);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n/** One spawn, jittered a hair by the seed so three seeds are not the same picture. */\\nfunction placeSpawn(op: Op, spawn: Spawn, phase: number): Target {\\n  const jx = jitter(op.seed, op.nextId) ;\\n  const jz = jitter(op.seed, op.nextId + 17);\\n  return addTarget(op, { ...spawn, x: spawn.x + jx, z: spawn.z + jz, x2: spawn.x2 !== undefined ? spawn.x2 + jx : undefined, z2: spawn.z2 !== undefined ? spawn.z2 + jz : undefined }, phase);\\n}\\n\\n/** A stable offset in -0.08..0.08. Seed 1 is not zero, so paths still differ. */\\nexport function jitter(seed: number, n: number): number {\\n  const x = Math.sin(seed * 12.9898 + n * 78.233) * 43758.5453;\\n  return (x - Math.floor(x) - 0.5) * 0.16;\\n\",\"total_lines\":507}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"c88c08d3-9740-4042-b6b2-30a62eeb73c0"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_47","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Spreading Record<string, unknown> into Spawn will not typecheck.\n\nPatching world spawnPhase links, act lift done state, body colleague flaps, and birth type. Then writing cases, bot, and tests. Guide paths remain live without jitter. Wound gel is planned as abrasions. Scanner mass stays hidden when locked; reveal restores visibility.\n\nGuide unlock matches the 'lump' tag. Targets must be 1.5m apart to avoid tool overlap; gel radius 0.7, drain 0.75, and nearest limit 0.85 make 1.6m spacing safe. Wounds need 2m separation to prevent suture stitching the wrong one. Field bounds are x ±8 and z ±5, with content kept in -3..3 x and -2..2 z so co-op duplicates at +0.8 still fit.\n\nSolace radius 0.9 requires it to be alone in its phase, with other targets kept outside radius+0.25. The bot will derive the suture u index from stitches and use a 0.15 distance threshold to switch stitch sides.\n\nThe bot's path from +0.35 to -0.35 crosses the stitch line in several steps. The segmentSide changes during the crossing step, which adds the stitch at the correct position. The 0.35 offset keeps the bot within the 0.8 distance limit. When moving perpendicular to a horizontal wound, segmentU stays constant.\n\nShort wounds will have need set to 2 explicitly so stitch positions are predictable. The bot calculates stitch u as Math.min(0.85, (stitches + 0.5) / need). It flips side when already close to the desired side.\n\nThe bot approaches the stitch point and activates the suture tool once it is within 0.2 meters or the side has already flipped. The first arrival sets the initial sutureSide, and subsequent arrivals at the opposite side trigger a crossing at the updated u position.\n\nStitch spacing requires the u difference multiplied by wound length to exceed 0.3825. A 0.5-length wound with need 2 yields only 0.25 spacing, so it would stall. The plan is to use a 1.1-length wound with need 2 instead, giving 0.55 spacing.\n\nGuide cuts will only activate when the bot is close to the current point or already cutting. Laser use is restricted to within 0.45 meters; beyond that, burn returns false and the bodyContact slip after 60 ticks would trigger.\n\nScalpel use is restricted to within range of the guide; otherwise body slip triggers after 12 ticks. Drain can be used early during travel without risk. Gift binders are handled by starting the lock on the first frame and returning early while the lock is active, halting all movement.\n\nGift binding takes about 90 ticks, safely under the 180-tick minimum. The lock starts at tick+40, and gift activation is restricted to that exact frame so holding the button on later frames does not score an extra beat.\n\nGift and code will not be pressed simultaneously; code takes priority. The bot will check for the injected phase and any leftover optional targets before selecting targets, ensuring it does not interfere with them. Until code is received, the bot waits to avoid disrupting vitals; once code arrives, it paddles.\n\nThe bot's hand logic now follows a strict priority: idle when not playing, paddle on the band during code, pulse gift while locked, inject if needed, wait for code to clear, then pick targets. On the flatline phase, code arrives after stepVitals, so the bot idles until it exists and then paddles. Targets will not be spawned on code phases. Stillpoint is optional.\n\nThe bot will press gift once at the start of Stillpoint and Second Pulse phases when charges are available. This gifts during the lock window before working, slowing bleeds for demonstration. The giftUsed flag prevents repeated presses.\n\nMercy binders will gift via their tag even if the teach gift already fired, since charges remain. Case 8 will not gift before its bind phase to preserve a charge for the binder lock.\n\nConsult test is a unit test for two seats pressing gift within 60 ticks. I will write it directly. I will avoid `**` in op files and use Math.hypot instead. I will add a small debug test running case 1 first, then expand.\n\nA TOOL_INDEX constant maps tool names to numbers. Solace uses scalpel, reverie uses laser, and mass uses forceps. Laser becomes the fallback for solace when generation is three or higher. Revere uses laser but falls back to scanner if hidden. Mass uses forceps but falls back to scanner if hidden; it is skipped while locked because forceps notice() returns false and the nearest target would be lost.\n\nA `workable` helper filters targets by phase, locks, flaps, and vulnerability. Matrons that are not vulnerable are skipped; if only one exists and it is invulnerable, the bot idles nearby and waits for its signature to change.\n\nHidden locked mass is workable because the `!hidden` check is skipped, so it gets scanned and then blocked until unlocked. Hidden nodules are also workable and get scanned. Decoys are workable and get scanned. Flap targets are prioritized over under targets while closed; once a flap is done, its under targets become workable.\n\nColleague marks flaps done at the end of the first step when under targets exist, so the bot may not need to lift. If the colleague does not mark it done, the bot will set the done state inside lift(). Multi-seat case 5 is not required. In Solace split, the bot must keep using the scalpel while moving across.\n\nSolace children spawn at x±0.35 with a 0.6 radius, so they may overlap; the bot will target one center and work them sequentially. The split stroke requires the bot to be inside a radius+0.3 boundary. I am ready to write the code now, beginning with cases.ts exports.\n\ncases.ts will export a filtered `STORY_CASES` list and a `caseById` helper. Postcards must be original and avoid banned words. The file will stay under 600 lines, with helpers and nine cases estimated at roughly 440 total. Story scene IDs are reserved for later. Tests will log the exact failure reason.\n\nSyringe release logic is confirmed correct. Laser burns stay under the six-second heat limit, so reverie damage fits within one cycle. Motes last only 3.6 seconds, safely below the limit.\n\nStall signatures will now include burn, cut, stitch, volume, dose, HP, mercy kills, and laser damage. The debug test for case 1 will use a high timeout and log the exact failure reason and phase. Vitest will run with verbose output. Test files need `@file` for eslint. Prettier settings are printWidth 120 and single quotes. The sim-side ban on `**` likely applies only to scenes and cast.\n\nI will also fix a double space before a semicolon in world.ts placeSpawn. The linkFlaps helper maps each flap tag to its target ID and links under targets by stripping the 'under:' prefix.\n\nThe linkFlaps helper runs at the end of spawnPhase. Duplicate flaps with matching tags map to the last ID, so under targets link to the final flap; this is messy for co-op but case 1 has no flaps. Guide paths are not jittered, though finishCut bleeders spawn at the jittered target.x. Cut path indexing advances when distance to the current vertex drops below 0.24, and the bot aims at the next vertex.\n\nThe bot must be within 0.45 meters of the first cut point before starting a stroke. The guide's x and z are jittered, but the path itself is not, so nearest targets still use the path for distance checks. Case durations are set from 240 to 480 seconds, vitals from 60 to 100, and gift charges are being mapped per case.\n\nGift charges are now mapped per case. Cases 1, 2, and 3 have none. Case 4 has two. Case 5 has one. Case 6 has two and a swap flag. Case 7 has two with code. Case 8 has two with code and swap. Solo seat zero always uses stillpoint. Swap toggles the second seat. Colleague holds flaps in case 6.\n\nCase 3 vitals were recalculated with reduced content. Resident bleed drains about 3.15 per second, dropping from 60 to roughly 35 in 19 seconds. Attending bleed adds 4.1 per second, leaving about 27. Stitching six wounds takes roughly eight seconds. Intern drain is only 0.6, so the intern stays safe. The clock remains comfortable.\n\nIf stitching six wounds takes twelve seconds, the attending could flatline during phase one. That would trigger code and then the scripted flatline phase would kill them immediately because patient.coded is already true. Phase one must therefore be survivable on the attending alone.\n\nCase 3 phase one will use two wounds instead of six. Lowering global vitalsDrain is not viable. The bot may need to stitch faster. Patching wound bleed defaults to respect spawn bleed values is also under consideration.\n\nThe wound bleed patch was abandoned. The default 0.45 drain will remain, and fewer wounds will be used in triage cases instead.\n\nPool volume growth does not increase bleed rate; it only lengthens the drain. I will proceed and tune after testing. Case 4 lull drain is roughly 1.8 per second plus nuzzle damage every six seconds. Starting at 75 vitals, the bot loses about twelve before cocoons and matron spawn, then continues draining for another twenty seconds.\n\nA stillpoint auto-activation will trigger when vitals drop below 45, the seat holds a stillpoint gift with charges and no active lock. This halves field-time drain to 25 seconds and should keep case 3 and case 4 survivable.\n\nGifts pause work for about two seconds during lock, and the drain stays at thirty field-time units while the lock is active. Stillpoint then quarters that rate.\n\nA risk emerged for case eight: if vitals dip twice, both stillpoint charges are consumed. The bind phase would then fail because it forces a gift via tag.\n\nAuto-gift will be skipped initially and added only if tests show it is needed. A debug dump will be included in the test. The `birth` function type signature will be fixed to resolve an eslint unused warning.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-ff835414-8501-4e0e-873f-5549def918de-26","name":"grep","inpu