ond-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/op/cases.ts has been updated successfully.\",\"tool_output_for_prompt_concise\":\"The file /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/op/cases.ts has been updated.\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/op/cases.ts\",\"edits\":{\"details\":[{\"old_string\":\"    storyBefore: 'before-7',\\n    storyAfter: 'after-7',\\n    par: 1,\",\"old_line\":421,\"new_string\":\"    storyBefore: 'before-7',\\n    storyAfter: 'after-7',\\n    par: 17972,\",\"new_line\":421,\"context_before\":\"      { id: 'b', name: 'Table B' },\\n      { id: 'c', name: 'Table C' },\\n    ],\\n\",\"context_after\":\"    phases: [\\n      phase(\\n        'nodes',\\n\",\"line_prefix\":\"\"}]}}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-7e171863-87fe-4609-9892-d3121b94650f-75","content":"{\"type\":\"SearchReplace\",\"EditsApplied\":{\"old_string\":\"    storyBefore: 'before-8',\\n    storyAfter: 'after-8',\\n    par: 1,\",\"new_string\":\"    storyBefore: 'before-8',\\n    storyAfter: 'after-8',\\n    par: 33008,\",\"tool_output_for_prompt\":\"The file /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/op/cases.ts has been updated successfully.\",\"tool_output_for_prompt_concise\":\"The file /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/op/cases.ts has been updated.\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/op/cases.ts\",\"edits\":{\"details\":[{\"old_string\":\"    storyBefore: 'before-8',\\n    storyAfter: 'after-8',\\n    par: 1,\",\"old_line\":475,\"new_string\":\"    storyBefore: 'before-8',\\n    storyAfter: 'after-8',\\n    par: 33008,\",\"new_line\":475,\"context_before\":\"      },\\n    ],\\n    sites: [{ id: 'heart', name: 'Heart' }],\\n\",\"context_after\":\"    phases: [\\n      phase('open', 'Open. He does not get a speech.', 'clear', [curve(-1.3, 0.4, 1.3, 0.3, 0.12)], '', 'A short guide, then her.'),\\n      phase('mercy-motes', 'Anesthesia. Six motes, then she is open.', 'clear', motes(), '', 'Laser each mote. Binder on the three points when they show.'),\\n\",\"line_prefix\":\"\"}]}}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-7e171863-87fe-4609-9892-d3121b94650f-76","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The starter game's page (labs/game/index.html): it starts the engine (`createEngine`, engine/app/engine.ts) on\\n * the scene `game:play` (labs/game/scenes/play.ts) with the arena's text registered for the view, offers the bot's\\n * replay to `?replay=bot`, and shows the round each drawn frame in the HTML/CSS overlay's HUD: the gems, the clock,\\n * the controls, the best round kept across sessions (labs/game/best.ts, through the engine's store) and, when the\\n * round ends, how it ended. Enter then starts a new round on the next seed. Escape (or the pad's Start) pauses the\\n * round under a menu (`createMenu`: Resume, New round), which keeps its keys out of play while it is open\\n * (`createEngine({ modal })`); the store keeps the best round and any keys rebound (`createEngine({ store })`). The\\n * camera is the chase camera unless the URL names one (`?cam=iso`, `?cam3=orbit`).\\n10→ *\\n * Everything else a page does (routes, the renderer, the frame loop, the devices, `window.__engine`, the error\\n * overlay, the touch controls) is the engine's, so this file is the game's own presentation only. It reads the run\\n * and never changes it: the HUD cannot change the hash.\\n *\\n * Game code: it imports only the engine's public API (engine/index.ts) and the game's own files.\\n *\\n * Open it at http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/game/ (`npm run dev`): `?seed=7&game.gems=20` plays another round,\\n * `?replay=bot` the bot's, `?cam=iso` another camera. Headless, `node x shot labs/game` reports what it draws.\\n */\\n20→/// <reference types=\\\"vite/client\\\" />\\nimport { createEngine, createMenu, createStore, type Engine, type EngineFrame, type Menu } from '../../engine';\\nimport { bestOf, recordBest } from './best';\\nimport { ARENA_TEXT, play, type Round } from './scenes/play';\\n\\n/** The page's query, with the chase camera when it names none: it follows the player round the arena. */\\nconst search = new URLSearchParams(location.search);\\nif (!search.has('cam') && !search.has('cam3')) search.set('cam3', 'chase');\\n\\n/** The game's replays, by name (`?replay=bot`), as text loaded on demand. */\\n30→const replays = { bot: () => import('./replays/bot.replay.json?raw').then((module) => module.default) };\\n\\n/** The started engine; null until `createEngine` resolves (its first frames are drawn before that). */\\nlet engine: Engine | null = null;\\n\\n/** Whether the round on screen has ended, so Enter starts the next. */\\nlet ended = false;\\n\\n/** The game's store: its best round across sessions; a replay's page plays in its demo mode, so it sets none. */\\nconst store = createStore({ prefix: 'my3dge-game:', mode: search.has('replay') ? 'demo' : 'play' });\\n40→\\n/** The fastest won round kept (s), and the run whose clock the page saw running, with the steps it started with. */\\nlet best = bestOf(store);\\nlet seen: { world: object; steps: number } | null = null;\\n\\n/** The pause menu while it is open; null while the round plays. */\\nlet paused: Menu | null = null;\\n\\n/** Opens the pause menu over the paused round, or reads it a frame: Resume (or Escape again), New round. */\\nfunction pauseMenu(dt: number): void {\\n50→  const [inspector, ui, input] = [engine?.inspector, engine?.ui, engine?.input];\\n  if (!inspector || !ui || !input) return;\\n  const close = () => {\\n    paused?.remove();\\n    paused = null;\\n    inspector.resume();\\n  };\\n  if (paused) return void paused.update(dt);\\n  if (!input.pressed('pause')) return;\\n  inspector.pause();\\n60→  const items = [\\n    { label: 'Resume', id: 'resume' },\\n    { label: 'New round', id: 'new' },\\n  ];\\n  paused = createMenu(ui.root, items, { input, id: 'pause', title: 'Paused', onCancel: close, onPick: (_i, item) => {\\n    if (typeof item === 'object' && item.id === 'new') inspector.seed(inspector.seed() + 1);\\n    close();\\n  } }); // prettier-ignore\\n}\\n\\n70→/** Shows the round in the HUD: the gems, the clock, the controls, and how the round ended. */\\nfunction showRound({ world }: EngineFrame): void {\\n  const ui = engine?.ui;\\n  const entity = world.query('round')[0] as unknown as { round: Round } | undefined;\\n  if (!ui || !entity) return;\\n  const { gems, collected, left, state } = entity.round;\\n  if (seen?.world !== world) seen = state === 'playing' ? { world, steps: left + world.tick } : null;\\n  const was = ended;\\n  ui.hud('gems', `Gems ${collected} / ${gems}`, { at: 'top-left' });\\n  ui.hud('clock', `${Math.ceil(left / world.hz)} s`, { at: 'top-right', color: left < 10 * world.hz ? '#ff8f6b' : '' });\\n80→  ui.hud('keys', 'WASD: run · Space: jump · Shift: dash · Esc: pause', { at: 'bottom', keys: true });\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The starter game's page (labs/game/index.html): it starts the engine (`createEngine`, engine/app/engine.ts) on\\n * the scene `game:play` (labs/game/scenes/play.ts) with the arena's text registered for the view, offers the bot's\\n * replay to `?replay=bot`, and shows the round each drawn frame in the HTML/CSS overlay's HUD: the gems, the clock,\\n * the controls, the best round kept across sessions (labs/game/best.ts, through the engine's store) and, when the\\n * round ends, how it ended. Enter then starts a new round on the next seed. Escape (or the pad's Start) pauses the\\n * round under a menu (`createMenu`: Resume, New round), which keeps its keys out of play while it is open\\n * (`createEngine({ modal })`); the store keeps the best round and any keys rebound (`createEngine({ store })`). The\\n * camera is the chase camera unless the URL names one (`?cam=iso`, `?cam3=orbit`).\\n10→ *\\n * Everything else a page does (routes, the renderer, the frame loop, the devices, `window.__engine`, the error\\n * overlay, the touch controls) is the engine's, so this file is the game's own presentation only. It reads the run\\n * and never changes it: the HUD cannot change the hash.\\n *\\n * Game code: it imports only the engine's public API (engine/index.ts) and the game's own files.\\n *\\n * Open it at http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/game/ (`npm run dev`): `?seed=7&game.gems=20` plays another round,\\n * `?replay=bot` the bot's, `?cam=iso` another camera. Headless, `node x shot labs/game` reports what it draws.\\n */\\n20→/// <reference types=\\\"vite/client\\\" />\\nimport { createEngine, createMenu, createStore, type Engine, type EngineFrame, type Menu } from '../../engine';\\nimport { bestOf, recordBest } from './best';\\nimport { ARENA_TEXT, play, type Round } from './scenes/play';\\n\\n/** The page's query, with the chase camera when it names none: it follows the player round the arena. */\\nconst search = new URLSearchParams(location.search);\\nif (!search.has('cam') && !search.has('cam3')) search.set('cam3', 'chase');\\n\\n/** The game's replays, by name (`?replay=bot`), as text loaded on demand. */\\n30→const replays = { bot: () => import('./replays/bot.replay.json?raw').then((module) => module.default) };\\n\\n/** The started engine; null until `createEngine` resolves (its first frames are drawn before that). */\\nlet engine: Engine | null = null;\\n\\n/** Whether the round on screen has ended, so Enter starts the next. */\\nlet ended = false;\\n\\n/** The game's store: its best round across sessions; a replay's page plays in its demo mode, so it sets none. */\\nconst store = createStore({ prefix: 'my3dge-game:', mode: search.has('replay') ? 'demo' : 'play' });\\n40→\\n/** The fastest won round kept (s), and the run whose clock the page saw running, with the steps it started with. */\\nlet best = bestOf(store);\\nlet seen: { world: object; steps: number } | null = null;\\n\\n/** The pause menu while it is open; null while the round plays. */\\nlet paused: Menu | null = null;\\n\\n/** Opens the pause menu over the paused round, or reads it a frame: Resume (or Escape again), New round. */\\nfunction pauseMenu(dt: number): void {\\n50→  const [inspector, ui, input] = [engine?.inspector, engine?.ui, engine?.input];\\n  if (!inspector || !ui || !input) return;\\n  const close = () => {\\n    paused?.remove();\\n    paused = null;\\n    inspector.resume();\\n  };\\n  if (paused) return void paused.update(dt);\\n  if (!input.pressed('pause')) return;\\n  inspector.pause();\\n60→  const items = [\\n    { label: 'Resume', id: 'resume' },\\n    { label: 'New round', id: 'new' },\\n  ];\\n  paused = createMenu(ui.root, items, { input, id: 'pause', title: 'Paused', onCancel: close, onPick: (_i, item) => {\\n    if (typeof item === 'object' && item.id === 'new') inspector.seed(inspector.seed() + 1);\\n    close();\\n  } }); // prettier-ignore\\n}\\n\\n70→/** Shows the round in the HUD: the gems, the clock, the controls, and how the round ended. */\\nfunction showRound({ world }: EngineFrame): void {\\n  const ui = engine?.ui;\\n  const entity = world.query('round')[0] as unknown as { round: Round } | undefined;\\n  if (!ui || !entity) return;\\n  const { gems, collected, left, state } = entity.round;\\n  if (seen?.world !== world) seen = state === 'playing' ? { world, steps: left + world.tick } : null;\\n  const was = ended;\\n  ui.hud('gems', `Gems ${collected} / ${gems}`, { at: 'top-left' });\\n  ui.hud('clock', `${Math.ceil(left / world.hz)} s`, { at: 'top-right', color: left < 10 * world.hz ? '#ff8f6b' : '' });\\n80→  ui.hud('keys', 'WASD: run · Space: jump · Shift: dash · Esc: pause', { at: 'bottom', keys: true });\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/main.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file The starter game's page (labs/game/index.html): it starts the engine (`createEngine`, engine/app/engine.ts) on\\n * the scene `game:play` (labs/game/scenes/play.ts) with the arena's text registered for the view, offers the bot's\\n * replay to `?replay=bot`, and shows the round each drawn frame in the HTML/CSS overlay's HUD: the gems, the clock,\\n * the controls, the best round kept across sessions (labs/game/best.ts, through the engine's store) and, when the\\n * round ends, how it ended. Enter then starts a new round on the next seed. Escape (or the pad's Start) pauses the\\n * round under a menu (`createMenu`: Resume, New round), which keeps its keys out of play while it is open\\n * (`createEngine({ modal })`); the store keeps the best round and any keys rebound (`createEngine({ store })`). The\\n * camera is the chase camera unless the URL names one (`?cam=iso`, `?cam3=orbit`).\\n *\\n * Everything else a page does (routes, the renderer, the frame loop, the devices, `window.__engine`, the error\\n * overlay, the touch controls) is the engine's, so this file is the game's own presentation only. It reads the run\\n * and never changes it: the HUD cannot change the hash.\\n *\\n * Game code: it imports only the engine's public API (engine/index.ts) and the game's own files.\\n *\\n * Open it at http://127.0.0.1:5173/labs/game/ (`npm run dev`): `?seed=7&game.gems=20` plays another round,\\n * `?replay=bot` the bot's, `?cam=iso` another camera. Headless, `node x shot labs/game` reports what it draws.\\n */\\n/// <reference types=\\\"vite/client\\\" />\\nimport { createEngine, createMenu, createStore, type Engine, type EngineFrame, type Menu } from '../../engine';\\nimport { bestOf, recordBest } from './best';\\nimport { ARENA_TEXT, play, type Round } from './scenes/play';\\n\\n/** The page's query, with the chase camera when it names none: it follows the player round the arena. */\\nconst search = new URLSearchParams(location.search);\\nif (!search.has('cam') && !search.has('cam3')) search.set('cam3', 'chase');\\n\\n/** The game's replays, by name (`?replay=bot`), as text loaded on demand. */\\nconst replays = { bot: () => import('./replays/bot.replay.json?raw').then((module) => module.default) };\\n\\n/** The started engine; null until `createEngine` resolves (its first frames are drawn before that). */\\nlet engine: Engine | null = null;\\n\\n/** Whether the round on screen has ended, so Enter starts the next. */\\nlet ended = false;\\n\\n/** The game's store: its best round across sessions; a replay's page plays in its demo mode, so it sets none. */\\nconst store = createStore({ prefix: 'my3dge-game:', mode: search.has('replay') ? 'demo' : 'play' });\\n\\n/** The fastest won round kept (s), and the run whose clock the page saw running, with the steps it started with. */\\nlet best = bestOf(store);\\nlet seen: { world: object; steps: number } | null = null;\\n\\n/** The pause menu while it is open; null while the round plays. */\\nlet paused: Menu | null = null;\\n\\n/** Opens the pause menu over the paused round, or reads it a frame: Resume (or Escape again), New round. */\\nfunction pauseMenu(dt: number): void {\\n  const [inspector, ui, input] = [engine?.inspector, engine?.ui, engine?.input];\\n  if (!inspector || !ui || !input) return;\\n  const close = () => {\\n    paused?.remove();\\n    paused = null;\\n    inspector.resume();\\n  };\\n  if (paused) return void paused.update(dt);\\n  if (!input.pressed('pause')) return;\\n  inspector.pause();\\n  const items = [\\n    { label: 'Resume', id: 'resume' },\\n    { label: 'New round', id: 'new' },\\n  ];\\n  paused = createMenu(ui.root, items, { input, id: 'pause', title: 'Paused', onCancel: close, onPick: (_i, item) => {\\n    if (typeof item === 'object' && item.id === 'new') inspector.seed(inspector.seed() + 1);\\n    close();\\n  } }); // prettier-ignore\\n}\\n\\n/** Shows the round in the HUD: the gems, the clock, the controls, and how the round ended. */\\nfunction showRound({ world }: EngineFrame): void {\\n  const ui = engine?.ui;\\n  const entity = world.query('round')[0] as unknown as { round: Round } | undefined;\\n  if (!ui || !entity) return;\\n  const { gems, collected, left, state } = entity.round;\\n  if (seen?.world !== world) seen = state === 'playing' ? { world, steps: left + world.tick } : null;\\n  const was = ended;\\n  ui.hud('gems', `Gems ${collected} / ${gems}`, { at: 'top-left' });\\n  ui.hud('clock', `${Math.ceil(left / world.hz)} s`, { at: 'top-right', color: left < 10 * world.hz ? '#ff8f6b' : '' });\\n  ui.hud('keys', 'WASD: run · Space: jump · Shift: dash · Esc: pause', { at: 'bottom', keys: true });\\n\",\"total_lines\":105}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-7e171863-87fe-4609-9892-d3121b94650f-77","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"739→## 19. Testing\\n740→\\n**One command: `npm run verify`** (`labs/game/tools/verify.mjs`; add it with `npm pkg set scripts.verify=\\\"node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs\\\"`). It prints only failures and one final line (`verify: 212 passed, 0 failed`). Rerun it only after changing the code it covers; `--fast` skips browsers. Use one or two browsers at a time. No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Make it the first thing you write, from this skeleton.\\n\\nTested on the starter, under the run's real long `TMPDIR`, with no setup: `verify: 8 passed, 0 failed` in 28 s (14 s with `--fast`). With a broken test, a lint error and a failing browser check, it printed exactly those three `FAIL` lines and `verify: 9 passed, 3 failed`, and exited 1. It passes `npm run check` itself. For a lint failure it prints the summary; run `npm run check` for the details.\\n\\n```js\\n/**\\n * @file The one command, `npm run verify`: runs every check quietly and prints only the failures, then one line with\\n * the count. `--fast` skips what needs a browser. It sets its own short TMPDIR and CHROMIUM_PATH, so it needs no setup,\\n * and removes the temporary folder when done. Exit 0 when nothing failed.\\n750→ */\\nimport { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\\nimport { existsSync, globSync, mkdtempSync, readFileSync, rmSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { homedir } from 'node:os';\\n\\nconst fast = process.argv.includes('--fast');\\nconst tmp = mkdtempSync('/tmp/g.');\\nconst chrome = globSync(`${homedir()}/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium-*/chrome-linux64/chrome`)[0];\\nconst env = { ...process.env, TMPDIR: tmp, CHROMIUM_PATH: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH ?? chrome };\\nconst run = (command, ...args) => spawnSync(command, args, { env, encoding: 'utf8', maxBuffer: 2 ** 28 });\\n760→\\nlet passed = 0;\\nconst failed = [];\\nconst note = (name, ok, detail = '') => (ok ? (passed += 1) : failed.push(`${name}${detail ? `: ${detail}` : ''}`));\\nconst tail = (result) => `${result.stdout}${result.stderr}`.trim().split('\\\\n').slice(-6).join(' | ').slice(0, 600);\\n\\n/** A step whose exit status is its verdict. */\\nconst step = (name, command, ...args) => {\\n  const result = run(command, ...args);\\n  note(name, result.status === 0, tail(result));\\n770→};\\n\\n/** Vitest, counted test by test from the JSON report npm test writes. */\\nfunction tests() {\\n  const report = 'out/test/report.json';\\n  rmSync(report, { force: true });\\n  const result = run('npm', 'test', '--silent');\\n  if (!existsSync(report)) return note('npm test', false, tail(result));\\n  for (const file of JSON.parse(readFileSync(report, 'utf8')).testResults) {\\n    for (const test of file.assertionResults) {\\n780→      note(\\n        test.fullName,\\n        test.status === 'passed',\\n        String(test.failureMessages?.[0] ?? '')\\n          .split('\\\\n')[0]\\n          .slice(0, 300),\\n      );\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n790→\\n/** A browser tool: it prints one JSON line per check, { name, ok, detail }. Add e2e and hub runs here. */\\nfunction browserTool(path) {\\n  const result = run('node', path);\\n  const lines = result.stdout.split('\\\\n').filter((line) => line.startsWith('{'));\\n  for (const line of lines) {\\n    const { name, ok, detail } = JSON.parse(line);\\n    note(name, ok, detail);\\n  }\\n  if (result.status !== 0 || lines.length === 0) note(path, false, tail(result));\\n800→}\\n\\nstep('npm run check', 'npm', 'run', '--silent', 'check');\\ntests();\\nif (!fast) {\\n  step('replays in Chromium', 'node', 'x', 'replay', 'labs/game/replays', '--browser', 'sim');\\n  for (const path of ['labs/game/tools/e2e.mjs', 'labs/game/tools/hub.mjs']) if (existsSync(path)) browserTool(path);\\n}\\nrmSync(tmp, { recursive: true, force: true });\\nfor (const failure of failed) console.log(`FAIL ${failure}`);\\n810→console.log(`verify: ${passed} passed, ${failed.length} failed`);\\nprocess.exit(failed.length ? 1 : 0);\\n```\\n\\n**Keep an issue log from the first day** in `labs/game/TESTING-LOG.md`: one line per issue a test or check caught, naming the test, and whether it was critical (game-breaking). TASK.md's Testing Feedback asks how many issues testing caught, how many were critical, and which tests helped most and least. You cannot answer honestly from memory at the end. Note engine friction in `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` as it happens, and prompt friction in the log, for the other two feedback sections.\\n\\n**Node (`npm test`, beside each module).**\\n\\n- Golden replay per case; capture and restore continue exactly.\\n- The bot wins every case on Resident on 3 seeds (`BOT_SEEDS=10` for a wider sweep before finishing); also on Intern and Attending for Cases 1 and 8; also with 2 and 4 bot surgeons.\\n820→- Each tool's rules, each rating band, each strain's state machine, each failure (flatline, clock, slips), Stillpoint's and Second Pulse's rules, flap clip timing.\\n- `validateCase` and `validateStory` over all data; no \\\"TODO\\\", \\\"placeholder\\\" or lorem in the text; no name from the §1 never-use list.\\n- **Whole-game playthrough without a browser:** the Director walks the story, all eight cases by bot, to credits and Free Surgery.\\n- **The sequencer:** a host and 3 clients in Node over a simulated network (latency, jitter, 5% loss on unreliable input, leavers): all hashes equal after 3,600 ticks. This protects the riskiest system cheaply.\\n- Settings: sliders within range and reset restores defaults.\\n- Every tool and target kind has an effect or a refusal line (§4). The exit interview parses and matches TASK.md's shape, and the About screen's data matches it.\\n\\n**Browser, against the deliverable (`labs/game/tools/e2e.mjs`, Playwright).** Launch with `executablePath: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH` and `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`, on `file://`.\\n\\n- Boots with no external request and no console errors; `__engine.ready`.\\n830→- **Whole-game playthrough:** a stub pad (`navigator.getGamepads` via `addInitScript`) walks title, Board, story and debriefs, with the bot playing the ops at a raised `timeScale`, to the credits. 640×360 is enough.\\n- Attract starts at 10 s idle; each of key, mouse, pad button and stick exits it; nothing saved.\\n- Prompts: glyphs change with last device; the Xbox glyph setting works.\\n- Every menu by pad only, then by mouse (hover moves focus, click picks).\\n- Dev mode opens from pause; a slider changes its setting live; reset works.\\n- Touch, in Chromium's phone and tablet profiles, tapping with `page.touchscreen`: exact message, settings button works, no touch layer, attract runs.\\n- No WebGPU, through the system Python's Playwright: Firefox, and WebKit with an iPhone profile (the closest here to an iPhone, not iOS Safari; both have WebGL only). Expect the plain message of §10. An iPhone's real behaviour is checked only this far.\\n- Multiplayer stand-in: there is no second player here, so two browser pages stand in for one. The harness does this with frames running real copies of the game, and clicking a frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\\n- Audio: music bus 0.1 by default; an `AnalyserNode` hears music and a beep; effects render with an `OfflineAudioContext`; the file holds no audio data or `<audio>`.\\n- Couch: two stub pads each claim a seat and move their own cursor.\\n840→- Story and HUD elements carry `data-game` attributes so scripts can read them through the DOM.\\n\\n**Hub:** the four harness runs of §16, plus the game on its own.\\n\\n**Seeing it.** Prefer numbers: `node x shot labs/game --ids` for coverage and what is visible. Use screenshots only for what a script cannot check: at 1920×1080 for each screen once, and the field in each case. WebGPU canvases can come out blank in headless screenshots; `node x shot --png` draws through the engine and works.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 20. Build order\\n\\n850→Each step ends with its tests green. Get the core feeling right before spreading out.\\n\\n1. **Setup and packaging first.** Starter builds to one file, runs from `file://`. `verify` exists. `game:main` with runs.\\n2. **The field.** Live sheet with breath and heartbeat, under `look:field`, with drapes and the lamp. Mouse and pad cursor, hand and scalpel. Draw one incision on a guide: this must already look and feel good. Spend time here.\\n3. **Case 1 complete:** Drain, Forceps, Suture, Syringe, Gel, radial, vitals, clock, ratings, HUD, Odie's lines, failure and debrief, and the bot winning it.\\n4. **Front end:** title, Board, story scene with two characters and voices, briefing, pause, Settings, glyphs, saves. Then attract, then dev mode, since they speed all later work.\\n5. **Case 2 and 3:** Scalpel, Scanner, Laser, sites, Code, Stillpoint and the pulse-lock.\\n6. **Hub early:** session flow, card, sequencer test in Node, then the harness on the one case you have, while it is still cheap to change.\\n7. **Strains and flaps:** Cases 4, 5, 6, with Second Pulse, seats, AI colleague, Swap, Consult, couch co-op.\",\"content_concise\":\"739→## 19. Testing\\n740→\\n**One command: `npm run verify`** (`labs/game/tools/verify.mjs`; add it with `npm pkg set scripts.verify=\\\"node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs\\\"`). It prints only failures and one final line (`verify: 212 passed, 0 failed`). Rerun it only after changing the code it covers; `--fast` skips browsers. Use one or two browsers at a time. No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Make it the first thing you write, from this skeleton.\\n\\nTested on the starter, under the run's real long `TMPDIR`, with no setup: `verify: 8 passed, 0 failed` in 28 s (14 s with `--fast`). With a broken test, a lint error and a failing browser check, it printed exactly those three `FAIL` lines and `verify: 9 passed, 3 failed`, and exited 1. It passes `npm run check` itself. For a lint failure it prints the summary; run `npm run check` for the details.\\n\\n```js\\n/**\\n * @file The one command, `npm run verify`: runs every check quietly and prints only the failures, then one line with\\n * the count. `--fast` skips what needs a browser. It sets its own short TMPDIR and CHROMIUM_PATH, so it needs no setup,\\n * and removes the temporary folder when done. Exit 0 when nothing failed.\\n750→ */\\nimport { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\\nimport { existsSync, globSync, mkdtempSync, readFileSync, rmSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { homedir } from 'node:os';\\n\\nconst fast = process.argv.includes('--fast');\\nconst tmp = mkdtempSync('/tmp/g.');\\nconst chrome = globSync(`${homedir()}/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium-*/chrome-linux64/chrome`)[0];\\nconst env = { ...process.env, TMPDIR: tmp, CHROMIUM_PATH: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH ?? chrome };\\nconst run = (command, ...args) => spawnSync(command, args, { env, encoding: 'utf8', maxBuffer: 2 ** 28 });\\n760→\\nlet passed = 0;\\nconst failed = [];\\nconst note = (name, ok, detail = '') => (ok ? (passed += 1) : failed.push(`${name}${detail ? `: ${detail}` : ''}`));\\nconst tail = (result) => `${result.stdout}${result.stderr}`.trim().split('\\\\n').slice(-6).join(' | ').slice(0, 600);\\n\\n/** A step whose exit status is its verdict. */\\nconst step = (name, command, ...args) => {\\n  const result = run(command, ...args);\\n  note(name, result.status === 0, tail(result));\\n770→};\\n\\n/** Vitest, counted test by test from the JSON report npm test writes. */\\nfunction tests() {\\n  const report = 'out/test/report.json';\\n  rmSync(report, { force: true });\\n  const result = run('npm', 'test', '--silent');\\n  if (!existsSync(report)) return note('npm test', false, tail(result));\\n  for (const file of JSON.parse(readFileSync(report, 'utf8')).testResults) {\\n    for (const test of file.assertionResults) {\\n780→      note(\\n        test.fullName,\\n        test.status === 'passed',\\n        String(test.failureMessages?.[0] ?? '')\\n          .split('\\\\n')[0]\\n          .slice(0, 300),\\n      );\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n790→\\n/** A browser tool: it prints one JSON line per check, { name, ok, detail }. Add e2e and hub runs here. */\\nfunction browserTool(path) {\\n  const result = run('node', path);\\n  const lines = result.stdout.split('\\\\n').filter((line) => line.startsWith('{'));\\n  for (const line of lines) {\\n    const { name, ok, detail } = JSON.parse(line);\\n    note(name, ok, detail);\\n  }\\n  if (result.status !== 0 || lines.length === 0) note(path, false, tail(result));\\n800→}\\n\\nstep('npm run check', 'npm', 'run', '--silent', 'check');\\ntests();\\nif (!fast) {\\n  step('replays in Chromium', 'node', 'x', 'replay', 'labs/game/replays', '--browser', 'sim');\\n  for (const path of ['labs/game/tools/e2e.mjs', 'labs/game/tools/hub.mjs']) if (existsSync(path)) browserTool(path);\\n}\\nrmSync(tmp, { recursive: true, force: true });\\nfor (const failure of failed) console.log(`FAIL ${failure}`);\\n810→console.log(`verify: ${passed} passed, ${failed.length} failed`);\\nprocess.exit(failed.length ? 1 : 0);\\n```\\n\\n**Keep an issue log from the first day** in `labs/game/TESTING-LOG.md`: one line per issue a test or check caught, naming the test, and whether it was critical (game-breaking). TASK.md's Testing Feedback asks how many issues testing caught, how many were critical, and which tests helped most and least. You cannot answer honestly from memory at the end. Note engine friction in `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` as it happens, and prompt friction in the log, for the other two feedback sections.\\n\\n**Node (`npm test`, beside each module).**\\n\\n- Golden replay per case; capture and restore continue exactly.\\n- The bot wins every case on Resident on 3 seeds (`BOT_SEEDS=10` for a wider sweep before finishing); also on Intern and Attending for Cases 1 and 8; also with 2 and 4 bot surgeons.\\n820→- Each tool's rules, each rating band, each strain's state machine, each failure (flatline, clock, slips), Stillpoint's and Second Pulse's rules, flap clip timing.\\n- `validateCase` and `validateStory` over all data; no \\\"TODO\\\", \\\"placeholder\\\" or lorem in the text; no name from the §1 never-use list.\\n- **Whole-game playthrough without a browser:** the Director walks the story, all eight cases by bot, to credits and Free Surgery.\\n- **The sequencer:** a host and 3 clients in Node over a simulated network (latency, jitter, 5% loss on unreliable input, leavers): all hashes equal after 3,600 ticks. This protects the riskiest system cheaply.\\n- Settings: sliders within range and reset restores defaults.\\n- Every tool and target kind has an effect or a refusal line (§4). The exit interview parses and matches TASK.md's shape, and the About screen's data matches it.\\n\\n**Browser, against the deliverable (`labs/game/tools/e2e.mjs`, Playwright).** Launch with `executablePath: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH` and `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`, on `file://`.\\n\\n- Boots with no external request and no console errors; `__engine.ready`.\\n830→- **Whole-game playthrough:** a stub pad (`navigator.getGamepads` via `addInitScript`) walks title, Board, story and debriefs, with the bot playing the ops at a raised `timeScale`, to the credits. 640×360 is enough.\\n- Attract starts at 10 s idle; each of key, mouse, pad button and stick exits it; nothing saved.\\n- Prompts: glyphs change with last device; the Xbox glyph setting works.\\n- Every menu by pad only, then by mouse (hover moves focus, click picks).\\n- Dev mode opens from pause; a slider changes its setting live; reset works.\\n- Touch, in Chromium's phone and tablet profiles, tapping with `page.touchscreen`: exact message, settings button works, no touch layer, attract runs.\\n- No WebGPU, through the system Python's Playwright: Firefox, and WebKit with an iPhone profile (the closest here to an iPhone, not iOS Safari; both have WebGL only). Expect the plain message of §10. An iPhone's real behaviour is checked only this far.\\n- Multiplayer stand-in: there is no second player here, so two browser pages stand in for one. The harness does this with frames running real copies of the game, and clicking a frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\\n- Audio: music bus 0.1 by default; an `AnalyserNode` hears music and a beep; effects render with an `OfflineAudioContext`; the file holds no audio data or `<audio>`.\\n- Couch: two stub pads each claim a seat and move their own cursor.\\n840→- Story and HUD elements carry `data-game` attributes so scripts can read them through the DOM.\\n\\n**Hub:** the four harness runs of §16, plus the game on its own.\\n\\n**Seeing it.** Prefer numbers: `node x shot labs/game --ids` for coverage and what is visible. Use screenshots only for what a script cannot check: at 1920×1080 for each screen once, and the field in each case. WebGPU canvases can come out blank in headless screenshots; `node x shot --png` draws through the engine and works.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 20. Build order\\n\\n850→Each step ends with its tests green. Get the core feeling right before spreading out.\\n\\n1. **Setup and packaging first.** Starter builds to one file, runs from `file://`. `verify` exists. `game:main` with runs.\\n2. **The field.** Live sheet with breath and heartbeat, under `look:field`, with drapes and the lamp. Mouse and pad cursor, hand and scalpel. Draw one incision on a guide: this must already look and feel good. Spend time here.\\n3. **Case 1 complete:** Drain, Forceps, Suture, Syringe, Gel, radial, vitals, clock, ratings, HUD, Odie's lines, failure and debrief, and the bot winning it.\\n4. **Front end:** title, Board, story scene with two characters and voices, briefing, pause, Settings, glyphs, saves. Then attract, then dev mode, since they speed all later work.\\n5. **Case 2 and 3:** Scalpel, Scanner, Laser, sites, Code, Stillpoint and the pulse-lock.\\n6. **Hub early:** session flow, card, sequencer test in Node, then the harness on the one case you have, while it is still cheap to change.\\n7. **Strains and flaps:** Cases 4, 5, 6, with Second Pulse, seats, AI colleague, Swap, Consult, couch co-op.\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":739,\"limit\":120,\"raw_output\":\"## 19. Testing\\n\\n**One command: `npm run verify`** (`labs/game/tools/verify.mjs`; add it with `npm pkg set scripts.verify=\\\"node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs\\\"`). It prints only failures and one final line (`verify: 212 passed, 0 failed`). Rerun it only after changing the code it covers; `--fast` skips browsers. Use one or two browsers at a time. No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Make it the first thing you write, from this skeleton.\\n\\nTested on the starter, under the run's real long `TMPDIR`, with no setup: `verify: 8 passed, 0 failed` in 28 s (14 s with `--fast`). With a broken test, a lint error and a failing browser check, it printed exactly those three `FAIL` lines and `verify: 9 passed, 3 failed`, and exited 1. It passes `npm run check` itself. For a lint failure it prints the summary; run `npm run check` for the details.\\n\\n```js\\n/**\\n * @file The one command, `npm run verify`: runs every check quietly and prints only the failures, then one line with\\n * the count. `--fast` skips what needs a browser. It sets its own short TMPDIR and CHROMIUM_PATH, so it needs no setup,\\n * and removes the temporary folder when done. Exit 0 when nothing failed.\\n */\\nimport { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\\nimport { existsSync, globSync, mkdtempSync, readFileSync, rmSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { homedir } from 'node:os';\\n\\nconst fast = process.argv.includes('--fast');\\nconst tmp = mkdtempSync('/tmp/g.');\\nconst chrome = globSync(`${homedir()}/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium-*/chrome-linux64/chrome`)[0];\\nconst env = { ...process.env, TMPDIR: tmp, CHROMIUM_PATH: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH ?? chrome };\\nconst run = (command, ...args) => spawnSync(command, args, { env, encoding: 'utf8', maxBuffer: 2 ** 28 });\\n\\nlet passed = 0;\\nconst failed = [];\\nconst note = (name, ok, detail = '') => (ok ? (passed += 1) : failed.push(`${name}${detail ? `: ${detail}` : ''}`));\\nconst tail = (result) => `${result.stdout}${result.stderr}`.trim().split('\\\\n').slice(-6).join(' | ').slice(0, 600);\\n\\n/** A step whose exit status is its verdict. */\\nconst step = (name, command, ...args) => {\\n  const result = run(command, ...args);\\n  note(name, result.status === 0, tail(result));\\n};\\n\\n/** Vitest, counted test by test from the JSON report npm test writes. */\\nfunction tests() {\\n  const report = 'out/test/report.json';\\n  rmSync(report, { force: true });\\n  const result = run('npm', 'test', '--silent');\\n  if (!existsSync(report)) return note('npm test', false, tail(result));\\n  for (const file of JSON.parse(readFileSync(report, 'utf8')).testResults) {\\n    for (const test of file.assertionResults) {\\n      note(\\n        test.fullName,\\n        test.status === 'passed',\\n        String(test.failureMessages?.[0] ?? '')\\n          .split('\\\\n')[0]\\n          .slice(0, 300),\\n      );\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n/** A browser tool: it prints one JSON line per check, { name, ok, detail }. Add e2e and hub runs here. */\\nfunction browserTool(path) {\\n  const result = run('node', path);\\n  const lines = result.stdout.split('\\\\n').filter((line) => line.startsWith('{'));\\n  for (const line of lines) {\\n    const { name, ok, detail } = JSON.parse(line);\\n    note(name, ok, detail);\\n  }\\n  if (result.status !== 0 || lines.length === 0) note(path, false, tail(result));\\n}\\n\\nstep('npm run check', 'npm', 'run', '--silent', 'check');\\ntests();\\nif (!fast) {\\n  step('replays in Chromium', 'node', 'x', 'replay', 'labs/game/replays', '--browser', 'sim');\\n  for (const path of ['labs/game/tools/e2e.mjs', 'labs/game/tools/hub.mjs']) if (existsSync(path)) browserTool(path);\\n}\\nrmSync(tmp, { recursive: true, force: true });\\nfor (const failure of failed) console.log(`FAIL ${failure}`);\\nconsole.log(`verify: ${passed} passed, ${failed.length} failed`);\\nprocess.exit(failed.length ? 1 : 0);\\n```\\n\\n**Keep an issue log from the first day** in `labs/game/TESTING-LOG.md`: one line per issue a test or check caught, naming the test, and whether it was critical (game-breaking). TASK.md's Testing Feedback asks how many issues testing caught, how many were critical, and which tests helped most and least. You cannot answer honestly from memory at the end. Note engine friction in `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` as it happens, and prompt friction in the log, for the other two feedback sections.\\n\\n**Node (`npm test`, beside each module).**\\n\\n- Golden replay per case; capture and restore continue exactly.\\n- The bot wins every case on Resident on 3 seeds (`BOT_SEEDS=10` for a wider sweep before finishing); also on Intern and Attending for Cases 1 and 8; also with 2 and 4 bot surgeons.\\n- Each tool's rules, each rating band, each strain's state machine, each failure (flatline, clock, slips), Stillpoint's and Second Pulse's rules, flap clip timing.\\n- `validateCase` and `validateStory` over all data; no \\\"TODO\\\", \\\"placeholder\\\" or lorem in the text; no name from the §1 never-use list.\\n- **Whole-game playthrough without a browser:** the Director walks the story, all eight cases by bot, to credits and Free Surgery.\\n- **The sequencer:** a host and 3 clients in Node over a simulated network (latency, jitter, 5% loss on unreliable input, leavers): all hashes equal after 3,600 ticks. This protects the riskiest system cheaply.\\n- Settings: sliders within range and reset restores defaults.\\n- Every tool and target kind has an effect or a refusal line (§4). The exit interview parses and matches TASK.md's shape, and the About screen's data matches it.\\n\\n**Browser, against the deliverable (`labs/game/tools/e2e.mjs`, Playwright).** Launch with `executablePath: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH` and `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`, on `file://`.\\n\\n- Boots with no external request and no console errors; `__engine.ready`.\\n- **Whole-game playthrough:** a stub pad (`navigator.getGamepads` via `addInitScript`) walks title, Board, story and debriefs, with the bot playing the ops at a raised `timeScale`, to the credits. 640×360 is enough.\\n- Attract starts at 10 s idle; each of key, mouse, pad button and stick exits it; nothing saved.\\n- Prompts: glyphs change with last device; the Xbox glyph setting works.\\n- Every menu by pad only, then by mouse (hover moves focus, click picks).\\n- Dev mode opens from pause; a slider changes its setting live; reset works.\\n- Touch, in Chromium's phone and tablet profiles, tapping with `page.touchscreen`: exact message, settings button works, no touch layer, attract runs.\\n- No WebGPU, through the system Python's Playwright: Firefox, and WebKit with an iPhone profile (the closest here to an iPhone, not iOS Safari; both have WebGL only). Expect the plain message of §10. An iPhone's real behaviour is checked only this far.\\n- Multiplayer stand-in: there is no second player here, so two browser pages stand in for one. The harness does this with frames running real copies of the game, and clicking a frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\\n- Audio: music bus 0.1 by default; an `AnalyserNode` hears music and a beep; effects render with an `OfflineAudioContext`; the file holds no audio data or `<audio>`.\\n- Couch: two stub pads each claim a seat and move their own cursor.\\n- Story and HUD elements carry `data-game` attributes so scripts can read them through the DOM.\\n\\n**Hub:** the four harness runs of §16, plus the game on its own.\\n\\n**Seeing it.** Prefer numbers: `node x shot labs/game --ids` for coverage and what is visible. Use screenshots only for what a script cannot check: at 1920×1080 for each screen once, and the field in each case. WebGPU canvases can come out blank in headless screenshots; `node x shot --png` draws through the engine and works.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 20. Build order\\n\\nEach step ends with its tests green. Get the core feeling right before spreading out.\\n\\n1. **Setup and packaging first.** Starter builds to one file, runs from `file://`. `verify` exists. `game:main` with runs.\\n2. **The field.** Live sheet with breath and heartbeat, under `look:field`, with drapes and the lamp. Mouse and pad cursor, hand and scalpel. Draw one incision on a guide: this must already look and feel good. Spend time here.\\n3. **Case 1 complete:** Drain, Forceps, Suture, Syringe, Gel, radial, vitals, clock, ratings, HUD, Odie's lines, failure and debrief, and the bot winning it.\\n4. **Front end:** title, Board, story scene with two characters and voices, briefing, pause, Settings, glyphs, saves. Then attract, then dev mode, since they speed all later work.\\n5. **Case 2 and 3:** Scalpel, Scanner, Laser, sites, Code, Stillpoint and the pulse-lock.\\n6. **Hub early:** session flow, card, sequencer test in Node, then the harness on the one case you have, while it is still cheap to change.\\n7. **Strains and flaps:** Cases 4, 5, 6, with Second Pulse, seats, AI colleague, Swap, Consult, couch co-op.\\n\",\"total_lines\":952}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"525c5b48-72d7-4218-9a47-7d7dacac9f6b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_69","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The bot wins all cases. I will continue building the rest of the game.\n\nWriting simulation tests first, then story, director, voice, and sequencer. The page will be scaffolded last to save tokens. I'll set the par value after measuring.\n\nRunning a quick node check to measure the current gala score and set par. The op.test.ts suite will cover table completion, drain effects, syringes, ratings, clock and slip failures, code outcomes, and growAway.\n\nThe test plan now covers stillpoint deltas, pulses, consults, lull telegraphs, solace splits, reverie submerges, balm lacquers, mercy transitions, difficulty, and sliders. The bot suite should finish in about one second. Story will need roughly three hundred twenty lines of dialogue across sixteen scenes.\n\nStory fits in one file under six hundred lines if boxes are one-liners. The director will walk the scenes. The page is the hardest part for local boot. I will check the engine signature and index.html to plan a compact build that checks WebGPU, inlines the session, draws the field, and overlays all screens.\n\nI will check act.ts line count first. Then I will write op.test.ts and the story, director, voice, and sequencer in this stretch. I am drafting a `go` helper to drive hands through operations for the mechanism tests.\n\nThe `go` helper is now complete. It spreads a partial hand over an idle copy and steps the operation. A drain test is being drafted: it creates a pool, targets it with a syringe tool, and asserts the pool finishes after thirty steps.\n\nThe drain test is complete. A scalpel refusal on a pool is being drafted to assert the refusal event and preserve volume and slips. Syringe charging and injection steps are planned, along with direct rateCut and rateInject calls. A clock failure test will set the clock to fifty and verify status loss.\n\nA slip test is being drafted: scalpel on an empty target for twelve steps, repeated until the resident limit of five slips is exceeded. A code success test is also planned: zero vitals, one step to start code, then paddling through the band until code clears and vitals reach at least twenty-five.\n\nA code-fail test is being drafted: paddle outside the band twice after code starts, then assert loss. A no-code test is also planned: disable code and zero vitals, step once, and expect loss. The growAway test is beginning with a second site and a pool target.\n\nThe growAway test now steps one hundred twenty times with a seat on site zero and expects the pool volume to increase. A stillpoint gift test is drafted: set simple mode, charge the gift, press it, step once, and assert the field delta equals twenty-five. A second pulse test is also planned: activate simple mode, press the second gift, add a bleeding pool, step sixty times, and verify vitals do not drop before a clean action heals them. The consult test is beginning with a two-seat operation.\n\nConsult tests will charge both seats with stillpoint in simple mode and assert a consult event. Lull telegraph is tested by adding the target and stepping four hundred times to trigger the arming event. Solace split is simulated by manually moving a scalpel across the site over forty steps.\n\nReverie tests will step a scanner use and laser to surface, then wait two hundred steps for submerge. Balm lacquer tests will place a wound and balm cell within one point two, step once, and assert the wound lacquer while suture refuses. Mercy phase tests will verify the case definition contains the three mercy phase IDs.\n\nA unit test will set mercyKills to six, remove the motes, and step to verify binders spawn. A difficulty test is also planned: create two first-cut cases with intern and attending levels.\n\nThe difficulty test now asserts that intern mode allows a higher clock maximum and a ten-slip limit, while attending mode caps both at lower values. Slider tests will set drain to ninety-nine and slips to three, then verify the limits and confirm reset restores defaults. Rank tests will check that a perfect score yields an S for residents and a star for attendings, and a half-score yields a C. Bot tests will be written as one loop per case in bot.test.ts.\n\nThe bot suite will use `it.each` to run every case with seeds one through three and both difficulty levels. Each run asserts the operation wins. Resident seed one also checks that the score stays within a small margin of the case par.\n\nThe bot suite will compare seed two and three against seed one to verify score consistency. A quick score print will run while the test file is written, and act.ts line count will be checked.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-18899b0e-b6c2-4262-ae0f-969c40bb4eaa-78","name":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731 && wc -l labs/game/op/*.ts && ./node_modules/.bin/tsx -e \"\nimport { runBot } from './labs/game/op/bot.ts';\nimport { createFromCase } from './labs/game/op/world.ts';\nconst op = createFromCase('case:gala-shift', { seed: 1, difficulty: 'resident' });\nrunBot(op);\nconsole.log(op.score, op.status);\n\"","description":"Count lines and print gala score"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6739,"output_tokens":2929,"cache_read_input_tokens":180224,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"7410a239-b7a0-489e-8b16-9fea328e4e08"}
