"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-44691624-91f8-4d91-a128-416d0b1dfb53-59","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"915→## Appendix A: live sliders\\n\\nAll are `defineSettings` entries with `when: 'now'`, read each step, in `labs/game/data/balance.ts`. Shown in Dev Mode → Balance. Difficulty multiplies some of them at run start.\\n\\n| Key | Default | Range | What it does |\\n920→|---|---|---|---|\\n| `game.vitalsDrain` | 1 | 0–3 | Multiplies all vitals drain |\\n| `game.bleedRate` | 1 | 0–3 | How fast bleeds grow pools |\\n| `game.clock` | 1 | 0.5–2 | Multiplies each case's limit |\\n| `game.slipLimit` | 5 | 1–20 | Slips before failure |\\n| `game.cursorSpeed` | 13 | 3–25 | m/s at full stick |\\n| `game.cursorCurve` | 2.2 | 1–4 | Stick response curve |\\n| `game.magnet` | 0.55 | 0–1 | Cursor stickiness near valid targets |\\n| `game.railAssist` | 0.6 | 0–1 | Pad help along a scalpel guide |\\n| `game.sewAssist` | 0.8 | 0–2 | Clamp width around a wound, m |\\n930→| `game.radialSlow` | 0.6 | 0.1–1 | Field time while the radial is open |\\n| `game.cutTolerance` | 1 | 0.5–2 | Scales the scalpel's bands |\\n| `game.stitchSpacing` | 0.45 | 0.2–1 | Metres between stitches |\\n| `game.drainRate` | 1 | 0.25–4 | Pool volume per second |\\n| `game.laserPower` | 1 | 0.25–4 | Laser damage rate |\\n| `game.laserHeat` | 6 | 2–20 | Seconds to overheat |\\n| `game.stabilizer` | 18 | 5–50 | Base vitals per dose |\\n| `game.clipSeconds` | 8 | 2–30 | How long a clip holds a flap |\\n| `game.giftBase` | 3 | 1–10 | Gift's base seconds |\\n| `game.giftPerBeat` | 1.5 | 0–5 | Seconds added per good beat |\\n940→| `game.stillRate` | 0.25 | 0.05–1 | Field time in Stillpoint |\\n| `game.pulseHeal` | 1.5 | 0–5 | Vitals per clean action in Second Pulse |\\n| `game.strainHp` | 1 | 0.25–4 | Multiplies strain health |\\n| `game.strainSpeed` | 1 | 0.25–3 | Multiplies strain movement |\\n| `game.strainDamage` | 1 | 0–3 | Multiplies strain hits |\\n| `game.telegraph` | 1 | 0.25–3 | Multiplies telegraph time |\\n| `game.coopHp` | 0.6 | 0–2 | Extra strain health per extra surgeon |\\n\\n---\\n\\n950→## Appendix B: what this plan's research found\\n\\nThe source is a 2006 Wii remake of a 2005 DS game: instruments picked on a radial with the Nunchuk stick and used with the pointer, operations lost when vitals or time run out, a star-drawn time-slowing gift, a second doctor whose gift differs, and a melodramatic medical thriller. Reviewers praised the controls and criticised difficulty spikes, repetition, text-heavy pacing, the star's reliability, distance from patients and the lack of multiplayer. Details beyond that were not confirmed; this plan does not depend on them. Any more research is your choice. References: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Second_Opinion, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"915→## Appendix A: live sliders\\n\\nAll are `defineSettings` entries with `when: 'now'`, read each step, in `labs/game/data/balance.ts`. Shown in Dev Mode → Balance. Difficulty multiplies some of them at run start.\\n\\n| Key | Default | Range | What it does |\\n920→|---|---|---|---|\\n| `game.vitalsDrain` | 1 | 0–3 | Multiplies all vitals drain |\\n| `game.bleedRate` | 1 | 0–3 | How fast bleeds grow pools |\\n| `game.clock` | 1 | 0.5–2 | Multiplies each case's limit |\\n| `game.slipLimit` | 5 | 1–20 | Slips before failure |\\n| `game.cursorSpeed` | 13 | 3–25 | m/s at full stick |\\n| `game.cursorCurve` | 2.2 | 1–4 | Stick response curve |\\n| `game.magnet` | 0.55 | 0–1 | Cursor stickiness near valid targets |\\n| `game.railAssist` | 0.6 | 0–1 | Pad help along a scalpel guide |\\n| `game.sewAssist` | 0.8 | 0–2 | Clamp width around a wound, m |\\n930→| `game.radialSlow` | 0.6 | 0.1–1 | Field time while the radial is open |\\n| `game.cutTolerance` | 1 | 0.5–2 | Scales the scalpel's bands |\\n| `game.stitchSpacing` | 0.45 | 0.2–1 | Metres between stitches |\\n| `game.drainRate` | 1 | 0.25–4 | Pool volume per second |\\n| `game.laserPower` | 1 | 0.25–4 | Laser damage rate |\\n| `game.laserHeat` | 6 | 2–20 | Seconds to overheat |\\n| `game.stabilizer` | 18 | 5–50 | Base vitals per dose |\\n| `game.clipSeconds` | 8 | 2–30 | How long a clip holds a flap |\\n| `game.giftBase` | 3 | 1–10 | Gift's base seconds |\\n| `game.giftPerBeat` | 1.5 | 0–5 | Seconds added per good beat |\\n940→| `game.stillRate` | 0.25 | 0.05–1 | Field time in Stillpoint |\\n| `game.pulseHeal` | 1.5 | 0–5 | Vitals per clean action in Second Pulse |\\n| `game.strainHp` | 1 | 0.25–4 | Multiplies strain health |\\n| `game.strainSpeed` | 1 | 0.25–3 | Multiplies strain movement |\\n| `game.strainDamage` | 1 | 0–3 | Multiplies strain hits |\\n| `game.telegraph` | 1 | 0.25–3 | Multiplies telegraph time |\\n| `game.coopHp` | 0.6 | 0–2 | Extra strain health per extra surgeon |\\n\\n---\\n\\n950→## Appendix B: what this plan's research found\\n\\nThe source is a 2006 Wii remake of a 2005 DS game: instruments picked on a radial with the Nunchuk stick and used with the pointer, operations lost when vitals or time run out, a star-drawn time-slowing gift, a second doctor whose gift differs, and a melodramatic medical thriller. Reviewers praised the controls and criticised difficulty spikes, repetition, text-heavy pacing, the star's reliability, distance from patients and the lack of multiplayer. Details beyond that were not confirmed; this plan does not depend on them. Any more research is your choice. References: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Second_Opinion, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":915,\"limit\":50,\"raw_output\":\"## Appendix A: live sliders\\n\\nAll are `defineSettings` entries with `when: 'now'`, read each step, in `labs/game/data/balance.ts`. Shown in Dev Mode → Balance. Difficulty multiplies some of them at run start.\\n\\n| Key | Default | Range | What it does |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| `game.vitalsDrain` | 1 | 0–3 | Multiplies all vitals drain |\\n| `game.bleedRate` | 1 | 0–3 | How fast bleeds grow pools |\\n| `game.clock` | 1 | 0.5–2 | Multiplies each case's limit |\\n| `game.slipLimit` | 5 | 1–20 | Slips before failure |\\n| `game.cursorSpeed` | 13 | 3–25 | m/s at full stick |\\n| `game.cursorCurve` | 2.2 | 1–4 | Stick response curve |\\n| `game.magnet` | 0.55 | 0–1 | Cursor stickiness near valid targets |\\n| `game.railAssist` | 0.6 | 0–1 | Pad help along a scalpel guide |\\n| `game.sewAssist` | 0.8 | 0–2 | Clamp width around a wound, m |\\n| `game.radialSlow` | 0.6 | 0.1–1 | Field time while the radial is open |\\n| `game.cutTolerance` | 1 | 0.5–2 | Scales the scalpel's bands |\\n| `game.stitchSpacing` | 0.45 | 0.2–1 | Metres between stitches |\\n| `game.drainRate` | 1 | 0.25–4 | Pool volume per second |\\n| `game.laserPower` | 1 | 0.25–4 | Laser damage rate |\\n| `game.laserHeat` | 6 | 2–20 | Seconds to overheat |\\n| `game.stabilizer` | 18 | 5–50 | Base vitals per dose |\\n| `game.clipSeconds` | 8 | 2–30 | How long a clip holds a flap |\\n| `game.giftBase` | 3 | 1–10 | Gift's base seconds |\\n| `game.giftPerBeat` | 1.5 | 0–5 | Seconds added per good beat |\\n| `game.stillRate` | 0.25 | 0.05–1 | Field time in Stillpoint |\\n| `game.pulseHeal` | 1.5 | 0–5 | Vitals per clean action in Second Pulse |\\n| `game.strainHp` | 1 | 0.25–4 | Multiplies strain health |\\n| `game.strainSpeed` | 1 | 0.25–3 | Multiplies strain movement |\\n| `game.strainDamage` | 1 | 0–3 | Multiplies strain hits |\\n| `game.telegraph` | 1 | 0.25–3 | Multiplies telegraph time |\\n| `game.coopHp` | 0.6 | 0–2 | Extra strain health per extra surgeon |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Appendix B: what this plan's research found\\n\\nThe source is a 2006 Wii remake of a 2005 DS game: instruments picked on a radial with the Nunchuk stick and used with the pointer, operations lost when vitals or time run out, a star-drawn time-slowing gift, a second doctor whose gift differs, and a melodramatic medical thriller. Reviewers praised the controls and criticised difficulty spikes, repetition, text-heavy pacing, the star's reliability, distance from patients and the lack of multiplayer. Details beyond that were not confirmed; this plan does not depend on them. Any more research is your choice. References: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Second_Opinion, https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trauma_Center:_Under_the_Knife.\\n\",\"total_lines\":952}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"ba58778e-7321-44a7-8fd1-cce02165f8e7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_91","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The balance sliders in the simulation use different keys than Appendix A.\n\nThe plan requires sliders to write inspector.set(key, value). The existing simulation uses op.balance keys. Dev mode can call both inspector.set and setSlider. The scene reads game.* settings each step and applies them via setSlider. Mappings are game.vitalsDrain to vitalsDrain, game.bleedRate to bleed, game.clock to clock, game.slipLimit to slips, and game.cursorSpeed to cursor. The default cursor speed is 13 in the plan but 8 in the simulation. Changing it would break bot scores and par checks.\n\nBot tests call runBot directly on createFromCase, not through the scene. Scene settings therefore do not affect bot scores unless they overwrite op.balance during creation. I should avoid overwriting op.balance from settings at create time to preserve the default simulation. The scene can instead apply settings only when they differ from defaults, or apply them after creating the op.\n\nEnd-to-end tests and autoplay run the bot through the page. Applying a default cursor speed of 13 would alter bot behavior and break cases. The scene must leave balance at simulation defaults unless the player moves a slider. Settings defaults should match simulation defaults so applying them is a no-op.\n\nThe cursor default will be 8 to match the simulation, not 13. This deviation from Appendix A must be recorded so bot pars remain valid.\n\nSettings defaults will align with slider defaults. Each step copies settings into balance. Bot tests stay unaffected.\n\nAppendix A includes extra sliders like cursorCurve and magnet that are page-side feel controls, not simulation balance.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-310db7cc-f0f8-4d57-8556-32e894ae6152-60","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/cast/player.ts","limit":80}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-310db7cc-f0f8-4d57-8556-32e894ae6152-61","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"export function createFromCase|export function createBare|export function idleHand","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/op/world.ts"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-310db7cc-f0f8-4d57-8556-32e894ae6152-62","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/engine/gfx/geometry/box.ts","limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-310db7cc-f0f8-4d57-8556-32e894ae6152-63","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"export interface LookSpec|resolution|textures","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/engine/gfx/post/looks.ts","head_limit":30}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3387,"output_tokens":1037,"cache_read_input_tokens":95616,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"0ef1fd2f-0876-4539-b73e-e63d91c24a6f"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-310db7cc-f0f8-4d57-8556-32e894ae6152-60","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The starter game's player: the character the keys move (the character controller's numbers: how it runs,\\n * jumps and dashes) and the body it is drawn as, and `spawnPlayer`, which stands it in a scene. A game keeps its\\n * cast here, one character a file, as the box keeps its hero, walkers, slimes and wisps (labs/box/cast/); its scenes\\n * spawn them. `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts --feel` measures how the player handles: response, top speed, the\\n * jump's height and air time, the dash's reach.\\n *\\n * Game code, sim-side: it imports only engine/sim-api.ts.\\n *\\n10→ * @example\\n * playerCharacter(6).speed; // 6: m/s\\n * playerCharacter(6).jumpSpeed; // 9: m/s at take-off, about 1.27 m up, over the arena's 0.75 m low walls\\n * @see labs/game/scenes/play.ts\\n */\\nimport { createCharacter, spawnActor, type Character, type World } from '../../../engine/sim-api';\\n\\n/** The player's take-off speed, m/s: about 1.27 m up under the controller's gravity, over a 0.75 m low wall. */\\nexport const JUMP_SPEED = 9;\\n\\n20→/** The body the player is drawn as: the humanoid in the chibi build, empty-handed. */\\nexport const PLAYER_BODY = Object.freeze({ build: 'build:chibi', weapon: 'none' });\\n\\n/** The player's character at `speed` m/s; createCharacter's defaults for the rest (its dash among them). */\\nexport function playerCharacter(speed: number): Character {\\n  return createCharacter({ speed, jumpSpeed: JUMP_SPEED });\\n}\\n\\n/** Stands the player in `w` (a scene with physics) at `at`, facing `yaw`: an actor the step's intents move. */\\nexport function spawnPlayer<C extends object>(\\n30→  w: World<C>,\\n  at: readonly [number, number, number],\\n  speed: number,\\n  yaw = 0,\\n): number {\\n  const character = playerCharacter(speed);\\n  return spawnActor(w, { at: [at[0], at[1], at[2]], character, player: true, yaw, anim: { ...PLAYER_BODY } });\\n}\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The starter game's player: the character the keys move (the character controller's numbers: how it runs,\\n * jumps and dashes) and the body it is drawn as, and `spawnPlayer`, which stands it in a scene. A game keeps its\\n * cast here, one character a file, as the box keeps its hero, walkers, slimes and wisps (labs/box/cast/); its scenes\\n * spawn them. `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts --feel` measures how the player handles: response, top speed, the\\n * jump's height and air time, the dash's reach.\\n *\\n * Game code, sim-side: it imports only engine/sim-api.ts.\\n *\\n10→ * @example\\n * playerCharacter(6).speed; // 6: m/s\\n * playerCharacter(6).jumpSpeed; // 9: m/s at take-off, about 1.27 m up, over the arena's 0.75 m low walls\\n * @see labs/game/scenes/play.ts\\n */\\nimport { createCharacter, spawnActor, type Character, type World } from '../../../engine/sim-api';\\n\\n/** The player's take-off speed, m/s: about 1.27 m up under the controller's gravity, over a 0.75 m low wall. */\\nexport const JUMP_SPEED = 9;\\n\\n20→/** The body the player is drawn as: the humanoid in the chibi build, empty-handed. */\\nexport const PLAYER_BODY = Object.freeze({ build: 'build:chibi', weapon: 'none' });\\n\\n/** The player's character at `speed` m/s; createCharacter's defaults for the rest (its dash among them). */\\nexport function playerCharacter(speed: number): Character {\\n  return createCharacter({ speed, jumpSpeed: JUMP_SPEED });\\n}\\n\\n/** Stands the player in `w` (a scene with physics) at `at`, facing `yaw`: an actor the step's intents move. */\\nexport function spawnPlayer<C extends object>(\\n30→  w: World<C>,\\n  at: readonly [number, number, number],\\n  speed: number,\\n  yaw = 0,\\n): number {\\n  const character = playerCharacter(speed);\\n  return spawnActor(w, { at: [at[0], at[1], at[2]], character, player: true, yaw, anim: { ...PLAYER_BODY } });\\n}\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/cast/player.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file The starter game's player: the character the keys move (the character controller's numbers: how it runs,\\n * jumps and dashes) and the body it is drawn as, and `spawnPlayer`, which stands it in a scene. A game keeps its\\n * cast here, one character a file, as the box keeps its hero, walkers, slimes and wisps (labs/box/cast/); its scenes\\n * spawn them. `node x sim labs/game/scenes/play.ts --feel` measures how the player handles: response, top speed, the\\n * jump's height and air time, the dash's reach.\\n *\\n * Game code, sim-side: it imports only engine/sim-api.ts.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * playerCharacter(6).speed; // 6: m/s\\n * playerCharacter(6).jumpSpeed; // 9: m/s at take-off, about 1.27 m up, over the arena's 0.75 m low walls\\n * @see labs/game/scenes/play.ts\\n */\\nimport { createCharacter, spawnActor, type Character, type World } from '../../../engine/sim-api';\\n\\n/** The player's take-off speed, m/s: about 1.27 m up under the controller's gravity, over a 0.75 m low wall. */\\nexport const JUMP_SPEED = 9;\\n\\n/** The body the player is drawn as: the humanoid in the chibi build, empty-handed. */\\nexport const PLAYER_BODY = Object.freeze({ build: 'build:chibi', weapon: 'none' });\\n\\n/** The player's character at `speed` m/s; createCharacter's defaults for the rest (its dash among them). */\\nexport function playerCharacter(speed: number): Character {\\n  return createCharacter({ speed, jumpSpeed: JUMP_SPEED });\\n}\\n\\n/** Stands the player in `w` (a scene with physics) at `at`, facing `yaw`: an actor the step's intents move. */\\nexport function spawnPlayer<C extends object>(\\n  w: World<C>,\\n  at: readonly [number, number, number],\\n  speed: number,\\n  yaw = 0,\\n): number {\\n  const character = playerCharacter(speed);\\n  return spawnActor(w, { at: [at[0], at[1], at[2]], character, player: true, yaw, anim: { ...PLAYER_BODY } });\\n}\\n\",\"total_lines\":37}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-310db7cc-f0f8-4d57-8556-32e894ae6152-61","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,95,95,120,53,57,99,49,100,51,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,70,111,117,110,100,32,51,32,109,97,116,99,104,105,110,103,32,108,105,110,101,115,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,95,95,120,53,57,99,49,100,51,47,119,111,114,107,47,84,114,97,117,109,97,45,67,111,109,112,97,115,115,45,84,114,97,117,109,97,45,67,101,110,116,101,114,45,83,101,99,111,110,100,45,79,112,105,110,105,111,110,45,103,114,111,107,45,52,46,55,45,120,104,105,103,104,45,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,47,108,97,98,115,47,103,97,109,101,47,111,112,47,119,111,114,108,100,46,116,115,10,50,54,54,58,101,120,112,111,114,116,32,102,117,110,99,116,105,111,110,32,105,100,108,101,72,97,110,100,40,115,101,97,116,58,32,83,101,97,116,41,58,32,72,97,110,100,32,123,10,50,56,50,58,101,120,112,111,114,116,32,102,117,110,99,116,105,111,110,32,99,114,101,97,116,101,70,114,111,109,67,97,115,101,40,10,51,56,50,58,101,120,112,111,114,116,32,102,117,110,99,116,105,111,110,32,99,114,101,97,116,101,66,97,114,101,40,111,112,116,115,58,32,123,32,99,111,100,101,63,58,32,98,111,111,108,101,97,110,59,32,118,105,116,97,108,115,63,58,32,110,117,109,98,101,114,59,32,100,105,102,102,105,99,117,108,116,121,63,58,32,68,105,102,102,105,99,117,108,116,121,59,32,115,101,97,116,115,63,58,32,110,117,109,98,101,114,32,125,32,61,32,123,125,41,58,32,79,112,32,123,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":3,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/op/world.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":266,\"content\":\"export function idleHand(seat: Seat): Hand {\"},{\"line_number\":282,\"content\":\"export function createFromCase(\"},{\"line_number\":382,\"content\":\"export function createBare(opts: { code?: boolean; vitals?: number; difficulty?: Difficulty; seats?: number } = {}): Op {\"}]}]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-310db7cc-f0f8-4d57-8556-32e894ae6152-62","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file Boxes (PLAN.md WP 2.4): `boxGeometry({ width, height, depth })`, three.js's `BoxGeometry` with two changes\\n * the level meshes and props need: it stands on the ground (its bottom at y 0, centred in X and Z), and its UVs are\\n * in metres, so a texture keeps its density (16 texels per metre for the v1 textures) on every face whatever the\\n * box's size, instead of being stretched over each face. Each face keeps `BoxGeometry`'s orientation (u to the right\\n * and v up, seen from outside; the top's v towards -Z) and its order (+X, -X, +Y, -Y, +Z, -Z), with its UVs starting\\n * at 0 in its lower-left corner.\\n *\\n * `sidesThenTops` (engine/gfx/geometry/groups.ts) turns the six faces into two draw groups.\\n10→ *\\n * Carried from `boxGeo`, `my-3d2dge:src/stress-world/10-hall.js:253-257` (faces keeping 16 texels per metre).\\n *\\n * Invariants: 24 vertices, 12 triangles, flat normals; bounds [-width/2, 0, -depth/2] to [width/2, height, depth/2].\\n *\\n * @example\\n * const box = boxGeometry({ width: 2, height: 3, depth: 1 });\\n * box.getAttribute('position').count; // 24\\n * box.boundingBox; // null until computed; computeBoundingBox() gives min (-1, 0, -0.5), max (1, 3, 0.5)\\n * @see engine/gfx/geometry/kit.test.ts\\n20→ */\\nimport type { BufferGeometry } from 'three/webgpu';\\nimport { assertSizes } from './checks';\\nimport { createWriter, type Vec3 } from './writer';\\n\\n/** A box's size in metres. */\\nexport interface BoxOptions {\\n  width?: number;\\n  height?: number;\\n  depth?: number;\\n30→}\\n\\n/** The six faces as [outward normal, u axis, v axis], in `BoxGeometry`'s order. */\\nconst FACES: readonly [Vec3, Vec3, Vec3][] = [\\n  [\\n    [1, 0, 0],\\n    [0, 0, -1],\\n    [0, 1, 0],\\n  ],\\n  [\\n40→    [-1, 0, 0],\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file Boxes (PLAN.md WP 2.4): `boxGeometry({ width, height, depth })`, three.js's `BoxGeometry` with two changes\\n * the level meshes and props need: it stands on the ground (its bottom at y 0, centred in X and Z), and its UVs are\\n * in metres, so a texture keeps its density (16 texels per metre for the v1 textures) on every face whatever the\\n * box's size, instead of being stretched over each face. Each face keeps `BoxGeometry`'s orientation (u to the right\\n * and v up, seen from outside; the top's v towards -Z) and its order (+X, -X, +Y, -Y, +Z, -Z), with its UVs starting\\n * at 0 in its lower-left corner.\\n *\\n * `sidesThenTops` (engine/gfx/geometry/groups.ts) turns the six faces into two draw groups.\\n10→ *\\n * Carried from `boxGeo`, `my-3d2dge:src/stress-world/10-hall.js:253-257` (faces keeping 16 texels per metre).\\n *\\n * Invariants: 24 vertices, 12 triangles, flat normals; bounds [-width/2, 0, -depth/2] to [width/2, height, depth/2].\\n *\\n * @example\\n * const box = boxGeometry({ width: 2, height: 3, depth: 1 });\\n * box.getAttribute('position').count; // 24\\n * box.boundingBox; // null until computed; computeBoundingBox() gives min (-1, 0, -0.5), max (1, 3, 0.5)\\n * @see engine/gfx/geometry/kit.test.ts\\n20→ */\\nimport type { BufferGeometry } from 'three/webgpu';\\nimport { assertSizes } from './checks';\\nimport { createWriter, type Vec3 } from './writer';\\n\\n/** A box's size in metres. */\\nexport interface BoxOptions {\\n  width?: number;\\n  height?: number;\\n  depth?: number;\\n30→}\\n\\n/** The six faces as [outward normal, u axis, v axis], in `BoxGeometry`'s order. */\\nconst FACES: readonly [Vec3, Vec3, Vec3][] = [\\n  [\\n    [1, 0, 0],\\n    [0, 0, -1],\\n    [0, 1, 0],\\n  ],\\n  [\\n40→    [-1, 0, 0],\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/engine/gfx/geometry/box.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file Boxes (PLAN.md WP 2.4): `boxGeometry({ width, height, depth })`, three.js's `BoxGeometry` with two changes\\n * the level meshes and props need: it stands on the ground (its bottom at y 0, centred in X and Z), and its UVs are\\n * in metres, so a texture keeps its density (16 texels per metre for the v1 textures) on every face whatever the\\n * box's size, instead of being stretched over each face. Each face keeps `BoxGeometry`'s orientation (u to the right\\n * and v up, seen from outside; the top's v towards -Z) and its order (+X, -X, +Y, -Y, +Z, -Z), with its UVs starting\\n * at 0 in its lower-left corner.\\n *\\n * `sidesThenTops` (engine/gfx/geometry/groups.ts) turns the six faces into two draw groups.\\n *\\n * Carried from `boxGeo`, `my-3d2dge:src/stress-world/10-hall.js:253-257` (faces keeping 16 texels per metre).\\n *\\n * Invariants: 24 vertices, 12 triangles, flat normals; bounds [-width/2, 0, -depth/2] to [width/2, height, depth/2].\\n *\\n * @example\\n * const box = boxGeometry({ width: 2, height: 3, depth: 1 });\\n * box.getAttribute('position').count; // 24\\n * box.boundingBox; // null until computed; computeBoundingBox() gives min (-1, 0, -0.5), max (1, 3, 0.5)\\n * @see engine/gfx/geometry/kit.test.ts\\n */\\nimport type { BufferGeometry } from 'three/webgpu';\\nimport { assertSizes } from './checks';\\nimport { createWriter, type Vec3 } from './writer';\\n\\n/** A box's size in metres. */\\nexport interface BoxOptions {\\n  width?: number;\\n  height?: number;\\n  depth?: number;\\n}\\n\\n/** The six faces as [outward normal, u axis, v axis], in `BoxGeometry`'s order. */\\nconst FACES: readonly [Vec3, Vec3, Vec3][] = [\\n  [\\n    [1, 0, 0],\\n    [0, 0, -1],\\n    [0, 1, 0],\\n  ],\\n  [\\n    [-1, 0, 0],\\n\",\"total_lines\":82}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-310db7cc-f0f8-4d57-8556-32e894ae6152-63","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,95,95,120,53,57,99,49,100,51,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,70,111,117,110,100,32,49,48,32,109,97,116,99,104,105,110,103,32,108,105,110,101,115,10,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,95,95,120,53,57,99,49,100,51,47,119,111,114,107,47,84,114,97,117,109,97,45,67,111,109,112,97,115,115,45,84,114,97,117,109,97,45,67,101,110,116,101,114,45,83,101,99,111,110,100,45,79,112,105,110,105,111,110,45,103,114,111,107,45,52,46,55,45,120,104,105,103,104,45,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,47,101,110,103,105,110,101,47,103,102,120,47