{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-b4a45ad0-de2a-4250-aa29-72401271f16a-83","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The error overlay (PLAN.md §8.3's \\\"ready or error\\\", WP 2.7): what a page shows while it starts and when it\\n * fails, as page text over the canvas, since whoever opens a deployed page sees the page, not its console\\n * (shardfall:web/index.html:34-40, 62-65). `createOverlay(document)` uses the page's `#msg` element (labs/box/\\n * index.html has one, showing \\\"Loading…\\\" before any script runs) or adds one; `status(text)` shows a line,\\n * `error(record)` shows `CODE: message` and `Fix: …` and keeps the first error on screen (a later one is counted,\\n * never hides it), `hide()` clears it once the page is up.\\n *\\n * Invariants: DOM only, no engine state: the records it shows are the log's (`__engine.errors`), so the overlay and\\n10→ * the inspector never disagree; it never takes pointer events, so the canvas below still gets them.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * // const overlay = createOverlay(document);\\n * // overlay.error({ code: 'GFX_NO_WEBGPU', message: 'this browser has no WebGPU', fix: 'use Chrome 113 or later' });\\n * // overlay.text(); // 'GFX_NO_WEBGPU: this browser has no WebGPU\\\\nFix: use Chrome 113 or later'\\n * @see engine/dev/overlay.test.ts\\n */\\n\\n/** What the overlay shows for an error: a code, what happened, and the fix. */\\n20→export interface OverlayRecord {\\n  code: string;\\n  message: string;\\n  fix: string;\\n}\\n\\n/** The page's message line over the canvas. */\\nexport interface Overlay {\\n  /** Shows a status line (`Loading…`) unless an error is showing. */\\n  status(text: string): void;\\n30→  /** Shows an error; the first stays on screen, later ones are counted under it. */\\n  error(record: OverlayRecord): void;\\n  /** Hides the line unless an error is showing. */\\n  hide(): void;\\n  /** What the overlay shows now ('' when hidden). */\\n  text(): string;\\n}\\n\\n/** The styles of an overlay the page did not provide: centred page text that lets pointer events through. */\\nconst STYLE =\\n40→  'position:fixed;inset:0;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;padding:24px;' +\\n  'font:18px system-ui,sans-serif;color:#c9ccd4;text-align:center;white-space:pre-line;pointer-events:none';\\n\\n/** The overlay over `document`'s `#msg` element, made when the page has none. */\\nexport function createOverlay(document: Document): Overlay {\\n  let element = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('#msg');\\n  if (!element) {\\n    element = document.createElement('div');\\n    element.id = 'msg';\\n    element.setAttribute('role', 'status');\\n50→    element.setAttribute('style', STYLE);\\n    document.body.append(element);\\n  }\\n  const box = element;\\n  let first: OverlayRecord | null = null;\\n  let more = 0;\\n  const show = (text: string) => {\\n    box.textContent = text;\\n    box.hidden = text === '';\\n  };\\n60→  const errorText = () =>\\n    first\\n      ? `${first.code}: ${first.message}\\\\nFix: ${first.fix}${more ? `\\\\n(${more} more in __engine.errors)` : ''}`\\n      : '';\\n  return {\\n    status(text) {\\n      if (!first) show(text);\\n    },\\n    error(record) {\\n      if (first) more++;\\n70→      else first = { code: record.code, message: record.message, fix: record.fix };\\n      show(errorText());\\n    },\\n    hide() {\\n      if (!first) show('');\\n    },\\n    text: () => (box.hidden ? '' : (box.textContent ?? '')),\\n  };\\n}\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The error overlay (PLAN.md §8.3's \\\"ready or error\\\", WP 2.7): what a page shows while it starts and when it\\n * fails, as page text over the canvas, since whoever opens a deployed page sees the page, not its console\\n * (shardfall:web/index.html:34-40, 62-65). `createOverlay(document)` uses the page's `#msg` element (labs/box/\\n * index.html has one, showing \\\"Loading…\\\" before any script runs) or adds one; `status(text)` shows a line,\\n * `error(record)` shows `CODE: message` and `Fix: …` and keeps the first error on screen (a later one is counted,\\n * never hides it), `hide()` clears it once the page is up.\\n *\\n * Invariants: DOM only, no engine state: the records it shows are the log's (`__engine.errors`), so the overlay and\\n10→ * the inspector never disagree; it never takes pointer events, so the canvas below still gets them.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * // const overlay = createOverlay(document);\\n * // overlay.error({ code: 'GFX_NO_WEBGPU', message: 'this browser has no WebGPU', fix: 'use Chrome 113 or later' });\\n * // overlay.text(); // 'GFX_NO_WEBGPU: this browser has no WebGPU\\\\nFix: use Chrome 113 or later'\\n * @see engine/dev/overlay.test.ts\\n */\\n\\n/** What the overlay shows for an error: a code, what happened, and the fix. */\\n20→export interface OverlayRecord {\\n  code: string;\\n  message: string;\\n  fix: string;\\n}\\n\\n/** The page's message line over the canvas. */\\nexport interface Overlay {\\n  /** Shows a status line (`Loading…`) unless an error is showing. */\\n  status(text: string): void;\\n30→  /** Shows an error; the first stays on screen, later ones are counted under it. */\\n  error(record: OverlayRecord): void;\\n  /** Hides the line unless an error is showing. */\\n  hide(): void;\\n  /** What the overlay shows now ('' when hidden). */\\n  text(): string;\\n}\\n\\n/** The styles of an overlay the page did not provide: centred page text that lets pointer events through. */\\nconst STYLE =\\n40→  'position:fixed;inset:0;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;padding:24px;' +\\n  'font:18px system-ui,sans-serif;color:#c9ccd4;text-align:center;white-space:pre-line;pointer-events:none';\\n\\n/** The overlay over `document`'s `#msg` element, made when the page has none. */\\nexport function createOverlay(document: Document): Overlay {\\n  let element = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('#msg');\\n  if (!element) {\\n    element = document.createElement('div');\\n    element.id = 'msg';\\n    element.setAttribute('role', 'status');\\n50→    element.setAttribute('style', STYLE);\\n    document.body.append(element);\\n  }\\n  const box = element;\\n  let first: OverlayRecord | null = null;\\n  let more = 0;\\n  const show = (text: string) => {\\n    box.textContent = text;\\n    box.hidden = text === '';\\n  };\\n60→  const errorText = () =>\\n    first\\n      ? `${first.code}: ${first.message}\\\\nFix: ${first.fix}${more ? `\\\\n(${more} more in __engine.errors)` : ''}`\\n      : '';\\n  return {\\n    status(text) {\\n      if (!first) show(text);\\n    },\\n    error(record) {\\n      if (first) more++;\\n70→      else first = { code: record.code, message: record.message, fix: record.fix };\\n      show(errorText());\\n    },\\n    hide() {\\n      if (!first) show('');\\n    },\\n    text: () => (box.hidden ? '' : (box.textContent ?? '')),\\n  };\\n}\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/engine/dev/overlay.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file The error overlay (PLAN.md §8.3's \\\"ready or error\\\", WP 2.7): what a page shows while it starts and when it\\n * fails, as page text over the canvas, since whoever opens a deployed page sees the page, not its console\\n * (shardfall:web/index.html:34-40, 62-65). `createOverlay(document)` uses the page's `#msg` element (labs/box/\\n * index.html has one, showing \\\"Loading…\\\" before any script runs) or adds one; `status(text)` shows a line,\\n * `error(record)` shows `CODE: message` and `Fix: …` and keeps the first error on screen (a later one is counted,\\n * never hides it), `hide()` clears it once the page is up.\\n *\\n * Invariants: DOM only, no engine state: the records it shows are the log's (`__engine.errors`), so the overlay and\\n * the inspector never disagree; it never takes pointer events, so the canvas below still gets them.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * // const overlay = createOverlay(document);\\n * // overlay.error({ code: 'GFX_NO_WEBGPU', message: 'this browser has no WebGPU', fix: 'use Chrome 113 or later' });\\n * // overlay.text(); // 'GFX_NO_WEBGPU: this browser has no WebGPU\\\\nFix: use Chrome 113 or later'\\n * @see engine/dev/overlay.test.ts\\n */\\n\\n/** What the overlay shows for an error: a code, what happened, and the fix. */\\nexport interface OverlayRecord {\\n  code: string;\\n  message: string;\\n  fix: string;\\n}\\n\\n/** The page's message line over the canvas. */\\nexport interface Overlay {\\n  /** Shows a status line (`Loading…`) unless an error is showing. */\\n  status(text: string): void;\\n  /** Shows an error; the first stays on screen, later ones are counted under it. */\\n  error(record: OverlayRecord): void;\\n  /** Hides the line unless an error is showing. */\\n  hide(): void;\\n  /** What the overlay shows now ('' when hidden). */\\n  text(): string;\\n}\\n\\n/** The styles of an overlay the page did not provide: centred page text that lets pointer events through. */\\nconst STYLE =\\n  'position:fixed;inset:0;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;padding:24px;' +\\n  'font:18px system-ui,sans-serif;color:#c9ccd4;text-align:center;white-space:pre-line;pointer-events:none';\\n\\n/** The overlay over `document`'s `#msg` element, made when the page has none. */\\nexport function createOverlay(document: Document): Overlay {\\n  let element = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('#msg');\\n  if (!element) {\\n    element = document.createElement('div');\\n    element.id = 'msg';\\n    element.setAttribute('role', 'status');\\n    element.setAttribute('style', STYLE);\\n    document.body.append(element);\\n  }\\n  const box = element;\\n  let first: OverlayRecord | null = null;\\n  let more = 0;\\n  const show = (text: string) => {\\n    box.textContent = text;\\n    box.hidden = text === '';\\n  };\\n  const errorText = () =>\\n    first\\n      ? `${first.code}: ${first.message}\\\\nFix: ${first.fix}${more ? `\\\\n(${more} more in __engine.errors)` : ''}`\\n      : '';\\n  return {\\n    status(text) {\\n      if (!first) show(text);\\n    },\\n    error(record) {\\n      if (first) more++;\\n      else first = { code: record.code, message: record.message, fix: record.fix };\\n      show(errorText());\\n    },\\n    hide() {\\n      if (!first) show('');\\n    },\\n    text: () => (box.hidden ? '' : (box.textContent ?? '')),\\n  };\\n}\\n\",\"total_lines\":78}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"08228e88-dac2-4e91-a681-226e69eafbe2"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_239","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The engine uses #msg and keeps the first error.\n\nThe classic script should set #msg to a plain message and use a MutationObserver or setInterval to replace any GFX_NO_WEBGPU text with the plain version. This handles both missing navigator.gpu and missing GPU adapters.\n\nThe script detects touch devices and appends a controller or keyboard hint. It sets the message to 48px with pointer events disabled. If navigator.gpu is missing, it calls plainGpu() and reports the failure to AIHub. A setInterval loop will check window.__engine.errors to keep the message clean.\n\nThe interval loop scans window.__engine.errors for GFX_NO_WEBGPU and rewrites the message if found. If navigator.gpu is missing, Firefox may crash before the engine initializes, so the initial plainGpu call remains unless gameFailed overwrites it. The starter's gameFailed only writes when errors.length equals zero.\n\nA window.__needGpu flag will prevent gameFailed from overwriting the GPU message once it is shown. The session.js content must be embedded directly inside index.html so Vite can inline it correctly.\n\nI will curl session.js and build index.html with tools/one-file.mts. Code edits will run in parallel. campaign.ts will use case:gala-shift for the free surgery board and list all story cases. finishOp will advance to debrief and save the best score.\n\nfinishOp writes the score only when it is better than the previous best or no prior score exists. Replays of unfinished cases always save because the previous score is undefined. Replays of finished cases only save if the new score improves on the old one.\n\nThe `afterWin` handler returns to the board when `caseIndex` is negative. `openBoard` routes the free `case:gala-shift` board into a brief screen with `record` disabled and focus set to zero. The board list is built by mapping `storyCases().id`.\n\nCampaign now tracks a `boot` counter that increments on start, retry, and phase. The `keepPhase` flag is set to true during phase transitions and false otherwise. The start button advances from brief to operation when the case index is valid and matches the cleared index; otherwise it sets record to false.\n\nThe boot counter will also increment inside retryCase. The campaign interface and createCampaign factory need to declare the new fields, but existing tests should remain unaffected. Duplicate imports will be removed. The settings page back button will be fixed. Files will follow Prettier single-quote rules with semicolons and a 120-character print width, plus JSDoc @file headers on every labs file and no unused variables.\n\nThe hands module will define a `HandState` interface and a `readHands` function that returns hand positions, tool states, and button states. Mouse NDC coordinates will be cached in window listeners. Gamepad input will come from `navigator.getGamepads`, with each seat mapped to its connected pad; stick axes will use a 0.18 deadzone and curve.\n\nSeat zero will use keyboard input when no gamepad is connected. Cursor speed scales with the precision toggle and is multiplied by stick input and delta time. Hand positions clamp to ±8 horizontally and ±5 vertically. Edge pulses track gift presses per seat. The radial tool selector triggers on right-stick magnitude above 0.55, maps angle to eight tools, and commits on release; mouse delta or wheel may serve as an alternative.\n\nThe radial tool selector will track mouse movement angle from the press point while RMB is held, then equip the tool on release. Tool cycling will use L1/R1, wheel, and keys 1–8. The paddle button activates when both L2 and R2 are held together. Site pulses advance to the next site for one frame. Swap, hint, and drop actions will be triggered by holding the use action, pressing pad R2 or south, or using mouse left; hands input is suppressed during menus. The create button maps to index 9.\n\nStandard gamepad button mapping is being verified. Create maps to button 8 and Options to button 9. If Create is held, Options will be ignored for pause. Menu polling will run separately from hands so it still works during modal screens.\n\nThe `pollMenus` function will return boolean flags for accept, cancel, direction, pause, and any input. Keyboard input via `input.pressed` remains available during modal screens because the engine blocks simulation intents rather than raw device reads. System actions such as pause, uiAccept, and uiCancel are always registered.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-fb0fbb87-dd70-4bfa-aafa-d7f23db005b1-84","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"pressed\\(|lastDevice|held\\(","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/engine/input/input.ts","head_limit":25}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1066,"output_tokens":2439,"cache_read_input_tokens":126592,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"6abf323b-0a5f-44cf-b84c-0b7e9cb147da"}
