8,115,111,32,115,97,118,101,32,116,104,101,115,101,32,97,110,115,119,101,114,115,32,97,115,32,101,120,105,116,45,105,110,116,101,114,118,105,101,119,46,106,115,111,110,32,105,110,32,116,104,105,115,32,102,111,108,100,101,114,39,115,32,114,111,111,116,44,32,98,101,115,105,100,101,32,116,104,101,32,103,97,109,101,39,115,32,46,104,116,109,108,58,32,111,110,101,32,74,83,79,78,32,111,98,106,101,99,116,32,105,110,32,101,120,97,99,116,108,121,32,116,104,101,32,115,104,97,112,101,32,98,101,108,111,119,44,32,101,118,101,114,121,32,118,97,108,117,101,32,102,105,108,108,101,100,32,105,110,32,40,34,117,110,107,110,111,119,110,34,32,102,111,114,32,97,110,121,116,104,105,110,103,32,121,111,117,32,99,97,110,39,116,32,99,111,110,102,105,114,109,41,46,32,84,111,111,108,115,32,114,101,97,100,32,105,116,32,40,116,104,101,32,103,97,109,101,39,115,32,114,117,110,32,114,101,99,111,114,100,32,97,110,100,32,116,104,101,32,65,73,32,72,117,98,32,99,97,116,97,108,111,103,117,101,41,44,32,115,111,32,119,114,105,116,101,32,112,108,97,105,110,32,116,101,120,116,44,32,97,110,100,32,99,104,101,99,107,32,116,104,97,116,32,116,104,101,32,102,105,108,101,32,112,97,114,115,101,115,32,97,115,32,74,83,79,78,46,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":0,\"match_count\":6,\"file_matches\":[{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/TASK.md\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":32,\"content\":\"## This computer\"},{\"line_number\":66,\"content\":\"## Goal\"},{\"line_number\":82,\"content\":\"## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\"},{\"line_number\":89,\"content\":\"## Done\"},{\"line_number\":92,\"content\":\"## Source Information\"},{\"line_number\":109,\"content\":\"Exit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"607541b7-86d1-4fd4-8ba0-fb8a5d0b17ad"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_287","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"ZERO is exported, but Sample is not.\n\nTickMsg uses `hands` but live.ts uses `ticks`; casting handles this. A hash bug was spotted: the client compares the hash immediately upon receiving a batch, yet the hash reflects the state after the host applied the final tick of that same batch.\n\nThe hash is computed after each tick, so it already reflects the state of the last tick in the batch. The queued ticks are the ones just stepped, which were pushed before flushing.\n\nThe client compares the received hash to the engine hash before applying the batch. The host sends the hash after the last tick of that batch, so the two values will never match.\n\nThe fix pairs each hash with a `hashTick` index. The client stores the hash and compares it only after applying the tick whose index matches `hashTick`. The host sends `from` as the first tick index in the batch.\n\nThe simplest fix is to attach the hash only to the last tick of each batch. The client will compare it after applying that final tick, not on receipt. The view will track a pending hash and an applied tick count.\n\nThe client will attach the hash only to the final tick in each batch. It compares the engine hash after applying that tick. But if more than eight ticks are queued, view.book.hash reflects the latest stepped tick, which may be newer than the last tick actually sent.\n\nThe global hash is too stale. If the queue grows between flushes, the hash belongs to a tick newer than the batch. The fix stores the hash on each queued item instead. The host sends the hash only for the last item in the batch, and the client compares it after applying that exact tick.\n\nThe client will compare the hash only after applying the entire batch, not on receipt. The host must seed the random generator before starting its interval loop. A new divergence risk was spotted: `createEngine` steps during warmup before pausing, so different machines may diverge.\n\nThe engine must pause after the initial step, then seed with `info.seed`, then call ready and startRound. The onFrame resume logic is being fixed to always pause when a session exists, removing a race before startRound. timeScale will apply only to autoplay, not attract. Slider settings will be cached inside onFrame. speak will use currentBox. Sample will be exported. pointerover will target only menu items, requiring an import for items(). boot.ts is nearly 500 lines, so edits will stay small. TickMsg uses `hands` while live.ts uses `ticks`; the choice is whether to update TickMsg or leave it as-is.\n\nlive.ts has a compile error: it casts `data` to `Partial<TickMsg & IntentMsg>` but never imports TickMsg. That import needs to be added.\n\nI also need to verify the return types of createAudio, engine inspector methods, post.combine array support, SURGEON_MAP, and campaign Progress fields. I will typecheck after applying all fixes rather than reading every file.\n\nI will read the PLAN verification and TASK done sections in parallel with the compile fixes. The one-file build inlines the module script, but the dev index.html must still load session.js as a first inline classic script.\n\nThe source index.html will include the 16KB session.js inline so both dev and one-file builds load it first. A node script will assemble the file to avoid errors. The 10-foot UI CSS will be placed in a style tag. A classic script will detect missing WebGPU and display a browser requirement message.\n\nThe classic script must display the exact WebGPU requirement message plus the touch fallback line. The engine text overlay should be hidden so the classic error is not overwritten. Firefox crashes before module code runs, so the fallback message must be visible immediately.\n\nPushing an error before the module loads prevents gameFailed from overwriting the classic script message, since gameFailed only updates #msg when errors.length is zero. The engine overlay may still replace #msg when it loads.\n\nThe `gameFailed` fallback will check `!navigator.gpu` and call `showGpu()` to preserve the classic error message if the module import crashes before boot runs. In `main.ts`, the AI Hub connection and engine import are now parallelized so the hub can connect while the engine loads.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-22b51ca3-70bd-4f28-a5b5-c31afd1b85b2-16","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/ui/shell.ts","limit":50}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-22b51ca3-70bd-4f28-a5b5-c31afd1b85b2-17","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/TASK.md","offset":60,"limit":180}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-22b51ca3-70bd-4f28-a5b5-c31afd1b85b2-18","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"createAudio|interface Audio|duck\\(|volume\\(","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/engine/audio","glob":"*.ts","head_limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-22b51ca3-70bd-4f28-a5b5-c31afd1b85b2-19","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/PLAN.md","offset":680,"limit":180}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2599,"output_tokens":2586,"cache_read_input_tokens":54912,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"db2c3c66-b3db-4ff1-9c70-3bfc2886271c"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-22b51ca3-70bd-4f28-a5b5-c31afd1b85b2-16","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The HTML screens. One idea on each, big type, and a click or a pad both move the same highlight.\\n */\\nimport { caseById, storyCases } from '../op/cases';\\nimport {\\n  boardIds,\\n  currentBox,\\n  handbookPage,\\n  items,\\n10→  type Campaign,\\n} from '../app/campaign';\\nimport { aboutCards } from '../data/interview';\\nimport { TUNE } from '../scenes/tune';\\n\\n/** Extra lines the shell cannot know on its own. */\\nexport interface ShellExtra {\\n  prompt: string;\\n  hash: string;\\n  cursor: string;\\n20→  sliders: { setting: string; label: string; value: number }[];\\n  touch: boolean;\\n  card: { names: string[]; ready: number[]; left: string; asked: boolean } | null;\\n}\\n\\n/** Escape text that came from the game's own writing. Names still go through text nodes where a hub player is shown. */\\nfunction esc(value: string): string {\\n  return value.replace(/[&<>\\\"]/g, (char) => ({ '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;', '\\\"': '&quot;' })[char] ?? char);\\n}\\n\\n30→/** A menu row. The highlighted one carries the focus marker scripts look for. */\\nfunction rows(c: Campaign): string {\\n  return items(c)\\n    .map((item, index) => {\\n      const on = index === c.focus ? ' is-on' : '';\\n      return `<button type=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"row${on}\\\" data-act=\\\"${esc(item.id)}\\\" data-game=\\\"row\\\">${esc(item.label)}</button>`;\\n    })\\n    .join('');\\n}\\n\\n40→/** The case card on the board. */\\nfunction boardCard(c: Campaign): string {\\n  const ids = boardIds(c.progress);\\n  const id = ids[c.board] ?? ids[0];\\n  if (!id) return '';\\n  if (id === 'case:gala-shift') {\\n    return `<article class=\\\"card\\\" data-game=\\\"card\\\"><h2>The Gala Shift</h2><p>Two patients, one couch. Scores are not kept.</p></article>`;\\n  }\\n  const def = caseById(id);\\n  const index = storyCases().findIndex((item) => item.id === id);\\n50→  const rank = c.progress.ranks[index] ?? '—';\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The HTML screens. One idea on each, big type, and a click or a pad both move the same highlight.\\n */\\nimport { caseById, storyCases } from '../op/cases';\\nimport {\\n  boardIds,\\n  currentBox,\\n  handbookPage,\\n  items,\\n10→  type Campaign,\\n} from '../app/campaign';\\nimport { aboutCards } from '../data/interview';\\nimport { TUNE } from '../scenes/tune';\\n\\n/** Extra lines the shell cannot know on its own. */\\nexport interface ShellExtra {\\n  prompt: string;\\n  hash: string;\\n  cursor: string;\\n20→  sliders: { setting: string; label: string; value: number }[];\\n  touch: boolean;\\n  card: { names: string[]; ready: number[]; left: string; asked: boolean } | null;\\n}\\n\\n/** Escape text that came from the game's own writing. Names still go through text nodes where a hub player is shown. */\\nfunction esc(value: string): string {\\n  return value.replace(/[&<>\\\"]/g, (char) => ({ '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;', '\\\"': '&quot;' })[char] ?? char);\\n}\\n\\n30→/** A menu row. The highlighted one carries the focus marker scripts look for. */\\nfunction rows(c: Campaign): string {\\n  return items(c)\\n    .map((item, index) => {\\n      const on = index === c.focus ? ' is-on' : '';\\n      return `<button type=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"row${on}\\\" data-act=\\\"${esc(item.id)}\\\" data-game=\\\"row\\\">${esc(item.label)}</button>`;\\n    })\\n    .join('');\\n}\\n\\n40→/** The case card on the board. */\\nfunction boardCard(c: Campaign): string {\\n  const ids = boardIds(c.progress);\\n  const id = ids[c.board] ?? ids[0];\\n  if (!id) return '';\\n  if (id === 'case:gala-shift') {\\n    return `<article class=\\\"card\\\" data-game=\\\"card\\\"><h2>The Gala Shift</h2><p>Two patients, one couch. Scores are not kept.</p></article>`;\\n  }\\n  const def = caseById(id);\\n  const index = storyCases().findIndex((item) => item.id === id);\\n50→  const rank = c.progress.ranks[index] ?? '—';\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/ui/shell.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":50,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file The HTML screens. One idea on each, big type, and a click or a pad both move the same highlight.\\n */\\nimport { caseById, storyCases } from '../op/cases';\\nimport {\\n  boardIds,\\n  currentBox,\\n  handbookPage,\\n  items,\\n  type Campaign,\\n} from '../app/campaign';\\nimport { aboutCards } from '../data/interview';\\nimport { TUNE } from '../scenes/tune';\\n\\n/** Extra lines the shell cannot know on its own. */\\nexport interface ShellExtra {\\n  prompt: string;\\n  hash: string;\\n  cursor: string;\\n  sliders: { setting: string; label: string; value: number }[];\\n  touch: boolean;\\n  card: { names: string[]; ready: number[]; left: string; asked: boolean } | null;\\n}\\n\\n/** Escape text that came from the game's own writing. Names still go through text nodes where a hub player is shown. */\\nfunction esc(value: string): string {\\n  return value.replace(/[&<>\\\"]/g, (char) => ({ '&': '&amp;', '<': '&lt;', '>': '&gt;', '\\\"': '&quot;' })[char] ?? char);\\n}\\n\\n/** A menu row. The highlighted one carries the focus marker scripts look for. */\\nfunction rows(c: Campaign): string {\\n  return items(c)\\n    .map((item, index) => {\\n      const on = index === c.focus ? ' is-on' : '';\\n      return `<button type=\\\"button\\\" class=\\\"row${on}\\\" data-act=\\\"${esc(item.id)}\\\" data-game=\\\"row\\\">${esc(item.label)}</button>`;\\n    })\\n    .join('');\\n}\\n\\n/** The case card on the board. */\\nfunction boardCard(c: Campaign): string {\\n  const ids = boardIds(c.progress);\\n  const id = ids[c.board] ?? ids[0];\\n  if (!id) return '';\\n  if (id === 'case:gala-shift') {\\n    return `<article class=\\\"card\\\" data-game=\\\"card\\\"><h2>The Gala Shift</h2><p>Two patients, one couch. Scores are not kept.</p></article>`;\\n  }\\n  const def = caseById(id);\\n  const index = storyCases().findIndex((item) => item.id === id);\\n  const rank = c.progress.ranks[index] ?? '—';\\n\",\"total_lines\":163}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-22b51ca3-70bd-4f28-a5b5-c31afd1b85b2-17","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"60→\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\n70→Scope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\n80→Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\n90→No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:47:31\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n100→- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n110→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n120→    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\n130→Testing Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n140→- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n150→- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"60→\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\n70→Scope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\n80→Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\n90→No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:47:31\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n100→- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record