edback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\"},{\"line_number\":109,\"content\":\"Exit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"1717b207-6957-4c9b-bb95-62a8b41c58df"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_384","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Fixing the packedHands reference.\n\nRemoving the redundant packedHands call. Using the existing local gloveSpots(1)[0] instead.\n\nThe session tool selection uses the simulation's tool rather than the local cursor. This may be intentional if the host is authoritative.\n\nI will fix the compile error first.\n\nI also need to check PLAN sections 19 and 21 for checkboxes. I will review TASK.md hand-in requirements. I will compare interview.ts with exit-interview.json to determine what TESTING-LOG should say.\n\nI also found a bug in the hub cpExists function. It always returns true because spawn is never null. Missing files would cause cpSync to throw.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-2b6603e1-793c-467f-8e3b-d78c49a80d9f-15","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/PLAN.md","offset":739,"limit":180}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-2b6603e1-793c-467f-8e3b-d78c49a80d9f-16","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/TASK.md","offset":1,"limit":200}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-2b6603e1-793c-467f-8e3b-d78c49a80d9f-17","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"packedHands","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/app/boot.ts"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1995,"output_tokens":669,"cache_read_input_tokens":47104,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"17d065ea-3ed1-470a-bfe0-977f7ed8a720"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-2b6603e1-793c-467f-8e3b-d78c49a80d9f-15","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"739→## 19. Testing\\n740→\\n**One command: `npm run verify`** (`labs/game/tools/verify.mjs`; add it with `npm pkg set scripts.verify=\\\"node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs\\\"`). It prints only failures and one final line (`verify: 212 passed, 0 failed`). Rerun it only after changing the code it covers; `--fast` skips browsers. Use one or two browsers at a time. No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Make it the first thing you write, from this skeleton.\\n\\nTested on the starter, under the run's real long `TMPDIR`, with no setup: `verify: 8 passed, 0 failed` in 28 s (14 s with `--fast`). With a broken test, a lint error and a failing browser check, it printed exactly those three `FAIL` lines and `verify: 9 passed, 3 failed`, and exited 1. It passes `npm run check` itself. For a lint failure it prints the summary; run `npm run check` for the details.\\n\\n```js\\n/**\\n * @file The one command, `npm run verify`: runs every check quietly and prints only the failures, then one line with\\n * the count. `--fast` skips what needs a browser. It sets its own short TMPDIR and CHROMIUM_PATH, so it needs no setup,\\n * and removes the temporary folder when done. Exit 0 when nothing failed.\\n750→ */\\nimport { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\\nimport { existsSync, globSync, mkdtempSync, readFileSync, rmSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { homedir } from 'node:os';\\n\\nconst fast = process.argv.includes('--fast');\\nconst tmp = mkdtempSync('/tmp/g.');\\nconst chrome = globSync(`${homedir()}/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium-*/chrome-linux64/chrome`)[0];\\nconst env = { ...process.env, TMPDIR: tmp, CHROMIUM_PATH: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH ?? chrome };\\nconst run = (command, ...args) => spawnSync(command, args, { env, encoding: 'utf8', maxBuffer: 2 ** 28 });\\n760→\\nlet passed = 0;\\nconst failed = [];\\nconst note = (name, ok, detail = '') => (ok ? (passed += 1) : failed.push(`${name}${detail ? `: ${detail}` : ''}`));\\nconst tail = (result) => `${result.stdout}${result.stderr}`.trim().split('\\\\n').slice(-6).join(' | ').slice(0, 600);\\n\\n/** A step whose exit status is its verdict. */\\nconst step = (name, command, ...args) => {\\n  const result = run(command, ...args);\\n  note(name, result.status === 0, tail(result));\\n770→};\\n\\n/** Vitest, counted test by test from the JSON report npm test writes. */\\nfunction tests() {\\n  const report = 'out/test/report.json';\\n  rmSync(report, { force: true });\\n  const result = run('npm', 'test', '--silent');\\n  if (!existsSync(report)) return note('npm test', false, tail(result));\\n  for (const file of JSON.parse(readFileSync(report, 'utf8')).testResults) {\\n    for (const test of file.assertionResults) {\\n780→      note(\\n        test.fullName,\\n        test.status === 'passed',\\n        String(test.failureMessages?.[0] ?? '')\\n          .split('\\\\n')[0]\\n          .slice(0, 300),\\n      );\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n790→\\n/** A browser tool: it prints one JSON line per check, { name, ok, detail }. Add e2e and hub runs here. */\\nfunction browserTool(path) {\\n  const result = run('node', path);\\n  const lines = result.stdout.split('\\\\n').filter((line) => line.startsWith('{'));\\n  for (const line of lines) {\\n    const { name, ok, detail } = JSON.parse(line);\\n    note(name, ok, detail);\\n  }\\n  if (result.status !== 0 || lines.length === 0) note(path, false, tail(result));\\n800→}\\n\\nstep('npm run check', 'npm', 'run', '--silent', 'check');\\ntests();\\nif (!fast) {\\n  step('replays in Chromium', 'node', 'x', 'replay', 'labs/game/replays', '--browser', 'sim');\\n  for (const path of ['labs/game/tools/e2e.mjs', 'labs/game/tools/hub.mjs']) if (existsSync(path)) browserTool(path);\\n}\\nrmSync(tmp, { recursive: true, force: true });\\nfor (const failure of failed) console.log(`FAIL ${failure}`);\\n810→console.log(`verify: ${passed} passed, ${failed.length} failed`);\\nprocess.exit(failed.length ? 1 : 0);\\n```\\n\\n**Keep an issue log from the first day** in `labs/game/TESTING-LOG.md`: one line per issue a test or check caught, naming the test, and whether it was critical (game-breaking). TASK.md's Testing Feedback asks how many issues testing caught, how many were critical, and which tests helped most and least. You cannot answer honestly from memory at the end. Note engine friction in `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` as it happens, and prompt friction in the log, for the other two feedback sections.\\n\\n**Node (`npm test`, beside each module).**\\n\\n- Golden replay per case; capture and restore continue exactly.\\n- The bot wins every case on Resident on 3 seeds (`BOT_SEEDS=10` for a wider sweep before finishing); also on Intern and Attending for Cases 1 and 8; also with 2 and 4 bot surgeons.\\n820→- Each tool's rules, each rating band, each strain's state machine, each failure (flatline, clock, slips), Stillpoint's and Second Pulse's rules, flap clip timing.\\n- `validateCase` and `validateStory` over all data; no \\\"TODO\\\", \\\"placeholder\\\" or lorem in the text; no name from the §1 never-use list.\\n- **Whole-game playthrough without a browser:** the Director walks the story, all eight cases by bot, to credits and Free Surgery.\\n- **The sequencer:** a host and 3 clients in Node over a simulated network (latency, jitter, 5% loss on unreliable input, leavers): all hashes equal after 3,600 ticks. This protects the riskiest system cheaply.\\n- Settings: sliders within range and reset restores defaults.\\n- Every tool and target kind has an effect or a refusal line (§4). The exit interview parses and matches TASK.md's shape, and the About screen's data matches it.\\n\\n**Browser, against the deliverable (`labs/game/tools/e2e.mjs`, Playwright).** Launch with `executablePath: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH` and `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`, on `file://`.\\n\\n- Boots with no external request and no console errors; `__engine.ready`.\\n830→- **Whole-game playthrough:** a stub pad (`navigator.getGamepads` via `addInitScript`) walks title, Board, story and debriefs, with the bot playing the ops at a raised `timeScale`, to the credits. 640×360 is enough.\\n- Attract starts at 10 s idle; each of key, mouse, pad button and stick exits it; nothing saved.\\n- Prompts: glyphs change with last device; the Xbox glyph setting works.\\n- Every menu by pad only, then by mouse (hover moves focus, click picks).\\n- Dev mode opens from pause; a slider changes its setting live; reset works.\\n- Touch, in Chromium's phone and tablet profiles, tapping with `page.touchscreen`: exact message, settings button works, no touch layer, attract runs.\\n- No WebGPU, through the system Python's Playwright: Firefox, and WebKit with an iPhone profile (the closest here to an iPhone, not iOS Safari; both have WebGL only). Expect the plain message of §10. An iPhone's real behaviour is checked only this far.\\n- Multiplayer stand-in: there is no second player here, so two browser pages stand in for one. The harness does this with frames running real copies of the game, and clicking a frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\\n- Audio: music bus 0.1 by default; an `AnalyserNode` hears music and a beep; effects render with an `OfflineAudioContext`; the file holds no audio data or `<audio>`.\\n- Couch: two stub pads each claim a seat and move their own cursor.\\n840→- Story and HUD elements carry `data-game` attributes so scripts can read them through the DOM.\\n\\n**Hub:** the four harness runs of §16, plus the game on its own.\\n\\n**Seeing it.** Prefer numbers: `node x shot labs/game --ids` for coverage and what is visible. Use screenshots only for what a script cannot check: at 1920×1080 for each screen once, and the field in each case. WebGPU canvases can come out blank in headless screenshots; `node x shot --png` draws through the engine and works.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 20. Build order\\n\\n850→Each step ends with its tests green. Get the core feeling right before spreading out.\\n\\n1. **Setup and packaging first.** Starter builds to one file, runs from `file://`. `verify` exists. `game:main` with runs.\\n2. **The field.** Live sheet with breath and heartbeat, under `look:field`, with drapes and the lamp. Mouse and pad cursor, hand and scalpel. Draw one incision on a guide: this must already look and feel good. Spend time here.\\n3. **Case 1 complete:** Drain, Forceps, Suture, Syringe, Gel, radial, vitals, clock, ratings, HUD, Odie's lines, failure and debrief, and the bot winning it.\\n4. **Front end:** title, Board, story scene with two characters and voices, briefing, pause, Settings, glyphs, saves. Then attract, then dev mode, since they speed all later work.\\n5. **Case 2 and 3:** Scalpel, Scanner, Laser, sites, Code, Stillpoint and the pulse-lock.\\n6. **Hub early:** session flow, card, sequencer test in Node, then the harness on the one case you have, while it is still cheap to change.\\n7. **Strains and flaps:** Cases 4, 5, 6, with Second Pulse, seats, AI colleague, Swap, Consult, couch co-op.\\n8. **Cases 7 and 8, session case, Free Surgery, the ending.**\\n860→9. **All story, all songs, all postcards, all animations.** Polish every transition.\\n10. **Tune:** rank thresholds, clock, difficulty, using the bot's numbers. Check no phase has a dead stretch over 8 s for a bot playing at human speed. Then final `verify`, exit interview, About screen.\\n\\nIf you run out of something, cut polish per case, never a feature on the done list.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 21. Done\\n\\n**About/Credits (in game)**, paged, one card per item, big text. One screen each: Source Information (Date, Source, Plan, Genre and Perspective, Tech, Art Direction, Animation, Audio, Online, Testing, Research, Environment, Model, Usage, with your TASK.md's values for Date, Environment and Model), Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback (every question TASK.md asks, one card each), credits. Use \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you cannot confirm. Plan field: `PLAN.md`. Usage field: exactly `added on import`, in an element marked `data-about=\\\"usage\\\"` so the importer can replace it. Say in Online that network play is through AI Hub only, and couch co-op on one screen.\\n870→\\n**Exit interview.** Save the JSON exactly as TASK.md shapes it, beside the deliverable; a test parses it and checks its shape. Suggested catalogue values: `type` Game; `genre` Simulation; `source` Trauma Center: Second Opinion; `series` Trauma Center; `players` 1–4; `controls` \\\"Stick or mouse aims · R2 or click uses the tool · right stick or 1-8 picks a tool\\\"; 3 to 6 tags from `3d`, `first-person`, `co-op`, `story`, `boss fights`, `comedy`. Write your own `tagline` (a few words) and `description` (one or two sentences for the catalogue), in plain text. `name` is your title as on the title screen. Answer the feedback sections from your issue log (§19) and `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md`. Source Information hints: genre and perspective as in §1; tech: my-3dge at your commit, three.js r182 on WebGPU, Rapier 0.21, TypeScript, Vite, AI Hub session client; research: note this plan's research plus your own, or none.\\n\\n**Chat reply.** Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback, as TASK.md says.\\n\\n**Before you stop.**\\n\\n- `npm run verify` green; `npm run check` and `npm test` green; the bot still wins.\\n- No TODOs, placeholders, stubs, or lorem anywhere; no unused listed feature.\\n- The deliverable is current, in the place and under the name TASK.md gives.\\n880→- `labs/game/README.md` says what the game is and the calls you made; `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` lists what you needed from the engine. \\\"Local engine patches\\\" should be empty; the engine stays read-only.\\n- Every process you started is stopped by its PID; your temporary directory is removed.\\n\\n**Every TASK.md requirement, and where it is covered.**\\n\\n| TASK.md asks for | Here |\\n|---|---|\\n| Genre, perspective | §1 |\\n| Title by RNG from 50 | Yours, as TASK.md says; never named here |\\n| Research | Done for this plan (§1's review fixes, §17's tests); more is your choice |\\n890→| Single HTML file, dependencies | §18 |\\n| Build on the engine's full workspace; engine read-only | §17 |\\n| Art direction: native base, new assets | §11 |\\n| Animation: native base, new animations | §12 |\\n| Audio: code only, gibberish, music 10% | §13 |\\n| Online: AI Hub | §16 |\\n| Dev subfolder, stay in your folder, ports, PIDs, `file://` first | Intro, §16, §17.1, §21 |\\n| Performance unlimited, FPS out of scope | §11 |\\n| Testing: headless, one command (tested skeleton), playthrough, few screenshots, no review agents | §19 |\\n| This computer: stand-ins for pad, touch, audio, second player, TV; shared machine | §10, §16, §17.1, §19 |\\n900→| Original, feature-complete vertical slice, shows all mechanics | §1, §8, §20 |\\n| Player sees what they can and cannot do | §1, §4 refusals, §10 Handbook |\\n| Infrastructure for later building with designer tools | §15 Author tab, §17.5 |\\n| Dev mode from pause, tools, live sliders, reset | §15, Appendix A |\\n| Attract mode | §14 |\\n| Multiplayer in the hub and on its own | §6, §16 |\\n| Narrative tone | §9 |\\n| 10-foot, controller-first; native keyboard and mouse; hover; prompts; glyph option | §10 |\\n| No dense screens | §10 |\\n| Touch-only behaviour | §10, §19 |\\n910→| No TODOs, complete | §21 |\\n| Source Information, feedback, About screen, exit interview | §19 issue log, §21 |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Appendix A: live sliders\\n\\nAll are `defineSettings` entries with `when: 'now'`, read each step, in `labs/game/data/balance.ts`. Shown in Dev Mode → Balance. Difficulty multiplies some of them at run start.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"739→## 19. Testing\\n740→\\n**One command: `npm run verify`** (`labs/game/tools/verify.mjs`; add it with `npm pkg set scripts.verify=\\\"node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs\\\"`). It prints only failures and one final line (`verify: 212 passed, 0 failed`). Rerun it only after changing the code it covers; `--fast` skips browsers. Use one or two browsers at a time. No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Make it the first thing you write, from this skeleton.\\n\\nTested on the starter, under the run's real long `TMPDIR`, with no setup: `verify: 8 passed, 0 failed` in 28 s (14 s with `--fast`). With a broken test, a lint error and a failing browser check, it printed exactly those three `FAIL` lines and `verify: 9 passed, 3 failed`, and exited 1. It passes `npm run check` itself. For a lint failure it prints the summary; run `npm run check` for the details.\\n\\n```js\\n/**\\n * @file The one command, `npm run verify`: runs every check quietly and prints only the failures, then one line with\\n * the count. `--fast` skips what needs a browser. It sets its own short TMPDIR and CHROMIUM_PATH, so it needs no setup,\\n * and removes the temporary folder when done. Exit 0 when nothing failed.\\n750→ */\\nimport { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\\nimport { existsSync, globSync, mkdtempSync, readFileSync, rmSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { homedir } from 'node:os';\\n\\nconst fast = process.argv.includes('--fast');\\nconst tmp = mkdtempSync('/tmp/g.');\\nconst chrome = globSync(`${homedir()}/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium-*/chrome-linux64/chrome`)[0];\\nconst env = { ...process.env, TMPDIR: tmp, CHROMIUM_PATH: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH ?? chrome };\\nconst run = (command, ...args) => spawnSync(command, args, { env, encoding: 'utf8', maxBuffer: 2 ** 28 });\\n760→\\nlet passed = 0;\\nconst failed = [];\\nconst note = (name, ok, detail = '') => (ok ? (passed += 1) : failed.push(`${name}${detail ? `: ${detail}` : ''}`));\\nconst tail = (result) => `${result.stdout}${result.stderr}`.trim().split('\\\\n').slice(-6).join(' | ').slice(0, 600);\\n\\n/** A step whose exit status is its verdict. */\\nconst step = (name, command, ...args) => {\\n  const result = run(command, ...args);\\n  note(name, result.status === 0, tail(result));\\n770→};\\n\\n/** Vitest, counted test by test from the JSON report npm test writes. */\\nfunction tests() {\\n  const report = 'out/test/report.json';\\n  rmSync(report, { force: true });\\n  const result = run('npm', 'test', '--silent');\\n  if (!existsSync(report)) return note('npm test', false, tail(result));\\n  for (const file of JSON.parse(readFileSync(report, 'utf8')).testResults) {\\n    for (const test of file.assertionResults) {\\n780→      note(\\n        test.fullName,\\n        test.status === 'passed',\\n        String(test.failureMessages?.[0] ?? '')\\n          .split('\\\\n')[0]\\n          .slice(0, 300),\\n      );\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n790→\\n/** A browser tool: it prints one JSON line per check, { name, ok, detail }. Add e2e and hub runs here. */\\nfunction browserTool(path) {\\n  const result = run('node', path);\\n  const lines = result.stdout.split('\\\\n').filter((line) => line.startsWith('{'));\\n  for (const line of lines) {\\n    const { name, ok, detail } = JSON.parse(line);\\n    note(name, ok, detail);\\n  }\\n  if (result.status !== 0 || lines.length === 0) note(path, false, tail(result));\\n800→}\\n\\nstep('npm run check', 'npm', 'run', '--silent', 'check');\\ntests();\\nif (!fast) {\\n  step('replays in Chromium', 'node', 'x', 'replay', 'labs/game/replays', '--browser', 'sim');\\n  for (const path of ['labs/game/tools/e2e.mjs', 'labs/game/tools/hub.mjs']) if (existsSync(path)) browserTool(path);\\n}\\nrmSync(tmp, { recursive: true, force: true });\\nfor (const failure of failed) console.log(`FAIL ${failure}`);\\n810→console.log(`verify: ${passed} passed, ${failed.length} failed`);\\nprocess.exit(failed.length ? 1 : 0);\\n```\\n\\n**Keep an issue log from the first day** in `labs/game/TESTING-LOG.md`: one line per issue a test or check caught, naming the test, and whether it was critical (game-breaking). TASK.md's Testing Feedback asks how many issues testing caught, how many were critical, and which tests helped most and least. You cannot answer honestly from memory at the end. Note engine friction in `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` as it happens, and prompt friction in the log, for the other two feedback sections.\\n\\n**Node (`npm test`, beside each module).**\\n\\n- Golden replay per case; capture and restore continue exactly.\\n- The bot wins every case on Resident on 3 seeds (`BOT_SEEDS=10` for a wider sweep before finishing); also on Intern and Attending for Cases 1 and 8; also with 2 and 4 bot surgeons.\\n820→- Each tool's rules, each rating band, each strain's state machine, each failure (flatline, clock, slips), Stillpoint's and Second Pulse's rules, flap clip timing.\\n- `validateCase` and `validateStory` over all data; no \\\"TODO\\\", \\\"placeholder\\\" or lorem in the text; no name from the §1 never-use list.\\n- **Whole-game playthrough without a browser:** the Director walks the story, all eight cases by bot, to credits and Free Surgery.\\n- **The sequencer:** a host and 3 clients in Node over a simulated network (latency, jitter, 5% loss on unreliable input, leavers): all hashes equal after 3,600 ticks. This protects the riskiest system cheaply.\\n- Settings: sliders within range and reset restores defaults.\\n- Every tool and target kind has an effect or a refusal line (§4). The exit interview parses and matches TASK.md's shape, and the About screen's data matches it.\\n\\n**Browser, against the deliverable (`labs/game/tools/e2e.mjs`, Playwright).** Launch with `executablePath: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH` and `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`, on `file://`.\\n\\n- Boots with no external request and no console errors; `__engine.ready`.\\n830→- **Whole-game playthrough:** a stub pad (`navigator.getGamepads` via `addInitScript`) walks title, Board, story and debriefs, with the bot playing the ops at a raised `timeScale`, to the credits. 640×360 is enough.\\n- Attract starts at 10 s idle; each of key, mouse, pad button and stick exits it; nothing saved.\\n- Prompts: glyphs change with last device; the Xbox glyph setting works.\\n- Every menu by pad only, then by mouse (hover moves focus, click picks).\\n- Dev mode opens from pause; a slider changes its setting live; reset works.\\n- Touch, in Chromium's phone and tablet profiles, tapping with `page.touchscreen`: exact message, settings button works, no touch layer, attract runs.\\n- No WebGPU, through the system Python's Playwright: Firefox, and WebKit with an iPhone profile (the closest here to an iPhone, not iOS Safari; both have WebGL only). Expect the plain message of §10. An iPhone's real behaviour is checked only this far.\\n- Multiplayer stand-in: there is no second player here, so two browser pages stand in for one. The harness does this with frames running real copies of the game, and clicking a frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\\n- Audio: music bus 0.1 by default; an `AnalyserNode` hears music and a beep; effects render with an `OfflineAudioContext`; the file holds no audio data or `<audio>`.\\n- Couch: two stub pads each claim a seat and move their own cursor.\\n840→- Story and HUD elements carry `data-game` attributes so scripts can read them through the DOM.\\n\\n**Hub:** the four harness runs of §16, plus the game on its own.\\n\\n**Seeing it.** Prefer numbers: `node x shot labs/game --ids` for coverage and what is visible. Use screenshots only for what a script cannot check: at 1920×1080 for each screen once, and the field in each case. WebGPU canvases can come out blank in headless screenshots; `node x shot --png` draws through the engine and works.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 20. Build order\\n\\n850→Each step ends with its tests green. Get the core feeling right before spreading out.\\n\\n1. **Setup and packaging first.** Starter builds to one file, runs from `file://`. `verify` exists. `game:main` with runs.\\n2. **The field.** Live sheet with breath and heartbeat, under `look:field`, with drapes and the lamp. Mouse and pad cursor, hand and scalpel. Draw one incision on a guide: this must already look and feel good. Spend time here.\\n3. **Case 1 complete:** Drain, Forceps, Suture, Syringe, Gel, radial, vitals, clock, ratings, HUD, Odie's lines, failure and debrief, and the bot winning it.\\n4. **Front end:** title, Board, story scene with two characters and voices, briefing, pause, Settings, glyphs, saves. Then attract, then dev mode, since they speed all later work.\\n5. **Case 2 and 3:** Scalpel, Scanner, Laser, sites, Code, Stillpoint and the pulse-lock.\\n6. **Hub early:** session flow, card, sequencer test in Node, then the harness on the one case you have, while it is still cheap to change.\\n7. **Strains and flaps:** Cases 4, 5, 6, with Second Pulse, seats, AI colleague, Swap, Consult, couch co-op.\\n8. **Cases 7 and 8, session case, Free Surgery, the ending.**\\n860→9. **All story, all songs, all postcards, all animations.** Polish every transition.\\n10. **Tune:** rank thresholds, clock, difficulty, using the bot's numbers. Check no phase has a dead stretch over 8 s for a bot playing at human speed. Then final `verify`, exit interview, About screen.\\n\\nIf you run out of something, cut polish per case, never a feature on the done list.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 21. Done\\n\\n**About/Credits (in game)**, paged, one card per item, big text. One screen each: Source Information (Date, Source, Plan, Genre and Perspective, Tech, Art Direction, Animation, Audio, Online, Testing, Research, Environment, Model, Usage, with your TASK.md's values for Date, Environment and Model), Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback (every question TASK.md asks, one card each), credits. Use \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you cannot confirm. Plan field: `PLAN.md`. Usage field: exactly `added on import`, in an element marked `data-about=\\\"usage\\\"` so the importer can replace it. Say in Online that network play is through AI Hub only, and couch co-op on one screen.\\n870→\\n**Exit interview.** Save the JSON exactly as TASK.md shapes it, beside the deliverable; a test parses it and checks its shape. Suggested catalogue values: `type` Game; `genre` Simulation; `source` Trauma Center: Second Opinion; `series` Trauma Center; `players` 1–4; `controls` \\\"Stick or mouse aims · R2 or click uses the tool · right stick or 1-8 picks a tool\\\"; 3 to 6 tags from `3d`, `first-person`, `co-op`, `story`, `boss fights`, `comedy`. Write your own `tagline` (a few words) and `description` (one or two sentences for the catalogue), in plain text. `name` is your title as on the title screen. Answer the feedback sections from your issue log (§19) and `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md`. Source Information hints: genre and perspective as in §1; tech: my-3dge at your commit, three.js r182 on WebGPU, Rapier 0.21, TypeScript, Vite, AI Hub session client; research: note this plan's research plus your own, or none.\\n\\n**Chat reply.** Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback, as TASK.md says.\\n\\n**Before you stop.**\\n\\n- `npm run verify` green; `npm run check` and `npm test` green; the bot still wins.\\n- No TODOs, placeholders, stubs, or lorem anywhere; no unused listed feature.\\n- The deliverable is current, in the place and under the name TASK.md gives.\\n880→- `labs/game/README.md` says what the game is and the calls you made; `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` lists what you needed from the engine. \\\"Local engine patches\\\" should be empty; the engine stays read-only.\\n- Every process you started is stopped by its PID; your temporary directory is removed.\\n\\n**Every TASK.md requirement, and where it is covered.**\\n\\n| TASK.md asks for | Here |\\n|---|---|\\n| Genre, perspective | §1 |\\n| Title by RNG from 50 | Yours, as TASK.md says; never named here |\\n| Research | Done for this plan (§1's review fixes, §17's tests); more is your choice |\\n890→| Single HTML file, dependencies | §18 |\\n| Build on the engine's full workspace; engine read-only | §17 |\\n| Art direction: native base, new assets | §11 |\\n| Animation: native base, new animations | §12 |\\n| Audio: code only, gibberish, music 10% | §13 |\\n| Online: AI Hub | §16 |\\n| Dev subfolder, stay in your folder, ports, PIDs, `file://` first | Intro, §16, §17.1, §21 |\\n| Performance unlimited, FPS out of scope | §11 |\\n| Testing: headless, one command (tested skeleton), playthrough, few screenshots, no review agents | §19 |\\n| This computer: stand-ins for pad, touch, audio, second player, TV; shared machine | §10, §16, §17.1, §19 |\\n900→| Original, feature-complete vertical slice, shows all mechanics | §1, §8, §20 |\\n| Player sees what they can and cannot do | §1, §4 refusals, §10 Handbook |\\n| Infrastructure for later building with designer tools | §15 Author tab, §17.5 |\\n| Dev mode from pause, tools, live sliders, reset | §15, Appendix A |\\n| Attract mode | §14 |\\n| Multiplayer in the hub and on its own | §6, §16 |\\n| Narrative tone | §9 |\\n| 10-foot, controller-first; native keyboard and mouse; hover; prompts; glyph option | §10 |\\n| No dense screens | §10 |\\n| Touch-only behaviour | §10, §19 |\\n910→| No TODOs, complete | §21 |\\n| Source Information, feedback, About screen, exit interview | §19 issue log, §21 |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Appendix A: live sliders\\n\\nAll are `defineSettings` entries with `when: 'now'`, read each step, in `labs/game/data/balance.ts`. Shown in Dev Mode → Balance. Difficulty multiplies some of them at run start.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":739,\"limit\":180,\"raw_output\":\"## 19. Testing\\n\\n**One command: `npm run verify`** (`labs/game/tools/verify.mjs`; add it with `npm pkg set scripts.verify=\\\"node labs/game/tools/verify.mjs\\\"`). It prints only failures and one final line (`verify: 212 passed, 0 failed`). Rerun it only after changing the code it covers; `--fast` skips browsers. Use one or two browsers at a time. No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Make it the first thing you write, from this skeleton.\\n\\nTested on the starter, under the run's real long `TMPDIR`, with no setup: `verify: 8 passed, 0 failed` in 28 s (14 s with `--fast`). With a broken test, a lint error and a failing browser check, it printed exactly those three `FAIL` lines and `verify: 9 passed, 3 failed`, and exited 1. It passes `npm run check` itself. For a lint failure it prints the summary; run `npm run check` for the details.\\n\\n```js\\n/**\\n * @file The one command, `npm run verify`: runs every check quietly and prints only the failures, then one line with\\n * the count. `--fast` skips what needs a browser. It sets its own short TMPDIR and CHROMIUM_PATH, so it needs no setup,\\n * and removes the temporary folder when done. Exit 0 when nothing failed.\\n */\\nimport { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\\nimport { existsSync, globSync, mkdtempSync, readFileSync, rmSync } from 'node:fs';\\nimport { homedir } from 'node:os';\\n\\nconst fast = process.argv.includes('--fast');\\nconst tmp = mkdtempSync('/tmp/g.');\\nconst chrome = globSync(`${homedir()}/.cache/ms-playwright/chromium-*/chrome-linux64/chrome`)[0];\\nconst env = { ...process.env, TMPDIR: tmp, CHROMIUM_PATH: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH ?? chrome };\\nconst run = (command, ...args) => spawnSync(command, args, { env, encoding: 'utf8', maxBuffer: 2 ** 28 });\\n\\nlet passed = 0;\\nconst failed = [];\\nconst note = (name, ok, detail = '') => (ok ? (passed += 1) : failed.push(`${name}${detail ? `: ${detail}` : ''}`));\\nconst tail = (result) => `${result.stdout}${result.stderr}`.trim().split('\\\\n').slice(-6).join(' | ').slice(0, 600);\\n\\n/** A step whose exit status is its verdict. */\\nconst step = (name, command, ...args) => {\\n  const result = run(command, ...args);\\n  note(name, result.status === 0, tail(result));\\n};\\n\\n/** Vitest, counted test by test from the JSON report npm test writes. */\\nfunction tests() {\\n  const report = 'out/test/report.json';\\n  rmSync(report, { force: true });\\n  const result = run('npm', 'test', '--silent');\\n  if (!existsSync(report)) return note('npm test', false, tail(result));\\n  for (const file of JSON.parse(readFileSync(report, 'utf8')).testResults) {\\n    for (const test of file.assertionResults) {\\n      note(\\n        test.fullName,\\n        test.status === 'passed',\\n        String(test.failureMessages?.[0] ?? '')\\n          .split('\\\\n')[0]\\n          .slice(0, 300),\\n      );\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n/** A browser tool: it prints one JSON line per check, { name, ok, detail }. Add e2e and hub runs here. */\\nfunction browserTool(path) {\\n  const result = run('node', path);\\n  const lines = result.stdout.split('\\\\n').filter((line) => line.startsWith('{'));\\n  for (const line of lines) {\\n    const { name, ok, detail } = JSON.parse(line);\\n    note(name, ok, detail);\\n  }\\n  if (result.status !== 0 || lines.length === 0) note(path, false, tail(result));\\n}\\n\\nstep('npm run check', 'npm', 'run', '--silent', 'check');\\ntests();\\nif (!fast) {\\n  step('replays in Chromium', 'node', 'x', 'replay', 'labs/game/replays', '--browser', 'sim');\\n  for (const path of ['labs/game/tools/e2e.mjs', 'labs/game/tools/hub.mjs']) if (existsSync(path)) browserTool(path);\\n}\\nrmSync(tmp, { recursive: true, force: true });\\nfor (const failure of failed) console.log(`FAIL ${failure}`);\\nconsole.log(`verify: ${passed} passed, ${failed.length} failed`);\\nprocess.exit(failed.length ? 1 : 0);\\n```\\n\\n**Keep an issue log from the first day** in `labs/game/TESTING-LOG.md`: one line per issue a test or check caught, naming the test, and whether it was critical (game-breaking). TASK.md's Testing Feedback asks how many issues testing caught, how many were critical, and which tests helped most and least. You cannot answer honestly from memory at the end. Note engine friction in `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` as it happens, and prompt friction in the log, for the other two feedback sections.\\n\\n**Node (`npm test`, beside each module).**\\n\\n- Golden replay per case; capture and restore continue exactly.\\n- The bot wins every case on Resident on 3 seeds (`BOT_SEEDS=10` for a wider sweep before finishing); also on Intern and Attending for Cases 1 and 8; also with 2 and 4 bot surgeons.\\n- Each tool's rules, each rating band, each strain's state machine, each failure (flatline, clock, slips), Stillpoint's and Second Pulse's rules, flap clip timing.\\n- `validateCase` and `validateStory` over all data; no \\\"TODO\\\", \\\"placeholder\\\" or lorem in the text; no name from the §1 never-use list.\\n- **Whole-game playthrough without a browser:** the Director walks the story, all eight cases by bot, to credits and Free Surgery.\\n- **The sequencer:** a host and 3 clients in Node over a simulated network (latency, jitter, 5% loss on unreliable input, leavers): all hashes equal after 3,600 ticks. This protects the riskiest system cheaply.\\n- Settings: sliders within range and reset restores defaults.\\n- Every tool and target kind has an effect or a refusal line (§4). The exit interview parses and matches TASK.md's shape, and the About screen's data matches it.\\n\\n**Browser, against the deliverable (`labs/game/tools/e2e.mjs`, Playwright).** Launch with `executablePath: process.env.CHROMIUM_PATH` and `--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`, on `file://`.\\n\\n- Boots with no external request and no console errors; `__engine.ready`.\\n- **Whole-game playthrough:** a stub pad (`navigator.getGamepads` via `addInitScript`) walks title, Board, story and debriefs, with the bot playing the ops at a raised `timeScale`, to the credits. 640×360 is enough.\\n- Attract starts at 10 s idle; each of key, mouse, pad button and stick exits it; nothing saved.\\n- Prompts: glyphs change with last device; the Xbox glyph setting works.\\n- Every menu by pad only, then by mouse (hover moves focus, click picks).\\n- Dev mode opens from pause; a slider changes its setting live; reset works.\\n- Touch, in Chromium's phone and tablet profiles, tapping with `page.touchscreen`: exact message, settings button works, no touch layer, attract runs.\\n- No WebGPU, through the system Python's Playwright: Firefox, and WebKit with an iPhone profile (the closest here to an iPhone, not iOS Safari; both have WebGL only). Expect the plain message of §10. An iPhone's real behaviour is checked only this far.\\n- Multiplayer stand-in: there is no second player here, so two browser pages stand in for one. The harness does this with frames running real copies of the game, and clicking a frame gives it the keyboard and mouse.\\n- Audio: music bus 0.1 by default; an `AnalyserNode` hears music and a beep; effects render with an `OfflineAudioContext`; the file holds no audio data or `<audio>`.\\n- Couch: two stub pads each claim a seat and move their own cursor.\\n- Story and HUD elements carry `data-game` attributes so scripts can read them through the DOM.\\n\\n**Hub:** the four harness runs of §16, plus the game on its own.\\n\\n**Seeing it.** Prefer numbers: `node x shot labs/game --ids` for coverage and what is visible. Use screenshots only for what a script cannot check: at 1920×1080 for each screen once, and the field in each case. WebGPU canvases can come out blank in headless screenshots; `node x shot --png` draws through the engine and works.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 20. Build order\\n\\nEach step ends with its tests green. Get the core feeling right before spreading out.\\n\\n1. **Setup and packaging first.** Starter builds to one file, runs from `file://`. `verify` exists. `game:main` with runs.\\n2. **The field.** Live sheet with breath and heartbeat, under `look:field`, with drapes and the lamp. Mouse and pad cursor, hand and scalpel. Draw one incision on a guide: this must already look and feel good. Spend time here.\\n3. **Case 1 complete:** Drain, Forceps, Suture, Syringe, Gel, radial, vitals, clock, ratings, HUD, Odie's lines, failure and debrief, and the bot winning it.\\n4. **Front end:** title, Board, story scene with two characters and voices, briefing, pause, Settings, glyphs, saves. Then attract, then dev mode, since they speed all later work.\\n5. **Case 2 and 3:** Scalpel, Scanner, Laser, sites, Code, Stillpoint and the pulse-lock.\\n6. **Hub early:** session flow, card, sequencer test in Node, then the harness on the one case you have, while it is still cheap to change.\\n7. **Strains and flaps:** Cases 4, 5, 6, with Second Pulse, seats, AI colleague, Swap, Consult, couch co-op.\\n8. **Cases 7 and 8, session case, Free Surgery, the ending.**\\n9. **All story, all songs, all postcards, all animations.** Polish every transition.\\n10. **Tune:** rank thresholds, clock, difficulty, using the bot's numbers. Check no phase has a dead stretch over 8 s for a bot playing at human speed. Then final `verify`, exit interview, About screen.\\n\\nIf you run out of something, cut polish per case, never a feature on the done list.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 21. Done\\n\\n**About/Credits (in game)**, paged, one card per item, big text. One screen each: Source Information (Date, Source, Plan, Genre and Perspective, Tech, Art Direction, Animation, Audio, Online, Testing, Research, Environment, Model, Usage, with your TASK.md's values for Date, Environment and Model), Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, Prompt Feedback (every question TASK.md asks, one card each), credits. Use \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you cannot confirm. Plan field: `PLAN.md`. Usage field: exactly `added on import`, in an element marked `data-about=\\\"usage\\\"` so the importer can replace it. Say in Online that network play is through AI Hub only, and couch co-op on one screen.\\n\\n**Exit interview.** Save the JSON exactly as TASK.md shapes it, beside the deliverable; a test parses it and checks its shape. Suggested catalogue values: `type` Game; `genre` Simulation; `source` Trauma Center: Second Opinion; `series` Trauma Center; `players` 1–4; `controls` \\\"Stick or mouse aims · R2 or click uses the tool · right stick or 1-8 picks a tool\\\"; 3 to 6 tags from `3d`, `first-person`, `co-op`, `story`, `boss fights`, `comedy`. Write your own `tagline` (a few words) and `description` (one or two sentences for the catalogue), in plain text. `name` is your title as on the title screen. Answer the feedback sections from your issue log (§19) and `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md`. Source Information hints: genre and perspective as in §1; tech: my-3dge at your commit, three.js r182 on WebGPU, Rapier 0.21, TypeScript, Vite, AI Hub session client; research: note this plan's research plus your own, or none.\\n\\n**Chat reply.** Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback, as TASK.md says.\\n\\n**Before you stop.**\\n\\n- `npm run verify` green; `npm run check` and `npm test` green; the bot still wins.\\n- No TODOs, placeholders, stubs, or lorem anywhere; no unused listed feature.\\n- The deliverable is current, in the place and under the name TASK.md gives.\\n- `labs/game/README.md` says what the game is and the calls you made; `ENGINE-FEEDBACK.md` lists what you needed from the engine. \\\"Local engine patches\\\" should be empty; the engine stays read-only.\\n- Every process you started is stopped by its PID; your temporary directory is removed.\\n\\n**Every TASK.md requirement, and where it is covered.**\\n\\n| TASK.md asks for | Here |\\n|---|---|\\n| Genre, perspective | §1 |\\n| Title by RNG from 50 | Yours, as TASK.md says; never named here |\\n| Research | Done for this plan (§1's review fixes, §17's tests); more is your choice |\\n| Single HTML file, dependencies | §18 |\\n| Build on the engine's full workspace; engine read-only | §17 |\\n| Art direction: native base, new assets | §11 |\\n| Animation: native base, new animations | §12 |\\n| Audio: code only, gibberish, music 10% | §13 |\\n| Online: AI Hub | §16 |\\n| Dev subfolder, stay in your folder, ports, PIDs, `file://` first | Intro, §16, §17.1, §21 |\\n| Performance unlimited, FPS out of scope | §11 |\\n| Testing: headless, one command (tested skeleton), playthrough, few screenshots, no review agents | §19 |\\n| This computer: stand-ins for pad, touch, audio, second player, TV; shared machine | §10, §16, §17.1, §19 |\\n| Original, feature-complete vertical slice, shows all mechanics | §1, §8, §20 |\\n| Player sees what they can and cannot do | §1, §4 refusals, §10 Handbook |\\n| Infrastructure for later building with designer tools | §15 Author tab, §17.5 |\\n| Dev mode from pause, tools, live sliders, reset | §15, Appendix A |\\n| Attract mode | §14 |\\n| Multiplayer in the hub and on its own | §6, §16 |\\n| Narrative tone | §9 |\\n| 10-foot, controller-first; native keyboard and mouse; hover; prompts; glyph option | §10 |\\n| No dense screens | §10 |\\n| Touch-only behaviour | §10, §19 |\\n| No TODOs, complete | §21 |\\n| Source Information, feedback, About screen, exit interview | §19 issue log, §21 |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Appendix A: live sliders\\n\\nAll are `defineSettings` entries with `when: 'now'`, read each step, in `labs/game/data/balance.ts`. Shown in Dev Mode → Balance. Difficulty multiplies some of them at run start.\\n\\n\",\"total_lines\":952}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-2b6603e1-793c-467f-8e3b-d78c49a80d9f-16","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→SOURCE: Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n10→\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge#make-a-game-with-my-3dge, in the engine's full workspace. The engine stays read-only, as the AGENTS.md it gives your game says: add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n20→\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n30→Effort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n40→  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\n50→No script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n60→\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\n70→Scope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\n80→Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\n90→No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:47:31\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n100→- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n110→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n120→    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\n130→Testing Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n140→- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n150→- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→SOURCE: Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\n\\nGenre: Pick the game genre(s) that best fit the source and would make the best game.\\n\\nPerspective: Pick the camera perspective(s) that best fit the genre and source and would make the best game.\\n\\nGame Title: A playful riff on SOURCE's name. Silently brainstorm 50 titles, then pick one with a real random number generator (e.g., run code).\\n\\nResearch: Whether and how much to research is your choice.\\n10→\\nPackage: For ease of deployment and sharing provide a single html file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731].html. Dependencies that can be downloaded do not have to be included in the html file if that is easier.\\n\\nTech Stack: Build on https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge#make-a-game-with-my-3dge, in the engine's full workspace. The engine stays read-only, as the AGENTS.md it gives your game says: add whatever systems, art assets and animations the game needs on top of it. The rest of the tech stack is your choice.\\n\\nArt Direction: Use the engine’s native style as the base. Make new art assets for whatever makes the game feel like the source; the engine’s asset library is fine for generic props.\\n\\nAnimation: Use the engine’s native animation system as the base. Make new animations for whatever makes the game feel like the source.\\n\\nAudio: All music, sound effects and voices are written in code, with no audio files. Voices are Animalese/Simlish-style gibberish. Music is to be set to 10% volume by default.\\n20→\\nOnline: https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub\\n\\nWorking Environment and deliverable:\\nCreate a subfolder called [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731] to keep all your dev files in. You can develop separate modules, then bundle into a single file [GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731].html in root when you’re done. You may use external CDN dependencies. Do not read files outside what you need: other agents work in the folders beside this one. They share this machine’s ports and processes too. Prefer file:// and the engine’s headless tools. If you need a server (the AI Hub harness does), bind 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks (`python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` prints it) and confirm the page is your game before trusting a result. Stop only processes you started, by their PID: never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever holds a port.\\n\\nPerformance: Treat the performance budget as unlimited. Add as much detail, enemies, effects, lights, and shadows as the scene needs. Out of scope (handled later): FPS targets, optimization.\\n\\nTesting: Test as much as you like with headless tools: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game. Keep them behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, and rerun it only after changing the code it covers. Use screenshots only for what a script can’t check. Don’t use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\\n\\n30→Effort: Spend effort however you see fit.\\n\\n## This computer\\nChecked 2026-11-04 20:05 (America/Vancouver) by node check-environment.mjs.\\n\\n- **Computer:** Worki9: 13th Gen Intel(R) Core(TM) i9-13900K, 32 cores, 20 GB RAM, 8 GB swap. Ubuntu 26.04.1 LTS on WSL2 (Linux 6.18.33.1-microsoft-standard-WSL2).\\n- **Space:** 850 GB free in the home folder. /tmp: 9.8 GB, in memory (tmpfs), 8.2 GB free when checked.\\n- **Display:** none for runs (they run with DISPLAY unset), so a headed browser needs xvfb-run.\\n- **Browsers:** Playwright 1.63.0 for Python (from playwright.sync_api import sync_playwright), with Chromium 153.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered; WebGPU, software-rendered, when started with --enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader), Firefox 155.0 (WebGL 2, software-rendered), WebKit 26.6 (WebGL 2) already downloaded. Phone and tablet device profiles are built in.\\n  WebGPU only works on a secure page: file:// or http://127.0.0.1. A WebGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n40→  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\n50→No script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n60→\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\n70→Scope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\n80→Narrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\n90→No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:47:31\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n100→- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n110→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\"