":6,\"content\":\" * as `window.__engine`, starts the renderer (WebGPU or nothing, engine/gfx/renderer.ts) and the post-processing chain\"},{\"line_number\":10,\"content\":\" * into intents (engine/input/input.ts; `__engine.input` drives the same session), fixed steps from the clock, the\"},{\"line_number\":14,\"content\":\" * Ready or error (§8.3): `__engine.ready` turns true once the first frame is done on the GPU, never after a startup\"},{\"line_number\":15,\"content\":\" * error; every failure (a frame that throws stops the loop) is one record in `__engine.errors` (the log's), shown as\"},{\"line_number\":17,\"content\":\" * (`GFX_NO_WEBGPU`) the sim still runs, and `__engine` still steps it; only `render` and `shot` throw.\"},{\"line_number\":19,\"content\":\" * Every frame drawn is timed by the run's stats meter, which reads the renderer's counts (`__engine.stats()`,\"},{\"line_number\":91,\"content\":\"    fix: 'the console names the cause; reload the page to start again, or read __engine.state() to see where the run stands',\"},{\"line_number\":99,\"content\":\"    fix: 'read __engine.errors for GFX_WARMUP_FAILED; node x shot <page> reports it headless',\"},{\"line_number\":149,\"content\":\"  /** The inspector, also `window.__engine`; null when the scene could not start. */\"},{\"line_number\":236,\"content\":\"  Object.assign(window, { __engine: signals });\"},{\"line_number\":295,\"content\":\"  Object.assign(window, { __engine: started.inspector });\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/engine/app/routes.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":13,\"content\":\" *   and `__engine.set` reach the same settings the same way. `routeQuery` writes a query back.\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/engine/app/animMember.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":12,\"content\":\" * engine/app/feelMember.ts is), so `createHeadless` and `window.__engine` both list it.\"}]},{\"path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/engine/app/viewMembers.ts\",\"matches\":[{\"line_number\":4,\"content\":\" * kind `inspectorMember` (engine/dev/members.ts), so `window.__engine` in a page and `createHeadless` in Node list the\"},{\"line_number\":18,\"content\":\" * // __engine.camera.set('cam=topdown').describe; // what the camera shows, in words\"},{\"line_number\":19,\"content\":\" * // __engine.scene.dump({ filter: 'wall', depth: 2 }); // the walls, one line each\"}]}]}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-4377f054-0fb1-4cc1-a513-d26d83f773d3-45","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\n    \\\"date\\\": \\\"2026-11-04 22:47:31\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\\",\\n    \\\"plan\\\": \\\"PLAN.md\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"Surgical simulation\\\",\\n    \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"Close overhead, a fixed camera on the operating field\\\",\\n    \\\"tech\\\": \\\"my-3dge at 2dbd829, three.js 0.182.0 on WebGPU, Rapier 0.21.0, TypeScript 5.9, Vite 7.3, AI Hub session client 1.4.0\\\",\\n    \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"The engine’s native PBR look at full resolution: wet stylised tissue, teal drapes, brass and amber lamps\\\",\\n10→    \\\"animation\\\": \\\"The engine’s animator for the surgeon. The sheet, breath, heartbeat and gloves are rewritten every frame\\\",\\n    \\\"audio\\\": \\\"Songs and effects written as chip voices. Voices are syllable gibberish. Music starts at 10%\\\",\\n    \\\"online\\\": \\\"AI Hub shared-world sessions only. Couch co-op for up to four devices on one screen. No other network\\\",\\n    \\\"testing\\\": \\\"Headless sim tests, one verify command, Chromium from file://, Firefox and WebKit for the WebGPU message. No physical controller, touch screen, second person, or iPhone\\\",\\n    \\\"research\\\": \\\"The plan’s own research notes. No further research\\\",\\n    \\\"environment\\\": \\\"Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\\",\\n    \\\"model\\\": \\\"xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\\",\\n    \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\n20→    \\\"Eighteen issues were caught and written in labs/game/TESTING-LOG.md.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ten were game-breaking: a restore stepped the wrong body, the gala flap never finished, Reverie left the surface early, Solace children stacked, the syringe bound the wrong target, stitches landed off the wound, co-op copies shared one point, an outline width the engine rejects, a sound id the engine already owns, and a look and a song that used fields the engine does not have.\\\",\\n    \\\"The most helpful tests were the bot, which had to win every required case and seed, and the content walk that plays the whole shift to the credits.\\\",\\n    \\\"The least helpful was the voice-key equality check. It only shows that one line keeps the same syllable list. The starter gem tests had to stay green and do not cover an operation.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\n    \\\"Headless play, capture, settings that restart a scene in place, and the one-file build. A game can be proven without a screen.\\\",\\n    \\\"Yes. The live operation sat in a module, and capture restored only the world, so the next step used the body from before the rewind until the chart snapshot was adopted. A view setting cannot be read from the sim, so the radial-slow slider cannot change the hashed clock.\\\",\\n    \\\"The operation itself: tools, vitals, the five strains, ratings, the bot, the lockstep, and the campaign screens. The engine does not know what a surgery is.\\\",\\n    \\\"There is no scene hook after a restore, and no public way to rewrite a geometry the page kept. Both showed up as a mismatched hash and a mesh that could not be repainted.\\\",\\n30→    \\\"Nothing. The engine stayed read-only. Local engine patches is empty.\\\",\\n    \\\"A callback on the scene after restore, or a supported way to update a kept mesh, so a living sheet and a rewind do not depend on a private object.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\n    \\\"The plan named the rules, the files, the hub boot order, and the traps on this machine. The operation could be built from that.\\\",\\n    \\\"The sheet was specified very dense, while the same plan warned that four browser copies are killed when memory runs out. The source’s subtitle is banned as a title and allowed as an ordinary line, which is easy to mix up.\\\",\\n    \\\"Covering eight operations, the story, couch play, and the four-copy hub check on a machine that ends the largest browser.\\\",\\n    \\\"Split the four-copy hub check from the campaign-length walk, and name a sheet size that fits that check.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n40→    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Trauma Compass\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"Keep the line, then close.\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"A lamp-lit night at Harbor Light General. Eight operations, four surgeons at one table, and a patient who has to last until the last stitch.\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"Simulation\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"Trauma Center\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"Stick or mouse aims · R2 or click uses the tool · right stick or 1-8 picks a tool\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\n50→      \\\"3d\\\",\\n      \\\"co-op\\\",\\n      \\\"story\\\",\\n      \\\"boss fights\\\",\\n      \\\"comedy\\\"\\n    ]\\n  }\\n}\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\n    \\\"date\\\": \\\"2026-11-04 22:47:31\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\\",\\n    \\\"plan\\\": \\\"PLAN.md\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"Surgical simulation\\\",\\n    \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"Close overhead, a fixed camera on the operating field\\\",\\n    \\\"tech\\\": \\\"my-3dge at 2dbd829, three.js 0.182.0 on WebGPU, Rapier 0.21.0, TypeScript 5.9, Vite 7.3, AI Hub session client 1.4.0\\\",\\n    \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"The engine’s native PBR look at full resolution: wet stylised tissue, teal drapes, brass and amber lamps\\\",\\n10→    \\\"animation\\\": \\\"The engine’s animator for the surgeon. The sheet, breath, heartbeat and gloves are rewritten every frame\\\",\\n    \\\"audio\\\": \\\"Songs and effects written as chip voices. Voices are syllable gibberish. Music starts at 10%\\\",\\n    \\\"online\\\": \\\"AI Hub shared-world sessions only. Couch co-op for up to four devices on one screen. No other network\\\",\\n    \\\"testing\\\": \\\"Headless sim tests, one verify command, Chromium from file://, Firefox and WebKit for the WebGPU message. No physical controller, touch screen, second person, or iPhone\\\",\\n    \\\"research\\\": \\\"The plan’s own research notes. No further research\\\",\\n    \\\"environment\\\": \\\"Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\\",\\n    \\\"model\\\": \\\"xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\\",\\n    \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\n20→    \\\"Eighteen issues were caught and written in labs/game/TESTING-LOG.md.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ten were game-breaking: a restore stepped the wrong body, the gala flap never finished, Reverie left the surface early, Solace children stacked, the syringe bound the wrong target, stitches landed off the wound, co-op copies shared one point, an outline width the engine rejects, a sound id the engine already owns, and a look and a song that used fields the engine does not have.\\\",\\n    \\\"The most helpful tests were the bot, which had to win every required case and seed, and the content walk that plays the whole shift to the credits.\\\",\\n    \\\"The least helpful was the voice-key equality check. It only shows that one line keeps the same syllable list. The starter gem tests had to stay green and do not cover an operation.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\n    \\\"Headless play, capture, settings that restart a scene in place, and the one-file build. A game can be proven without a screen.\\\",\\n    \\\"Yes. The live operation sat in a module, and capture restored only the world, so the next step used the body from before the rewind until the chart snapshot was adopted. A view setting cannot be read from the sim, so the radial-slow slider cannot change the hashed clock.\\\",\\n    \\\"The operation itself: tools, vitals, the five strains, ratings, the bot, the lockstep, and the campaign screens. The engine does not know what a surgery is.\\\",\\n    \\\"There is no scene hook after a restore, and no public way to rewrite a geometry the page kept. Both showed up as a mismatched hash and a mesh that could not be repainted.\\\",\\n30→    \\\"Nothing. The engine stayed read-only. Local engine patches is empty.\\\",\\n    \\\"A callback on the scene after restore, or a supported way to update a kept mesh, so a living sheet and a rewind do not depend on a private object.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\n    \\\"The plan named the rules, the files, the hub boot order, and the traps on this machine. The operation could be built from that.\\\",\\n    \\\"The sheet was specified very dense, while the same plan warned that four browser copies are killed when memory runs out. The source’s subtitle is banned as a title and allowed as an ordinary line, which is easy to mix up.\\\",\\n    \\\"Covering eight operations, the story, couch play, and the four-copy hub check on a machine that ends the largest browser.\\\",\\n    \\\"Split the four-copy hub check from the campaign-length walk, and name a sheet size that fits that check.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n40→    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Trauma Compass\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"Keep the line, then close.\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"A lamp-lit night at Harbor Light General. Eight operations, four surgeons at one table, and a patient who has to last until the last stitch.\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"Simulation\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"Trauma Center\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"Stick or mouse aims · R2 or click uses the tool · right stick or 1-8 picks a tool\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\n50→      \\\"3d\\\",\\n      \\\"co-op\\\",\\n      \\\"story\\\",\\n      \\\"boss fights\\\",\\n      \\\"comedy\\\"\\n    ]\\n  }\\n}\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/exit-interview.json\",\"offset\":null,\"raw_output\":\"{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\n    \\\"date\\\": \\\"2026-11-04 22:47:31\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\\",\\n    \\\"plan\\\": \\\"PLAN.md\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"Surgical simulation\\\",\\n    \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"Close overhead, a fixed camera on the operating field\\\",\\n    \\\"tech\\\": \\\"my-3dge at 2dbd829, three.js 0.182.0 on WebGPU, Rapier 0.21.0, TypeScript 5.9, Vite 7.3, AI Hub session client 1.4.0\\\",\\n    \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"The engine’s native PBR look at full resolution: wet stylised tissue, teal drapes, brass and amber lamps\\\",\\n    \\\"animation\\\": \\\"The engine’s animator for the surgeon. The sheet, breath, heartbeat and gloves are rewritten every frame\\\",\\n    \\\"audio\\\": \\\"Songs and effects written as chip voices. Voices are syllable gibberish. Music starts at 10%\\\",\\n    \\\"online\\\": \\\"AI Hub shared-world sessions only. Couch co-op for up to four devices on one screen. No other network\\\",\\n    \\\"testing\\\": \\\"Headless sim tests, one verify command, Chromium from file://, Firefox and WebKit for the WebGPU message. No physical controller, touch screen, second person, or iPhone\\\",\\n    \\\"research\\\": \\\"The plan’s own research notes. No further research\\\",\\n    \\\"environment\\\": \\\"Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\\",\\n    \\\"model\\\": \\\"xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\\",\\n    \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"\\n  },\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\n    \\\"Eighteen issues were caught and written in labs/game/TESTING-LOG.md.\\\",\\n    \\\"Ten were game-breaking: a restore stepped the wrong body, the gala flap never finished, Reverie left the surface early, Solace children stacked, the syringe bound the wrong target, stitches landed off the wound, co-op copies shared one point, an outline width the engine rejects, a sound id the engine already owns, and a look and a song that used fields the engine does not have.\\\",\\n    \\\"The most helpful tests were the bot, which had to win every required case and seed, and the content walk that plays the whole shift to the credits.\\\",\\n    \\\"The least helpful was the voice-key equality check. It only shows that one line keeps the same syllable list. The starter gem tests had to stay green and do not cover an operation.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\n    \\\"Headless play, capture, settings that restart a scene in place, and the one-file build. A game can be proven without a screen.\\\",\\n    \\\"Yes. The live operation sat in a module, and capture restored only the world, so the next step used the body from before the rewind until the chart snapshot was adopted. A view setting cannot be read from the sim, so the radial-slow slider cannot change the hashed clock.\\\",\\n    \\\"The operation itself: tools, vitals, the five strains, ratings, the bot, the lockstep, and the campaign screens. The engine does not know what a surgery is.\\\",\\n    \\\"There is no scene hook after a restore, and no public way to rewrite a geometry the page kept. Both showed up as a mismatched hash and a mesh that could not be repainted.\\\",\\n    \\\"Nothing. The engine stayed read-only. Local engine patches is empty.\\\",\\n    \\\"A callback on the scene after restore, or a supported way to update a kept mesh, so a living sheet and a rewind do not depend on a private object.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\n    \\\"The plan named the rules, the files, the hub boot order, and the traps on this machine. The operation could be built from that.\\\",\\n    \\\"The sheet was specified very dense, while the same plan warned that four browser copies are killed when memory runs out. The source’s subtitle is banned as a title and allowed as an ordinary line, which is easy to mix up.\\\",\\n    \\\"Covering eight operations, the story, couch play, and the four-copy hub check on a machine that ends the largest browser.\\\",\\n    \\\"Split the four-copy hub check from the campaign-length walk, and name a sheet size that fits that check.\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"Trauma Compass\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"Keep the line, then close.\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"A lamp-lit night at Harbor Light General. Eight operations, four surgeons at one table, and a patient who has to last until the last stitch.\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"Simulation\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"Trauma Center\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"Stick or mouse aims · R2 or click uses the tool · right stick or 1-8 picks a tool\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\n      \\\"3d\\\",\\n      \\\"co-op\\\",\\n      \\\"story\\\",\\n      \\\"boss fights\\\",\\n      \\\"comedy\\\"\\n    ]\\n  }\\n}\\n\",\"total_lines\":57}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-4377f054-0fb1-4cc1-a513-d26d83f773d3-46","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The exit interview. The About screen and exit-interview.json are this object, so they cannot disagree.\\n * Usage is exactly the words the importer replaces.\\n */\\n\\n/** One About card. */\\nexport interface AboutCard {\\n  kicker: string;\\n  title: string;\\n10→  body: string;\\n  usage?: boolean;\\n}\\n\\n/** The catalogue and the four feedback blocks, in the shape TASK.md asks for. */\\nexport const INTERVIEW = {\\n  sourceInformation: {\\n    date: '2026-11-04 22:47:31',\\n    source: 'Trauma Center: Second Opinion',\\n    plan: 'PLAN.md',\\n20→    genre: 'Surgical simulation',\\n    perspective: 'Close overhead, a fixed camera on the operating field',\\n    tech: 'my-3dge at 2dbd829, three.js 0.182.0 on WebGPU, Rapier 0.21.0, TypeScript 5.9, Vite 7.3, AI Hub session client 1.4.0',\\n    artDirection:\\n      'The engine’s native PBR look at full resolution: wet stylised tissue, teal drapes, brass and amber lamps',\\n    animation:\\n      'The engine’s animator for the surgeon. The sheet, breath, heartbeat and gloves are rewritten every frame',\\n    audio: 'Songs and effects written as chip voices. Voices are syllable gibberish. Music starts at 10%',\\n    online: 'AI Hub shared-world sessions only. Couch co-op for up to four devices on one screen. No other network',\\n    testing:\\n30→      'Headless sim tests, one verify command, Chromium from file://, Firefox and WebKit for the WebGPU message. No physical controller, touch screen, second person, or iPhone',\\n    research: 'The plan’s own research notes. No further research',\\n    environment: 'Grok CLI (headless); Linux',\\n    model: 'xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh',\\n    usage: 'added on import',\\n  },\\n  testingFeedback: [\\n    'Eighteen issues were caught and written in labs/game/TESTING-LOG.md.',\\n    'Ten were game-breaking: a restore stepped the wrong body, the gala flap never finished, Reverie left the surface early, Solace children stacked, the syringe bound the wrong target, stitches landed off the wound, co-op copies shared one point, an outline width the engine rejects, a sound id the engine already owns, and a look and a song that used fields the engine does not have.',\\n    'The most helpful tests were the bot, which had to win every required case and seed, and the content walk that plays the whole shift to the credits.',\\n40→    'The least helpful was the voice-key equality check. It only shows that one line keeps the same syllable list. The starter gem tests had to stay green and do not cover an operation.',\\n  ],\\n  engineFeedback: [\\n    'Headless play, capture, settings that restart a scene in place, and the one-file build. A game can be proven without a screen.',\\n    'Yes. The live operation sat in a module, and capture restored only the world, so the next step used the body from before the rewind until the chart snapshot was adopted. A view setting cannot be read from the sim, so the radial-slow slider cannot change the hashed clock.',\\n    'The operation itself: tools, vitals, the five strains, ratings, the bot, the lockstep, and the campaign screens. The engine does not know what a surgery is.',\\n    'There is no scene hook after a restore, and no public way to rewrite a geometry the page kept. Both showed up as a mismatched hash and a mesh that could not be repainted.',\\n    'Nothing. The engine stayed read-only. Local engine patches is empty.',\\n    'A callback on the scene after restore, or a supported way to update a kept mesh, so a living sheet and a rewind do not depend on a private object.',\\n  ],\\n50→  promptFeedback: [\\n    'The plan named the rules, the files, the hub boot order, and the traps on this machine. The operation could be built from that.',\\n    'The sheet was specified very dense, while the same plan warned that four browser copies are killed when memory runs out. The source’s subtitle is banned as a title and allowed as an ordinary line, which is easy to mix up.',\\n    'Covering eight operations, the story, couch play, and the four-copy hub check on a machine that ends the largest browser.',\\n    'Split the four-copy hub check from the campaign-length walk, and name a sheet size that fits that check.',\\n  ],\\n  aiHub: {\\n    name: 'Trauma Compass',\\n    tagline: 'Keep the line, then close.',\\n    description:\\n60→      'A lamp-lit night at Harbor Light General. Eight operations, four surgeons at one table, and a patient who has to last until the last stitch.',\\n    type: 'Game',\\n    genre: 'Simulation',\\n    source: 'Trauma Center: Second Opinion',\\n    series: 'Trauma Center',\\n    players: '1–4',\\n    controls: 'Stick or mouse aims · R2 or click uses the tool · right stick or 1-8 picks a tool',\\n    tags: ['3d', 'co-op', 'story', 'boss fights', 'comedy'],\\n  },\\n} as const;\\n70→\\n/** Source fields, then each feedback answer, then the credits. One card each. */\\nexport function aboutCards(): AboutCard[] {\\n  const info = INTERVIEW.sourceInformation;\\n  const fields: [string, string][] = [\\n    ['Date', info.date],\\n    ['Source', info.source],\\n    ['Plan', info.plan],\\n    ['Genre and Perspective', `${info.genre}. ${info.perspective}.`],\\n    ['Tech', info.tech],\\n80→    ['Art Direction', info.artDirection],\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file The exit interview. The About screen and exit-interview.json are this object, so they cannot disagree.\\n * Usage is exactly the words the importer replaces.\\n */\\n\\n/** One About card. */\\nexport interface AboutCard {\\n  kicker: string;\\n  title: string;\\n10→  body: string;\\n  usage?: boolean;\\n}\\n\\n/** The catalogue and the four feedback blocks, in the shape TASK.md asks for. */\\nexport const INTERVIEW = {\\n  sourceInformation: {\\n    date: '2026-11-04 22:47:31',\\n    source: 'Trauma Center: Second Opinion',\\n    plan: 'PLAN.md',\\n20→    genre: 'Surgical simulation',\\n    perspective: 'Close overhead, a fixed camera on the operating field',\\n    tech: 'my-3dge at 2dbd829, three.js 0.182.0 on WebGPU, Rapier 0.21.0, TypeScript 5.9, Vite 7.3, AI Hub session client 1.4.0',\\n    artDirection:\\n      'The engine’s native PBR look at full resolution: wet stylised tissue, teal drapes, brass and amber lamps',\\n    animation:\\n      'The engine’s animator for the surgeon. The sheet, breath, heartbeat and gloves are rewritten every frame',\\n    audio: 'Songs and effects written as chip voices. Voices are syllable gibberish. Music starts at 10%',\\n    online: 'AI Hub shared-world sessions only. Couch co-op for up to four devices on one screen. No other network',\\n    testing:\\n30→      'Headless sim tests, one verify command, Chromium from file://, Firefox and WebKit for the WebGPU message. No physical controller, touch screen, second person, or iPhone',\\n    research: 'The plan’s own research notes. No further research',\\n    environment: 'Grok CLI (headless); Linux',\\n    model: 'xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh',\\n    usage: 'added on import',\\n  },\\n  testingFeedback: [\\n    'Eighteen issues were caught and written in labs/game/TESTING-LOG.md.',\\n    'Ten were game-breaking: a restore stepped the wrong body, the gala flap never finished, Reverie left the surface early, Solace children stacked, the syringe bound the wrong target, stitches landed off the wound, co-op copies shared one point, an outline width the engine rejects, a sound id the engine already owns, and a look and a song that used fields the engine does not have.',\\n    'The most helpful tests were the bot, which had to win every required case and seed, and the content walk that plays the whole shift to the credits.',\\n40→    'The least helpful was the voice-key equality check. It only shows that one line keeps the same syllable list. The starter gem tests had to stay green and do not cover an operation.',\\n  ],\\n  engineFeedback: [\\n    'Headless play, capture, settings that restart a scene in place, and the one-file build. A game can be proven without a screen.',\\n    'Yes. The live operation sat in a module, and capture restored only the world, so the next step used the body from before the rewind until the chart snapshot was adopted. A view setting cannot be read from the sim, so the radial-slow slider cannot change the hashed clock.',\\n    'The operation itself: tools, vitals, the five strains, ratings, the bot, the lockstep, and the campaign screens. The engine does not know what a surgery is.',\\n    'There is no scene hook after a restore, and no public way to rewrite a geometry the page kept. Both showed up as a mismatched hash and a mesh that could not be repainted.',\\n    'Nothing. The engine stayed read-only. Local engine patches is empty.',\\n    'A callback on the scene after restore, or a supported way to update a kept mesh, so a living sheet and a rewind do not depend on a private object.',\\n  ],\\n50→  promptFeedback: [\\n    'The plan named the rules, the files, the hub boot order, and the traps on this machine. The operation could be built from that.',\\n    'The sheet was specified very dense, while the same plan warned that four browser copies are killed when memory runs out. The source’s subtitle is banned as a title and allowed as an ordinary line, which is easy to mix up.',\\n    'Covering eight operations, the story, couch play, and the four-copy hub check on a machine that ends the largest browser.',\\n    'Split the four-copy hub check from the campaign-length walk, and name a sheet size that fits that check.',\\n  ],\\n  aiHub: {\\n    name: 'Trauma Compass',\\n    tagline: 'Keep the line, then close.',\\n    description:\\n60→      'A lamp-lit night at Harbor Light General. Eight operations, four surgeons at one table, and a patient who has to last until the last stitch.',\\n    type: 'Game',\\n    genre: 'Simulation',\\n    source: 'Trauma Center: Second Opinion',\\n    series: 'Trauma Center',\\n    players: '1–4',\\n    controls: 'Stick or mouse aims · R2 or click uses the tool · right stick or 1-8 picks a tool',\\n    tags: ['3d', 'co-op', 'story', 'boss fights', 'comedy'],\\n  },\\n} as const;\\n70→\\n/** Source fields, then each feedback answer, then the credits. One card each. */\\nexport function aboutCards(): AboutCard[] {\\n  const info = INTERVIEW.sourceInformation;\\n  const fields: [string, string][] = [\\n    ['Date', info.date],\\n    ['Source', info.source],\\n    ['Plan', info.plan],\\n    ['Genre and Perspective', `${info.genre}. ${info.perspective}.`],\\n    ['Tech', info.tech],\\n80→    ['Art Direction', info.artDirection],\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/data/interview.ts\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file The exit interview. The About screen and exit-interview.json are this object, so they cannot disagree.\\n * Usage is exactly the words the importer replaces.\\n */\\n\\n/** One About card. */\\nexport interface AboutCard {\\n  kicker: string;\\n  title: string;\\n  body: string;\\n  usage?: boolean;\\n}\\n\\n/** The catalogue and the four feedback blocks, in the shape TASK.md asks for. */\\nexport const INTERVIEW = {\\n  sourceInformation: {\\n    date: '2026-11-04 22:47:31',\\n    source: 'Trauma Center: Second Opinion',\\n    plan: 'PLAN.md',\\n    genre: 'Surgical simulation',\\n    perspective: 'Close overhead, a fixed camera on the operating field',\\n    tech: 'my-3dge at 2dbd829, three.js 0.182.0 on WebGPU, Rapier 0.21.0, TypeScript 5.9, Vite 7.3, AI Hub session client 1.4.0',\\n    artDirection:\\n      'The engine’s native PBR look at full resolution: wet stylised tissue, teal drapes, brass and amber lamps',\\n    animation:\\n      'The engine’s animator for the surgeon. The sheet, breath, heartbeat and gloves are rewritten every frame',\\n    audio: 'Songs and effects written as chip voices. Voices are syllable gibberish. Music starts at 10%',\\n    online: 'AI Hub shared-world sessions only. Couch co-op for up to four devices on one screen. No other network',\\n    testing:\\n      'Headless sim tests, one verify command, Chromium from file://, Firefox and WebKit for the WebGPU message. No physical controller, touch screen, second person, or iPhone',\\n    research: 'The plan’s own research notes. No further research',\\n    environment: 'Grok CLI (headless); Linux',\\n    model: 'xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh',\\n    usage: 'added on import',\\n  },\\n  testingFeedback: [\\n    'Eighteen issues were caught and written in labs/game/TESTING-LOG.md.',\\n    'Ten were game-breaking: a restore stepped the wrong body, the gala flap never finished, Reverie left the surface early, Solace children stacked, the syringe bound the wrong target, stitches landed off the wound, co-op copies shared one point, an outline width the engine rejects, a sound id the engine already owns, and a look and a song that used fields the engine does not have.',\\n    'The most helpful tests were the bot, which had to win every required case and seed, and the content walk that plays the whole shift to the credits.',\\n    'The least helpful was the voice-key equality check. It only shows that one line keeps the same syllable list. The starter gem tests had to stay green and do not cover an operation.',\\n  ],\\n  engineFeedback: [\\n    'Headless play, capture, settings that restart a scene in place, and the one-file build. A game can be proven without a screen.',\\n    'Yes. The live operation sat in a module, and capture restored only the world, so the next step used the body from before the rewind until the chart snapshot was adopted. A view setting cannot be read from the sim, so the radial-slow slider cannot change the hashed clock.',\\n    'The operation itself: tools, vitals, the five strains, ratings, the bot, the lockstep, and the campaign screens. The engine does not know what a surgery is.',\\n    'There is no scene hook after a restore, and no public way to rewrite a geometry the page kept. Both showed up as a mismatched hash and a mesh that could not be repainted.',\\n    'Nothing. The engine stayed read-only. Local engine patches is empty.',\\n    'A callback on the scene after restore, or a supported way to update a kept mesh, so a living sheet and a rewind do not depend on a private object.',\\n  ],\\n  promptFeedback: [\\n    'The plan named the rules, the files, the hub boot order, and the traps on this machine. The operation could be built from that.',\\n    'The sheet was specified very dense, while the same plan warned that four browser copies are killed when memory runs out. The source’s subtitle is banned as a title and allowed as an ordinary line, which is easy to mix up.',\\n    'Covering eight operations, the story, couch play, and the four-copy hub check on a machine that ends the largest browser.',\\n    'Split the four-copy hub check from the campaign-length walk, and name a sheet size that fits that check.',\\n  ],\\n  aiHub: {\\n    name: 'Trauma Compass',\\n    tagline: 'Keep the line, then close.',\\n    description:\\n      'A lamp-lit night at Harbor Light General. Eight operations, four surgeons at one table, and a patient who has to last until the last stitch.',\\n    type: 'Game',\\n    genre: 'Simulation',\\n    source: 'Trauma Center: Second Opinion',\\n    series: 'Trauma Center',\\n    players: '1–4',\\n    controls: 'Stick or mouse aims · R2 or click uses the tool · right stick or 1-8 picks a tool',\\n    tags: ['3d', 'co-op', 'story', 'boss fights', 'comedy'],\\n  },\\n} as const;\\n\\n/** Source fields, then each feedback answer, then the credits. One card each. */\\nexport function aboutCards(): AboutCard[] {\\n  const info = INTERVIEW.sourceInformation;\\n  const fields: [string, string][] = [\\n    ['Date', info.date],\\n    ['Source', info.source],\\n    ['Plan', info.plan],\\n    ['Genre and Perspective', `${info.genre}. ${info.perspective}.`],\\n    ['Tech', info.tech],\\n    ['Art Direction', info.artDirection],\\n\",\"total_lines\":131}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"86720c5c-9221-4b0b-af8e-2ff5318138ef"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_585","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The interview is complete and matches the requirements.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-ded8055b-d1bb-4189-a17c-54e5b3132fad-47","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/engine/gfx/shot.ts","offset":1,"limit":80}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-ded8055b-d1bb-4189-a17c-54e5b3132fad-48","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"function shotForJson|pixels|coverage|filled","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/engine/app/drawing.ts"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4646,"output_tokens":264,"cache_read_input_tokens":91008,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"75314004-3492-4036-bf04-9c0ded563a5d"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-ded8055b-d1bb-4189-a17c-54e5b3132fad-47","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→/**\\n * @file Shots (PLAN.md §8.5, §8.3's `shot`): what the frame draws, read back from the GPU as numbers and text, never\\n * as a committed image. `shot(gfx, view, options)` draws the view the way the frame does (`view.draw`, a post pass,\\n * or `render()`), into an RGBA8 render target instead of the canvas: the target is set as the renderer's output\\n * target, so the scene goes through the same internal target, pipelines and output pass (tone mapping, colour space)\\n * as the frame, and a shot at the drawing buffer's size (the default) matches the canvas pixel for pixel. A shot of\\n * another `size` through a post pass draws it with the renderer at that size (`atSize`, put back after): r182's\\n * screen coordinates follow the drawing buffer while an output target is set, so the chain drawn at the canvas's size\\n * into a smaller target would show only its top-left corner, and every filter sizes itself as at a canvas. It then\\n10→ * draws the ID pass (engine/gfx/idpass.ts) into a second target with no samples, reads both back with\\n * `readRenderTargetPixelsAsync`, and returns what was asked: the pixels, the ID pass (`visible`, `unseen`), the look\\n * metrics and their notes (engine/gfx/lookMetrics.ts), the 48 × 27 text thumbnail with its object map\\n * (engine/gfx/thumbnail.ts), and numbered marks drawn on a copy of the pixels (`marked`, for `x shot --marks`).\\n * `shotForJson` turns a result into plain JSON (pixels as base64), the shape `__engine.shot` returns and `x shot`\\n * (tools/cmd/shot.ts) reads.\\n *\\n * r182's readback keeps WebGPU's row padding: `copyTextureToBuffer` aligns each row to 256 bytes and returns the\\n * padded buffer, so a 48-pixel-wide read comes back in 64-pixel rows (PLAN.md §4.8). `readPixels` strips it\\n * (`stripRowPadding`), as shardfall's capture does (`shardfall:crates/pav_render/src/capture.rs:31-71`), and reports\\n20→ * the padded layout it read (`readback`).\\n *\\n * This is the one module that reads the GPU back (ESLint allows `readRenderTargetPixelsAsync` here and in\\n * engine/gfx/enhanced/timing.ts only), and what it reads goes to tools and tests, never to the sim (doctrine: WebGPU\\n * only). A shot builds pipelines of its own on first use (the ID material, the output pass into RGBA8), counted in\\n * `built`; it never changes what the next frame draws.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * stripRowPadding(new Uint8Array(256 + 8), 2, 2).length; // 16: two rows of 2 RGBA pixels, the padding dropped\\n * // const { ids, metrics, thumbnail } = await shot(gfx, { scene, camera });\\n30→ * @see engine/gfx/shot.test.ts\\n * @see tests/e2e/shot.spec.ts\\n */\\nimport { RenderTarget, Vector2, type PerspectiveCamera, type WebGPURenderer } from 'three/webgpu';\\nimport { codeError, defineCodes } from '../core/log';\\nimport { drawMarks, marksOf, type Mark } from './idmarks';\\nimport { decodeIds, listDrawables, withIdScene, type IdPass, type IdTable } from './idpass';\\nimport { judgeLook, lookMetrics, type LookMetrics, type LookNote } from './lookMetrics';\\nimport type { GpuTiming } from './features';\\nimport { advanceFrame, type PipelineCounter } from './pipelines';\\n40→import { thumbnail, type Thumbnail } from './thumbnail';\\nimport type { Warmup, WarmupView } from './warmup';\\n\\n/** The codes this module raises, with their fixes. */\\nexport const SHOT_CODES = defineCodes('gfx', {\\n  GFX_BAD_SHOT_SIZE: {\\n    template: 'a shot must be at least 1 × 1 whole pixels, not {width} × {height}',\\n    fix: \\\"pass size: { width, height } in whole pixels of at least 1, or leave it out to shoot at the drawing buffer's size\\\",\\n  },\\n});\\n50→\\n/** What to shoot and what to return. */\\nexport interface ShotOptions {\\n  /** Draw the ID pass and return it (default true). */\\n  ids?: boolean;\\n  /** Measure the look and judge it (default true). */\\n  metrics?: boolean;\\n  /** Build the text thumbnail (default true). */\\n  thumbnail?: boolean;\\n  /** Number what the ID pass sees on a copy of the pixels, `marked` (default false; draws the ID pass). */\\n60→  marks?: boolean;\\n  /** Return the pixels (default false). */\\n  pixels?: boolean;\\n  /** The size in pixels; the drawing buffer's by default, which is what the frame draws. */\\n  size?: { width: number; height: number };\\n  /** The protagonist's name, for scenes where no object carries `userData.protagonist`. */\\n  protagonist?: string;\\n  /** How the notes name the frame ('the shot' by default). */\\n  where?: string;\\n}\\n70→\\n/** The readback's padded layout: bytes per row as WebGPU copied them, and the buffer's length. */\\nexport interface Readback {\\n  bytesPerRow: number;\\n  bytes: number;\\n}\\n\\n/** What a shot returns: its size, the readback's layout, what was asked, pipelines built, and how long it took. */\\nexport interface ShotResult {\\n  width: number;\\n80→  height: number;\",\"content_concise\":\"1→/**\\n * @file Shots (PLAN.md §8.5, §8.3's `shot`): what the frame draws, read back from the GPU as numbers and text, never\\n * as a committed image. `shot(gfx, view, options)` draws the view the way the frame does (`view.draw`, a post pass,\\n * or `render()`), into an RGBA8 render target instead of the canvas: the target is set as the renderer's output\\n * target, so the scene goes through the same internal target, pipelines and output pass (tone mapping, colour space)\\n * as the frame, and a shot at the drawing buffer's size (the default) matches the canvas pixel for pixel. A shot of\\n * another `size` through a post pass draws it with the renderer at that size (`atSize`, put back after): r182's\\n * screen coordinates follow the drawing buffer while an output target is set, so the chain drawn at the canvas's size\\n * into a smaller target would show only its top-left corner, and every filter sizes itself as at a canvas. It then\\n10→ * draws the ID pass (engine/gfx/idpass.ts) into a second target with no samples, reads both back with\\n * `readRenderTargetPixelsAsync`, and returns what was asked: the pixels, the ID pass (`visible`, `unseen`), the look\\n * metrics and their notes (engine/gfx/lookMetrics.ts), the 48 × 27 text thumbnail with its object map\\n * (engine/gfx/thumbnail.ts), and numbered marks drawn on a copy of the pixels (`marked`, for `x shot --marks`).\\n * `shotForJson` turns a result into plain JSON (pixels as base64), the shape `__engine.shot` returns and `x shot`\\n * (tools/cmd/shot.ts) reads.\\n *\\n * r182's readback keeps WebGPU's row padding: `copyTextureToBuffer` aligns each row to 256 bytes and returns the\\n * padded buffer, so a 48-pixel-wide read comes back in 64-pixel rows (PLAN.md §4.8). `readPixels` strips it\\n * (`stripRowPadding`), as shardfall's capture does (`shardfall:crates/pav_render/src/capture.rs:31-71`), and reports\\n20→ * the padded layout it read (`readback`).\\n *\\n * This is the one module that reads the GPU back (ESLint allows `readRenderTargetPixelsAsync` here and in\\n * engine/gfx/enhanced/timing.ts only), and what it reads goes to tools and tests, never to the sim (doctrine: WebGPU\\n * only). A shot builds pipelines of its own on first use (the ID material, the output pass into RGBA8), counted in\\n * `built`; it never changes what the next frame draws.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * stripRowPadding(new Uint8Array(256 + 8), 2, 2).length; // 16: two rows of 2 RGBA pixels, the padding dropped\\n * // const { ids, metrics, thumbnail } = await shot(gfx, { scene, camera });\\n30→ * @see engine/gfx/shot.test.ts\\n * @see tests/e2e/shot.spec.ts\\n */\\nimport { RenderTarget, Vector2, type PerspectiveCamera, type WebGPURenderer } from 'three/webgpu';\\nimport { codeError, defineCodes } from '../core/log';\\nimport { drawMarks, marksOf, type Mark } from './idmarks';\\nimport { decodeIds, listDrawables, withIdScene, type IdPass, type IdTable } from './idpass';\\nimport { judgeLook, lookMetrics, type LookMetrics, type LookNote } from './lookMetrics';\\nimport type { GpuTiming } from './features';\\nimport { advanceFrame, type PipelineCounter } from './pipelines';\\n40→import { thumbnail, type Thumbnail } from './thumbnail';\\nimport type { Warmup, WarmupView } from './warmup';\\n\\n/** The codes this module raises, with their fixes. */\\nexport const SHOT_CODES = defineCodes('gfx', {\\n  GFX_BAD_SHOT_SIZE: {\\n    template: 'a shot must be at least 1 × 1 whole pixels, not {width} × {height}',\\n    fix: \\\"pass size: { width, height } in whole pixels of at least 1, or leave it out to shoot at the drawing buffer's size\\\",\\n  },\\n});\\n50→\\n/** What to shoot and what to return. */\\nexport interface ShotOptions {\\n  /** Draw the ID pass and return it (default true). */\\n  ids?: boolean;\\n  /** Measure the look and judge it (default true). */\\n  metrics?: boolean;\\n  /** Build the text thumbnail (default true). */\\n  thumbnail?: boolean;\\n  /** Number what the ID pass sees on a copy of the pixels, `marked` (default false; draws the ID pass). */\\n60→  marks?: boolean;\\n  /** Return the pixels (default false). */\\n  pixels?: boolean;\\n  /** The size in pixels; the drawing buffer's by default, which is what the frame draws. */\\n  size?: { width: number; height: number };\\n  /** The protagonist's name, for scenes where no object carries `userData.protagonist`. */\\n  protagonist?: string;\\n  /** How the notes name the frame ('the shot' by default). */\\n  where?: string;\\n}\\n70→\\n/** The readback's padded layout: bytes per row as WebGPU copied them, and the buffer's length. */\\nexport interface Readback {\\n  bytesPerRow: number;\\n  bytes: number;\\n}\\n\\n/** What a shot returns: its size, the readback's layout, what was asked, pipelines built, and how long it took. */\\nexport interface ShotResult {\\n  width: number;\\n80→  height: number;\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/engine/gfx/shot.ts\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":80,\"raw_output\":\"/**\\n * @file Shots (PLAN.md §8.5, §8.3's `shot`): what the frame draws, read back from the GPU as numbers and text, never\\n * as a committed image. `shot(gfx, view, options)` draws the view the way the frame does (`view.draw`, a post pass,\\n * or `render()`), into an RGBA8 render target instead of the canvas: the target is set as the renderer's output\\n * target, so the scene goes through the same internal target, pipelines and output pass (tone mapping, colour space)\\n * as the frame, and a shot at the drawing buffer's size (the default) matches the canvas pixel for pixel. A shot of\\n * another `size` through a post pass draws it with the renderer at that size (`atSize`, put back after): r182's\\n * screen coordinates follow the drawing buffer while an output target is set, so the chain drawn at the canvas's size\\n * into a smaller target would show only its top-left corner, and every filter sizes itself as at a canvas. It then\\n * draws the ID pass (engine/gfx/idpass.ts) into a second target with no samples, reads both back with\\n * `readRenderTargetPixelsAsync`, and returns what was asked: the pixels, the ID pass (`visible`, `unseen`), the look\\n * metrics and their notes (engine/gfx/lookMetrics.ts), the 48 × 27 text thumbnail with its object map\\n * (engine/gfx/thumbnail.ts), and numbered marks drawn on a copy of the pixels (`marked`, for `x shot --marks`).\\n * `shotForJson` turns a result into plain JSON (pixels as base64), the shape `__engine.shot` returns and `x shot`\\n * (tools/cmd/shot.ts) reads.\\n *\\n * r182's readback keeps WebGPU's row padding: `copyTextureToBuffer` aligns each row to 256 bytes and returns the\\n * padded buffer, so a 48-pixel-wide read comes back in 64-pixel rows (PLAN.md §4.8). `readPixels` strips it\\n * (`stripRowPadding`), as shardfall's capture does (`shardfall:crates/pav_render/src/capture.rs:31-71`), and reports\\n * the padded layout it read (`readback`).\\n *\\n * This is the one module that reads the GPU back (ESLint allows `readRenderTargetPixelsAsync` here and in\\n * engine/gfx/enhanced/timing.ts only), and what it reads goes to tools and tests, never to the sim (doctrine: WebGPU\\n * only). A shot builds pipelines of its own on first use (the ID material, the output pass into RGBA8), counted in\\n * `built`; it never changes what the next frame draws.\\n *\\n * @example\\n * stripRowPadding(new Uint8Array(256 + 8), 2, 2).length; // 16: two rows of 2 RGBA pixels, the padding dropped\\n * // const { ids, metrics, thumbnail } = await shot(gfx, { scene, camera });\\n * @see engine/gfx/shot.test.ts\\n * @see tests/e2e/shot.spec.ts\\n */\\nimport { RenderTarget, Vector2, type PerspectiveCamera, type WebGPURenderer } from 'three/webgpu';\\nimport { codeError, defineCodes } from '../core/log';\\nimport { drawMarks, marksOf, type Mark } from './idmarks';\\nimport { decodeIds, listDrawables, withIdScene, type IdPass, type IdTable } from './idpass';\\nimport { judgeLook, lookMetrics, type LookMetrics, type LookNote } from './lookMetrics';\\nimport type { GpuTiming } from './features';\\nimport { advanceFrame, type PipelineCounter } from './pipelines';\\nimport { thumbnail, type Thumbnail } from './thumbnail';\\nimport type { Warmup, WarmupView } from './warmup';\\n\\n/** The codes this module raises, with their fixes. */\\nexport const SHOT_CODES = defineCodes('gfx', {\\n  GFX_BAD_SHOT_SIZE: {\\n    template: 'a shot must be at least 1 × 1 whole pixels, not {width} × {height}',\\n    fix: \\\"pass size: { width, height } in whole pixels of at least 1, or leave it out to shoot at the drawing buffer's size\\\",\\n  },\\n});\\n\\n/** What to shoot and what to return. */\\nexport interface ShotOptions {\\n  /** Draw the ID pass and return it (default true). */\\n  ids?: boolean;\\n  /** Measure the look and judge it (default true). */\\n  metrics?: boolean;\\n  /** Build the text thumbnail (default true). */\\n  thumbnail?: boolean;\\n  /** Number what the ID pass sees on a copy of the pixels, `marked` (default false; draws the ID pass). */\\n  marks?: boolean;\\n  /** Return the pixels (default false). */\\n  pixels?: boolean;\\n  /** The size in pixels; the drawing buffer's by default, which is what the frame draws. */\\n  size?: { width: number; height: number };\\n  /** The protagonist's name, for scenes where no object carries `userData.protagonist`. */\\n  protagonist?: string;\\n  /** How the notes name the frame ('the shot' by default). */\\n  where?: string;\\n}\\n\\n/** The readback's padded layout: bytes per row as WebGPU copied them, and the buffer's length. */\\nexport interface Readback {\\n  bytesPerRow: number;\\n  bytes: number;\\n}\\n\\n/** What a shot returns: its size, the readback's layout, what was asked, pipelines built, and how long it took. */\\nexport interface ShotResult {\\n  width: number;\\n  height: number;\\n\",\"total_lines\":270}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-ded8055b-d1bb-4189-a17c-54e5b3132fad-48","content":"{\"type\":\"GrepSearch\",\"stdout\":[60,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,32,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,112,97,116,104,61,34,47,104,111,109,101,47,109,105,99,104,97,47,100,101,118,47,99,99,45,109,97,116,114,105,120,47,114,117,110,115,47,103,114,111,107,45,120,104,105,103,104,46,98,117,105,108,100,45,118,50,95,95,50,48,50,54,49,49,48,52,45,50,50,52,55,51,49,95,95,120,53,57,99,49,100,51,47,119,111,114,107,34,62,10,78,111,32,109,97,116,99,104,101,115,32,102,111,117,110,100,10,60,47,119,111,114,107,115,112,97,99,101,95,114,101,115,117,108,116,62],\"stderr\":[],\"exit_code\":1,\"match_count\":0,\"file_matches\":[]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3