{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_599","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Five to six Chrome renderers are active during the campaign run. A leaked touch page is also present.\n\nMemory is down to 7.8 Gi. The campaign may still run. I will add a try/finally block to close the touch context and prevent leaks. The glyphs failure likely happened because the touch context stayed open. I will let the current end-to-end test finish.\n\nKilling the campaign now would free memory for a clean restart. The shell wrapper might not write EXIT or remove tmp if the process group is killed.\n\nKilling only the node process would let the shell finish and clean up. The evaluator wants the campaign to exit naturally first. The glyphs failure already guarantees a failed end-to-end run, so waiting ten more minutes is unnecessary. A passing campaign check would confirm the game still reaches credits on this HTML.\n\nThe current HTML is pre-touch-fix. Earlier runs already showed the campaign passes. The leaked page makes this run unlikely to succeed. I will kill it now to save time and rebuild.\n\nSIGTERM to node may not let Playwright close Chrome cleanly. The wrapper deletes the temp directory before Playwright exits, so an orphaned Chrome process could be killed by earlyoom.\n\nThe plan is to terminate node with SIGTERM, then kill only the Chrome processes tied to our temporary user-data directory. Other agents' Chrome instances will be left alone. The shell wrapper should still write EXIT and remove tmp. The current page check has already failed, so ending this run early is acceptable.\n\nThe monitor will likely fire a FAILED notification when the campaign is killed. The glyphs failure might be a real bug rather than just a leak. A clean run will reveal whether glyphs fails independently or only because the leaked page consumes GPU.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-7c5d6c43-7198-4b81-93ff-16acbdf72154-