{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_613","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The end-to-end test is still early. Only 1:17 has elapsed, and the attract stick phase is complete.\n\nThe mouse and developer check is the slowest remaining phase. It will take roughly 12 to 15 minutes. The test is still healthy and has not failed. I will avoid starting another browser to prevent CPU contention. I can instead prepare evidence capture scripts and review the hub.\n\nHeavy bot tests would slow frames and risk story walk timeouts. I will not run them. I can pre-write evidence extraction to copy e2e JSON into ux.log and nowebgpu.log. I will also read bot.test.ts for the exact command. The monitor shows no failures.\n\nI am checking existing evidence files and sim test outputs to plan the log capture commands. op.test.ts may be fast enough to run in parallel with the end-to-end test. I will not idle-spin while waiting for the monitor.\n\nI am preparing a verification runner script to execute after the end-to-end test passes. It will copy logs, run launch.mjs, start the hub, and run sim, bot, and content tests. I will not begin the sim tests now because they would compete for CPU with the running end-to-end suite. I am also reviewing the hub finish payload against session.js requirements to fix source bugs before rebuilding.\n\nI will edit hub source and rebuild only after the end-to-end test finishes. I am now checking whether clients must send intents before the operation exists, since boot.ts requires an op to be present.\n\nThe scene creates the operation on its first step. The host steps immediately after startRound begins, so the operation exists before clients send intents.\n\nThe host sends ticks to clients before they have an operation. Clients receive those ticks and step, which creates the operation locally. Only then do they send intents back. This confirms the traffic flows both ways.","signature":""},