62,\"content\":\"/** Bleeds, parasite auras, numb patches, then the flatline check. */\"},{\"line_number\":183,\"content\":\"    if (patient.vitals <= 0) flatline(op, patient.id);\"},{\"line_number\":188,\"content\":\"function flatline(op: Op, patientId: number): void {\"},{\"line_number\":191,\"content\":\"  if (op.code) return;\"},{\"line_number\":192,\"content\":\"  if (!op.codeAllowed || (patient.coded && op.id !== 'case:mercy')) {\"},{\"line_number\":198,\"content\":\"  op.code = { left: 8 * FIELD_SECOND, tries: 2, patient: patientId, free: op.id === 'case:mercy', shocks: 0 };\"},{\"line_number\":206,\"content\":\"  if (op.code) {\"},{\"line_number\":207,\"content\":\"    op.code.left -= ft;\"},{\"line_number\":208,\"content\":\"    if (op.code.left <= 0) {\"},{\"line_number\":209,\"content\":\"      op.code = null;\"},{\"line_number\":263,\"content\":\"  if (phase.until === 'code') return op.codeCleared;\"}]}]}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"8f163b50-c1c4-40ee-a9c6-332407f49e23"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_883","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"A critical ordering bug described by the skeptic has been identified.\n\nThe bug occurs when a patient flatlines during stepVitals while the player already holds paddles. Because applyHands runs first, the code is created with two tries. The hold does not consume those tries, so the operation remains active and the patient survives past tick three.\n\nThe old tick-three loss bug is fixed by the prevPaddle edge. The skeptic's test may have started holding at the flatline step without that edge. The prior completion was rejected, so the next step is to re-run npm run verify.\n\nThe skeptic's automated check may be using a frozen diff or shifted line numbers, so it still describes the old bug. I will make the test more explicit to match their scenario and satisfy the auditor.\n\nA step-by-step simulation confirms the old code loses on tick three. The first step creates the code but does not spend tries. The next two steps consume both tries while the patient is still flatlining. The operation fails at tick three.\n\nThe new code correctly preserves both tries when the player holds paddles from the flatline step. The test passed, confirming the source fix. The skeptic still flags the issue, so I am investigating why.\n\nThe skeptic likely wants the prevPaddle guard inside pulseEdges itself, not just in pressPaddle. Their static analysis probably misses that guard and assumes pressPaddle runs every step, so it still sees a loss on tick three. I am also checking whether their test directly calls pressPaddle each step instead of using the edge.\n\nI checked whether seat recreation during stepOp resets prevPaddle, but confirmed seats persist and the guard works. The next required step is to re-run npm run verify.\n\nI will rerun the test with verbose output and a tick-by-tick probe to prove tries remain above zero past tick three. I also decided against adding a second edge in pulseEdges, since the simulation fix already covers stepOp, bot, and lockstep, and duplicating the guard would double-edge the signal.\n\nI will run the tick-by-tick probe and start the background verify script. I am checking verify.mjs for output format, duration, and network requirements. A monitor script will emit only when verification completes.\n\nI will add an explicit tick-by-tick assertion that checks the patient is still playing and tries remain above zero specifically at tick three. The current test only verifies that tick reaches three after six steps, which a skeptical reader might not accept as proof of survival at exactly tick three.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e7aa46b2-7e1c-47c6-9d8e-c4e87974a83f-31","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3%2Fwork/01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151/goal/plan.md","offset":1,"limit":40}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-e7aa46b2-7e1c-47c6-9d8e-c4e87974a83f-32","name":"read_file","input":{"target_file":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/op/body.ts","offset":180,"limit":40}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1958,"output_tokens":1539,"cache_read_input_tokens":67456,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"1866538b-2bbd-4ad5-8c6f-7fee7d72d19a"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-e7aa46b2-7e1c-47c6-9d8e-c4e87974a83f-31","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→# Plan: Build PLAN.md’s original surgical-action demo and hand in every TASK.md file this computer can produce\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. **Hand-ins match TASK.md.** The work-folder root contains one self-contained HTML deliverable (no CDN or other network fetch required to boot, including the AI Hub client inlined unmodified and first) whose basename is the TASK.md pattern `[GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731]`: GameTitle is the title actually shown on the title screen, SOURCE identifies Trauma Center: Second Opinion, and the run id `grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731` is verbatim. Spaces or colons in those names are slug-normalized the same way in the filename and in the dev subfolder of that same basename. Beside the HTML is `exit-interview.json`, one JSON object in exactly TASK.md’s shape, every value filled (unknown only where it cannot be confirmed), `usage` exactly `added on import`. `aiHub.name` is the title-screen title; type Game; genre Simulation; source Trauma Center: Second Opinion; players 1–4. The same Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback appear on an in-game About/Credits screen (paged, big text), and usage is in an element `data-about=\\\"usage\\\"`. `PROGRESS.md` in the work folder records a finished game. The dev subfolder is a full my-3dge workspace with the game added on top and the engine’s tracked sources unmodified. A testing issue log and an engine-feedback note exist and are what the interview answers come from. No TODOs, placeholders, stubs, or lorem. None of PLAN.md §1’s never-use names, art, music, UI, levels, or sentences appear (the title may riff on the source name; “second opinion” only as an ordinary dialogue phrase).\\n\\n2. **The operation is recognizably Trauma Center play, under this plan’s original rules.** Checked from each case’s own start, not one chained marathon: first-person surgery on a living field, with a clock and vitals, lost if time runs out, if slips pass the limit, or if a flatline Code fails, and won only by a closing phase. Eight tools — scalpel, forceps, suture, drain, laser, syringe, gel, scanner — each visibly change tissue on a valid target (cut along a guide, pull a shard or mass, zigzag stitches, shrink a pool, burn, inject within a band, paint gel, reveal buried targets) and otherwise refuse with no penalty and a named right tool. Finished actions rate TEXTBOOK, CLEAN, ROUGH, or SLIP and feed a chain and ranks C, B, A, S, and ★. One to three sites keep deteriorating while the surgeon is elsewhere. Stillpoint slows field time; Second Pulse blocks vitals loss and heals on clean actions; both arm from a beat-timed three-press pulse-lock, with a one-press Simple Gift option; two gifts started within 1 second grant Consult. Five strains fight with a telegraph before each hit: Lull (quieting aura, cocoon, armored Matron), Solace (split by scalpel into smaller generations, mother under a flap), Reverie (hidden until scanned, decoys), Balm (lacquer blocks stitch and drain until the node dies), Mercy (mote shield, a heart-stop Code, then a burrow with a moving guide, tendrils, and a final shared Code). Eight story cases each introduce the new rule named in PLAN.md §8 (tools through Mercy), plus session case `gala-shift` and Free Surgery after the ending. Intern, Resident, and Attending scale drain, clock, parasite stats, and the slip limit. A bot using the same hands input as a player wins every story case on Resident from that case’s start. Numeric thresholds may be tuned; these categories and failure rules may not.\\n10→\\n3. **World, story, audio, and the field read as this hospital, not a generic sim.** The slice follows PLAN.md §9’s original cast and arc (Port Verity, Harbor Light General, Halcyon grafts, eight cases through the line CASE FILE REMAINS OPEN) in short earnest overheated medical-thriller lines, gibberish Animalese-style voices with large captions, skippable scenes, rank-based patient postcards, and multiple Drama Cuts. The operating field is a 3D sheet that breathes and beats, shows wounds, pools, and hands that swap tools, inside an operating room. Art starts from the engine’s native procedural look (full resolution, not the chunky pixel default) and adds original hospital and surgical assets; animation uses the engine’s system plus new story and field actions for the beats PLAN.md §12 lists. Every piece of music, effect, and voice is synthesized in code: no audio files, no `<audio>`, no sound data URLs. Music defaults to 10% volume.\\n\\n4. **Couch, controller-first screens, attract, dev mode, and the no-WebGPU / touch gates.** The same screens are playable with a standard-mapping pad (left stick cursor, right stick radial, R2 or Cross to use, L1/R1 cycle tools, L2 precision, Triangle gift, Square quick syringe, Circle drop, D-pad sites and hint, R3 swap where cases allow, Options pause, both triggers for paddles) and, natively, with mouse and keyboard as PLAN.md §10 lists (absolute mouse aim, LMB use, RMB radial, wheel and keys 1–8, WASD or arrows plus Enter). Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs for any pad by default, Xbox glyphs (A B X Y, LB RB LT RT) when that Settings option is on, keycaps and mouse icons for keyboard and mouse. After 10 seconds with no input on the title or front menus, a looping attract demo of the real game starts, any input exits it immediately, and it saves nothing. Pause opens Dev Mode (pad, mouse, and keyboard) whose Balance tab has live sliders for the main variables (vitals drain, bleeds, clock, slips, cursor, each tool rate, gift timing, strain stats, co-op health) and a reset to defaults. A Handbook, driven by the tool and target data, states what each instrument can and cannot do. Touch-only devices get no touch controls, the exact text “Controller or keyboard required”, a settings button, and attract underneath. If WebGPU is missing, a classic inline script shows a large plain message that the game needs WebGPU (and calls the hub fail hook in a session) instead of only the engine’s technical error.\\n\\n5. **On its own and in AI Hub, and the shipped page actually starts.** With no hub session the full story and local couch co-op for up to four seats run as a normal game, and About says network play is AI Hub only. In a session the page follows AI Hub shared-world: the `hub:session` meta tags from PLAN.md §16 (`players` 1-4, mode shared-world, minutes 5, the goal and howto strings, `hub:webgpu` required), no title menu, a pre-op card, ready without an extra press once the sim can run, the Gala Shift from the session seed, host-sequenced hands so every copy’s state hash matches, and a finish payload with standings. `npm run verify` is the one test command: it prints only failures and one summary line, and a green run reports zero failures. Loading the deliverable HTML from `file://` twice yields the same success: no page errors, the engine ready, the drawing surface at the requested size, and a substantially filled view of the title or an operation (a blank headless WebGPU screenshot is not this check; the engine’s own shot or the game’s state is). A driven pad or key input changes that observable.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: In the dev workspace, add or extend the engine’s existing `npm test` suite so it calls the shipped sim from a fresh start (not a copy of the rules). Assert tool-on-target effects and a refusal for every pair that has no effect, rating bands, vitals and clock failure, slip limit, flatline Code success and failure, other sites worsening while away, Stillpoint and Second Pulse, Consult, each strain’s distinguishing transition (including Mercy’s three phases), difficulty scaling, and balance sliders staying in range with reset restoring defaults. Capture the run to `{SCRATCH}/sim.log`. Pass only if those assertions pass.\\n2. gating: Same suite: for three seeds, the shipped bot wins each of the eight story cases on Resident from that case’s own start; Cases 1 and 8 also on Intern and Attending; a 2-seat and a 4-seat bot run finish. A separate test drives the shipped flow from the title through story, all eight operations (bot), debrief, credits, and Free Surgery unlocked, with no browser. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/bot.log`. Pass only if each case win and the ending unlock are reported, not merely that the process exited.\\n20→3. gating: Assert story and case data cover the §8 case list and §9 arc, every scene is 12–35 short boxes, postcard lines exist per rank, voice synthesis is deterministic per line, and a scan of game text finds no never-use names and no TODO, placeholder, or lorem. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/content.log`.\\n4. gating: From the dev folder run `npm run verify` (it must set a short `TMPDIR` itself). Capture the full log to `{SCRATCH}/verify.log`. Pass only if the process exits 0 and the last line is `verify: <N> passed, 0 failed`. The command must include the sim tests above, the engine check, and browser checks against the root deliverable HTML, not only against a dev server.\\n5. gating: Launch the deliverable HTML from `file://` twice, sequentially, in headless Chromium via the already-installed browser (`CHROMIUM_PATH`, WebGPU software flags, short `TMPDIR`, no `playwright install`). Each run: zero page errors, engine ready, canvas pixel size equal to the requested size, and either the engine shot path or in-page state shows a substantially filled title or operating field (painted coverage of most of the surface, not a few pixels). Inject a standard-mapping stub pad or a key and assert a real change (menu focus moves, or an operation cursor or tool changes). Write `{SCRATCH}/launch-1/` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2/` (log, state, shot). Both runs must succeed the same way. If the browser process cannot start, save that output as `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.log`; sim tests in steps 1–3 then stand as the launch bar. A shot that succeeds and is blank or nearly empty is a failure, not an unavailable readback. Do not treat a known-blank WebGPU canvas bitmap from a plain screenshot as the field when the engine shot or state readback shows the scene.\\n6. gating: Against that same HTML, scripted checks (DOM and engine state, not a watched playthrough) must show: attract starts after 10 seconds of no input and a key, a mouse move, a pad button, and a stick past the dead zone each exit it with nothing saved; glyphs follow the last device and the Xbox setting; Dev Mode opens from pause, one slider changes the live value, reset restores it; two stub pads claim two couch seats and move two cursors; an AnalyserNode or offline render shows music present at the default 0.1 music volume and a synthesized effect, and the file contains no audio file, `<audio>` tag, or audio data URL. Touch phone and tablet profiles, tapped via the touchscreen API, show exactly “Controller or keyboard required”, a working settings control, no engine touch layer, and attract still running. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/ux.log`.\\n7. gating: Serve the deliverable with `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (record the printed port and PID). Run the AI Hub harness scenarios from PLAN.md §16 (`players=2&auto=1`, `players=4` with latency 150, jitter 50, loss 5, `scenario=host-leave`, `scenario=server`) and require `window.__hubCheck.pass` plus zero state-hash mismatches. Also open the game with no session and confirm the title flow. Stop that server by its PID. A Node test of the shipped sequencer (host plus three clients, latency, jitter, 5% loss on unreliable input, a leaver) must keep hashes equal across 3600 ticks. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`. Run the four-browser harness alone. If earlyoom kills it, capture that text and retry that run once; if it still dies, the 2-player harness plus the Node sequencer test are the bar only when `{SCRATCH}/hub-oom.log` shows the kill. Do not claim the 4-player run passed.\\n8. gating: Load the HTML in Firefox and in WebKit with an iPhone profile (system Python Playwright 1.63, no browser install). Pass only if both show the plain WebGPU-required message and do not run the operation. Capture the visible text to `{SCRATCH}/nowebgpu.log`. This is not iOS Safari.\\n9. evidence: Engine shots at 1920×1080 of the title, board, a story scene, pause, dev mode, handbook, and the field in each case, under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Absence does not fail the goal when steps 1–8 passed.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- FPS targets, optimization, or raising sheet density past what software WebGPU can keep up in a session.\\n30→- Any campaign past the eight story cases, the Gala Shift, and Free Surgery, or Trauma Team specialties (endoscopy, orthopedics, forensics, diagnosis).\\n- Source names, the star gesture, copied levels, sentences, UI, or music. A physical DualSense, a real second person, a listener, a television, or real iOS Safari.\\n- Touch-screen surgery controls, online play other than AI Hub shared-world, new npm dependencies, and any edit to engine sources.\\n- Exact untuned coefficients from PLAN.md where it says numbers are starting values.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work folder only. Other agents may use sibling folders, shared ports, and `/tmp`; game files stay in this folder. A short `/tmp` `TMPDIR` is allowed only for tool sockets, and it is removed at the end.\\n- Dev subfolder: `git clone` of `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge` (full workspace, not the lean page; `docs/API.md` wins if HEAD is newer than `e7a1243`), then `npm ci`. Game code, data, tests, and the verify script are added on top. Chromium for engine tools comes from the existing Playwright cache via `CHROMIUM_PATH`. Do not run `scripts/setup.sh` or `playwright install`.\\n- AI Hub client from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub` (`session.js`), pasted unmodified. Harness files used only under the dev folder’s test output.\\n- Hand-ins in the work-folder root: the HTML deliverable, `exit-interview.json`, `PROGRESS.md`.\\n40→- Defining source mechanics (Wikipedia; StrategyWiki gameplay page) are first-person timed surgery, vitals, a radial of scalpel, forceps, suture, drain, laser, syringe, gel, and scanner, action grades and letter ranks, a time-slow gift and a second gift that rewards clean actions, defibrillator, parasite bosses, and visual-novel scenes between operations. PLAN.md already remaps these to original names; criteria use that remap.\",\"content_concise\":\"1→# Plan: Build PLAN.md’s original surgical-action demo and hand in every TASK.md file this computer can produce\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. **Hand-ins match TASK.md.** The work-folder root contains one self-contained HTML deliverable (no CDN or other network fetch required to boot, including the AI Hub client inlined unmodified and first) whose basename is the TASK.md pattern `[GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731]`: GameTitle is the title actually shown on the title screen, SOURCE identifies Trauma Center: Second Opinion, and the run id `grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731` is verbatim. Spaces or colons in those names are slug-normalized the same way in the filename and in the dev subfolder of that same basename. Beside the HTML is `exit-interview.json`, one JSON object in exactly TASK.md’s shape, every value filled (unknown only where it cannot be confirmed), `usage` exactly `added on import`. `aiHub.name` is the title-screen title; type Game; genre Simulation; source Trauma Center: Second Opinion; players 1–4. The same Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback appear on an in-game About/Credits screen (paged, big text), and usage is in an element `data-about=\\\"usage\\\"`. `PROGRESS.md` in the work folder records a finished game. The dev subfolder is a full my-3dge workspace with the game added on top and the engine’s tracked sources unmodified. A testing issue log and an engine-feedback note exist and are what the interview answers come from. No TODOs, placeholders, stubs, or lorem. None of PLAN.md §1’s never-use names, art, music, UI, levels, or sentences appear (the title may riff on the source name; “second opinion” only as an ordinary dialogue phrase).\\n\\n2. **The operation is recognizably Trauma Center play, under this plan’s original rules.** Checked from each case’s own start, not one chained marathon: first-person surgery on a living field, with a clock and vitals, lost if time runs out, if slips pass the limit, or if a flatline Code fails, and won only by a closing phase. Eight tools — scalpel, forceps, suture, drain, laser, syringe, gel, scanner — each visibly change tissue on a valid target (cut along a guide, pull a shard or mass, zigzag stitches, shrink a pool, burn, inject within a band, paint gel, reveal buried targets) and otherwise refuse with no penalty and a named right tool. Finished actions rate TEXTBOOK, CLEAN, ROUGH, or SLIP and feed a chain and ranks C, B, A, S, and ★. One to three sites keep deteriorating while the surgeon is elsewhere. Stillpoint slows field time; Second Pulse blocks vitals loss and heals on clean actions; both arm from a beat-timed three-press pulse-lock, with a one-press Simple Gift option; two gifts started within 1 second grant Consult. Five strains fight with a telegraph before each hit: Lull (quieting aura, cocoon, armored Matron), Solace (split by scalpel into smaller generations, mother under a flap), Reverie (hidden until scanned, decoys), Balm (lacquer blocks stitch and drain until the node dies), Mercy (mote shield, a heart-stop Code, then a burrow with a moving guide, tendrils, and a final shared Code). Eight story cases each introduce the new rule named in PLAN.md §8 (tools through Mercy), plus session case `gala-shift` and Free Surgery after the ending. Intern, Resident, and Attending scale drain, clock, parasite stats, and the slip limit. A bot using the same hands input as a player wins every story case on Resident from that case’s start. Numeric thresholds may be tuned; these categories and failure rules may not.\\n10→\\n3. **World, story, audio, and the field read as this hospital, not a generic sim.** The slice follows PLAN.md §9’s original cast and arc (Port Verity, Harbor Light General, Halcyon grafts, eight cases through the line CASE FILE REMAINS OPEN) in short earnest overheated medical-thriller lines, gibberish Animalese-style voices with large captions, skippable scenes, rank-based patient postcards, and multiple Drama Cuts. The operating field is a 3D sheet that breathes and beats, shows wounds, pools, and hands that swap tools, inside an operating room. Art starts from the engine’s native procedural look (full resolution, not the chunky pixel default) and adds original hospital and surgical assets; animation uses the engine’s system plus new story and field actions for the beats PLAN.md §12 lists. Every piece of music, effect, and voice is synthesized in code: no audio files, no `<audio>`, no sound data URLs. Music defaults to 10% volume.\\n\\n4. **Couch, controller-first screens, attract, dev mode, and the no-WebGPU / touch gates.** The same screens are playable with a standard-mapping pad (left stick cursor, right stick radial, R2 or Cross to use, L1/R1 cycle tools, L2 precision, Triangle gift, Square quick syringe, Circle drop, D-pad sites and hint, R3 swap where cases allow, Options pause, both triggers for paddles) and, natively, with mouse and keyboard as PLAN.md §10 lists (absolute mouse aim, LMB use, RMB radial, wheel and keys 1–8, WASD or arrows plus Enter). Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs for any pad by default, Xbox glyphs (A B X Y, LB RB LT RT) when that Settings option is on, keycaps and mouse icons for keyboard and mouse. After 10 seconds with no input on the title or front menus, a looping attract demo of the real game starts, any input exits it immediately, and it saves nothing. Pause opens Dev Mode (pad, mouse, and keyboard) whose Balance tab has live sliders for the main variables (vitals drain, bleeds, clock, slips, cursor, each tool rate, gift timing, strain stats, co-op health) and a reset to defaults. A Handbook, driven by the tool and target data, states what each instrument can and cannot do. Touch-only devices get no touch controls, the exact text “Controller or keyboard required”, a settings button, and attract underneath. If WebGPU is missing, a classic inline script shows a large plain message that the game needs WebGPU (and calls the hub fail hook in a session) instead of only the engine’s technical error.\\n\\n5. **On its own and in AI Hub, and the shipped page actually starts.** With no hub session the full story and local couch co-op for up to four seats run as a normal game, and About says network play is AI Hub only. In a session the page follows AI Hub shared-world: the `hub:session` meta tags from PLAN.md §16 (`players` 1-4, mode shared-world, minutes 5, the goal and howto strings, `hub:webgpu` required), no title menu, a pre-op card, ready without an extra press once the sim can run, the Gala Shift from the session seed, host-sequenced hands so every copy’s state hash matches, and a finish payload with standings. `npm run verify` is the one test command: it prints only failures and one summary line, and a green run reports zero failures. Loading the deliverable HTML from `file://` twice yields the same success: no page errors, the engine ready, the drawing surface at the requested size, and a substantially filled view of the title or an operation (a blank headless WebGPU screenshot is not this check; the engine’s own shot or the game’s state is). A driven pad or key input changes that observable.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: In the dev workspace, add or extend the engine’s existing `npm test` suite so it calls the shipped sim from a fresh start (not a copy of the rules). Assert tool-on-target effects and a refusal for every pair that has no effect, rating bands, vitals and clock failure, slip limit, flatline Code success and failure, other sites worsening while away, Stillpoint and Second Pulse, Consult, each strain’s distinguishing transition (including Mercy’s three phases), difficulty scaling, and balance sliders staying in range with reset restoring defaults. Capture the run to `{SCRATCH}/sim.log`. Pass only if those assertions pass.\\n2. gating: Same suite: for three seeds, the shipped bot wins each of the eight story cases on Resident from that case’s own start; Cases 1 and 8 also on Intern and Attending; a 2-seat and a 4-seat bot run finish. A separate test drives the shipped flow from the title through story, all eight operations (bot), debrief, credits, and Free Surgery unlocked, with no browser. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/bot.log`. Pass only if each case win and the ending unlock are reported, not merely that the process exited.\\n20→3. gating: Assert story and case data cover the §8 case list and §9 arc, every scene is 12–35 short boxes, postcard lines exist per rank, voice synthesis is deterministic per line, and a scan of game text finds no never-use names and no TODO, placeholder, or lorem. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/content.log`.\\n4. gating: From the dev folder run `npm run verify` (it must set a short `TMPDIR` itself). Capture the full log to `{SCRATCH}/verify.log`. Pass only if the process exits 0 and the last line is `verify: <N> passed, 0 failed`. The command must include the sim tests above, the engine check, and browser checks against the root deliverable HTML, not only against a dev server.\\n5. gating: Launch the deliverable HTML from `file://` twice, sequentially, in headless Chromium via the already-installed browser (`CHROMIUM_PATH`, WebGPU software flags, short `TMPDIR`, no `playwright install`). Each run: zero page errors, engine ready, canvas pixel size equal to the requested size, and either the engine shot path or in-page state shows a substantially filled title or operating field (painted coverage of most of the surface, not a few pixels). Inject a standard-mapping stub pad or a key and assert a real change (menu focus moves, or an operation cursor or tool changes). Write `{SCRATCH}/launch-1/` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2/` (log, state, shot). Both runs must succeed the same way. If the browser process cannot start, save that output as `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.log`; sim tests in steps 1–3 then stand as the launch bar. A shot that succeeds and is blank or nearly empty is a failure, not an unavailable readback. Do not treat a known-blank WebGPU canvas bitmap from a plain screenshot as the field when the engine shot or state readback shows the scene.\\n6. gating: Against that same HTML, scripted checks (DOM and engine state, not a watched playthrough) must show: attract starts after 10 seconds of no input and a key, a mouse move, a pad button, and a stick past the dead zone each exit it with nothing saved; glyphs follow the last device and the Xbox setting; Dev Mode opens from pause, one slider changes the live value, reset restores it; two stub pads claim two couch seats and move two cursors; an AnalyserNode or offline render shows music present at the default 0.1 music volume and a synthesized effect, and the file contains no audio file, `<audio>` tag, or audio data URL. Touch phone and tablet profiles, tapped via the touchscreen API, show exactly “Controller or keyboard required”, a working settings control, no engine touch layer, and attract still running. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/ux.log`.\\n7. gating: Serve the deliverable with `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (record the printed port and PID). Run the AI Hub harness scenarios from PLAN.md §16 (`players=2&auto=1`, `players=4` with latency 150, jitter 50, loss 5, `scenario=host-leave`, `scenario=server`) and require `window.__hubCheck.pass` plus zero state-hash mismatches. Also open the game with no session and confirm the title flow. Stop that server by its PID. A Node test of the shipped sequencer (host plus three clients, latency, jitter, 5% loss on unreliable input, a leaver) must keep hashes equal across 3600 ticks. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`. Run the four-browser harness alone. If earlyoom kills it, capture that text and retry that run once; if it still dies, the 2-player harness plus the Node sequencer test are the bar only when `{SCRATCH}/hub-oom.log` shows the kill. Do not claim the 4-player run passed.\\n8. gating: Load the HTML in Firefox and in WebKit with an iPhone profile (system Python Playwright 1.63, no browser install). Pass only if both show the plain WebGPU-required message and do not run the operation. Capture the visible text to `{SCRATCH}/nowebgpu.log`. This is not iOS Safari.\\n9. evidence: Engine shots at 1920×1080 of the title, board, a story scene, pause, dev mode, handbook, and the field in each case, under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Absence does not fail the goal when steps 1–8 passed.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- FPS targets, optimization, or raising sheet density past what software WebGPU can keep up in a session.\\n30→- Any campaign past the eight story cases, the Gala Shift, and Free Surgery, or Trauma Team specialties (endoscopy, orthopedics, forensics, diagnosis).\\n- Source names, the star gesture, copied levels, sentences, UI, or music. A physical DualSense, a real second person, a listener, a television, or real iOS Safari.\\n- Touch-screen surgery controls, online play other than AI Hub shared-world, new npm dependencies, and any edit to engine sources.\\n- Exact untuned coefficients from PLAN.md where it says numbers are starting values.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work folder only. Other agents may use sibling folders, shared ports, and `/tmp`; game files stay in this folder. A short `/tmp` `TMPDIR` is allowed only for tool sockets, and it is removed at the end.\\n- Dev subfolder: `git clone` of `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge` (full workspace, not the lean page; `docs/API.md` wins if HEAD is newer than `e7a1243`), then `npm ci`. Game code, data, tests, and the verify script are added on top. Chromium for engine tools comes from the existing Playwright cache via `CHROMIUM_PATH`. Do not run `scripts/setup.sh` or `playwright install`.\\n- AI Hub client from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub` (`session.js`), pasted unmodified. Harness files used only under the dev folder’s test output.\\n- Hand-ins in the work-folder root: the HTML deliverable, `exit-interview.json`, `PROGRESS.md`.\\n40→- Defining source mechanics (Wikipedia; StrategyWiki gameplay page) are first-person timed surgery, vitals, a radial of scalpel, forceps, suture, drain, laser, syringe, gel, and scanner, action grades and letter ranks, a time-slow gift and a second gift that rewards clean actions, defibrillator, parasite bosses, and visual-novel scenes between operations. PLAN.md already remaps these to original names; criteria use that remap.\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3%2Fwork/01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151/goal/plan.md\",\"offset\":1,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"# Plan: Build PLAN.md’s original surgical-action demo and hand in every TASK.md file this computer can produce\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. **Hand-ins match TASK.md.** The work-folder root contains one self-contained HTML deliverable (no CDN or other network fetch required to boot, including the AI Hub client inlined unmodified and first) whose basename is the TASK.md pattern `[GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731]`: GameTitle is the title actually shown on the title screen, SOURCE identifies Trauma Center: Second Opinion, and the run id `grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731` is verbatim. Spaces or colons in those names are slug-normalized the same way in the filename and in the dev subfolder of that same basename. Beside the HTML is `exit-interview.json`, one JSON object in exactly TASK.md’s shape, every value filled (unknown only where it cannot be confirmed), `usage` exactly `added on import`. `aiHub.name` is the title-screen title; type Game; genre Simulation; source Trauma Center: Second Opinion; players 1–4. The same Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback appear on an in-game About/Credits screen (paged, big text), and usage is in an element `data-about=\\\"usage\\\"`. `PROGRESS.md` in the work folder records a finished game. The dev subfolder is a full my-3dge workspace with the game added on top and the engine’s tracked sources unmodified. A testing issue log and an engine-feedback note exist and are what the interview answers come from. No TODOs, placeholders, stubs, or lorem. None of PLAN.md §1’s never-use names, art, music, UI, levels, or sentences appear (the title may riff on the source name; “second opinion” only as an ordinary dialogue phrase).\\n\\n2. **The operation is recognizably Trauma Center play, under this plan’s original rules.** Checked from each case’s own start, not one chained marathon: first-person surgery on a living field, with a clock and vitals, lost if time runs out, if slips pass the limit, or if a flatline Code fails, and won only by a closing phase. Eight tools — scalpel, forceps, suture, drain, laser, syringe, gel, scanner — each visibly change tissue on a valid target (cut along a guide, pull a shard or mass, zigzag stitches, shrink a pool, burn, inject within a band, paint gel, reveal buried targets) and otherwise refuse with no penalty and a named right tool. Finished actions rate TEXTBOOK, CLEAN, ROUGH, or SLIP and feed a chain and ranks C, B, A, S, and ★. One to three sites keep deteriorating while the surgeon is elsewhere. Stillpoint slows field time; Second Pulse blocks vitals loss and heals on clean actions; both arm from a beat-timed three-press pulse-lock, with a one-press Simple Gift option; two gifts started within 1 second grant Consult. Five strains fight with a telegraph before each hit: Lull (quieting aura, cocoon, armored Matron), Solace (split by scalpel into smaller generations, mother under a flap), Reverie (hidden until scanned, decoys), Balm (lacquer blocks stitch and drain until the node dies), Mercy (mote shield, a heart-stop Code, then a burrow with a moving guide, tendrils, and a final shared Code). Eight story cases each introduce the new rule named in PLAN.md §8 (tools through Mercy), plus session case `gala-shift` and Free Surgery after the ending. Intern, Resident, and Attending scale drain, clock, parasite stats, and the slip limit. A bot using the same hands input as a player wins every story case on Resident from that case’s start. Numeric thresholds may be tuned; these categories and failure rules may not.\\n\\n3. **World, story, audio, and the field read as this hospital, not a generic sim.** The slice follows PLAN.md §9’s original cast and arc (Port Verity, Harbor Light General, Halcyon grafts, eight cases through the line CASE FILE REMAINS OPEN) in short earnest overheated medical-thriller lines, gibberish Animalese-style voices with large captions, skippable scenes, rank-based patient postcards, and multiple Drama Cuts. The operating field is a 3D sheet that breathes and beats, shows wounds, pools, and hands that swap tools, inside an operating room. Art starts from the engine’s native procedural look (full resolution, not the chunky pixel default) and adds original hospital and surgical assets; animation uses the engine’s system plus new story and field actions for the beats PLAN.md §12 lists. Every piece of music, effect, and voice is synthesized in code: no audio files, no `<audio>`, no sound data URLs. Music defaults to 10% volume.\\n\\n4. **Couch, controller-first screens, attract, dev mode, and the no-WebGPU / touch gates.** The same screens are playable with a standard-mapping pad (left stick cursor, right stick radial, R2 or Cross to use, L1/R1 cycle tools, L2 precision, Triangle gift, Square quick syringe, Circle drop, D-pad sites and hint, R3 swap where cases allow, Options pause, both triggers for paddles) and, natively, with mouse and keyboard as PLAN.md §10 lists (absolute mouse aim, LMB use, RMB radial, wheel and keys 1–8, WASD or arrows plus Enter). Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs for any pad by default, Xbox glyphs (A B X Y, LB RB LT RT) when that Settings option is on, keycaps and mouse icons for keyboard and mouse. After 10 seconds with no input on the title or front menus, a looping attract demo of the real game starts, any input exits it immediately, and it saves nothing. Pause opens Dev Mode (pad, mouse, and keyboard) whose Balance tab has live sliders for the main variables (vitals drain, bleeds, clock, slips, cursor, each tool rate, gift timing, strain stats, co-op health) and a reset to defaults. A Handbook, driven by the tool and target data, states what each instrument can and cannot do. Touch-only devices get no touch controls, the exact text “Controller or keyboard required”, a settings button, and attract underneath. If WebGPU is missing, a classic inline script shows a large plain message that the game needs WebGPU (and calls the hub fail hook in a session) instead of only the engine’s technical error.\\n\\n5. **On its own and in AI Hub, and the shipped page actually starts.** With no hub session the full story and local couch co-op for up to four seats run as a normal game, and About says network play is AI Hub only. In a session the page follows AI Hub shared-world: the `hub:session` meta tags from PLAN.md §16 (`players` 1-4, mode shared-world, minutes 5, the goal and howto strings, `hub:webgpu` required), no title menu, a pre-op card, ready without an extra press once the sim can run, the Gala Shift from the session seed, host-sequenced hands so every copy’s state hash matches, and a finish payload with standings. `npm run verify` is the one test command: it prints only failures and one summary line, and a green run reports zero failures. Loading the deliverable HTML from `file://` twice yields the same success: no page errors, the engine ready, the drawing surface at the requested size, and a substantially filled view of the title or an operation (a blank headless WebGPU screenshot is not this check; the engine’s own shot or the game’s state is). A driven pad or key input changes that observable.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: In the dev workspace, add or extend the engine’s existing `npm test` suite so it calls the shipped sim from a fresh start (not a copy of the rules). Assert tool-on-target effects and a refusal for every pair that has no effect, rating bands, vitals and clock failure, slip limit, flatline Code success and failure, other sites worsening while away, Stillpoint and Second Pulse, Consult, each strain’s distinguishing transition (including Mercy’s three phases), difficulty scaling, and balance sliders staying in range with reset restoring defaults. Capture the run to `{SCRATCH}/sim.log`. Pass only if those assertions pass.\\n2. gating: Same suite: for three seeds, the shipped bot wins each of the eight story cases on Resident from that case’s own start; Cases 1 and 8 also on Intern and Attending; a 2-seat and a 4-seat bot run finish. A separate test drives the shipped flow from the title through story, all eight operations (bot), debrief, credits, and Free Surgery unlocked, with no browser. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/bot.log`. Pass only if each case win and the ending unlock are reported, not merely that the process exited.\\n3. gating: Assert story and case data cover the §8 case list and §9 arc, every scene is 12–35 short boxes, postcard lines exist per rank, voice synthesis is deterministic per line, and a scan of game text finds no never-use names and no TODO, placeholder, or lorem. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/content.log`.\\n4. gating: From the dev folder run `npm run verify` (it must set a short `TMPDIR` itself). Capture the full log to `{SCRATCH}/verify.log`. Pass only if the process exits 0 and the last line is `verify: <N> passed, 0 failed`. The command must include the sim tests above, the engine check, and browser checks against the root deliverable HTML, not only against a dev server.\\n5. gating: Launch the deliverable HTML from `file://` twice, sequentially, in headless Chromium via the already-installed browser (`CHROMIUM_PATH`, WebGPU software flags, short `TMPDIR`, no `playwright install`). Each run: zero page errors, engine ready, canvas pixel size equal to the requested size, and either the engine shot path or in-page state shows a substantially filled title or operating field (painted coverage of most of the surface, not a few pixels). Inject a standard-mapping stub pad or a key and assert a real change (menu focus moves, or an operation cursor or tool changes). Write `{SCRATCH}/launch-1/` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2/` (log, state, shot). Both runs must succeed the same way. If the browser process cannot start, save that output as `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.log`; sim tests in steps 1–3 then stand as the launch bar. A shot that succeeds and is blank or nearly empty is a failure, not an unavailable readback. Do not treat a known-blank WebGPU canvas bitmap from a plain screenshot as the field when the engine shot or state readback shows the scene.\\n6. gating: Against that same HTML, scripted checks (DOM and engine state, not a watched playthrough) must show: attract starts after 10 seconds of no input and a key, a mouse move, a pad button, and a stick past the dead zone each exit it with nothing saved; glyphs follow the last device and the Xbox setting; Dev Mode opens from pause, one slider changes the live value, reset restores it; two stub pads claim two couch seats and move two cursors; an AnalyserNode or offline render shows music present at the default 0.1 music volume and a synthesized effect, and the file contains no audio file, `<audio>` tag, or audio data URL. Touch phone and tablet profiles, tapped via the touchscreen API, show exactly “Controller or keyboard required”, a working settings control, no engine touch layer, and attract still running. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/ux.log`.\\n7. gating: Serve the deliverable with `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (record the printed port and PID). Run the AI Hub harness scenarios from PLAN.md §16 (`players=2&auto=1`, `players=4` with latency 150, jitter 50, loss 5, `scenario=host-leave`, `scenario=server`) and require `window.__hubCheck.pass` plus zero state-hash mismatches. Also open the game with no session and confirm the title flow. Stop that server by its PID. A Node test of the shipped sequencer (host plus three clients, latency, jitter, 5% loss on unreliable input, a leaver) must keep hashes equal across 3600 ticks. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`. Run the four-browser harness alone. If earlyoom kills it, capture that text and retry that run once; if it still dies, the 2-player harness plus the Node sequencer test are the bar only when `{SCRATCH}/hub-oom.log` shows the kill. Do not claim the 4-player run passed.\\n8. gating: Load the HTML in Firefox and in WebKit with an iPhone profile (system Python Playwright 1.63, no browser install). Pass only if both show the plain WebGPU-required message and do not run the operation. Capture the visible text to `{SCRATCH}/nowebgpu.log`. This is not iOS Safari.\\n9. evidence: Engine shots at 1920×1080 of the title, board, a story scene, pause, dev mode, handbook, and the field in each case, under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Absence does not fail the goal when steps 1–8 passed.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- FPS targets, optimization, or raising sheet density past what software WebGPU can keep up in a session.\\n- Any campaign past the eight story cases, the Gala Shift, and Free Surgery, or Trauma Team specialties (endoscopy, orthopedics, forensics, diagnosis).\\n- Source names, the star gesture, copied levels, sentences, UI, or music. A physical DualSense, a real second person, a listener, a television, or real iOS Safari.\\n- Touch-screen surgery controls, online play other than AI Hub shared-world, new npm dependencies, and any edit to engine sources.\\n- Exact untuned coefficients from PLAN.md where it says numbers are starting values.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work folder only. Other agents may use sibling folders, shared ports, and `/tmp`; game files stay in this folder. A short `/tmp` `TMPDIR` is allowed only for tool sockets, and it is removed at the end.\\n- Dev subfolder: `git clone` of `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge` (full workspace, not the lean page; `docs/API.md` wins if HEAD is newer than `e7a1243`), then `npm ci`. Game code, data, tests, and the verify script are added on top. Chromium for engine tools comes from the existing Playwright cache via `CHROMIUM_PATH`. Do not run `scripts/setup.sh` or `playwright install`.\\n- AI Hub client from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub` (`session.js`), pasted unmodified. Harness files used only under the dev folder’s test output.\\n- Hand-ins in the work-folder root: the HTML deliverable, `exit-interview.json`, `PROGRESS.md`.\\n- Defining source mechanics (Wikipedia; StrategyWiki gameplay page) are first-person timed surgery, vitals, a radial of scalpel, forceps, suture, drain, laser, syringe, gel, and scanner, action grades and letter ranks, a time-slow gift and a second gift that rewards clean actions, defibrillator, parasite bosses, and visual-novel scenes between operations. PLAN.md already remaps these to original names; criteria use that remap.\\n\",\"total_lines\":66}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-e7aa46b2-7e1c-47c6-9d8e-c4e87974a83f-32","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"180→    if (!shielded && !op.holdVitals) patient.vitals -= drain * seconds * scale * diff;\\n    if (op.attract) patient.vitals = Math.max(30, patient.vitals);\\n    patient.vitals = Math.min(100, patient.vitals);\\n    if (patient.vitals <= 0) flatline(op, patient.id);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n/** Vitals hit zero. */\\nfunction flatline(op: Op, patientId: number): void {\\n  const patient = op.patients[patientId];\\n190→  patient.vitals = 0;\\n  if (op.code) return;\\n  if (!op.codeAllowed || (patient.coded && op.id !== 'case:mercy')) {\\n    patient.alive = false;\\n    failOp(op, `${patient.name} is gone.`);\\n    return;\\n  }\\n  patient.coded = op.id !== 'case:mercy';\\n  op.code = { left: 8 * FIELD_SECOND, tries: 2, patient: patientId, free: op.id === 'case:mercy', shocks: 0 };\\n  op.events.push({ type: 'code', text: 'Paddles.' });\\n200→}\\n\\n/** Gifts, the Code timer, the clock, and the phase. */\\nexport function stepBody(op: Op, ft: number): void {\\n  tickLocks(op);\\n  for (const seat of op.seats) if (seat.giftLeft > 0 && seat.lock < 0) seat.giftLeft -= 1;\\n  if (op.code) {\\n    op.code.left -= ft;\\n    if (op.code.left <= 0) {\\n      op.code = null;\\n210→      failOp(op, 'The line stays flat.');\\n    }\\n  }\\n  if (!op.attract && !op.holdClock) op.clock -= ft / Math.max(0.4, op.balance.clock);\\n  if (op.clock <= 0 && op.status === 'playing') {\\n    op.clock = 0;\\n    failOp(op, 'Time.');\\n  }\\n  stepColleague(op);\\n  advance(op);\",\"content_concise\":\"180→    if (!shielded && !op.holdVitals) patient.vitals -= drain * seconds * scale * diff;\\n    if (op.attract) patient.vitals = Math.max(30, patient.vitals);\\n    patient.vitals = Math.min(100, patient.vitals);\\n    if (patient.vitals <= 0) flatline(op, patient.id);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n/** Vitals hit zero. */\\nfunction flatline(op: Op, patientId: number): void {\\n  const patient = op.patients[patientId];\\n190→  patient.vitals = 0;\\n  if (op.code) return;\\n  if (!op.codeAllowed || (patient.coded && op.id !== 'case:mercy')) {\\n    patient.alive = false;\\n    failOp(op, `${patient.name} is gone.`);\\n    return;\\n  }\\n  patient.coded = op.id !== 'case:mercy';\\n  op.code = { left: 8 * FIELD_SECOND, tries: 2, patient: patientId, free: op.id === 'case:mercy', shocks: 0 };\\n  op.events.push({ type: 'code', text: 'Paddles.' });\\n200→}\\n\\n/** Gifts, the Code timer, the clock, and the phase. */\\nexport function stepBody(op: Op, ft: number): void {\\n  tickLocks(op);\\n  for (const seat of op.seats) if (seat.giftLeft > 0 && seat.lock < 0) seat.giftLeft -= 1;\\n  if (op.code) {\\n    op.code.left -= ft;\\n    if (op.code.left <= 0) {\\n      op.code = null;\\n210→      failOp(op, 'The line stays flat.');\\n    }\\n  }\\n  if (!op.attract && !op.holdClock) op.clock -= ft / Math.max(0.4, op.balance.clock);\\n  if (op.clock <= 0 && op.status === 'playing') {\\n    op.clock = 0;\\n    failOp(op, 'Time.');\\n  }\\n  stepColleague(op);\\n  advance(op);\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/op/body.ts\",\"offset\":180,\"limit\":40,\"raw_output\":\"    if (!shielded && !op.holdVitals) patient.vitals -= drain * seconds * scale * diff;\\n    if (op.attract) patient.vitals = Math.max(30, patient.vitals);\\n    patient.vitals = Math.min(100, patient.vitals);\\n    if (patient.vitals <= 0) flatline(op, patient.id);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n/** Vitals hit zero. */\\nfunction flatline(op: Op, patientId: number): void {\\n  const patient = op.patients[patientId];\\n  patient.vitals = 0;\\n  if (op.code) return;\\n  if (!op.codeAllowed || (patient.coded && op.id !== 'case:mercy')) {\\n    patient.alive = false;\\n    failOp(op, `${patient.name} is gone.`);\\n    return;\\n  }\\n  patient.coded = op.id !== 'case:mercy';\\n  op.code = { left: 8 * FIELD_SECOND, tries: 2, patient: patientId, free: op.id === 'case:mercy', shocks: 0 };\\n  op.events.push({ type: 'code', text: 'Paddles.' });\\n}\\n\\n/** Gifts, the Code timer, the clock, and the phase. */\\nexport function stepBody(op: Op, ft: number): void {\\n  tickLocks(op);\\n  for (const seat of op.seats) if (seat.giftLeft > 0 && seat.lock < 0) seat.giftLeft -= 1;\\n  if (op.code) {\\n    op.code.left -= ft;\\n    if (op.code.left <= 0) {\\n      op.code = null;\\n      failOp(op, 'The line stays flat.');\\n    }\\n  }\\n  if (!op.attract && !op.holdClock) op.clock -= ft / Math.max(0.4, op.balance.clock);\\n  if (op.clock <= 0 && op.status === 'playing') {\\n    op.clock = 0;\\n    failOp(op, 'Time.');\\n  }\\n  stepColleague(op);\\n  advance(op);\\n\",\"total_lines\":297}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"e3ad3235-c2ba-4243-8450-4a2b8b70023b"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_884","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The test should hold the paddle for several off-band steps and assert the game remains active with tries left.\n\nThe test passed. The evaluator wants `npm run verify` re-run. A subtle bug in body.ts:132 remains. The hold timing logic appears correct.\n\nThe line number in body.ts:132 is outdated; the guard now sits at line 49. The code appears correct. A tick-by-tick probe will be run to document the exact skeptic scenario. The test starts at flatline rather than past it, so it may not be testing the intended boundary.\n\nstepOp is the only entry point for paddle presses, so pulseEdges and hands cannot bypass prevPaddle. A null-hands edge case was noted but ruled out. Multiple seats holding paddles in one step would both trigger pressPaddle, though this concerns a single controller hand rather than two separate seats. The next steps are to run the tick-by-tick probe, save the logs, and then execute npm run verify.\n\nThe old verify.log will be archived as verify-r4.log before the new run. The probe script will write to scratch and import from the package source via tsx.\n\nThe probe script will import from absolute paths under `/abs/labs/game/op/` so tsx can resolve the package modules.\n\nThe probe will loop through eight ticks with the paddle held, logging tick, status, tries, codeBand, fail, and prevPaddle. A second run will release and press twice off band to confirm two misses still fail. Both outputs go to scratch/paddle-hold.log. The gating sim.log will be refreshed from the current vitest run. Verify starts only after the probe finishes.\n\nThe probe is CPU-only and fast. I will check for other verify processes in this directory before starting. I also need to read the end of verify.mjs to match its summary line format.\n\nA static analyzer might miss the `!seat.prevPaddle` guard if its snapshot is stale. Moving that edge check inside `pressPaddle` itself would be redundant. `makeSeat` initializes `prevPaddle`, and an undefined value on old saves would still trigger a press on the first step, so no extra initialization is needed.\n\nI checked that `createFromCase` and seat copies preserve `prevPaddle`, so hash restores do not reset it. The test correctly includes both a scenario that idles first and one that starts directly at flatline, avoiding any charge of starting past the boundary.\n\nA monitor script will watch the verify process and check its log for a zero-failure exit. The run is expected to last about eleven minutes.\n\nThe monitor script will watch the verify log file for an EXIT: line instead of using a shell PID. The command writes its own process ID and exit status into scratch/verify.log, so the monitor can detect completion without needing the original shell pid.\n\nThe monitor script will wait for the `EXIT:` line in the log before judging success or failure. Verify continues running even after an early step fails, so it must reach the final exit status to know the true result.\n\nI will block this turn for the full verify run, using a 15-minute timeout so I can capture the actual summary line before claiming completion. The skill normally recommends backgrounding long jobs, but blocking is acceptable here.\n\nI will background verify and monitor it, then do other safe work before waiting for the result. I must not edit code or start browsers during the run, since verify reads files at each phase and changes would muddy the results. I am checking whether anything needs fixing before starting.","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-b857d0d6-e25b-40e9-ba59-55e675d362a3-33","name":"grep","input":{"pattern":"verify:","path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/tools/verify.mjs"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"call-b857d0d6-e25b-40e9-ba59-55e675d362a3-34","name":"run_terminal_command","input":{"command":"PKG=\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731\"\nSCRATCH=\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/tmp/grok-goal-0593a9c03d8d/implementer\"\n# busy check: only this package's verify\nbusy=0\nfor pid in $(pgrep -f 'labs/game/tools/verify.mjs' || true); do\n  cwd=$(readlink -f /proc/$pid/cwd 2>/dev/null || true)\n  if [ \"$cwd\" = \"$PKG\" ]; then echo \"BUSY $pid\"; busy=1; fi\ndone\nif [ \"$busy\" = 0 ]; then echo FREE; fi\ncat > \"$SCRATCH/paddle-hold.mts\" << 'EOF'\nimport { stepOp } from \"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/op/step.ts\";\nimport { addTarget, createBare, idleHand } from \"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/op/world.ts\";\nimport { codeBand } from \"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/Trauma-Compass-Trauma-Center-Second-Opinion-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731/labs/game/op/body.ts\";\n\nconst op = createBare({ vitals: 1 });\naddTarget(op, { kind: \"mass\", x: 6, z: 4, hidden: false, locked: true }, 0);\nop.patients[0].vitals = 0;\nfor (let i = 0; i < 8; i++) {\n  const hands = op.seats.map((seat, index) => (index === 0 ? { ...idleHand(seat), paddle: true } : idleHand(seat)));\n  stepOp(op, hands);\n  console.log(JSON.stringify({\n    step: i + 1,\n    tick: op.tick,\n    status: op.status,\n    fail: op.fail,\n    band: codeBand(op),\n    tries: op.code?.tries ?? null,\n    prevPaddle: op.seats[0].prevPaddle,\n  }));\n}\nconst ok = op.tick >= 3 && op.status === \"playing\" && op.code !== null && (op.code?.tries ?? 0) > 0;\nconsole.log(ok ? \"HOLD_OK\" : \"HOLD_FAIL\");\nif (!ok) process.exit(1);\nEOF\ncd \"$PKG\"\ntd=$(mktemp -d /tmp/g.XXXXXX)\nexport TMPDIR=\"$td\"\n./node_modules/.bin/tsx \"$SCRATCH/paddle-hold.mts\" > \"$SCRATCH/paddle-hold.log\" 2>&1\necho PROBE:$? >> \"$SCRATCH/paddle-hold.log\"\nrm -rf \"$td\"\nunset TMPDIR\ncat \"$SCRATCH/paddle-hold.log\"","description":"Probe held paddles from flatline via stepOp"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":5486,"output_tokens":3118,"cache_read_input_tokens":69376,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0}},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"4cd4d030-9fa5-438e-82ad-afef3e45f53b"}
