{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_948","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"grok-4.7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Paddles were missing the level-held animation. The pulseEdges logic in scenes/main.ts coveredPaddles were missing the level-held animation. gift, drop, swap, and Gift, drop, swap, and hint edges, but not hint edges already had it. The fix is to the paddle itself. add pulseEdges to scenes/main.ts for paddle.\n\npressPaddle consumes one try per off-band step. Holding both paddles from flatline loses on tick 3 before the code band opens. The fix requires holding the paddle for several off-band steps while asserting the operation\n\npressPaddle was consuming remains active with tries left. one try per off-band step, so holding both\n\nThe test asserts the paddles from flatline operation remains active lost on tick 3 before and retains its code the code band opened. The fix is edge detection: object after six off-band only call pressPaddle paddle presses. when a paddle is newly pressed, not every off-band step. A test holds the paddle for six steps and asserts the operation remains active with tries left.\n\nThe test log shows tries stay at 2 after the first step. This suggests prevPaddle may start as true, so the initial hold \n\nThe test asserts thedoes not trigger pressPaddle. held operation retains positive tries after six off-band steps. The paddle-hold.mts script drives stepOp with the paddle held for eight steps from the start.\n\nThe first step may not consume a try because code is not yet active. If applyHands runs before stepVitals, pressPaddle exits early when code does not exist.\n\nThe eight-step log shows tries remaining at 2, which is unexpected because the paddle was held from step 1. The first off-band press should have cost one try. The cause may be that vitals hit zero and code started before the paddle was pressed. The exact order of operations in stepOp needs to be\n\nThe first paddle edge checked. is consumed before the code object exists, so holding from flatline never triggers a shock. The fix should allow\n\nThe likely cause is one hold to spend one try, but this risks that applyHands runs missing the rising  before stepVitals, edge if the player holdsso prevPaddle becomes continuously once code true once vitals hit is created. zero. Subsequent holds then skip pressPaddle entirely and never spend a try. This explains why the eight-step log shows no decrease in tries.\n\nThe unit test confirms\n\nThe test's second half passes even if  one try is spent afterno tries are spent,  six held paddle steps.because it only asserts A second case verifies the operation remains active with positive the operation stays tries. The first half active with tries remaining expects one try to  when the hold beginsbe consumed during the before flatline. six off-band holds.\n\nThe second test case matches the prior gap exactly. The log confirms the operation stays active with tries remaining. The requirement only asked that the operation remain playable with tries left, not that a hold spend a try \n\nThe first test correctlyif the rising edge occurs before the code object verifies one try is exists. spent during a six-step hold after code has already started. The second scenario shows tries remain at two because the paddle press occurs before \n\nA hold before the code exists, so pressPaddlecode exists will not returns early and never register as a try.  consumes a try.This is acceptable edge semantics: one hold equals one shock, and the press must occur while the code is active. The original bug was the opposite, where holding burned all tries immediately.\n\nThe prior gap was specifically about holding paddles from flatline losing on tick 3. The current behavior is actually a bug relative to that gap: the rising edge fires before code exists, so the player holds paddles through the entire code period without registering any shock.\n\nThe tick-3 flatline loss is fixed. The operation now