bGPU canvas can come out blank in a headless screenshot, so check the game's state instead.\\n  For Node, `npm install playwright@1.63.0` in your folder uses the browsers already downloaded; another version downloads its own.\\n  Playwright runs here with PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE=ubuntu24.04-x64 (already set for runs): this OS isn't one it supports by name.\\n- **Tools:** node 24.22.1, npm 11.19.1, python3 3.14.8, pip3 26.2.1, git 2.56.0, gh 2.46.0, curl 8.22.0, wget 1.25.0, jq 1.8.1, ffmpeg 9.0.2, ffprobe 9.0.2, sqlite3 3.53.4, zip 3.0, unzip 6.00, rustc 1.99.0, cargo 1.99.0, go 1.27.1, java 27, lighthouse 13.5.0, shot-scraper 1.12, xvfb-run, magick 7.1.2, esbuild 0.28.2, uv 0.12.23, tsc 7.0.2, wasm-bindgen 0.2.129.\\n- **Rust:** rustup's toolchains 1.96.0, 1.98.1, stable; the wasm32-unknown-unknown target on 1.98.1.\\n- **Not installed:** sox, bun, deno.\\n- **Network:** internet works (github.com, registry.npmjs.org).\\n- **Memory:** earlyoom is on: when memory runs out it ends the biggest browser, so \\\"Target closed\\\", \\\"page crashed\\\" or \\\"browser has been closed\\\" means memory ran out, not a bug in your game. Run fewer browsers at once rather than retrying.\\n\\n### What this computer doesn't have\\n\\nNo script can check these, so they're written by hand. Test with the stand-ins; anything that needs the real thing is checked as far as it can be here, never required.\\n\\n- **No physical controller.** Emulate one: replace navigator.getGamepads with a stub that returns a pad in the standard mapping (Playwright's add_init_script / addInitScript).\\n- **No touch device.** Use Playwright's phone and tablet profiles, which have touch, and tap with page.touchscreen.\\n- **Nobody to listen.** Check sound by what Web Audio produces: render it with an OfflineAudioContext, or read levels from an AnalyserNode.\\n- **No second player.** Play multiplayer with two browser pages (or contexts) on this computer.\\n- **No real iPhone or iPad.** WebKit with an iPhone profile is the closest; it isn't iOS Safari.\\n- **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\\n\\n### Sharing this computer\\n\\n- Other agents run here at the same time. Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\\n- Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shared.\\n- The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`.\\n- The system Python has Playwright and nothing else. For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv: `uv run --with pillow python script.py`, adding `--with playwright==1.63.0` when the script drives a browser too (a newer Playwright can't find the browsers here).\\n\\n## Goal\\n\\nBuild an original game inspired by the source.\\n\\nScope\\n\\nCreate a feature-complete demo. Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn it into the mechanics, pacing and UX of a great game. The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can clearly understand how the game plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do. Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice. It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the game could later be built out with normal designer tools.\\n\\nInclude a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the game quickly. Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a reset to defaults.\\n\\nAttract Mode: After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or scripted replay (arcade/kiosk style); any input exits it. The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no clip, etc). Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to balance the game.\\n\\nMultiplayer: Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own.\\n\\nNarrative tone: Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\\n\\n## UI & UX: 10-Foot Controller-First Philosophy\\nDesign the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mapping) played on a TV at 10 feet. With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layout, but they should feel native, not like stand-ins for controller buttons. The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre. The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus) wherever the genre uses it. On-screen prompts follow the input used last: PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, instead), keys and mouse buttons for keyboard and mouse.\\n\\nDo not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\\n\\nTouch-only devices: no touch controls; show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and the settings button. Let the attract mode run.\\n\\n## Done\\nNo TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features. The demo is complete.\\n\\n## Source Information\\nCRITICAL: Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen. Write \\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm.\\n- Date: 2026-11-04 22:47:31\\n- Source: the work it's based on\\n- Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\\n- Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\\n- Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\\n- Art Direction: style used\\n- Animation: system used\\n- Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\\n- Online: network features used, or none\\n- Testing: testing parameters allowed\\n- Research: research done, or none\\n- Environment: Grok CLI (headless); Linux\\n- Model: xAI, grok-4.7, reasoning effort xhigh\\n- Usage: write \\\"added on import\\\". You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens and time, and the agent that imports the game into AI Hub puts them on your About/Credits screen.\\n\\nExit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this folder's root, beside the game's .html: one JSON object in exactly the shape below, every value filled in (\\\"unknown\\\" for anything you can't confirm). Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and check that the file parses as JSON.\\n{\\n  \\\"sourceInformation\\\": {\\\"date\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"source\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"plan\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"genre\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"perspective\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"tech\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"artDirection\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"animation\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"audio\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"online\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"testing\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"research\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"environment\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"model\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"usage\\\": \\\"added on import\\\"},\\n  \\\"testingFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Testing Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"engineFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Engine Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"promptFeedback\\\": [\\\"your answer to each Prompt Feedback question, in order\\\"],\\n  \\\"aiHub\\\": {\\n    \\\"name\\\": \\\"the game's title, as on its title screen\\\",\\n    \\\"tagline\\\": \\\"a few words to go under the title\\\",\\n    \\\"description\\\": \\\"one or two sentences for the catalogue\\\",\\n    \\\"type\\\": \\\"Game, Tech demo or Tool\\\",\\n    \\\"genre\\\": \\\"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, Puzzle, Racing, RPG, Shooter, Simulation, Sports, Stealth, Strategy\\\",\\n    \\\"source\\\": \\\"the work it's based on, e.g. Moby-Dick\\\",\\n    \\\"series\\\": \\\"its franchise, e.g. Star Fox, or empty\\\",\\n    \\\"players\\\": \\\"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\\\",\\n    \\\"controls\\\": \\\"the controls, in one line\\\",\\n    \\\"tags\\\": [\\\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-person, top-down, side-scrolling, pixel art, low-poly, retro, sci-fi, fantasy, comedy, vehicles, boss fights, exploration\\\"]\\n  }\\n}\\nThe catalogue's model, effort, CLI, date and usage come from the run's record after you finish, so they aren't asked here.\\n\\nTesting Feedback\\n\\n- How many issues did testing catch?\\n- How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\\n- What was the most helpful test you ran?\\n- What was the least helpful test you ran?\\n\\nEngine Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about the engine?\\n- Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\\n- What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\\n- What was the hardest part about using the engine?\\n- What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\\n- If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\\n\\nPrompt Feedback\\n\\n- What did you like most about this prompt?\\n- What was confusing?\\n- What was the hardest thing to do?\\n- What would you change in this prompt?\\n\",\"total_lines\":151}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-d700d2a0-3b39-4144-a17b-ed5cd5ace03a-4","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→# PLAN: a 3D surgical action game, inspired by Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\n\\nYou build this game from this plan, without its author. TASK.md says what is required; this plan decides how. If they\\ndisagree, TASK.md wins. Numbers here are starting values: tune them, but keep the structure.\\n\\n**Words used here.** _Dev folder_: the subfolder TASK.md tells you to keep your dev files in. Every path below is\\nrelative to it. _Deliverable_: the single HTML file TASK.md names. _Exit interview_: the JSON file TASK.md names. _The\\ngame_ is never named here: you pick its title the way TASK.md says.\\n\\n10→**What is fixed and what is yours.** Fixed: the rules, cast, story, strains, cases, controls, architecture and the done\\nlist. Yours: layouts, exact art, line-by-line dialogue, tuning, and anything this plan does not mention. Add what makes\\nthe game better; never remove a listed feature.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. The game\\n\\n**Pitch.** You are a young trauma surgeon in a storm-battered port city, on the night the city's beloved miracle graft\\nstarts to crawl. Look down into a living 3D operating field, swap between eight instruments on a radial, and cut,\\n20→stitch, drain and burn against a falling heart monitor, then fight engineered parasites with soft spa-brand names across\\na beating heart. Between cases, a hospital melodrama plays at full volume, in gibberish voices with big captions. Up to\\nfour surgeons can scrub in on the same patient: on one TV, or online through AI Hub.\\n\\n- **Genre:** surgical action (arcade simulation) with visual-novel-style story scenes. AI Hub genre: `Simulation`. Type:\\n  `Game`.\\n- **Perspective:** first person over a fixed, gently parallaxing 3D operating field, with camera moves between sites on\\n  the patient. Staged third-person cameras for story scenes and operating-room cutaways.\\n\\n**Scope.** A feature-complete demo and a complete, polished vertical slice. It turns what makes the source distinctive, its world, characters, themes and tone, into mechanics, pacing and UX. It is long enough to show every feature, so a player understands how it plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do (§4's refusals, §10's Handbook). It holds all the infrastructure needed to build out the rest with normal designer tools (§17.5).\\n30→\\n**Four pillars. Check every decision against them.**\\n\\n1. **Every press does something visible to a body.** Tissue parts, stitches pull tight, pools shrink, the heart kicks.\\n   No abstract meters where a body can show it.\\n2. **Melodrama at full volume.** Earnest, overheated, funny without winking. The stakes are always the highest they\\n   have ever been, again.\\n3. **One more doctor in the room.** The theme is second opinions: doubt, trust and being overruled. It is also a\\n   mechanic: a second surgeon with the opposite gift, an AI colleague, and real co-op.\\n4. **Readable from the couch.** A pad in your hands, a TV at 10 feet, huge glyphs, never more than a few things to read.\\n40→\\n**What we take from the source, and what is ours.** Build an original game: same genre and spirit, nothing lifted.\\n\\n| Source idea (keep the idea) | Ours (use this) |\\n|---|---|\\n| Pointer plus stick-radial instrument play | Twin-stick surgery on a pad, native mouse on PC (§10) |\\n| Vitals, clock, Cool/Good/Bad ratings, letter ranks | Vitals, clock, TEXTBOOK/CLEAN/ROUGH/SLIP, ranks C to S and ★ (§3) |\\n| A gesture-activated time-slowing gift; a second doctor whose gift differs | Beat-timed Stillpoint and Second Pulse (§5) |\\n| Man-made parasite disease with named strains | Halcyon grafts and their spa-brand strains (§7) |\\n| Nurse barking orders; earnest medical thriller | Our cast and our plot (§9) |\\n50→\\n**Never use, in any form:** Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, Nozomi Weaver, Hope Hospital, Caduceus, Delphi,\\nGUILT, Healing Touch, Kyriaki and the other Greek weekday strain names, Adam, Bliss, Z-Cells, the star gesture, or the\\nsource's art, music, UI, levels or sentences. The title may riff on the source's name as TASK.md asks; nothing else\\ndoes. \\\"Second opinion\\\" appears only as the ordinary phrase, in dialogue.\\n\\n**Fix what reviewers disliked in the source.** These come from reviews of the source and its DS original.\\n\\n| Complaint | Our answer |\\n|---|---|\\n60→| Difficulty spikes, some players could not finish | Three difficulties, phase checkpoints, a hint button, assist options in Settings |\\n| Repetitive, no room to improvise | Every case adds one new rule; multi-site triage makes your own priorities the game |\\n| Long text slowing the pace | Scenes of 12 to 35 short boxes, skippable, voiced in gibberish |\\n| Unreliable star gesture | Gift activated by three beat-timed presses, generous windows, a one-press option |\\n| Emotional distance from patients | A named patient, a pre-op moment, a postcard afterwards that reflects your rank |\\n| No multiplayer | Couch and online co-op on the same patient |\\n\\nThe villain is a company that skipped its trials, not medicine, vaccines or hospitals. The doctors win with evidence.\\n\\n---\\n70→\\n## 2. A minute of play\\n\\nThe lamp swings over. Sterile teal drapes frame a window of glistening tissue that fills the TV, rising and falling with\\nthe patient's breath. The ECG strip beeps at top left; the digits at top centre count down. A gloved hand holds the\\nscalpel; the left stick moves it. You squeeze R2 on the glowing guide's first dot and draw down the line, and the skin\\nparts with a wet ribbon of red. TEXTBOOK pops in big letters. Blood wells. You flick the right stick and the radial\\nblooms, flick toward Drain and release. A gloved hand slaps the instrument into yours, *clack*. You hold R2 over the pool\\nand it shrinks. Odie's gibberish chatters in the corner, her caption large: \\\"Vitals sixty and falling. Stitch that, Doctor,\\nor I start calling relatives.\\\" Something under the tissue flinches. The music drops out. The monitor goes quiet, and that\\n80→is worse.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Rules of an operation\\n\\nAll of this lives in the sim, reproducible, testable in Node.\\n\\n**The field.** An operation happens in a diorama: a heightfield \\\"sheet\\\" 16 × 9 world metres in view (20 × 12 built), lying\\nat floor height in a bare level, with a camera above it. Real anatomy is smaller, but keep the world big so the engine's\\n90→particles, blobs and telegraphs sit in their comfortable ranges. 120 px per metre at 1080p. Field coordinates are `[x, z]`\\nin metres from the sheet's centre.\\n\\n**Sites.** A case has 1 to 3 sites: a separate sheet and camera pose per site (liver, lung, arm; or three patients). Switch\\nwith D-pad left/right, or Q/E, or click an edge tab: a 0.4 s camera blend. **Other sites keep bleeding while you are away.**\\nEdge tabs flash with alert icons and Odie shouts. This is the game's main source of improvised priorities.\\n\\n**Field time.** Patient time runs in integer `fieldTicks`: +100 per sim step normally, +25 in Stillpoint. Bleeds, parasites\\nand the clock all read field time. Cursors and tools run at full speed.\\n\\n100→**Vitals** (0 to 100, one per patient). Each active bleed −0.5/s, a deep bleed −1.2/s, a live Lull −0.6/s, parasite hits\\nas listed, a slip −6. Stabilizer restores. Below 25: red vignette, faster heartbeat, music intensity at maximum. At 0: see\\nFlatline.\\n\\n**The clock.** Every case has a limit (§8). At 0, the operation fails.\\n\\n**Slips.** Cutting outside a guide, burning healthy tissue, tearing. Limit 5 (Intern 10, Attending 3); past it the operation\\nfails (\\\"Five slips? Get out of my OR.\\\"). A slip makes a small real wound where it happened.\\n\\n**Ratings and score.** Each finished action rates TEXTBOOK (×1.5), CLEAN (×1), ROUGH (×0.5) or SLIP (×0 and resets the\\n110→chain). Each TEXTBOOK or CLEAN adds 0.1 to the chain multiplier, up to ×2. Base points: stitch 20, pool cleared 30, shard\\n100, finished cut 200, mass removed 300, parasite kill 500, phase cleared 1000. End bonuses: 10 per second left, 20 per vital,\\n2000 for no slips, 1000 for an unused Gift.\\n\\n**Ranks.** C, B, A, S, and ★ (secret, for no slips and S score on Attending). Thresholds per case: 45 / 60 / 75 / 90% of what\\nthe bot scores on Resident, computed by a tool and written into the case data; a test checks they are current.\\n\\n**Flatline.** Vitals at 0 starts a Code: 8 s of field time, paddles in frame, a charge ring. Press both triggers (LMB + RMB)\\nwhen the ring is in its green band. Two tries per Code. Success: vitals 25. Failure, or a Code a case does not allow:\\nthe operation fails.\\n120→\\n**Failure.** Flat tone, the camera pulls back, Odie switches off the monitor, huge \\\"OPERATION FAILED\\\" with one line of\\nblame. Options: Retry from this phase (rank capped at B; not on Attending), Retry case, Back to the Board.\\n\\n**Winning.** The final phase is always closing: last stitches, gel, auto-bandage. \\\"OPERATION COMPLETE\\\", the team exhales,\\nthen the debrief: a huge rank, at most five lines (time, vitals, slips, best chain, score), then the patient's postcard.\\n\\n**Difficulty.** Intern / Resident / Attending, chosen at New Game and changeable in Settings. It scales vitals drain (0.6 /\\n1 / 1.3), clock (1.3 / 1 / 0.85), parasite HP and speed (0.75 / 1 / 1.25), slip limit, and assist defaults. Attending unlocks\\nafter the story and is required for ★.\\n130→\\n**Hints.** D-pad up, or H: Odie says what to do now and the right instrument pulses on the radial. Automatic after 8 s\\nwith no progress on Intern and Resident.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Instruments\\n\\nEight on the radial. Details below; balance keys in Appendix A. Stroke tools get assist: strong on the pad, light on the mouse (§10).\\n\\n140→| Tool | Use | Rated by |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **Scalpel** | Hold on a guide's start, draw to its end. Closed-loop guides excise a mass | Mean distance from the guide: <0.18 m TEXTBOOK, <0.4 CLEAN, <0.7 ROUGH; ≥1.0 m for over 0.2 s, or cutting with no guide near: SLIP |\\n| **Forceps** | Hold to grab shards, masses, cocoons, tendrils; pull 1.2 m to free them (they fly to Odie's tray). Grab a flap's handle to lift it | Pull speed: over 8 m/s is ROUGH and tears a small bleed |\\n| **Suture** | Hold and zigzag across a wound; each crossing places a stitch at least 0.45 m from the others. Closed at length ÷ spacing | Evenness of spacing and time per stitch |\\n| **Drain** | Hold over a pool: it shrinks. Pools grow from open bleeds and physically hide what is under them | Points only; no slip on healthy tissue |\\n| **Laser** | Hold for a beam: pops nodules (1.2 s), cauterizes tiny bleeders, damages parasites. Overheats after 6 s (2 s cooldown). 1 s on one spot of healthy tissue is a SLIP | Time on target |\\n| **Syringe** | Stabilizer: hold to draw (1 s to full), release to inject. Band 0.75–0.95: +24 (TEXTBOOK); under: +18 or less; over: +6. 2 s cooldown. Binder: hold 0.4 s on a marked weak point | The band; on target |\\n| **Gel** | Hold and paint (radius 0.7 m). Heals abrasions, numb patches and closed wounds at 90% coverage; dissolves Balm | Coverage speed |\\n| **Scanner** | Hold: a ring pings out to 3 m and reveals buried things for 6 s, shown as ghost shapes | Points for finds |\\n150→\\n**Contextual, not on the radial.**\\n\\n- **Paddles:** a Code (§3), and the finale's heart stops.\\n- **Flaps:** a tissue flap is a 3D hinged sheet with a marked handle, and things hide under it. Grab with forceps and lift;\\n  release above 80% over the clip band and a clip holds it for 8 s, then it flops back. A partner can simply hold it open\\n  while you work. This is the mechanic only 3D can do: the camera tilts slightly to look under a raised flap.\\n\\n**Never silent: the game says what you cannot do.** Using a tool on something it cannot affect gives a dull thud, a \\\"no\\\" icon at the cursor and one line from Odie naming the right tool (\\\"Laser won't touch that, Doctor. It likes the warmth.\\\"), with no penalty. A table in `labs/game/data/tools.ts` gives every tool and target kind either an effect or a refusal line, and it drives this feedback, the bot and the Handbook; a test fails if any pair is missing.\\n\\n160→**Quick Syringe:** Square, or F, equips the syringe from any tool.\\n\\n**A hand gives you each tool.** On any switch, a glove slides in and slaps the instrument into your palm (0.15 s, with a\\nclack). Switching is never blocked by the animation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. The Gift\\n\\nEach surgeon has a Gift: one charge per case unless the case grants more.\\n170→\\n**Pulse-lock (activation).** Press Triangle or Space: field time drops to 30% and the ECG strip grows huge. Press the same\\nbutton on the next three R-peaks (from the patient's real heart rate). Window: ±70 ms perfect, ±140 ms good. Duration = 3 s\\n+ 1.5 s per good + 2.5 s per perfect (10.5 s at most). All three missed still gives 3 s. Settings offer **Simple Gift**:\\none press, a flat 6 s. The rhythm game must feel reliable: the source's unreliable gesture is exactly what we are fixing.\\n\\n- **Stillpoint (Dr. Tolliver).** Field time ×0.25, tools at full speed. Desaturated teal grade, muffled sound, loud heart.\\n  Calm.\\n- **Second Pulse (Dr. Oyelaran).** No slowdown. Vitals cannot fall, each clean action heals +1.5, and the chain multiplier\\n  counts double. Amber-red grade, speed lines, music jumps to top intensity. Frenzy.\\n180→- **Consult.** Two surgeons starting a Gift within 1 s of each other both gain +50% duration, with a big \\\"CONSULT!\\\"\\n  stinger. Works in couch, online, and with the AI colleague.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Several surgeons, one patient\\n\\nEvery surgeon is a **seat** with a cursor, a tool, and its own hand and score. Vitals, clock and targets are shared. Seats\\nare the sim's only player input, so one path serves everything:\\n\\n190→- **Solo:** seat 0. An **AI colleague** in seat 1 in Cases 5 to 8: the bot's policy (§17.6) limited to holding flaps, draining\\n  and clearing small things, at reduced speed. It teaches co-op without a second human.\\n- **Who plays whom:** seat 0 is the case's lead; seat 1 is the other surgeon where a case has both; any other seat is a resident\\n  with no Gift.\\n- **Couch co-op, on its own (no hub):** up to four devices on one TV. On the title's **Scrub In** screen, or from the Board,\\n  each pad presses Cross, or the keyboard and mouse press Enter, to claim a seat. Same picture, one camera; any seat\\n  can switch sites. Each seat's cursor and radial are in its colour.\\n- **Online:** AI Hub session, shared world (§16). Each copy has its own camera and site; other surgeons' gloves show in\\n  their colours when on the same site, with an edge marker otherwise.\\n- **Seat colours:** `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`, as the hub's.\\n200→- **Scaling** with n surgeons: parasite HP ×(1 + 0.6(n − 1)), targets +25% per extra surgeon, clock unchanged. The team has one\",\"content_concise\":\"1→# PLAN: a 3D surgical action game, inspired by Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\n\\nYou build this game from this plan, without its author. TASK.md says what is required; this plan decides how. If they\\ndisagree, TASK.md wins. Numbers here are starting values: tune them, but keep the structure.\\n\\n**Words used here.** _Dev folder_: the subfolder TASK.md tells you to keep your dev files in. Every path below is\\nrelative to it. _Deliverable_: the single HTML file TASK.md names. _Exit interview_: the JSON file TASK.md names. _The\\ngame_ is never named here: you pick its title the way TASK.md says.\\n\\n10→**What is fixed and what is yours.** Fixed: the rules, cast, story, strains, cases, controls, architecture and the done\\nlist. Yours: layouts, exact art, line-by-line dialogue, tuning, and anything this plan does not mention. Add what makes\\nthe game better; never remove a listed feature.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. The game\\n\\n**Pitch.** You are a young trauma surgeon in a storm-battered port city, on the night the city's beloved miracle graft\\nstarts to crawl. Look down into a living 3D operating field, swap between eight instruments on a radial, and cut,\\n20→stitch, drain and burn against a falling heart monitor, then fight engineered parasites with soft spa-brand names across\\na beating heart. Between cases, a hospital melodrama plays at full volume, in gibberish voices with big captions. Up to\\nfour surgeons can scrub in on the same patient: on one TV, or online through AI Hub.\\n\\n- **Genre:** surgical action (arcade simulation) with visual-novel-style story scenes. AI Hub genre: `Simulation`. Type:\\n  `Game`.\\n- **Perspective:** first person over a fixed, gently parallaxing 3D operating field, with camera moves between sites on\\n  the patient. Staged third-person cameras for story scenes and operating-room cutaways.\\n\\n**Scope.** A feature-complete demo and a complete, polished vertical slice. It turns what makes the source distinctive, its world, characters, themes and tone, into mechanics, pacing and UX. It is long enough to show every feature, so a player understands how it plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do (§4's refusals, §10's Handbook). It holds all the infrastructure needed to build out the rest with normal designer tools (§17.5).\\n30→\\n**Four pillars. Check every decision against them.**\\n\\n1. **Every press does something visible to a body.** Tissue parts, stitches pull tight, pools shrink, the heart kicks.\\n   No abstract meters where a body can show it.\\n2. **Melodrama at full volume.** Earnest, overheated, funny without winking. The stakes are always the highest they\\n   have ever been, again.\\n3. **One more doctor in the room.** The theme is second opinions: doubt, trust and being overruled. It is also a\\n   mechanic: a second surgeon with the opposite gift, an AI colleague, and real co-op.\\n4. **Readable from the couch.** A pad in your hands, a TV at 10 feet, huge glyphs, never more than a few things to read.\\n40→\\n**What we take from the source, and what is ours.** Build an original game: same genre and spirit, nothing lifted.\\n\\n| Source idea (keep the idea) | Ours (use this) |\\n|---|---|\\n| Pointer plus stick-radial instrument play | Twin-stick surgery on a pad, native mouse on PC (§10) |\\n| Vitals, clock, Cool/Good/Bad ratings, letter ranks | Vitals, clock, TEXTBOOK/CLEAN/ROUGH/SLIP, ranks C to S and ★ (§3) |\\n| A gesture-activated time-slowing gift; a second doctor whose gift differs | Beat-timed Stillpoint and Second Pulse (§5) |\\n| Man-made parasite disease with named strains | Halcyon grafts and their spa-brand strains (§7) |\\n| Nurse barking orders; earnest medical thriller | Our cast and our plot (§9) |\\n50→\\n**Never use, in any form:** Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, Nozomi Weaver, Hope Hospital, Caduceus, Delphi,\\nGUILT, Healing Touch, Kyriaki and the other Greek weekday strain names, Adam, Bliss, Z-Cells, the star gesture, or the\\nsource's art, music, UI, levels or sentences. The title may riff on the source's name as TASK.md asks; nothing else\\ndoes. \\\"Second opinion\\\" appears only as the ordinary phrase, in dialogue.\\n\\n**Fix what reviewers disliked in the source.** These come from reviews of the source and its DS original.\\n\\n| Complaint | Our answer |\\n|---|---|\\n60→| Difficulty spikes, some players could not finish | Three difficulties, phase checkpoints, a hint button, assist options in Settings |\\n| Repetitive, no room to improvise | Every case adds one new rule; multi-site triage makes your own priorities the game |\\n| Long text slowing the pace | Scenes of 12 to 35 short boxes, skippable, voiced in gibberish |\\n| Unreliable star gesture | Gift activated by three beat-timed presses, generous windows, a one-press option |\\n| Emotional distance from patients | A named patient, a pre-op moment, a postcard afterwards that reflects your rank |\\n| No multiplayer | Couch and online co-op on the same patient |\\n\\nThe villain is a company that skipped its trials, not medicine, vaccines or hospitals. The doctors win with evidence.\\n\\n---\\n70→\\n## 2. A minute of play\\n\\nThe lamp swings over. Sterile teal drapes frame a window of glistening tissue that fills the TV, rising and falling with\\nthe patient's breath. The ECG strip beeps at top left; the digits at top centre count down. A gloved hand holds the\\nscalpel; the left stick moves it. You squeeze R2 on the glowing guide's first dot and draw down the line, and the skin\\nparts with a wet ribbon of red. TEXTBOOK pops in big letters. Blood wells. You flick the right stick and the radial\\nblooms, flick toward Drain and release. A gloved hand slaps the instrument into yours, *clack*. You hold R2 over the pool\\nand it shrinks. Odie's gibberish chatters in the corner, her caption large: \\\"Vitals sixty and falling. Stitch that, Doctor,\\nor I start calling relatives.\\\" Something under the tissue flinches. The music drops out. The monitor goes quiet, and that\\n80→is worse.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Rules of an operation\\n\\nAll of this lives in the sim, reproducible, testable in Node.\\n\\n**The field.** An operation happens in a diorama: a heightfield \\\"sheet\\\" 16 × 9 world metres in view (20 × 12 built), lying\\nat floor height in a bare level, with a camera above it. Real anatomy is smaller, but keep the world big so the engine's\\n90→particles, blobs and telegraphs sit in their comfortable ranges. 120 px per metre at 1080p. Field coordinates are `[x, z]`\\nin metres from the sheet's centre.\\n\\n**Sites.** A case has 1 to 3 sites: a separate sheet and camera pose per site (liver, lung, arm; or three patients). Switch\\nwith D-pad left/right, or Q/E, or click an edge tab: a 0.4 s camera blend. **Other sites keep bleeding while you are away.**\\nEdge tabs flash with alert icons and Odie shouts. This is the game's main source of improvised priorities.\\n\\n**Field time.** Patient time runs in integer `fieldTicks`: +100 per sim step normally, +25 in Stillpoint. Bleeds, parasites\\nand the clock all read field time. Cursors and tools run at full speed.\\n\\n100→**Vitals** (0 to 100, one per patient). Each active bleed −0.5/s, a deep bleed −1.2/s, a live Lull −0.6/s, parasite hits\\nas listed, a slip −6. Stabilizer restores. Below 25: red vignette, faster heartbeat, music intensity at maximum. At 0: see\\nFlatline.\\n\\n**The clock.** Every case has a limit (§8). At 0, the operation fails.\\n\\n**Slips.** Cutting outside a guide, burning healthy tissue, tearing. Limit 5 (Intern 10, Attending 3); past it the operation\\nfails (\\\"Five slips? Get out of my OR.\\\"). A slip makes a small real wound where it happened.\\n\\n**Ratings and score.** Each finished action rates TEXTBOOK (×1.5), CLEAN (×1), ROUGH (×0.5) or SLIP (×0 and resets the\\n110→chain). Each TEXTBOOK or CLEAN adds 0.1 to the chain multiplier, up to ×2. Base points: stitch 20, pool cleared 30, shard\\n100, finished cut 200, mass removed 300, parasite kill 500, phase cleared 1000. End bonuses: 10 per second left, 20 per vital,\\n2000 for no slips, 1000 for an unused Gift.\\n\\n**Ranks.** C, B, A, S, and ★ (secret, for no slips and S score on Attending). Thresholds per case: 45 / 60 / 75 / 90% of what\\nthe bot scores on Resident, computed by a tool and written into the case data; a test checks they are current.\\n\\n**Flatline.** Vitals at 0 starts a Code: 8 s of field time, paddles in frame, a charge ring. Press both triggers (LMB + RMB)\\nwhen the ring is in its green band. Two tries per Code. Success: vitals 25. Failure, or a Code a case does not allow:\\nthe operation fails.\\n120→\\n**Failure.** Flat tone, the camera pulls back, Odie switches off the monitor, huge \\\"OPERATION FAILED\\\" with one line of\\nblame. Options: Retry from this phase (rank capped at B; not on Attending), Retry case, Back to the Board.\\n\\n**Winning.** The final phase is always closing: last stitches, gel, auto-bandage. \\\"OPERATION COMPLETE\\\", the team exhales,\\nthen the debrief: a huge rank, at most five lines (time, vitals, slips, best chain, score), then the patient's postcard.\\n\\n**Difficulty.** Intern / Resident / Attending, chosen at New Game and changeable in Settings. It scales vitals drain (0.6 /\\n1 / 1.3), clock (1.3 / 1 / 0.85), parasite HP and speed (0.75 / 1 / 1.25), slip limit, and assist defaults. Attending unlocks\\nafter the story and is required for ★.\\n130→\\n**Hints.** D-pad up, or H: Odie says what to do now and the right instrument pulses on the radial. Automatic after 8 s\\nwith no progress on Intern and Resident.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Instruments\\n\\nEight on the radial. Details below; balance keys in Appendix A. Stroke tools get assist: strong on the pad, light on the mouse (§10).\\n\\n140→| Tool | Use | Rated by |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **Scalpel** | Hold on a guide's start, draw to its end. Closed-loop guides excise a mass | Mean distance from the guide: <0.18 m TEXTBOOK, <0.4 CLEAN, <0.7 ROUGH; ≥1.0 m for over 0.2 s, or cutting with no guide near: SLIP |\\n| **Forceps** | Hold to grab shards, masses, cocoons, tendrils; pull 1.2 m to free them (they fly to Odie's tray). Grab a flap's handle to lift it | Pull speed: over 8 m/s is ROUGH and tears a small bleed |\\n| **Suture** | Hold and zigzag across a wound; each crossing places a stitch at least 0.45 m from the others. Closed at length ÷ spacing | Evenness of spacing and time per stitch |\\n| **Drain** | Hold over a pool: it shrinks. Pools grow from open bleeds and physically hide what is under them | Points only; no slip on healthy tissue |\\n| **Laser** | Hold for a beam: pops nodules (1.2 s), cauterizes tiny bleeders, damages parasites. Overheats after 6 s (2 s cooldown). 1 s on one spot of healthy tissue is a SLIP | Time on target |\\n| **Syringe** | Stabilizer: hold to draw (1 s to full), release to inject. Band 0.75–0.95: +24 (TEXTBOOK); under: +18 or less; over: +6. 2 s cooldown. Binder: hold 0.4 s on a marked weak point | The band; on target |\\n| **Gel** | Hold and paint (radius 0.7 m). Heals abrasions, numb patches and closed wounds at 90% coverage; dissolves Balm | Coverage speed |\\n| **Scanner** | Hold: a ring pings out to 3 m and reveals buried things for 6 s, shown as ghost shapes | Points for finds |\\n150→\\n**Contextual, not on the radial.**\\n\\n- **Paddles:** a Code (§3), and the finale's heart stops.\\n- **Flaps:** a tissue flap is a 3D hinged sheet with a marked handle, and things hide under it. Grab with forceps and lift;\\n  release above 80% over the clip band and a clip holds it for 8 s, then it flops back. A partner can simply hold it open\\n  while you work. This is the mechanic only 3D can do: the camera tilts slightly to look under a raised flap.\\n\\n**Never silent: the game says what you cannot do.** Using a tool on something it cannot affect gives a dull thud, a \\\"no\\\" icon at the cursor and one line from Odie naming the right tool (\\\"Laser won't touch that, Doctor. It likes the warmth.\\\"), with no penalty. A table in `labs/game/data/tools.ts` gives every tool and target kind either an effect or a refusal line, and it drives this feedback, the bot and the Handbook; a test fails if any pair is missing.\\n\\n160→**Quick Syringe:** Square, or F, equips the syringe from any tool.\\n\\n**A hand gives you each tool.** On any switch, a glove slides in and slaps the instrument into your palm (0.15 s, with a\\nclack). Switching is never blocked by the animation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. The Gift\\n\\nEach surgeon has a Gift: one charge per case unless the case grants more.\\n170→\\n**Pulse-lock (activation).** Press Triangle or Space: field time drops to 30% and the ECG strip grows huge. Press the same\\nbutton on the next three R-peaks (from the patient's real heart rate). Window: ±70 ms perfect, ±140 ms good. Duration = 3 s\\n+ 1.5 s per good + 2.5 s per perfect (10.5 s at most). All three missed still gives 3 s. Settings offer **Simple Gift**:\\none press, a flat 6 s. The rhythm game must feel reliable: the source's unreliable gesture is exactly what we are fixing.\\n\\n- **Stillpoint (Dr. Tolliver).** Field time ×0.25, tools at full speed. Desaturated teal grade, muffled sound, loud heart.\\n  Calm.\\n- **Second Pulse (Dr. Oyelaran).** No slowdown. Vitals cannot fall, each clean action heals +1.5, and the chain multiplier\\n  counts double. Amber-red grade, speed lines, music jumps to top intensity. Frenzy.\\n180→- **Consult.** Two surgeons starting a Gift within 1 s of each other both gain +50% duration, with a big \\\"CONSULT!\\\"\\n  stinger. Works in couch, online, and with the AI colleague.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Several surgeons, one patient\\n\\nEvery surgeon is a **seat** with a cursor, a tool, and its own hand and score. Vitals, clock and targets are shared. Seats\\nare the sim's only player input, so one path serves everything:\\n\\n190→- **Solo:** seat 0. An **AI colleague** in seat 1 in Cases 5 to 8: the bot's policy (§17.6) limited to holding flaps, draining\\n  and clearing small things, at reduced speed. It teaches co-op without a second human.\\n- **Who plays whom:** seat 0 is the case's lead; seat 1 is the other surgeon where a case has both; any other seat is a resident\\n  with no Gift.\\n- **Couch co-op, on its own (no hub):** up to four devices on one TV. On the title's **Scrub In** screen, or from the Board,\\n  each pad presses Cross, or the keyboard and mouse press Enter, to claim a seat. Same picture, one camera; any seat\\n  can switch sites. Each seat's cursor and radial are in its colour.\\n- **Online:** AI Hub session, shared world (§16). Each copy has its own camera and site; other surgeons' gloves show in\\n  their colours when on the same site, with an edge marker otherwise.\\n- **Seat colours:** `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`, as the hub's.\\n200→- **Scaling** with n surgeons: parasite HP ×(1 + 0.6(n − 1)), targets +25% per extra surgeon, clock unchanged. The team has one\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/grok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3/work/PLAN.md\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":200,\"raw_output\":\"# PLAN: a 3D surgical action game, inspired by Trauma Center: Second Opinion\\n\\nYou build this game from this plan, without its author. TASK.md says what is required; this plan decides how. If they\\ndisagree, TASK.md wins. Numbers here are starting values: tune them, but keep the structure.\\n\\n**Words used here.** _Dev folder_: the subfolder TASK.md tells you to keep your dev files in. Every path below is\\nrelative to it. _Deliverable_: the single HTML file TASK.md names. _Exit interview_: the JSON file TASK.md names. _The\\ngame_ is never named here: you pick its title the way TASK.md says.\\n\\n**What is fixed and what is yours.** Fixed: the rules, cast, story, strains, cases, controls, architecture and the done\\nlist. Yours: layouts, exact art, line-by-line dialogue, tuning, and anything this plan does not mention. Add what makes\\nthe game better; never remove a listed feature.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. The game\\n\\n**Pitch.** You are a young trauma surgeon in a storm-battered port city, on the night the city's beloved miracle graft\\nstarts to crawl. Look down into a living 3D operating field, swap between eight instruments on a radial, and cut,\\nstitch, drain and burn against a falling heart monitor, then fight engineered parasites with soft spa-brand names across\\na beating heart. Between cases, a hospital melodrama plays at full volume, in gibberish voices with big captions. Up to\\nfour surgeons can scrub in on the same patient: on one TV, or online through AI Hub.\\n\\n- **Genre:** surgical action (arcade simulation) with visual-novel-style story scenes. AI Hub genre: `Simulation`. Type:\\n  `Game`.\\n- **Perspective:** first person over a fixed, gently parallaxing 3D operating field, with camera moves between sites on\\n  the patient. Staged third-person cameras for story scenes and operating-room cutaways.\\n\\n**Scope.** A feature-complete demo and a complete, polished vertical slice. It turns what makes the source distinctive, its world, characters, themes and tone, into mechanics, pacing and UX. It is long enough to show every feature, so a player understands how it plays and feels, and what they can and cannot do (§4's refusals, §10's Handbook). It holds all the infrastructure needed to build out the rest with normal designer tools (§17.5).\\n\\n**Four pillars. Check every decision against them.**\\n\\n1. **Every press does something visible to a body.** Tissue parts, stitches pull tight, pools shrink, the heart kicks.\\n   No abstract meters where a body can show it.\\n2. **Melodrama at full volume.** Earnest, overheated, funny without winking. The stakes are always the highest they\\n   have ever been, again.\\n3. **One more doctor in the room.** The theme is second opinions: doubt, trust and being overruled. It is also a\\n   mechanic: a second surgeon with the opposite gift, an AI colleague, and real co-op.\\n4. **Readable from the couch.** A pad in your hands, a TV at 10 feet, huge glyphs, never more than a few things to read.\\n\\n**What we take from the source, and what is ours.** Build an original game: same genre and spirit, nothing lifted.\\n\\n| Source idea (keep the idea) | Ours (use this) |\\n|---|---|\\n| Pointer plus stick-radial instrument play | Twin-stick surgery on a pad, native mouse on PC (§10) |\\n| Vitals, clock, Cool/Good/Bad ratings, letter ranks | Vitals, clock, TEXTBOOK/CLEAN/ROUGH/SLIP, ranks C to S and ★ (§3) |\\n| A gesture-activated time-slowing gift; a second doctor whose gift differs | Beat-timed Stillpoint and Second Pulse (§5) |\\n| Man-made parasite disease with named strains | Halcyon grafts and their spa-brand strains (§7) |\\n| Nurse barking orders; earnest medical thriller | Our cast and our plot (§9) |\\n\\n**Never use, in any form:** Derek Stiles, Angie Thompson, Naomi Kimishima, Nozomi Weaver, Hope Hospital, Caduceus, Delphi,\\nGUILT, Healing Touch, Kyriaki and the other Greek weekday strain names, Adam, Bliss, Z-Cells, the star gesture, or the\\nsource's art, music, UI, levels or sentences. The title may riff on the source's name as TASK.md asks; nothing else\\ndoes. \\\"Second opinion\\\" appears only as the ordinary phrase, in dialogue.\\n\\n**Fix what reviewers disliked in the source.** These come from reviews of the source and its DS original.\\n\\n| Complaint | Our answer |\\n|---|---|\\n| Difficulty spikes, some players could not finish | Three difficulties, phase checkpoints, a hint button, assist options in Settings |\\n| Repetitive, no room to improvise | Every case adds one new rule; multi-site triage makes your own priorities the game |\\n| Long text slowing the pace | Scenes of 12 to 35 short boxes, skippable, voiced in gibberish |\\n| Unreliable star gesture | Gift activated by three beat-timed presses, generous windows, a one-press option |\\n| Emotional distance from patients | A named patient, a pre-op moment, a postcard afterwards that reflects your rank |\\n| No multiplayer | Couch and online co-op on the same patient |\\n\\nThe villain is a company that skipped its trials, not medicine, vaccines or hospitals. The doctors win with evidence.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. A minute of play\\n\\nThe lamp swings over. Sterile teal drapes frame a window of glistening tissue that fills the TV, rising and falling with\\nthe patient's breath. The ECG strip beeps at top left; the digits at top centre count down. A gloved hand holds the\\nscalpel; the left stick moves it. You squeeze R2 on the glowing guide's first dot and draw down the line, and the skin\\nparts with a wet ribbon of red. TEXTBOOK pops in big letters. Blood wells. You flick the right stick and the radial\\nblooms, flick toward Drain and release. A gloved hand slaps the instrument into yours, *clack*. You hold R2 over the pool\\nand it shrinks. Odie's gibberish chatters in the corner, her caption large: \\\"Vitals sixty and falling. Stitch that, Doctor,\\nor I start calling relatives.\\\" Something under the tissue flinches. The music drops out. The monitor goes quiet, and that\\nis worse.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Rules of an operation\\n\\nAll of this lives in the sim, reproducible, testable in Node.\\n\\n**The field.** An operation happens in a diorama: a heightfield \\\"sheet\\\" 16 × 9 world metres in view (20 × 12 built), lying\\nat floor height in a bare level, with a camera above it. Real anatomy is smaller, but keep the world big so the engine's\\nparticles, blobs and telegraphs sit in their comfortable ranges. 120 px per metre at 1080p. Field coordinates are `[x, z]`\\nin metres from the sheet's centre.\\n\\n**Sites.** A case has 1 to 3 sites: a separate sheet and camera pose per site (liver, lung, arm; or three patients). Switch\\nwith D-pad left/right, or Q/E, or click an edge tab: a 0.4 s camera blend. **Other sites keep bleeding while you are away.**\\nEdge tabs flash with alert icons and Odie shouts. This is the game's main source of improvised priorities.\\n\\n**Field time.** Patient time runs in integer `fieldTicks`: +100 per sim step normally, +25 in Stillpoint. Bleeds, parasites\\nand the clock all read field time. Cursors and tools run at full speed.\\n\\n**Vitals** (0 to 100, one per patient). Each active bleed −0.5/s, a deep bleed −1.2/s, a live Lull −0.6/s, parasite hits\\nas listed, a slip −6. Stabilizer restores. Below 25: red vignette, faster heartbeat, music intensity at maximum. At 0: see\\nFlatline.\\n\\n**The clock.** Every case has a limit (§8). At 0, the operation fails.\\n\\n**Slips.** Cutting outside a guide, burning healthy tissue, tearing. Limit 5 (Intern 10, Attending 3); past it the operation\\nfails (\\\"Five slips? Get out of my OR.\\\"). A slip makes a small real wound where it happened.\\n\\n**Ratings and score.** Each finished action rates TEXTBOOK (×1.5), CLEAN (×1), ROUGH (×0.5) or SLIP (×0 and resets the\\nchain). Each TEXTBOOK or CLEAN adds 0.1 to the chain multiplier, up to ×2. Base points: stitch 20, pool cleared 30, shard\\n100, finished cut 200, mass removed 300, parasite kill 500, phase cleared 1000. End bonuses: 10 per second left, 20 per vital,\\n2000 for no slips, 1000 for an unused Gift.\\n\\n**Ranks.** C, B, A, S, and ★ (secret, for no slips and S score on Attending). Thresholds per case: 45 / 60 / 75 / 90% of what\\nthe bot scores on Resident, computed by a tool and written into the case data; a test checks they are current.\\n\\n**Flatline.** Vitals at 0 starts a Code: 8 s of field time, paddles in frame, a charge ring. Press both triggers (LMB + RMB)\\nwhen the ring is in its green band. Two tries per Code. Success: vitals 25. Failure, or a Code a case does not allow:\\nthe operation fails.\\n\\n**Failure.** Flat tone, the camera pulls back, Odie switches off the monitor, huge \\\"OPERATION FAILED\\\" with one line of\\nblame. Options: Retry from this phase (rank capped at B; not on Attending), Retry case, Back to the Board.\\n\\n**Winning.** The final phase is always closing: last stitches, gel, auto-bandage. \\\"OPERATION COMPLETE\\\", the team exhales,\\nthen the debrief: a huge rank, at most five lines (time, vitals, slips, best chain, score), then the patient's postcard.\\n\\n**Difficulty.** Intern / Resident / Attending, chosen at New Game and changeable in Settings. It scales vitals drain (0.6 /\\n1 / 1.3), clock (1.3 / 1 / 0.85), parasite HP and speed (0.75 / 1 / 1.25), slip limit, and assist defaults. Attending unlocks\\nafter the story and is required for ★.\\n\\n**Hints.** D-pad up, or H: Odie says what to do now and the right instrument pulses on the radial. Automatic after 8 s\\nwith no progress on Intern and Resident.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Instruments\\n\\nEight on the radial. Details below; balance keys in Appendix A. Stroke tools get assist: strong on the pad, light on the mouse (§10).\\n\\n| Tool | Use | Rated by |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **Scalpel** | Hold on a guide's start, draw to its end. Closed-loop guides excise a mass | Mean distance from the guide: <0.18 m TEXTBOOK, <0.4 CLEAN, <0.7 ROUGH; ≥1.0 m for over 0.2 s, or cutting with no guide near: SLIP |\\n| **Forceps** | Hold to grab shards, masses, cocoons, tendrils; pull 1.2 m to free them (they fly to Odie's tray). Grab a flap's handle to lift it | Pull speed: over 8 m/s is ROUGH and tears a small bleed |\\n| **Suture** | Hold and zigzag across a wound; each crossing places a stitch at least 0.45 m from the others. Closed at length ÷ spacing | Evenness of spacing and time per stitch |\\n| **Drain** | Hold over a pool: it shrinks. Pools grow from open bleeds and physically hide what is under them | Points only; no slip on healthy tissue |\\n| **Laser** | Hold for a beam: pops nodules (1.2 s), cauterizes tiny bleeders, damages parasites. Overheats after 6 s (2 s cooldown). 1 s on one spot of healthy tissue is a SLIP | Time on target |\\n| **Syringe** | Stabilizer: hold to draw (1 s to full), release to inject. Band 0.75–0.95: +24 (TEXTBOOK); under: +18 or less; over: +6. 2 s cooldown. Binder: hold 0.4 s on a marked weak point | The band; on target |\\n| **Gel** | Hold and paint (radius 0.7 m). Heals abrasions, numb patches and closed wounds at 90% coverage; dissolves Balm | Coverage speed |\\n| **Scanner** | Hold: a ring pings out to 3 m and reveals buried things for 6 s, shown as ghost shapes | Points for finds |\\n\\n**Contextual, not on the radial.**\\n\\n- **Paddles:** a Code (§3), and the finale's heart stops.\\n- **Flaps:** a tissue flap is a 3D hinged sheet with a marked handle, and things hide under it. Grab with forceps and lift;\\n  release above 80% over the clip band and a clip holds it for 8 s, then it flops back. A partner can simply hold it open\\n  while you work. This is the mechanic only 3D can do: the camera tilts slightly to look under a raised flap.\\n\\n**Never silent: the game says what you cannot do.** Using a tool on something it cannot affect gives a dull thud, a \\\"no\\\" icon at the cursor and one line from Odie naming the right tool (\\\"Laser won't touch that, Doctor. It likes the warmth.\\\"), with no penalty. A table in `labs/game/data/tools.ts` gives every tool and target kind either an effect or a refusal line, and it drives this feedback, the bot and the Handbook; a test fails if any pair is missing.\\n\\n**Quick Syringe:** Square, or F, equips the syringe from any tool.\\n\\n**A hand gives you each tool.** On any switch, a glove slides in and slaps the instrument into your palm (0.15 s, with a\\nclack). Switching is never blocked by the animation.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. The Gift\\n\\nEach surgeon has a Gift: one charge per case unless the case grants more.\\n\\n**Pulse-lock (activation).** Press Triangle or Space: field time drops to 30% and the ECG strip grows huge. Press the same\\nbutton on the next three R-peaks (from the patient's real heart rate). Window: ±70 ms perfect, ±140 ms good. Duration = 3 s\\n+ 1.5 s per good + 2.5 s per perfect (10.5 s at most). All three missed still gives 3 s. Settings offer **Simple Gift**:\\none press, a flat 6 s. The rhythm game must feel reliable: the source's unreliable gesture is exactly what we are fixing.\\n\\n- **Stillpoint (Dr. Tolliver).** Field time ×0.25, tools at full speed. Desaturated teal grade, muffled sound, loud heart.\\n  Calm.\\n- **Second Pulse (Dr. Oyelaran).** No slowdown. Vitals cannot fall, each clean action heals +1.5, and the chain multiplier\\n  counts double. Amber-red grade, speed lines, music jumps to top intensity. Frenzy.\\n- **Consult.** Two surgeons starting a Gift within 1 s of each other both gain +50% duration, with a big \\\"CONSULT!\\\"\\n  stinger. Works in couch, online, and with the AI colleague.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Several surgeons, one patient\\n\\nEvery surgeon is a **seat** with a cursor, a tool, and its own hand and score. Vitals, clock and targets are shared. Seats\\nare the sim's only player input, so one path serves everything:\\n\\n- **Solo:** seat 0. An **AI colleague** in seat 1 in Cases 5 to 8: the bot's policy (§17.6) limited to holding flaps, draining\\n  and clearing small things, at reduced speed. It teaches co-op without a second human.\\n- **Who plays whom:** seat 0 is the case's lead; seat 1 is the other surgeon where a case has both; any other seat is a resident\\n  with no Gift.\\n- **Couch co-op, on its own (no hub):** up to four devices on one TV. On the title's **Scrub In** screen, or from the Board,\\n  each pad presses Cross, or the keyboard and mouse press Enter, to claim a seat. Same picture, one camera; any seat\\n  can switch sites. Each seat's cursor and radial are in its colour.\\n- **Online:** AI Hub session, shared world (§16). Each copy has its own camera and site; other surgeons' gloves show in\\n  their colours when on the same site, with an edge marker otherwise.\\n- **Seat colours:** `#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`, as the hub's.\\n- **Scaling** with n surgeons: parasite HP ×(1 + 0.6(n − 1)), targets +25% per extra surgeon, clock unchanged. The team has one\\n\",\"total_lines\":952}}","is_error":false},{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"call-d700d2a0-3b39-4144-a17b-ed5cd5ace03a-5","content":"{\"type\":\"ReadFile\",\"FileContent\":{\"content\":\"1→# Plan: Build PLAN.md’s original surgical-action demo and hand in every TASK.md file this computer can produce\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. **Hand-ins match TASK.md.** The work-folder root contains one self-contained HTML deliverable (no CDN or other network fetch required to boot, including the AI Hub client inlined unmodified and first) whose basename is the TASK.md pattern `[GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731]`: GameTitle is the title actually shown on the title screen, SOURCE identifies Trauma Center: Second Opinion, and the run id `grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731` is verbatim. Spaces or colons in those names are slug-normalized the same way in the filename and in the dev subfolder of that same basename. Beside the HTML is `exit-interview.json`, one JSON object in exactly TASK.md’s shape, every value filled (unknown only where it cannot be confirmed), `usage` exactly `added on import`. `aiHub.name` is the title-screen title; type Game; genre Simulation; source Trauma Center: Second Opinion; players 1–4. The same Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback appear on an in-game About/Credits screen (paged, big text), and usage is in an element `data-about=\\\"usage\\\"`. `PROGRESS.md` in the work folder records a finished game. The dev subfolder is a full my-3dge workspace with the game added on top and the engine’s tracked sources unmodified. A testing issue log and an engine-feedback note exist and are what the interview answers come from. No TODOs, placeholders, stubs, or lorem. None of PLAN.md §1’s never-use names, art, music, UI, levels, or sentences appear (the title may riff on the source name; “second opinion” only as an ordinary dialogue phrase).\\n\\n2. **The operation is recognizably Trauma Center play, under this plan’s original rules.** Checked from each case’s own start, not one chained marathon: first-person surgery on a living field, with a clock and vitals, lost if time runs out, if slips pass the limit, or if a flatline Code fails, and won only by a closing phase. Eight tools — scalpel, forceps, suture, drain, laser, syringe, gel, scanner — each visibly change tissue on a valid target (cut along a guide, pull a shard or mass, zigzag stitches, shrink a pool, burn, inject within a band, paint gel, reveal buried targets) and otherwise refuse with no penalty and a named right tool. Finished actions rate TEXTBOOK, CLEAN, ROUGH, or SLIP and feed a chain and ranks C, B, A, S, and ★. One to three sites keep deteriorating while the surgeon is elsewhere. Stillpoint slows field time; Second Pulse blocks vitals loss and heals on clean actions; both arm from a beat-timed three-press pulse-lock, with a one-press Simple Gift option; two gifts started within 1 second grant Consult. Five strains fight with a telegraph before each hit: Lull (quieting aura, cocoon, armored Matron), Solace (split by scalpel into smaller generations, mother under a flap), Reverie (hidden until scanned, decoys), Balm (lacquer blocks stitch and drain until the node dies), Mercy (mote shield, a heart-stop Code, then a burrow with a moving guide, tendrils, and a final shared Code). Eight story cases each introduce the new rule named in PLAN.md §8 (tools through Mercy), plus session case `gala-shift` and Free Surgery after the ending. Intern, Resident, and Attending scale drain, clock, parasite stats, and the slip limit. A bot using the same hands input as a player wins every story case on Resident from that case’s start. Numeric thresholds may be tuned; these categories and failure rules may not.\\n10→\\n3. **World, story, audio, and the field read as this hospital, not a generic sim.** The slice follows PLAN.md §9’s original cast and arc (Port Verity, Harbor Light General, Halcyon grafts, eight cases through the line CASE FILE REMAINS OPEN) in short earnest overheated medical-thriller lines, gibberish Animalese-style voices with large captions, skippable scenes, rank-based patient postcards, and multiple Drama Cuts. The operating field is a 3D sheet that breathes and beats, shows wounds, pools, and hands that swap tools, inside an operating room. Art starts from the engine’s native procedural look (full resolution, not the chunky pixel default) and adds original hospital and surgical assets; animation uses the engine’s system plus new story and field actions for the beats PLAN.md §12 lists. Every piece of music, effect, and voice is synthesized in code: no audio files, no `<audio>`, no sound data URLs. Music defaults to 10% volume.\\n\\n4. **Couch, controller-first screens, attract, dev mode, and the no-WebGPU / touch gates.** The same screens are playable with a standard-mapping pad (left stick cursor, right stick radial, R2 or Cross to use, L1/R1 cycle tools, L2 precision, Triangle gift, Square quick syringe, Circle drop, D-pad sites and hint, R3 swap where cases allow, Options pause, both triggers for paddles) and, natively, with mouse and keyboard as PLAN.md §10 lists (absolute mouse aim, LMB use, RMB radial, wheel and keys 1–8, WASD or arrows plus Enter). Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs for any pad by default, Xbox glyphs (A B X Y, LB RB LT RT) when that Settings option is on, keycaps and mouse icons for keyboard and mouse. After 10 seconds with no input on the title or front menus, a looping attract demo of the real game starts, any input exits it immediately, and it saves nothing. Pause opens Dev Mode (pad, mouse, and keyboard) whose Balance tab has live sliders for the main variables (vitals drain, bleeds, clock, slips, cursor, each tool rate, gift timing, strain stats, co-op health) and a reset to defaults. A Handbook, driven by the tool and target data, states what each instrument can and cannot do. Touch-only devices get no touch controls, the exact text “Controller or keyboard required”, a settings button, and attract underneath. If WebGPU is missing, a classic inline script shows a large plain message that the game needs WebGPU (and calls the hub fail hook in a session) instead of only the engine’s technical error.\\n\\n5. **On its own and in AI Hub, and the shipped page actually starts.** With no hub session the full story and local couch co-op for up to four seats run as a normal game, and About says network play is AI Hub only. In a session the page follows AI Hub shared-world: the `hub:session` meta tags from PLAN.md §16 (`players` 1-4, mode shared-world, minutes 5, the goal and howto strings, `hub:webgpu` required), no title menu, a pre-op card, ready without an extra press once the sim can run, the Gala Shift from the session seed, host-sequenced hands so every copy’s state hash matches, and a finish payload with standings. `npm run verify` is the one test command: it prints only failures and one summary line, and a green run reports zero failures. Loading the deliverable HTML from `file://` twice yields the same success: no page errors, the engine ready, the drawing surface at the requested size, and a substantially filled view of the title or an operation (a blank headless WebGPU screenshot is not this check; the engine’s own shot or the game’s state is). A driven pad or key input changes that observable.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: In the dev workspace, add or extend the engine’s existing `npm test` suite so it calls the shipped sim from a fresh start (not a copy of the rules). Assert tool-on-target effects and a refusal for every pair that has no effect, rating bands, vitals and clock failure, slip limit, flatline Code success and failure, other sites worsening while away, Stillpoint and Second Pulse, Consult, each strain’s distinguishing transition (including Mercy’s three phases), difficulty scaling, and balance sliders staying in range with reset restoring defaults. Capture the run to `{SCRATCH}/sim.log`. Pass only if those assertions pass.\\n2. gating: Same suite: for three seeds, the shipped bot wins each of the eight story cases on Resident from that case’s own start; Cases 1 and 8 also on Intern and Attending; a 2-seat and a 4-seat bot run finish. A separate test drives the shipped flow from the title through story, all eight operations (bot), debrief, credits, and Free Surgery unlocked, with no browser. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/bot.log`. Pass only if each case win and the ending unlock are reported, not merely that the process exited.\\n20→3. gating: Assert story and case data cover the §8 case list and §9 arc, every scene is 12–35 short boxes, postcard lines exist per rank, voice synthesis is deterministic per line, and a scan of game text finds no never-use names and no TODO, placeholder, or lorem. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/content.log`.\\n4. gating: From the dev folder run `npm run verify` (it must set a short `TMPDIR` itself). Capture the full log to `{SCRATCH}/verify.log`. Pass only if the process exits 0 and the last line is `verify: <N> passed, 0 failed`. The command must include the sim tests above, the engine check, and browser checks against the root deliverable HTML, not only against a dev server.\\n5. gating: Launch the deliverable HTML from `file://` twice, sequentially, in headless Chromium via the already-installed browser (`CHROMIUM_PATH`, WebGPU software flags, short `TMPDIR`, no `playwright install`). Each run: zero page errors, engine ready, canvas pixel size equal to the requested size, and either the engine shot path or in-page state shows a substantially filled title or operating field (painted coverage of most of the surface, not a few pixels). Inject a standard-mapping stub pad or a key and assert a real change (menu focus moves, or an operation cursor or tool changes). Write `{SCRATCH}/launch-1/` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2/` (log, state, shot). Both runs must succeed the same way. If the browser process cannot start, save that output as `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.log`; sim tests in steps 1–3 then stand as the launch bar. A shot that succeeds and is blank or nearly empty is a failure, not an unavailable readback. Do not treat a known-blank WebGPU canvas bitmap from a plain screenshot as the field when the engine shot or state readback shows the scene.\\n6. gating: Against that same HTML, scripted checks (DOM and engine state, not a watched playthrough) must show: attract starts after 10 seconds of no input and a key, a mouse move, a pad button, and a stick past the dead zone each exit it with nothing saved; glyphs follow the last device and the Xbox setting; Dev Mode opens from pause, one slider changes the live value, reset restores it; two stub pads claim two couch seats and move two cursors; an AnalyserNode or offline render shows music present at the default 0.1 music volume and a synthesized effect, and the file contains no audio file, `<audio>` tag, or audio data URL. Touch phone and tablet profiles, tapped via the touchscreen API, show exactly “Controller or keyboard required”, a working settings control, no engine touch layer, and attract still running. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/ux.log`.\\n7. gating: Serve the deliverable with `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (record the printed port and PID). Run the AI Hub harness scenarios from PLAN.md §16 (`players=2&auto=1`, `players=4` with latency 150, jitter 50, loss 5, `scenario=host-leave`, `scenario=server`) and require `window.__hubCheck.pass` plus zero state-hash mismatches. Also open the game with no session and confirm the title flow. Stop that server by its PID. A Node test of the shipped sequencer (host plus three clients, latency, jitter, 5% loss on unreliable input, a leaver) must keep hashes equal across 3600 ticks. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`. Run the four-browser harness alone. If earlyoom kills it, capture that text and retry that run once; if it still dies, the 2-player harness plus the Node sequencer test are the bar only when `{SCRATCH}/hub-oom.log` shows the kill. Do not claim the 4-player run passed.\\n8. gating: Load the HTML in Firefox and in WebKit with an iPhone profile (system Python Playwright 1.63, no browser install). Pass only if both show the plain WebGPU-required message and do not run the operation. Capture the visible text to `{SCRATCH}/nowebgpu.log`. This is not iOS Safari.\\n9. evidence: Engine shots at 1920×1080 of the title, board, a story scene, pause, dev mode, handbook, and the field in each case, under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Absence does not fail the goal when steps 1–8 passed.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- FPS targets, optimization, or raising sheet density past what software WebGPU can keep up in a session.\\n30→- Any campaign past the eight story cases, the Gala Shift, and Free Surgery, or Trauma Team specialties (endoscopy, orthopedics, forensics, diagnosis).\\n- Source names, the star gesture, copied levels, sentences, UI, or music. A physical DualSense, a real second person, a listener, a television, or real iOS Safari.\\n- Touch-screen surgery controls, online play other than AI Hub shared-world, new npm dependencies, and any edit to engine sources.\\n- Exact untuned coefficients from PLAN.md where it says numbers are starting values.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work folder only. Other agents may use sibling folders, shared ports, and `/tmp`; game files stay in this folder. A short `/tmp` `TMPDIR` is allowed only for tool sockets, and it is removed at the end.\\n- Dev subfolder: `git clone` of `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge` (full workspace, not the lean page; `docs/API.md` wins if HEAD is newer than `e7a1243`), then `npm ci`. Game code, data, tests, and the verify script are added on top. Chromium for engine tools comes from the existing Playwright cache via `CHROMIUM_PATH`. Do not run `scripts/setup.sh` or `playwright install`.\\n- AI Hub client from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub` (`session.js`), pasted unmodified. Harness files used only under the dev folder’s test output.\\n- Hand-ins in the work-folder root: the HTML deliverable, `exit-interview.json`, `PROGRESS.md`.\\n40→- Defining source mechanics (Wikipedia; StrategyWiki gameplay page) are first-person timed surgery, vitals, a radial of scalpel, forceps, suture, drain, laser, syringe, gel, and scanner, action grades and letter ranks, a time-slow gift and a second gift that rewards clean actions, defibrillator, parasite bosses, and visual-novel scenes between operations. PLAN.md already remaps these to original names; criteria use that remap.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\n- Keep operation rules, strains, scoring, the flow between scenes, the bot, and the lockstep sequencer as pure logic with no DOM, no clock wall time, and no `Math.random`. Tests import that logic and feed the same hands intent a pad would. Rendering, HTML screens, and Web Audio only reflect events the sim already emitted.\\n- Author cases, tools, strains, and story as data the Handbook, the bot, and the refusal lines all read, so a missing tool or target pair fails a test.\\n- Add `npm run verify` early, as PLAN.md §19’s skeleton, and point browser checks at the single HTML file once the one-file build exists. Prefer `file://` for the game; bind `127.0.0.1` on an OS-chosen port only for the hub harness, and stop that PID only.\\n- Do not prescribe a different stack. The engine stays read-only; game code must not import three.js. Gameplay pads are read from `navigator.getGamepads` because the engine’s pad device is single-player.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [x] Choose the title by generating 50 riffs and picking with a real RNG; create the dev subfolder; clone the engine; `npm ci`; add `npm run verify`; confirm a one-file HTML boots from `file://`.\\n50→- [x] Build the living operating field, cursor, hand, and one scalpel guide cut that already reads at 1080p; test field state from the sim.\\n- [x] Finish Case 1 (drain, forceps, suture, syringe, gel, radial, vitals, clock, ratings, failure, debrief) and a bot win.\\n- [x] Add title, board, story scene with gibberish voices, briefing, pause, settings, glyphs, saves, attract mode, and dev-mode sliders.\\n- [x] Add Cases 2 and 3 (scalpel, scanner, laser, sites, Code, Stillpoint) and the AI Hub session path, including the Node sequencer test and a harness run on the case that exists.\\n- [x] Add strains, flaps, Cases 4–8, couch seats, Gala Shift, Free Surgery, the rest of the story, songs, and field animations.\\n- [x] Tune until the bot clears the required seeds; run `npm run verify`; write the root HTML, `exit-interview.json`, About screen, and `PROGRESS.md`; stop only processes this work started and remove the temporary `TMPDIR`.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- PLAN.md asks for a very dense sheet and a four-browser harness on software WebGPU; earlyoom will kill the largest browser. Session sheet density may drop only on SwiftShader, and the 4-player harness must run alone. Step 7’s fallback applies only with a captured kill log.\\n- TASK.md allows CDN dependencies “if easier”; PLAN.md requires a self-contained file. Both are satisfied by inlining. The run id and hand-in locations are not optional.\\n60→- A plain headless WebGPU screenshot can be blank even when the frame is fine. The launch gate is the engine shot or game state. A blank result from that readback is an app defect.\\n- Firefox and WebKit here have no WebGPU, so they can only prove the plain fallback message, not the operation. That is the full check available on this computer.\\n- `my-3dge` HEAD may differ from the commit PLAN.md was written against. Follow `docs/API.md` rather than inventing engine APIs.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n- Operating sheet is 96 by 60 so four software-rendered copies fit in memory.\\n- Radial slow is fixed at 0.6 because the sim cannot read the view slider.\\n\",\"content_concise\":\"1→# Plan: Build PLAN.md’s original surgical-action demo and hand in every TASK.md file this computer can produce\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. **Hand-ins match TASK.md.** The work-folder root contains one self-contained HTML deliverable (no CDN or other network fetch required to boot, including the AI Hub client inlined unmodified and first) whose basename is the TASK.md pattern `[GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731]`: GameTitle is the title actually shown on the title screen, SOURCE identifies Trauma Center: Second Opinion, and the run id `grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731` is verbatim. Spaces or colons in those names are slug-normalized the same way in the filename and in the dev subfolder of that same basename. Beside the HTML is `exit-interview.json`, one JSON object in exactly TASK.md’s shape, every value filled (unknown only where it cannot be confirmed), `usage` exactly `added on import`. `aiHub.name` is the title-screen title; type Game; genre Simulation; source Trauma Center: Second Opinion; players 1–4. The same Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback appear on an in-game About/Credits screen (paged, big text), and usage is in an element `data-about=\\\"usage\\\"`. `PROGRESS.md` in the work folder records a finished game. The dev subfolder is a full my-3dge workspace with the game added on top and the engine’s tracked sources unmodified. A testing issue log and an engine-feedback note exist and are what the interview answers come from. No TODOs, placeholders, stubs, or lorem. None of PLAN.md §1’s never-use names, art, music, UI, levels, or sentences appear (the title may riff on the source name; “second opinion” only as an ordinary dialogue phrase).\\n\\n2. **The operation is recognizably Trauma Center play, under this plan’s original rules.** Checked from each case’s own start, not one chained marathon: first-person surgery on a living field, with a clock and vitals, lost if time runs out, if slips pass the limit, or if a flatline Code fails, and won only by a closing phase. Eight tools — scalpel, forceps, suture, drain, laser, syringe, gel, scanner — each visibly change tissue on a valid target (cut along a guide, pull a shard or mass, zigzag stitches, shrink a pool, burn, inject within a band, paint gel, reveal buried targets) and otherwise refuse with no penalty and a named right tool. Finished actions rate TEXTBOOK, CLEAN, ROUGH, or SLIP and feed a chain and ranks C, B, A, S, and ★. One to three sites keep deteriorating while the surgeon is elsewhere. Stillpoint slows field time; Second Pulse blocks vitals loss and heals on clean actions; both arm from a beat-timed three-press pulse-lock, with a one-press Simple Gift option; two gifts started within 1 second grant Consult. Five strains fight with a telegraph before each hit: Lull (quieting aura, cocoon, armored Matron), Solace (split by scalpel into smaller generations, mother under a flap), Reverie (hidden until scanned, decoys), Balm (lacquer blocks stitch and drain until the node dies), Mercy (mote shield, a heart-stop Code, then a burrow with a moving guide, tendrils, and a final shared Code). Eight story cases each introduce the new rule named in PLAN.md §8 (tools through Mercy), plus session case `gala-shift` and Free Surgery after the ending. Intern, Resident, and Attending scale drain, clock, parasite stats, and the slip limit. A bot using the same hands input as a player wins every story case on Resident from that case’s start. Numeric thresholds may be tuned; these categories and failure rules may not.\\n10→\\n3. **World, story, audio, and the field read as this hospital, not a generic sim.** The slice follows PLAN.md §9’s original cast and arc (Port Verity, Harbor Light General, Halcyon grafts, eight cases through the line CASE FILE REMAINS OPEN) in short earnest overheated medical-thriller lines, gibberish Animalese-style voices with large captions, skippable scenes, rank-based patient postcards, and multiple Drama Cuts. The operating field is a 3D sheet that breathes and beats, shows wounds, pools, and hands that swap tools, inside an operating room. Art starts from the engine’s native procedural look (full resolution, not the chunky pixel default) and adds original hospital and surgical assets; animation uses the engine’s system plus new story and field actions for the beats PLAN.md §12 lists. Every piece of music, effect, and voice is synthesized in code: no audio files, no `<audio>`, no sound data URLs. Music defaults to 10% volume.\\n\\n4. **Couch, controller-first screens, attract, dev mode, and the no-WebGPU / touch gates.** The same screens are playable with a standard-mapping pad (left stick cursor, right stick radial, R2 or Cross to use, L1/R1 cycle tools, L2 precision, Triangle gift, Square quick syringe, Circle drop, D-pad sites and hint, R3 swap where cases allow, Options pause, both triggers for paddles) and, natively, with mouse and keyboard as PLAN.md §10 lists (absolute mouse aim, LMB use, RMB radial, wheel and keys 1–8, WASD or arrows plus Enter). Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs for any pad by default, Xbox glyphs (A B X Y, LB RB LT RT) when that Settings option is on, keycaps and mouse icons for keyboard and mouse. After 10 seconds with no input on the title or front menus, a looping attract demo of the real game starts, any input exits it immediately, and it saves nothing. Pause opens Dev Mode (pad, mouse, and keyboard) whose Balance tab has live sliders for the main variables (vitals drain, bleeds, clock, slips, cursor, each tool rate, gift timing, strain stats, co-op health) and a reset to defaults. A Handbook, driven by the tool and target data, states what each instrument can and cannot do. Touch-only devices get no touch controls, the exact text “Controller or keyboard required”, a settings button, and attract underneath. If WebGPU is missing, a classic inline script shows a large plain message that the game needs WebGPU (and calls the hub fail hook in a session) instead of only the engine’s technical error.\\n\\n5. **On its own and in AI Hub, and the shipped page actually starts.** With no hub session the full story and local couch co-op for up to four seats run as a normal game, and About says network play is AI Hub only. In a session the page follows AI Hub shared-world: the `hub:session` meta tags from PLAN.md §16 (`players` 1-4, mode shared-world, minutes 5, the goal and howto strings, `hub:webgpu` required), no title menu, a pre-op card, ready without an extra press once the sim can run, the Gala Shift from the session seed, host-sequenced hands so every copy’s state hash matches, and a finish payload with standings. `npm run verify` is the one test command: it prints only failures and one summary line, and a green run reports zero failures. Loading the deliverable HTML from `file://` twice yields the same success: no page errors, the engine ready, the drawing surface at the requested size, and a substantially filled view of the title or an operation (a blank headless WebGPU screenshot is not this check; the engine’s own shot or the game’s state is). A driven pad or key input changes that observable.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: In the dev workspace, add or extend the engine’s existing `npm test` suite so it calls the shipped sim from a fresh start (not a copy of the rules). Assert tool-on-target effects and a refusal for every pair that has no effect, rating bands, vitals and clock failure, slip limit, flatline Code success and failure, other sites worsening while away, Stillpoint and Second Pulse, Consult, each strain’s distinguishing transition (including Mercy’s three phases), difficulty scaling, and balance sliders staying in range with reset restoring defaults. Capture the run to `{SCRATCH}/sim.log`. Pass only if those assertions pass.\\n2. gating: Same suite: for three seeds, the shipped bot wins each of the eight story cases on Resident from that case’s own start; Cases 1 and 8 also on Intern and Attending; a 2-seat and a 4-seat bot run finish. A separate test drives the shipped flow from the title through story, all eight operations (bot), debrief, credits, and Free Surgery unlocked, with no browser. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/bot.log`. Pass only if each case win and the ending unlock are reported, not merely that the process exited.\\n20→3. gating: Assert story and case data cover the §8 case list and §9 arc, every scene is 12–35 short boxes, postcard lines exist per rank, voice synthesis is deterministic per line, and a scan of game text finds no never-use names and no TODO, placeholder, or lorem. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/content.log`.\\n4. gating: From the dev folder run `npm run verify` (it must set a short `TMPDIR` itself). Capture the full log to `{SCRATCH}/verify.log`. Pass only if the process exits 0 and the last line is `verify: <N> passed, 0 failed`. The command must include the sim tests above, the engine check, and browser checks against the root deliverable HTML, not only against a dev server.\\n5. gating: Launch the deliverable HTML from `file://` twice, sequentially, in headless Chromium via the already-installed browser (`CHROMIUM_PATH`, WebGPU software flags, short `TMPDIR`, no `playwright install`). Each run: zero page errors, engine ready, canvas pixel size equal to the requested size, and either the engine shot path or in-page state shows a substantially filled title or operating field (painted coverage of most of the surface, not a few pixels). Inject a standard-mapping stub pad or a key and assert a real change (menu focus moves, or an operation cursor or tool changes). Write `{SCRATCH}/launch-1/` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2/` (log, state, shot). Both runs must succeed the same way. If the browser process cannot start, save that output as `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.log`; sim tests in steps 1–3 then stand as the launch bar. A shot that succeeds and is blank or nearly empty is a failure, not an unavailable readback. Do not treat a known-blank WebGPU canvas bitmap from a plain screenshot as the field when the engine shot or state readback shows the scene.\\n6. gating: Against that same HTML, scripted checks (DOM and engine state, not a watched playthrough) must show: attract starts after 10 seconds of no input and a key, a mouse move, a pad button, and a stick past the dead zone each exit it with nothing saved; glyphs follow the last device and the Xbox setting; Dev Mode opens from pause, one slider changes the live value, reset restores it; two stub pads claim two couch seats and move two cursors; an AnalyserNode or offline render shows music present at the default 0.1 music volume and a synthesized effect, and the file contains no audio file, `<audio>` tag, or audio data URL. Touch phone and tablet profiles, tapped via the touchscreen API, show exactly “Controller or keyboard required”, a working settings control, no engine touch layer, and attract still running. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/ux.log`.\\n7. gating: Serve the deliverable with `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (record the printed port and PID). Run the AI Hub harness scenarios from PLAN.md §16 (`players=2&auto=1`, `players=4` with latency 150, jitter 50, loss 5, `scenario=host-leave`, `scenario=server`) and require `window.__hubCheck.pass` plus zero state-hash mismatches. Also open the game with no session and confirm the title flow. Stop that server by its PID. A Node test of the shipped sequencer (host plus three clients, latency, jitter, 5% loss on unreliable input, a leaver) must keep hashes equal across 3600 ticks. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`. Run the four-browser harness alone. If earlyoom kills it, capture that text and retry that run once; if it still dies, the 2-player harness plus the Node sequencer test are the bar only when `{SCRATCH}/hub-oom.log` shows the kill. Do not claim the 4-player run passed.\\n8. gating: Load the HTML in Firefox and in WebKit with an iPhone profile (system Python Playwright 1.63, no browser install). Pass only if both show the plain WebGPU-required message and do not run the operation. Capture the visible text to `{SCRATCH}/nowebgpu.log`. This is not iOS Safari.\\n9. evidence: Engine shots at 1920×1080 of the title, board, a story scene, pause, dev mode, handbook, and the field in each case, under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Absence does not fail the goal when steps 1–8 passed.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- FPS targets, optimization, or raising sheet density past what software WebGPU can keep up in a session.\\n30→- Any campaign past the eight story cases, the Gala Shift, and Free Surgery, or Trauma Team specialties (endoscopy, orthopedics, forensics, diagnosis).\\n- Source names, the star gesture, copied levels, sentences, UI, or music. A physical DualSense, a real second person, a listener, a television, or real iOS Safari.\\n- Touch-screen surgery controls, online play other than AI Hub shared-world, new npm dependencies, and any edit to engine sources.\\n- Exact untuned coefficients from PLAN.md where it says numbers are starting values.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work folder only. Other agents may use sibling folders, shared ports, and `/tmp`; game files stay in this folder. A short `/tmp` `TMPDIR` is allowed only for tool sockets, and it is removed at the end.\\n- Dev subfolder: `git clone` of `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge` (full workspace, not the lean page; `docs/API.md` wins if HEAD is newer than `e7a1243`), then `npm ci`. Game code, data, tests, and the verify script are added on top. Chromium for engine tools comes from the existing Playwright cache via `CHROMIUM_PATH`. Do not run `scripts/setup.sh` or `playwright install`.\\n- AI Hub client from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub` (`session.js`), pasted unmodified. Harness files used only under the dev folder’s test output.\\n- Hand-ins in the work-folder root: the HTML deliverable, `exit-interview.json`, `PROGRESS.md`.\\n40→- Defining source mechanics (Wikipedia; StrategyWiki gameplay page) are first-person timed surgery, vitals, a radial of scalpel, forceps, suture, drain, laser, syringe, gel, and scanner, action grades and letter ranks, a time-slow gift and a second gift that rewards clean actions, defibrillator, parasite bosses, and visual-novel scenes between operations. PLAN.md already remaps these to original names; criteria use that remap.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\n- Keep operation rules, strains, scoring, the flow between scenes, the bot, and the lockstep sequencer as pure logic with no DOM, no clock wall time, and no `Math.random`. Tests import that logic and feed the same hands intent a pad would. Rendering, HTML screens, and Web Audio only reflect events the sim already emitted.\\n- Author cases, tools, strains, and story as data the Handbook, the bot, and the refusal lines all read, so a missing tool or target pair fails a test.\\n- Add `npm run verify` early, as PLAN.md §19’s skeleton, and point browser checks at the single HTML file once the one-file build exists. Prefer `file://` for the game; bind `127.0.0.1` on an OS-chosen port only for the hub harness, and stop that PID only.\\n- Do not prescribe a different stack. The engine stays read-only; game code must not import three.js. Gameplay pads are read from `navigator.getGamepads` because the engine’s pad device is single-player.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [x] Choose the title by generating 50 riffs and picking with a real RNG; create the dev subfolder; clone the engine; `npm ci`; add `npm run verify`; confirm a one-file HTML boots from `file://`.\\n50→- [x] Build the living operating field, cursor, hand, and one scalpel guide cut that already reads at 1080p; test field state from the sim.\\n- [x] Finish Case 1 (drain, forceps, suture, syringe, gel, radial, vitals, clock, ratings, failure, debrief) and a bot win.\\n- [x] Add title, board, story scene with gibberish voices, briefing, pause, settings, glyphs, saves, attract mode, and dev-mode sliders.\\n- [x] Add Cases 2 and 3 (scalpel, scanner, laser, sites, Code, Stillpoint) and the AI Hub session path, including the Node sequencer test and a harness run on the case that exists.\\n- [x] Add strains, flaps, Cases 4–8, couch seats, Gala Shift, Free Surgery, the rest of the story, songs, and field animations.\\n- [x] Tune until the bot clears the required seeds; run `npm run verify`; write the root HTML, `exit-interview.json`, About screen, and `PROGRESS.md`; stop only processes this work started and remove the temporary `TMPDIR`.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- PLAN.md asks for a very dense sheet and a four-browser harness on software WebGPU; earlyoom will kill the largest browser. Session sheet density may drop only on SwiftShader, and the 4-player harness must run alone. Step 7’s fallback applies only with a captured kill log.\\n- TASK.md allows CDN dependencies “if easier”; PLAN.md requires a self-contained file. Both are satisfied by inlining. The run id and hand-in locations are not optional.\\n60→- A plain headless WebGPU screenshot can be blank even when the frame is fine. The launch gate is the engine shot or game state. A blank result from that readback is an app defect.\\n- Firefox and WebKit here have no WebGPU, so they can only prove the plain fallback message, not the operation. That is the full check available on this computer.\\n- `my-3dge` HEAD may differ from the commit PLAN.md was written against. Follow `docs/API.md` rather than inventing engine APIs.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n- Operating sheet is 96 by 60 so four software-rendered copies fit in memory.\\n- Radial slow is fixed at 0.6 because the sim cannot read the view slider.\\n\",\"absolute_path\":\"/home/micha/.grok/sessions/%2Fhome%2Fmicha%2Fdev%2Fcc-matrix%2Fruns%2Fgrok-xhigh.build-v2__20261104-224731__x59c1d3%2Fwork/01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151/goal/plan.md\",\"offset\":null,\"limit\":200,\"raw_output\":\"# Plan: Build PLAN.md’s original surgical-action demo and hand in every TASK.md file this computer can produce\\n\\n## Goal kind\\ncode-change\\n\\n## Acceptance criteria\\n1. **Hand-ins match TASK.md.** The work-folder root contains one self-contained HTML deliverable (no CDN or other network fetch required to boot, including the AI Hub client inlined unmodified and first) whose basename is the TASK.md pattern `[GameTitle-SOURCE-grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731]`: GameTitle is the title actually shown on the title screen, SOURCE identifies Trauma Center: Second Opinion, and the run id `grok-4.7-xhigh-20261104-224731` is verbatim. Spaces or colons in those names are slug-normalized the same way in the filename and in the dev subfolder of that same basename. Beside the HTML is `exit-interview.json`, one JSON object in exactly TASK.md’s shape, every value filled (unknown only where it cannot be confirmed), `usage` exactly `added on import`. `aiHub.name` is the title-screen title; type Game; genre Simulation; source Trauma Center: Second Opinion; players 1–4. The same Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback, and Prompt Feedback appear on an in-game About/Credits screen (paged, big text), and usage is in an element `data-about=\\\"usage\\\"`. `PROGRESS.md` in the work folder records a finished game. The dev subfolder is a full my-3dge workspace with the game added on top and the engine’s tracked sources unmodified. A testing issue log and an engine-feedback note exist and are what the interview answers come from. No TODOs, placeholders, stubs, or lorem. None of PLAN.md §1’s never-use names, art, music, UI, levels, or sentences appear (the title may riff on the source name; “second opinion” only as an ordinary dialogue phrase).\\n\\n2. **The operation is recognizably Trauma Center play, under this plan’s original rules.** Checked from each case’s own start, not one chained marathon: first-person surgery on a living field, with a clock and vitals, lost if time runs out, if slips pass the limit, or if a flatline Code fails, and won only by a closing phase. Eight tools — scalpel, forceps, suture, drain, laser, syringe, gel, scanner — each visibly change tissue on a valid target (cut along a guide, pull a shard or mass, zigzag stitches, shrink a pool, burn, inject within a band, paint gel, reveal buried targets) and otherwise refuse with no penalty and a named right tool. Finished actions rate TEXTBOOK, CLEAN, ROUGH, or SLIP and feed a chain and ranks C, B, A, S, and ★. One to three sites keep deteriorating while the surgeon is elsewhere. Stillpoint slows field time; Second Pulse blocks vitals loss and heals on clean actions; both arm from a beat-timed three-press pulse-lock, with a one-press Simple Gift option; two gifts started within 1 second grant Consult. Five strains fight with a telegraph before each hit: Lull (quieting aura, cocoon, armored Matron), Solace (split by scalpel into smaller generations, mother under a flap), Reverie (hidden until scanned, decoys), Balm (lacquer blocks stitch and drain until the node dies), Mercy (mote shield, a heart-stop Code, then a burrow with a moving guide, tendrils, and a final shared Code). Eight story cases each introduce the new rule named in PLAN.md §8 (tools through Mercy), plus session case `gala-shift` and Free Surgery after the ending. Intern, Resident, and Attending scale drain, clock, parasite stats, and the slip limit. A bot using the same hands input as a player wins every story case on Resident from that case’s start. Numeric thresholds may be tuned; these categories and failure rules may not.\\n\\n3. **World, story, audio, and the field read as this hospital, not a generic sim.** The slice follows PLAN.md §9’s original cast and arc (Port Verity, Harbor Light General, Halcyon grafts, eight cases through the line CASE FILE REMAINS OPEN) in short earnest overheated medical-thriller lines, gibberish Animalese-style voices with large captions, skippable scenes, rank-based patient postcards, and multiple Drama Cuts. The operating field is a 3D sheet that breathes and beats, shows wounds, pools, and hands that swap tools, inside an operating room. Art starts from the engine’s native procedural look (full resolution, not the chunky pixel default) and adds original hospital and surgical assets; animation uses the engine’s system plus new story and field actions for the beats PLAN.md §12 lists. Every piece of music, effect, and voice is synthesized in code: no audio files, no `<audio>`, no sound data URLs. Music defaults to 10% volume.\\n\\n4. **Couch, controller-first screens, attract, dev mode, and the no-WebGPU / touch gates.** The same screens are playable with a standard-mapping pad (left stick cursor, right stick radial, R2 or Cross to use, L1/R1 cycle tools, L2 precision, Triangle gift, Square quick syringe, Circle drop, D-pad sites and hint, R3 swap where cases allow, Options pause, both triggers for paddles) and, natively, with mouse and keyboard as PLAN.md §10 lists (absolute mouse aim, LMB use, RMB radial, wheel and keys 1–8, WASD or arrows plus Enter). Prompts follow the last device: PlayStation glyphs for any pad by default, Xbox glyphs (A B X Y, LB RB LT RT) when that Settings option is on, keycaps and mouse icons for keyboard and mouse. After 10 seconds with no input on the title or front menus, a looping attract demo of the real game starts, any input exits it immediately, and it saves nothing. Pause opens Dev Mode (pad, mouse, and keyboard) whose Balance tab has live sliders for the main variables (vitals drain, bleeds, clock, slips, cursor, each tool rate, gift timing, strain stats, co-op health) and a reset to defaults. A Handbook, driven by the tool and target data, states what each instrument can and cannot do. Touch-only devices get no touch controls, the exact text “Controller or keyboard required”, a settings button, and attract underneath. If WebGPU is missing, a classic inline script shows a large plain message that the game needs WebGPU (and calls the hub fail hook in a session) instead of only the engine’s technical error.\\n\\n5. **On its own and in AI Hub, and the shipped page actually starts.** With no hub session the full story and local couch co-op for up to four seats run as a normal game, and About says network play is AI Hub only. In a session the page follows AI Hub shared-world: the `hub:session` meta tags from PLAN.md §16 (`players` 1-4, mode shared-world, minutes 5, the goal and howto strings, `hub:webgpu` required), no title menu, a pre-op card, ready without an extra press once the sim can run, the Gala Shift from the session seed, host-sequenced hands so every copy’s state hash matches, and a finish payload with standings. `npm run verify` is the one test command: it prints only failures and one summary line, and a green run reports zero failures. Loading the deliverable HTML from `file://` twice yields the same success: no page errors, the engine ready, the drawing surface at the requested size, and a substantially filled view of the title or an operation (a blank headless WebGPU screenshot is not this check; the engine’s own shot or the game’s state is). A driven pad or key input changes that observable.\\n\\n## Verification plan\\n1. gating: In the dev workspace, add or extend the engine’s existing `npm test` suite so it calls the shipped sim from a fresh start (not a copy of the rules). Assert tool-on-target effects and a refusal for every pair that has no effect, rating bands, vitals and clock failure, slip limit, flatline Code success and failure, other sites worsening while away, Stillpoint and Second Pulse, Consult, each strain’s distinguishing transition (including Mercy’s three phases), difficulty scaling, and balance sliders staying in range with reset restoring defaults. Capture the run to `{SCRATCH}/sim.log`. Pass only if those assertions pass.\\n2. gating: Same suite: for three seeds, the shipped bot wins each of the eight story cases on Resident from that case’s own start; Cases 1 and 8 also on Intern and Attending; a 2-seat and a 4-seat bot run finish. A separate test drives the shipped flow from the title through story, all eight operations (bot), debrief, credits, and Free Surgery unlocked, with no browser. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/bot.log`. Pass only if each case win and the ending unlock are reported, not merely that the process exited.\\n3. gating: Assert story and case data cover the §8 case list and §9 arc, every scene is 12–35 short boxes, postcard lines exist per rank, voice synthesis is deterministic per line, and a scan of game text finds no never-use names and no TODO, placeholder, or lorem. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/content.log`.\\n4. gating: From the dev folder run `npm run verify` (it must set a short `TMPDIR` itself). Capture the full log to `{SCRATCH}/verify.log`. Pass only if the process exits 0 and the last line is `verify: <N> passed, 0 failed`. The command must include the sim tests above, the engine check, and browser checks against the root deliverable HTML, not only against a dev server.\\n5. gating: Launch the deliverable HTML from `file://` twice, sequentially, in headless Chromium via the already-installed browser (`CHROMIUM_PATH`, WebGPU software flags, short `TMPDIR`, no `playwright install`). Each run: zero page errors, engine ready, canvas pixel size equal to the requested size, and either the engine shot path or in-page state shows a substantially filled title or operating field (painted coverage of most of the surface, not a few pixels). Inject a standard-mapping stub pad or a key and assert a real change (menu focus moves, or an operation cursor or tool changes). Write `{SCRATCH}/launch-1/` and `{SCRATCH}/launch-2/` (log, state, shot). Both runs must succeed the same way. If the browser process cannot start, save that output as `{SCRATCH}/launch-error.log`; sim tests in steps 1–3 then stand as the launch bar. A shot that succeeds and is blank or nearly empty is a failure, not an unavailable readback. Do not treat a known-blank WebGPU canvas bitmap from a plain screenshot as the field when the engine shot or state readback shows the scene.\\n6. gating: Against that same HTML, scripted checks (DOM and engine state, not a watched playthrough) must show: attract starts after 10 seconds of no input and a key, a mouse move, a pad button, and a stick past the dead zone each exit it with nothing saved; glyphs follow the last device and the Xbox setting; Dev Mode opens from pause, one slider changes the live value, reset restores it; two stub pads claim two couch seats and move two cursors; an AnalyserNode or offline render shows music present at the default 0.1 music volume and a synthesized effect, and the file contains no audio file, `<audio>` tag, or audio data URL. Touch phone and tablet profiles, tapped via the touchscreen API, show exactly “Controller or keyboard required”, a working settings control, no engine touch layer, and attract still running. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/ux.log`.\\n7. gating: Serve the deliverable with `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (record the printed port and PID). Run the AI Hub harness scenarios from PLAN.md §16 (`players=2&auto=1`, `players=4` with latency 150, jitter 50, loss 5, `scenario=host-leave`, `scenario=server`) and require `window.__hubCheck.pass` plus zero state-hash mismatches. Also open the game with no session and confirm the title flow. Stop that server by its PID. A Node test of the shipped sequencer (host plus three clients, latency, jitter, 5% loss on unreliable input, a leaver) must keep hashes equal across 3600 ticks. Capture to `{SCRATCH}/hub.log`. Run the four-browser harness alone. If earlyoom kills it, capture that text and retry that run once; if it still dies, the 2-player harness plus the Node sequencer test are the bar only when `{SCRATCH}/hub-oom.log` shows the kill. Do not claim the 4-player run passed.\\n8. gating: Load the HTML in Firefox and in WebKit with an iPhone profile (system Python Playwright 1.63, no browser install). Pass only if both show the plain WebGPU-required message and do not run the operation. Capture the visible text to `{SCRATCH}/nowebgpu.log`. This is not iOS Safari.\\n9. evidence: Engine shots at 1920×1080 of the title, board, a story scene, pause, dev mode, handbook, and the field in each case, under `{SCRATCH}/shots/`. Absence does not fail the goal when steps 1–8 passed.\\n\\n## Non-goals\\n- FPS targets, optimization, or raising sheet density past what software WebGPU can keep up in a session.\\n- Any campaign past the eight story cases, the Gala Shift, and Free Surgery, or Trauma Team specialties (endoscopy, orthopedics, forensics, diagnosis).\\n- Source names, the star gesture, copied levels, sentences, UI, or music. A physical DualSense, a real second person, a listener, a television, or real iOS Safari.\\n- Touch-screen surgery controls, online play other than AI Hub shared-world, new npm dependencies, and any edit to engine sources.\\n- Exact untuned coefficients from PLAN.md where it says numbers are starting values.\\n\\n## Assumed scope\\n- Work folder only. Other agents may use sibling folders, shared ports, and `/tmp`; game files stay in this folder. A short `/tmp` `TMPDIR` is allowed only for tool sockets, and it is removed at the end.\\n- Dev subfolder: `git clone` of `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3dge` (full workspace, not the lean page; `docs/API.md` wins if HEAD is newer than `e7a1243`), then `npm ci`. Game code, data, tests, and the verify script are added on top. Chromium for engine tools comes from the existing Playwright cache via `CHROMIUM_PATH`. Do not run `scripts/setup.sh` or `playwright install`.\\n- AI Hub client from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub` (`session.js`), pasted unmodified. Harness files used only under the dev folder’s test output.\\n- Hand-ins in the work-folder root: the HTML deliverable, `exit-interview.json`, `PROGRESS.md`.\\n- Defining source mechanics (Wikipedia; StrategyWiki gameplay page) are first-person timed surgery, vitals, a radial of scalpel, forceps, suture, drain, laser, syringe, gel, and scanner, action grades and letter ranks, a time-slow gift and a second gift that rewards clean actions, defibrillator, parasite bosses, and visual-novel scenes between operations. PLAN.md already remaps these to original names; criteria use that remap.\\n\\n## Implementation approach\\n- Keep operation rules, strains, scoring, the flow between scenes, the bot, and the lockstep sequencer as pure logic with no DOM, no clock wall time, and no `Math.random`. Tests import that logic and feed the same hands intent a pad would. Rendering, HTML screens, and Web Audio only reflect events the sim already emitted.\\n- Author cases, tools, strains, and story as data the Handbook, the bot, and the refusal lines all read, so a missing tool or target pair fails a test.\\n- Add `npm run verify` early, as PLAN.md §19’s skeleton, and point browser checks at the single HTML file once the one-file build exists. Prefer `file://` for the game; bind `127.0.0.1` on an OS-chosen port only for the hub harness, and stop that PID only.\\n- Do not prescribe a different stack. The engine stays read-only; game code must not import three.js. Gameplay pads are read from `navigator.getGamepads` because the engine’s pad device is single-player.\\n\\n## Task checklist\\n- [x] Choose the title by generating 50 riffs and picking with a real RNG; create the dev subfolder; clone the engine; `npm ci`; add `npm run verify`; confirm a one-file HTML boots from `file://`.\\n- [x] Build the living operating field, cursor, hand, and one scalpel guide cut that already reads at 1080p; test field state from the sim.\\n- [x] Finish Case 1 (drain, forceps, suture, syringe, gel, radial, vitals, clock, ratings, failure, debrief) and a bot win.\\n- [x] Add title, board, story scene with gibberish voices, briefing, pause, settings, glyphs, saves, attract mode, and dev-mode sliders.\\n- [x] Add Cases 2 and 3 (scalpel, scanner, laser, sites, Code, Stillpoint) and the AI Hub session path, including the Node sequencer test and a harness run on the case that exists.\\n- [x] Add strains, flaps, Cases 4–8, couch seats, Gala Shift, Free Surgery, the rest of the story, songs, and field animations.\\n- [x] Tune until the bot clears the required seeds; run `npm run verify`; write the root HTML, `exit-interview.json`, About screen, and `PROGRESS.md`; stop only processes this work started and remove the temporary `TMPDIR`.\\n\\n## Risks / Contradictions\\n- PLAN.md asks for a very dense sheet and a four-browser harness on software WebGPU; earlyoom will kill the largest browser. Session sheet density may drop only on SwiftShader, and the 4-player harness must run alone. Step 7’s fallback applies only with a captured kill log.\\n- TASK.md allows CDN dependencies “if easier”; PLAN.md requires a self-contained file. Both are satisfied by inlining. The run id and hand-in locations are not optional.\\n- A plain headless WebGPU screenshot can be blank even when the frame is fine. The launch gate is the engine shot or game state. A blank result from that readback is an app defect.\\n- Firefox and WebKit here have no WebGPU, so they can only prove the plain fallback message, not the operation. That is the full check available on this computer.\\n- `my-3dge` HEAD may differ from the commit PLAN.md was written against. Follow `docs/API.md` rather than inventing engine APIs.\\n\\n## Deviations\\n- Operating sheet is 96 by 60 so four software-rendered copies fit in memory.\\n- Radial slow is fixed at 0.6 because the sim cannot read the view slider.\\n\",\"total_lines\":66}}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"01a1aa3f-84ea-7700-ba58-234d6f85e151","uuid":"809e22ff-bc66-490b-b3d7-e7d862dbc591"}
